\mt1 The Book of Genesis, in Hebrew Beresith. בראשית
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE BOOK OF GENESIS.
\ip This first Book of holy Scripture, called \it Genesis\it*, which signifieth \it birth\it* or \it beginning\it*, was written by Moyses, when he was designed by God to instruct and rule the children of Israel: As also the \f - \ft Genesis written by Moyses.\f* other four books following. The Author and authority of all which five books were ever \f - \ft Always authentical.\f* acknowledged by the faithful, both of the old and new Testament: and so accounted and esteemed by tradition, till Christ and his Apostles: who also confirmed them by their testimonies \f - \ft So known by Tradition, confirmed by Christ, alleged also by the Apostles.\f* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 19. Heb. 11. Iacob. 2. 1. Pet. 2. 2. Pet. 2.\+it*\f* and allegations of the same, as of holy Scriptures. From the creation until Moyses writ (which was above two thousand and four hundred years) the Church exercised Religion by revelations \f - \ft Religion revealed to special persons and so observed by traditions.\f* made to certain Patriarchs, and by Traditions from man to man, without any Scriptures or law written. But the peculiar people of God being more visibly separated from other nations, and many errors abounding in the world, God would for correction and confutation thereof have his will \f - \ft \+it S. Aug. ques. vet & novi Testam. cap. 3.\+it*\f* made further known to his children, and so remain amongst them in written record, by his faithful servant and Prophet Moyses. Who therefore declareth the Author and beginning of all things, \f - \ft Why Scripture was written.\f* that is, How all creatures were made by God, and of him have their being, and by him only are conserved. He teacheth expressly that there is one only God, against those that imagined and \f - \ft What Moyses specially sheweth in this book.\f* brought into the phantasies of men many gods. That the whole or universal substance of heaven and earth, with their ornaments and accidents, were made in time; against those that thought the first foundation thereof had ever been. That God doth govern the same; against those that say, all is ruled by destiny or by the stars, and not by the continual providence of God. That God is a rewarder of the good, and a punisher of evil, which sinners seem either not to know, or grossly to forget. And that God created all for man's use and benefit, which should make us grateful. \f - \ft Man most\f* Wherefore holy Moyses more particularly describeth the beginning of man; what he was at first; how he fell; how all mankind is come of one man: deducing the Genealogy of Adam, especially to Noe. Then how men being more and more defiled upon the earth, with wicked, especially \f - \ft The right line from Adam to Noe. particularly described.\f* carnal sins, were by God's just wrath drowned with an universal flood.
\ip Again, how a few reserved persons multiplied the world anew. But this offspring also falling into many sins, especially Idolatry and spiritual fornication, as those of the first age did to carnal offences, God still conserved some faithful and true servants. Of which Moyses specially \f - \ft \+it Gen. 10.\+it*\f* pursueth the line of Noe by Sem his first begotten son. Then describeth the particular \f - \ft The principal Patriarchs from Noe to the 12 sons of Israel.\f* vocations, lives, manners, notable sayings, and noble facts, with sincere religion of Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, and other holy Patriarchs: who lived before the written law. Likewise upon what occasion, and in what manner Jacob, otherwise called Israel, with all his progeny, descended from the Land of Canaan into Aegypt, and were there entertained. So this book containeth the history of two thousand three hundred and odd years. And it may be divided into eight parts. The \f - \ft This book divided into eight parts.\f* \f - \ft 1.\f* first containeth the Creation of Heaven and Earth, & other Creatures, and lastly of Man. chap. 1. & 2. The second part is of the transgression & fall of man, & his casting out of Paradise, of \f - \ft 2.\f* multiplication of men, and of sin, though still some were just, of the general flood, that drowned all except eight persons, & few other living creatures of the earth, from the third chap. to the 8. \f - \ft 3.\f*
\ip The third part is of the new increase and multiplication of the same, from the 8. chap. to the 11.
\ip The fourth, of the confusion of tongues and the division of nations, in the 11. chap. The \f - \ft 4.\f* \f - \ft 5.\f* fifth relateth Abraham's going forth of his country, God's promise, that in his seed all Nations should be blessed, & the commandment of Circumcision, from the 12. chap. to the 21. The \f - \ft 6.\f* sixth part recounteth the progeny, and other blessings, especially the great virtues of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, from the 21. chap. to the 37. The seventh part reporteth the selling of Joseph \f - \ft 7.\f* into Aegypt, and his advancement there, from the 37. chap. to the 46. The eighth and last \f - \ft 8.\f* part is of Jacob, and his progenies going into Aegypt, their entertainment there, and of Jacob's and finally of Joseph's death in the five last chapters.
\is THE SUM AND PARTITION OF THE HOLY BIBLE. With a brief note on the Canonical and Apocryphal Books.
\ip By the uniform consent of all learned Divines, the holy Bible, or written Word of God, \f - \ft How the holy Scriptures contain all knowledge necessary to salvation. The old and the new Testament shew the same God, Christ, Church and other Mysteries of Religion. The old more obscurely, & with less helps. The new more expressly and yieldeth more grace.\f* \f - \ft \+it S. Hierom Epist. ad Paulin. de omnib. S. Scrip. libris. S. Aug. prefat. in specul. li. 2. de doct. Christ. ca. 9. li. 1. de Gen. contra\+it*\f* containeth expressed or implied all things that man is to believe, to observe, and to avoid, for obtaining of eternal salvation. That is, all matters of faith and manners, by which we may know and serve God, and so be spiritually joined with him in this life, and in eternity. For both the old and new Testament propose and testify unto us one and the same God, the same Christ, the same Church, and other Mysteries of our belief, not differing in substance, but in manners of uttering; the Old more obscurely in figures and prophecies fortelling those things, which the New declareth (in great part) as done and performed. Whereupon saith S. Augustine: \it In the Old Testament the New lieth hidden; and in the New the Old lieth open\it*. And touching their names, wherein appeareth difference, \it the one\it* (saith the same Doctor) \it is called the old Testament, either because it proposeth promises of temporal things\it* (wherewith our old corruptness is allured) \it or in respect of the New, by which it is fulfilled, and in some part abolished. The other is called the New, because by it man is renewed, and hath promise of eternal life,\it* which shall never wax old nor decay. Likewise S. Gregory the Great testifieth this conformity and correspondence between the Old and New Testament, \f - \ft Apocryphal of two sorts. 1. Not declared canonical. 2. Rejected as erroneous.\f* affirming that the same is signified by the Prophet Ezechiel's vision of a wheel, which had four faces, or appearance of four wheels, the shape whereof \it was, as it were, a wheel in the midst of a wheel. What is this\it*, saith he, \it Nisi quod in Testamenti veteris litera Testamentum novum latuit per Allegoriam? but that in the letter of the old Testament, the New lay hidden by an Allegory?\it*
\ip And as the same is the sum and subject of both Testaments so both are divided (for the more principal parts thereof) into four sorts of Books: \it Legal, Historical, Sapiential, & Prophetical\it*. \f - \ft In both Testaments, are four sorts of Books.\f* The Legal books of the old Testament are the five Books of Moyses. \it Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus,\it* \f - \ft Legal.\f* \it Numeri\it*, and \it Deuteronomy\it*; whereto answer in the new Testament the four \it Gospels\it* of \it Saint Matthew, S. Mark, S. Luke\it*, and \it S. John\it*. Historical books of the old Testament are the Books of \f - \ft \+it S. Gregr. ho. 6 in Ezech. 1.\+it*\f* \it Iosue, Judges, Ruth\it*, four books of \it Kings\it*, two of \it Paralipomenon, Esdras\it*, with \it Nehemias, Tobias, Judith, Hester, Job\it*, and two of the \it Machabees\it*; unto which in the new Testament answer \it the Acts of the Apostles. Sapiential\it* of the old Testament are the \it Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Canticles, Book of\it* \f - \ft Sapiential.\f* \it wisdom\it*, and \it Ecclesiasticus\it*; and of like sort are in the new Testament the Epistles of S. Paul and of other Apostles. \it Prophetical\it* books are \it David's Psalter\it* (which is also \it Sapiential\it*, yea likewise \f - \ft Prophetical.\f* \it Legal\it* and \it Historical\it* ) the Books of \it Isaias, Jeremias\it*, with \it Baruch, Ezechiel, Daniel\it*, the twelve less Prophets, \it Osea, Ioel, Amos, Abdias, Ionas, Michaeas, Nahum, Abacuc, Sophonias, Aggaeus, Zacharias, Malachias\it*. And in the new Testament, \it the Apocalypse\it* of S. John the Apostle.
\ip All these Books are undoubtedly Canonical, as the Authors cited in the inner margent \f - \ft All these books recited are Canonical, and of infallible truth.\f* \f - \ft \+it Conc. Carth. An. D. 419. Conc. Laodic. cap. 59. Florent. Instruct. Armen. decret. 7. Trident. Sess. 4. S. Atha. in Synop. S. Aug. li. 2. doct. Christ. c. 8. Isidor. li. 6. Etymol. c. 1. & alibi. Nicep. l. 4. cap. 15. Euseb. l. 5. c. 8.\+it*\f* testify. And consequently all, and all the parts thereof, are of infallible truth. For otherwise, as S. Augustin teacheth, if any part were false or doubtful, all were uncertain. \it Once admitting falsehood\it* (saith he Epist. 8. ad Hieron.) \it in such sovereign authority, no parcel of these books should remain, which any way should seem hard to manners, or incredible to believe, but it might by this most pernicious rule be turned to an officious fiction of the author\it*. That is: If any error could be committed by the authors of Scriptures, either through ignorance, oblivion, or any other human frailty, whatsoever were produced, exception might be taken, and question made, whether the author had erred or no. True it is, that \it some of these books\it* (as we shall particularly discuss in their places) were sometimes doubted of by some Catholics, and called \it Apocryphal\it*, in that sense as the word \f - \ft Historical.\f* properly signifieth \it hidden\it*, or \it not apparent\it*. So \it S. Hierom\it* (in his prologue before the Latin Bible) \it calleth diverse books Apocryphal\it*, being not so evident, whether they were Divine Scripture, because they were not in the Jews' Cannon, nor at first in the Church's Canon, but were never rejected as false or erroneous. In which sense \it the Prayers of Manasses\it*, the \it third\it* book of \it Esdras, and\it* third of \it Machabees are yet called Apocryphal\it*. As for the fourth of Esdras, and fourth of Machabees there is more doubt. But diverse others, as the \it book ascribed to Enoch\it*, the \it Gospels\it* of \it S. Andrew, S. Thomas, S. Bartholomew\it*, and the like recited by S. Gelasius (Decreto de libris Ecclesiasticis dist. 15. Can. Sancta Romana) S. Innocentius the first (Epist. 3.) S. Hierom, Ep. ad Laetam, S. Augustin. l. 15. cap. 23. de civit. Dei. Origen homi. 2. in Cantica, are in a worse sense called Apocryphal, and are rejected as containing manifest errors, or feigned by Heretics Neither can a Christian Catholic be otherwise assured, which Books are Divine and Canonical Scriptures, but by declaration of the Catholic Church, which without interruption succeedeth the Apostles, to whom \it our Saviour\it* \f - \ft The Holy Ghost declareth by the Church which Books are Divine Scriptures.\f* \it promised, and sent the Holy-Ghost, to teach all truth\it*. For if any thing more than others, \f - \ft \+it Mat. 28. Ioan 14. 16. Act. 2. 20. 1. Tim. 3.\+it*\f* assuredly one chief and most necessary point is, to know and declare which Books are God's holy word, being of most singular importance.
\is THE SUM OF THE OLD TESTAMENT, as it is distinguished from the new.
\ip Notwithstanding the subject, and general argument of both Testaments is one and the same in substance, as is already said, yet they differ in time, in manner of uttering of Mysteries, in \f - \ft The old and new Testament differ in time. In manner of uttering. Variety of Precepts. Promises. Means.\f* variety of precepts and promises, also in means to observe the things exacted, & to attain to the end proposed. In regard whereof S. Hierom saith: \it Lex Moysi & omne vetus instrumentum\it* \f - \ft \+it Tomo. 3. quest. 10. Algasiae.\+it*\f* \it elementa mundi intelliguntur, quibus quasi elementis & Religionis exordiis Deum discimus. The law of Moyses and all the old Testament are understood the elements of the world, by which, as by first rudiments and beginnings, we learn to know God\it*. For that in it we have first the law of nature, and afterwards a law written, with premises of temporal rewards; as long life, land flowing with milk and honey, and the like; but \it it brought nothing to perfection\it*, as S. Paul saith, \it when gifts and\it* \f - \ft \+it Hebr. 7. 9. 10.\+it*\f* \it hosts were offered, which could not according to conscience make the observer perfect\it*. For the helps of that time were but \it infirma & egena elementa: weak & poor elements.\it* Likewise in general, \f - \ft \+it Gal. 4.\+it*\f* touching the punishments that sometimes happened to the people of the old Testament, when they transgressed, the same Apostle affirmeth, that \it all the same chanced to them in figure, and\it* \f - \ft The old Testament containeth figure of the new.\f* \f - \ft \+it 1. Cor. 10.\+it*\f* \it are written for our correption, upon whom the ends of the world are come\it*. So that the old Testament or \it Law, was but our pedagogue in Christ\it*. Yet it setteth forth to us the whole course of God's \f - \ft \+it Gal. 3.\+it*\f* Church, for the space of four thousand years, that is, from the beginning of the world until Christ our Redeemer, which Divines divide into six Ages , wherein was variety and change of her \f - \ft A continual visible Church from the beginning of the world to Christ.\f* \f - \ft Divided into six ages.\f* state, three under the Law of nature, and three others under the written Law. The seventh \f - \ft The same Mystical body, but\f* and last Age being this time of grace (wherein we now are) from Christ, to the day of general Judgement: as the world was made in six days, and in the seventh God is said to have rested, and therefore sanctified it, in other sort than the former six. The eighth will be after the Resurrection, during for all eternity.
\ip Which six Ages of the ancient Church and old Testament, are thus distributed. The first \f - \ft \+it Gen. 1. Gen. 8. Gen. 12. Genebrard. Chronolog. S. Aug. l. 15. c. 8. civit. Gal. 3. Exod. 16. 3. Reg. 6. 3. Reg. 7. 1. Esdra. 1.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The first age continued 1656. years.\f* from the Creation to Noe's flood, contained the space of 1656. years. The second from the \f - \ft The second 368. or 398.\f* flood to the going of Abraham out of his country, 368. or counting Cainan (Gen. 11. iuxta 72. and Luc. 3.) 398. years. The third from Abraham his going forth of his country, to the parting of the \f - \ft The third about. 430.\f* children of Israel out of Aegypt. Which some count to have continued 720. years, others (whom we follow) but 430. And thus far in the law of nature before the written law. The fourth age dured, \f - \ft The fourth 480.\f* 480. years. from the delivery of the children of Israel forth of Aegypt, to the foundation of the Temple in Hierusalem. The fifth Age was from the foundation of the Temple, to the captivity \f - \ft The fifth 430. different in state.\f* and transmigration of the Jews into Babylon, about 430. years. And the sixth Age dured about \f - \ft The sixth, near 640.\f* 640. years, from the Captivity of Babylon to Christ. In all which times God was acknowledged \f - \ft All the time from the creation to Christ about 4000. years.\f* and rightly served, by a continual visible Church, with true religion; the same and no other, which now that Church holdeth, that is called and known by the name of Catholic. As we intend, by God's assistance, to shew by brief Annotations, concerning diverse particular points now in Controversy, as the holy Text giveth occasion. And especially by way of recapitulation after every one of the six Ages, when we come to those passages in the History, where the same are ended.
\is OF MOYSES THE AUTHOR OF THE five first books.
\ip \it Moyses\it* (so called because he was taken from the water, as the name signifieth) was born \f - \ft MOYSES. signifieth, taken from the water.\f* \f - \ft \+it Exod. 6. Num. 26. 1. Par. 6. Ioseph. li. 2. Antiq. cap. 9. S. Aug. ser. 88. de temp. S. Greg. oratio. in laudem Basilij magni. S. Aug. li. 18. civit. c. 39.\+it*\f* in Aegypt, the son of \it Amram\it*, the son of \it Caath\it*, the son of \it Levi\it* the Patriarch, and so of \it Jacob, Isaac\it*, and \it Abraham\it*. His marvelous delivery from drowning, his education, excellent form, singular wisdom, heroical virtues, rare dexterity in all affairs, and whole life most admirable, are gathered out of holy Scriptures, by S. Gregory Bishop of Nyssen, into a brief Sum, most worthy to be read, but too large for this place. He was born about the year of the world two thousand four \f - \ft The excellency of Moyses.\f* hundred, long before all profane writers, yea before many of the Paynim's false gods, as S. Augustin declareth in diverse places of his most excellent book entitled \it of the City of God\it*. He lived in this world 120. years: Of which 40. were in Pharao's Court as the adopted son of Pharao's daughter: forty in banishment from Aegypt in Madian: and forty more he governed the people of Israel. His singular praises are also briefly touched in the last chapter of Deuteronomy, added by Iosue, and in the book of Ecclesiasticus. He died in the desert, and was buried in the vale of \f - \ft \+it Deut. 34. Eccli. 45. Glos. ord.\+it*\f* Moab, so secretly that no mortal man knew his sepulchre, lest the Jews, who were very prone \f - \ft His sepulchre not known to any man.\f* to Idolatry, should have adored his body with divine honour, for the greatness and multitude of his miracles, and for the singular estimation they had of him for the same.
\c 1
\cd God created heaven and earth, and all things therein; distinguishing and beautifying the same. Last of all, the sixth day he createth man; to whom he subjecteth all corporal things of this inferior world. \f - \ft The first part. Of the creation of all things. The Church readeth this book in her Office from Septuagesima till Passion Sunday. Also this first chapter & beginning of the second on Easter Eve before Mass.\f*
\p
\v 1 In \x - \xt Act. 14, 15. 17, 24.\x* the beginning \sc GOD\sc* created heaven and earth. \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq In the beginning. \ft ¹ Holy Moyses telleth what was done in the beginning of the world, and so forward even till his own time, writing above two thousand and four hundred years after the beginning. All which being incomprehesible by human wit or discourse, he knew partly by revelations from God, for he had the gift of Prophecy in most excellent sort; partly by traditions from his elders, who learned of their fathers. For until that time the Church had only traditions of such things as were revealed to special men, whereby we see the great authority of traditions, before there were scriptures. And since Scriptures were written they are also necessary, for three special reasons. ² First, for that we are only assured by tradition of the Church, that those books are indeed holy Scriptures, which are so accounted, and not by the Scripture it-self, for that were to prove the same by the same, until we be assured of some part, that proveth some other parts. And this made S. Augustin to say plainly, ³ that he could not believe the Gospel, except the Church told him which is the Gospel. ⁴ Secondly, holy Scriptures being once known to be the word of God, & so of most eminent authority ⁵ of all writings in the world, as S. Augustin, S. Hierome, & all other Fathers agree, yet for the true understanding of the same, both the Scripture it-self, and the ancient Fathers remit us to the Church, namely to those in the Church that are appointed by God's ordinance, in the high place that he hath chosen. Which were the High Priests in the old Testament, as appeareth Deut. 17. Mat. 23 Joan. 11. And in the new Testament, S. Peter & his Successors, for whom Christ prayed that his faith should not fail: & therefore commanded him to confirm his brethren, Luc. 22. ⁶ Thirdly, for things not expressed in particular in holy Scripture, the Scripture ⁷ & Fathers do likewise remit us to traditions, and to the judgement & testimony of the Church. Christ saying to his Apostles: He that heareth you heareth me. The Apostles doubted not to say: It seemed good to the Holy Ghost and to us. And S. Paul willed the Thessalonians to hold the traditions, which they had learned, whether it were by word, or by his Epistle. \fq ¹ \ft The Church had only traditions and no Scripture above 2400. years. \fq ² \ft Traditions necessary for three causes. 1. \fq ³ \ft \+it Cont. Epist. fund. c. 5.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft 2. \fq ⁵ \ft Scripture of most eminent authority. \fq ⁶ \ft 3. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it (a) Luc. 10, 16. Act. 15, 28. 2. Thess. 2.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq In the beginning God made heaven and earth. \ft ¹ All writers ² ancient & later find such difficulties in these first chapters, that some, otherwise very learned, have thought it not possible to understand the same according to the proper and usual signification of the words, as the letter may seem to sound, but expound all allegorically, as that by the waters above the firmament should be understood the blessed Angels, by the waters under the firmament wicked spirits, and the like. So did Origen and diverse that follow him therein. Yea S. Augustin in his books upon Genesis against the Manichees, written shortly after his conversion, when he could not find as he desired a good and probable sense agreeable to the words in their proper signification, expounded them mystically, but afterwards in his other books de Genesi ad literam, he gratefully acknowledgeth that God had given him further sight therein, and that now he supposed he could interpret all according to the proper signification of the words: yet so that he durst not nor would not addict himself to one sense, but that he was ready to embrace another, lest by sticking to his own judgement he might fail. So likewise S. Basil, S. Chrysostom, S. Ambrose, S. Hierome, S. Bede, and other greatest Doctors found and confessed great difficulties in these first chapters, which they with much study endeavoured to explicate. And therefore it is a wonder to see our Protestants and Puritans hold this Paradox, that Scripture are easy to be understood. Whereas both by testimony of those that have indeed studied & laboured in them, and by a little due consideration the contrary is most evident. ³ ⁴ For whosoever will look into the holy Scriptures, shall find that sometimes in shew one place seemeth contrary to another; sometimes the letter & phrase are obscure & ambiguous; sometimes the sentences unperfect. Again many speeches are prophetical, many parabolical, metaphorical, and uttered under other tropes & figures, and that in the literal sense. ⁵ Moreover there are three spiritual senses besides the literal, very frequent in holy Scripture. Allegorical, pertaining to Christ and the Church; Moral, pertaining to manners; and Anagogical, pertaining to the next life. As this word, Jerusalem, literally signifieth the head city of Jewry: Morally the soul of man: Allegorically the Church militant: and Anagogically the Church triumphant. And sometimes this (and the like of others) metaphorically in the literal sense signifieth the Church militant, & not the city of Jewry, as in the 12. chapter of the Hebrews: and sometimes the Church triumphant, as in the 21. of the Apocalypse. \fq ¹ \ft Scriptures hard. \fq ² \ft \+it (b) Ori. super. Gen. c. 1. Aug. l. 2. de Gen. cont. Manich. ca. 2. lib. 1. c. 18. & li. 8. c. 2. Bas. ho. 9. in Genes. Chriso. epist. 44. Amb. & Beda in examen. Jerome. Ep. ad Eust.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Why Scriptures are hard. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Gen. 1. v. 3. & 14. Exo. 20. 5. & 18. v. 20. Joan. 8, 25. Rom 8.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Three spiritual senses besides the Literal. Allegorical. Moral. Anagogical.\ef*
\v 2 \x - \xt Psal. 32, 6. 135, 5. Eccl. 10, 1.\x* And the earth was void and vacant, and darkness was upon the face of the depth: and the Spirit of God moved over the waters. \ef - \fr 1.2 \fq The spirit of God. \ft In the Hebrew it is signified, that the Spirit of God was on the waters to make them fertile, for that fishes & birds were to be procreated thereof; the word is merabepheth, incubabat, sat upon, to produce fruit (saith S. Hierom. from the waters, as a hen by her heat produceth life in the eggs. ¹ And the same S. Hierom, ² & before him Tertullian teach, that this was a figure of Baptism, which consisteth of water & the Holy Ghost. For as water in the beginning of the world received a certain vital virtue of the Holy Ghost to procreate new men. Whereupon Tertullian calleth Christians fishes, ³ because they are gotten from the waters, & thence have their first spiritual life. Let it not therefore seem strange (saith he) that in Baptism waters give life. \fq ¹ \ft A figure of Baptism. \fq ² \ft \+it Hier. Epist. 83. ad Ocea. Tert. de Baptis.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Christians called fishes.\ef*
\v 3 And God said: Be light made. And light was made.
\v 4 And God saw the light that it was good: and he divided the light from the darkness. \x - \xt Heb. 11, 3.\x*
\v 5 And he called the light, Day, and the darkness, Night: and there was evening & morning, that made one day.
\v 6 \x - \xt Job. 38. Jer. 10, 13.\x* God also said: Be \f a \fr 1.6 \ft The firmament is all the space from the earth to the highest stars: the lowest part divideth between the waters on the earth and the waters in the air. S. Aug. l. 11. de Gen. ad lit c. 4.\f* a firmament made amidst the waters: and let it divide between waters and waters.
\v 7 And God made a firmament, and divided the waters that were under the firmament, from those that were above the firmament. And it was so done.
\v 8 And God called the firmament, \f b \fr 1.8 \ft Likewise heaven is all the space above the earth; in whose lowest part are birds and waters, in the higher part stars: the highest is the Empyreal heaven. Esa. 66.\f* Heaven: and there was evening & morning that made the second day.
\v 9 God also said: let the waters that are under heaven be gathered together into one place: and let the dry land appear. And it was so done.
\v 10 And God called the dry land, Earth, and the gathering of waters together, he called Seas. And God saw that it was good.
\v 11 And said: Let the earth shoot forth green herbs, and such as may seed, & fruit-trees yielding fruit after his kind, such as may have seed in itself upon the earth. And it was so done.
\v 12 And the earth brought forth green herb, such as seedeth according to his kind, and tree that beareth fruit, having seed each one according to his kind. And God saw that it was good.
\v 13 And there was evening and morning that made the third day.
\v 14 Again God said: Be there lights made in the firmament of heaven, to divide the day and the night, and let them be \f c \fr 1.14 \ft The lights made the first day are disposed the fourth day in their proper courses for more distinction of times. S. Dionys. ca. 4. de divin. nom. S. Tho. p. 1. q. 67. a. 4. & q. 70. a. 2.\f* for signs & seasons, and days and years;
\v 15 to shine in the firmament of heaven, and to give light upon the earth. And it was so done.
\v 16 And God made two \f d \fr 1.16 \ft The Sun & Moon: for though the moon be the least visible star except Mercury, yet it giveth more light on the earth by reason it is nearer, & so Moyses speaketh according to the vulgar capacity and use of things. S. Aug. l. 2. de Gen. ad lit. cap. 16.\f* great lights; a greater light to govern the day, and a lesser light to govern the night; and stars. \ef - \fr 1.16 \fq Two great lights, & stars. \ft ¹ Here occureth another example of the hardness of holy Scripture. For if the two great lights (to wit the Sun & the Moon) & also the stars, were made the fourth day, and not before, as it may seem by the words in this place, then what was that light, & in what subject was it, that was made the first day? S. Basil, S. Gregory Nazianzen, Theodoret, & some others writing upon this place, do think that the light which was made the first day remained (though an accident) without his subject till the fourth day. And albeit most other Doctors rather think that the substance of the Sun & Moon, & of other planets & stars were created the first day, & the fourth day set in that order & course which now they keep, with more distinction for signs and seasons, and days and years; yet it is clear that the foresaid ancient Doctors judged it possible, ² that accidents may remain without their subject: which a Sacramentry will be loath to grant, lest it might be proved possible, as both these & all other Catholic Doctors believed & taught, that the accidents of bread and wine remain in the blessed Sacrament of the Eucharist without their subjects, which Protestants deny. \fq ¹ \ft Light being an accident remained without a subject, by the judgement of some learned Fathers. \fq ² \ft The accidents of bread and wine can remain by God's power without their subjects.\ef*
\v 17 And he set them in the firmament of heaven, to shine upon the earth,
\v 18 and to govern the day & the night, and to divide the light and the darkness. And God saw that it was good.
\v 19 And there was evening and morning that made the fourth day.
\v 20 God also said: Let the waters bring forth creeping creature having life, and flying fowl, over the earth under the firmament of heaven.
\v 21 And God created huge whales, and all living and moving creature, that the waters brought forth according to each sort, and all fowl according to their kind. And God saw that it was good.
\v 22 And he blessed them saying: Increase and multiply, and replenish the waters of the sea: and let the birds be multiplied upon the earth.
\v 23 And there was evening and morning that made the fifth day.
\v 24 God said moreover: Let the earth bring forth living creature in his kind, cattle, and such as creep, and beasts of the earth according to their kinds: and it was so done.
\v 25 And God made the beasts of the earth according to their kinds, and cattle, and all that creepeth on the earth in his kind. And God saw that it was good,
\v 26 \x - \xt Col. 3, 10.\x* and he said Let us make Man to our image & likeness: and let him have dominion over the fishes of the sea, and the fowls of the air, and the beasts, and the whole earth, and all creeping creature that moveth upon the earth. \ef - \fr 1.26 \fq Let us make man to our Image. \ft For better consideration of God's bounty towards us, & stirring ourselves to gratitude towards him, ¹ we may here note ten prerogatives bestowed on us by our Lord & Maker in our creation, above all other earthly creatures. ² First, whereas God by an imperial word of commandment made other creatures, \+it Fiat lux, fiat firmamentum\+it*: Be there light, Be there a firmament, intending to make man, he proceedeth familiarly, by way, as it were, of consultation, & as to his own use & service to make man, saying: \+it Let us make man to our image and likeness\+it*; that is to say, a reasonable creature with understanding and free will, which beasts have not. ³ Secondly, in this work God first insinuateth the high Mystery of the B. Trinity, or plurality of Persons in one God (because man is to believe the same) signifying the plurality of Persons by the words \+it Let us make\+it*, & \+it to our\+it*: and the unity in substance, by the words \+it image and likeness\+it*, the first in the plural number, the latter in the singular. ⁴ Thirdly, other creatures were produced by the waters & earth, \+it Let the waters bring forth\+it* (fish & fowl) \+it Let the earth bring forth\+it* (grass, & cattle, & other beasts) but God brought forth man, not by the earth, though of the earth, nor by water, nor by heaven, nor by Angels, but by himself, giving him a reasonable soul, not sensual only as to beasts, & the same not produced of any creature, but created immediately of nothing. ⁵ Fourthly, God gave man Paradise, a most pleasant place, to dwell in. ⁶ Fifthly, God gave man dominion & imperial authority over all living creatures under heaven. ⁷ Sixthly, man was created in that innocency of life, and integrity of all virtues, that his mind was wholly subject to God, his sense to reason, his body to his spirit, & all other living creatures obedient to him: even the terrible Lions, the cruel Tigers, the huge Elephants, and the wildest birds. ⁸ Seventhly, God brought them all to man, as to do him homage, and to take their names of him. Which by his excellent knowledge he gave them conformable to their natures. ⁹ Eighthly, God gave man in some sort an immortal body, that if he had kept God's commandment, he had lived long and pleasantly in this world, & so should have been translated to eternal life without dying. ¹⁰ Ninthly, God did not only adorn man with all natural knowledge and supernatural virtues, but also with the gift of prophecy. Whereby he knew that Eve was a bone of his bones, and flesh of his flesh, though being asleep he knew not when she was made. ¹¹ Tenthly, (which was the chief benefit of all) God conversed familiarly with man, and that in shape of man, which was a token of his marvelous great love to man, and a singular incitement of him to love God. Read more, if you please, of the dignity of man, and the benefits of God towards him in his creation, in S. Bernard upon the 99. Psalm, and upon the 61. chapter of Esaie. \fq ¹ \ft Ten prerogatives of man in his creation. \fq ² \ft 1. Made like to God. \fq ³ \ft 2. The Mystery of the B. Trinity insinuated in his creation. \fq ⁴ \ft 3. Produced by God himself. \fq ⁵ \ft 4. placed in Paradise. \fq ⁶ \ft 5. Lord of all earthly creatures. \fq ⁷ \ft 6. innocency. \fq ⁸ \ft 7. excellent knowledge. \fq ⁹ \ft 8. power to live ever. \fq ¹⁰ \ft 9. gift of prophecy. \fq ¹¹ \ft 10. God conversed familiarly with man.\ef*
\v 27 And God created man to his own image: to the image of God he created him, male and female he created them.
\v 28 And God blessed them, and saith: \x - \xt Mat. 19, 4.\x* Increase and multiply, and replenish the earth, and subdue it, and rule over the fishes and the sea, and fowls of the air & all living creatures that move upon the earth. \ef - \fr 1.28 \fq Increase and multiply. \ft ¹ Whether this be a commandment or no, at least it is a blessing, for so the words before convince, \+it God blessed them and said: Increase and multiply\+it*. He said the same also to brute creatures, which are not capable of a precept, but by this were made fertile. Whereby we see that God's blessing always worketh some real effect: as of fertility in this and other places, of multiplication of the loaves & fishes, Joan 6. ² And some real effect Christ's blessing must needs work also in the Blessed Sacrament. Mat. 26. Which can be no other but changing bread and wine into his body & blood, seeing himself expressly saith: \+it This is my body, this is my blood\+it*. And though God's blessing in this place be also a precept, yet it is not to all men for ever, ³ but for the propagation of mankind, which being long since aboundantly propagated, the obligation of the precept ceaseth the cause ceasing. So S. Cyprian, S. Hierome, S. Augustin, and other Fathers expound this place. And confirm the same by the text, for immediately God signifying to what end he spoke, saith: \+it and replenish the earth\+it*. Which being replenished, God's will is therein fulfilled. \fq ¹ \ft God's blessing always effectual. \fq ² \ft Especially in the holy Eucharist. \fq ³ \ft Not all men & women commanded to marry.\ef*
\v 29 And God said: Behold I have given you all manner of herb that seedeth upon the earth, and all trees that have in themselves seed of their own kind, to be your meat:
\v 30 and to all beasts of the earth, and to every fowl of the air, and to all that move upon the earth, and wherein there is life, that they may have to feed upon. And it was so done.
\v 31 And God saw all things that he had made, and \f e \fr 1.31 \ft Every creature in nature is good, but all considered together make the whole world perfect, most apt to man's use and God's glory. S. Aug. li. 1. de Gen. cont. Manich. ca. 21.\f* they were very good. And there was evening and morning that made the sixth day.
\c 2
\cd The work of six days being finished, God rested the seventh day, & blessed it. 8. Then placing man in Paradise (planted with beautiful & sweet trees, & watered with four rivers) 16. commandeth him not to eat of the tree of knowledge of good & evil. 18. And formed a woman of a rib of Adam.
\p
\v 1 The \x - \xt Exod. 20, 11. Deut. 5, 14. Heb. 4, 4.\x* heavens therefore & the earth were fully finished, and all the furniture of them.
\v 2 And the seventh day God ended his work which he had made; and rested \f 1 \fr 2.2 \ft God createth not new kinds of creatures, yet still worketh. Io. 5, 17. conserving & governing all things, and createth souls, grace, & glory of the same kind. S. Aug. l. 4. de Gen. ad lit. c. 12.\f* the seventh day, from all work that he had done. \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq The seventh day \ft ¹ All creatures being made in their kinds in six days, complete & perfect, God not needing (as men often do in their works) to perfect, polish, or amend the same, \+it rested the seventh day\+it*: & therefore the natural perfection of God's works is attributed to the seventh day, and the supernatural perfecting of men in eternal life, after the Resurrection, is attributed to the eighth day, as S. Augustin and other fathers teach. ² And for this cause \+it God blessed and sanctified the seventh day\+it*: and after we have in the Decalogue or ten commandments, that this day all should rest and abstain from works, yea and keep it festival, occupying themselves in spiritual exercises, service, and special worship of God, as the Jews did even till Christ's and his Apostles' time ³, praying & hearing the word of God read & expounded in the Sabboath day. Whereby we see that distinction of days pertaineth to Religion, the people of God thus observing the Sabboth in memory of the Creation, and diverse other feasts in memory of other benefits. ⁴ And we now keep the Sunday holy in memory of Christ's Resurrection, & other feasts in grateful remembrance of other Mysteries of Christ's Nativity, the coming of the Holy Ghost, & the like. Yea also feasts of his Blessed Mother, & other Saints, for the benefits received from Christ by them, & for more honour to Christ in them. So this Catholic observation of feasts is neither Judaical (which also in the Law was good, but now is abrogated) nor heathenish, for we honour not Iupiter, nor Iuno, nor any false god or goddess, but our Lord God Creator & Redeemer, & for his sake his best servants. ⁵ Whereof see the Annotations in the English new Testament. 4. chap. to the Galathians. Whereto we here only add these words of S. Basil, ⁶ which may serve for a general answer to the most common objection: \+it Honor servorum redundat in communem Dominum: The honour of the creatures redounceth to the common Lord or Master.\+it* So, saith he, the honour of Saints is the honour of Christ their Lord and ours. \fq ¹ \ft Observation of holy days by God's instruction. \fq ² \ft \+it in Psal 4. & 11.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 13, 14. Levit. 23.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Observation of festival days is religious, not Judaical, nor heathenish. \fq ⁵ \ft Honour of Saints is to the greater honour of Christ. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Homi. in 40 Martyres.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 And he blessed the seventh day & sanctified it: because in it he had ceased from all his work which God created to make.
\v 4 These are the generations of heaven & earth, when they were created in the day, when our Lord God made the heaven, and the earth,
\v 5 and every plant of the field, before it shot up in the earth, and every herb of the ground before it sprang. For our Lord God had not rained upon the earth; and man was not to till the earth:
\v 6 But a spring rose out of the earth, watering all the overmost part of the earth.
\v 7 Our Lord God therefore formed man of the slime of the earth: and \f a \fr 2.7 \ft Man's soul is immediately created by God, not produced of other substance, as the souls of beasts and plants are.\f* breathed into his face the breath of life, & man became a living soul. \x - \xt 1. Cor. 15, 45.\x*
\v 8 And our Lord God had \f b \fr 2.8 \ft Whether this Paradise be now extant is uncertain, though it be certain that Enoch & Elias are yet living in earth. \+it S. Aug. l. 2. cont. Pelagi. c. 23. See Pererius. l. 3. q. 5. & l. 7. q. ultima.\+it*\f* planted Paradise of pleasure from the beginning: wherein he placed man whom he had formed.
\v 9 And our Lord God brought forth of the ground all manner of trees, fair to behold, and pleasant to eat of: the tree of life also in the middle of Paradise: and the tree of knowledge of good and evil.
\v 10 And a river issued out of the place of pleasure to water Paradise, which from thence is divided into four heads.
\v 11 The name of one is Phison: that is it which compasseth all the land of Hevilath, where gold groweth.
\v 12 And the gold of that land is very good: there is found bdellium, and the stone onyx.
\v 13 And the name of the second river is Gehon: that is it which compasseth all the land of Ethiopia.
\v 14 And the name of the third river is Tigris: that same passeth along by the Assyrians. And the fourth river, the same is Euphrates.
\v 15 Our Lord God therefore took man, & put him in the Paradise of pleasure, to work, & keep it.
\v 16 And he commanded him saying: Of every tree of Paradise eat thou:
\v 17 But of the tree of knowledge of good & evil eat thou not. For in what day soever thou shalt eat of it, thou shalt die the death. \ef - \fr 2.17 \fq Of the tree of knowledge. \ft ¹ Besides the law of nature, by which Man was bound to direct all his actions according to the rule of reason; & besides the supernatural divine law, by which he was bound to believe & trust in God, & to love him above all things, having received the gifts of faith, hope, and charity; God gave him another particular law, that \+it he should not eat of the tree of knowledge of good and evil\+it*. And that for two special reasons, which S. Augustin ² noteth upon this place. ³ First, that God might declare himself to be Lord of man. Which was absolutely necessary for man, & nothing at all profitable to God, who needeth not our service, but we without his dominion should utterly fall to nothing. \+it Nec enim ipso non creante, &c. For he not creating us neither could we have been, nor he not conserving us could we remain, nor he not governing us, could we live rightly. Wherefore he only is our true Lord, whom not for his, but for our own profit & salvation we serve\+it*. ⁴ The other reason was, that God might give man matter wherein to exercise the virtue of obedience, and to shew himself a subject of God. Which could not be so properly & effectually declared by keeping other laws, nor the enormity of disobedience appear so evidently, as by fulfulling of God's will commanding him, or by doing his own will, moved to the contrary, in a thing of it-self indifferent, & only made unlawful, because it was forbid. ⁵ But let us hear S. Augustin's own words. \+it Nec potuit melius aut diligentius commendari quantum malum sit sola inobedientia, &c. Neither could it\+it* (saith this great Doctor) \+it be better, nor more exactly signified how bad a thing sole disobedience is, than where a man became guilty of iniquity because he touched that thing contrary to prohibition which if he, not forbidden, had touched, he had not sinned at all. For he that saith, for example sake, Touch not this herb,\+it* ⁶ \+it supposing it is poisonful, and doth forewarn one of death, if he touch it, death assuredly falleth on the contemner of the precept: yea though no man had prohibited, & he had touched, for he should die because the same thing bereaveth him of health and life, whether it had been forbidden him or no. Also when one forbiddeth that thing to be touched,\+it* ⁷ \+it which would not indeed prejudice him that toucheth, but him that forbiddeth, as if one take another's money, being forbid by him, whose the money is, it is a sin in him that is forbidden, because it is an injury to him that forbiddeth. But when that thing is touched which neither should hurt him that toucheth, nor any other, if it were not forbid, wherefore is it prohibited, but that the proper goodness of obedience, and the evil of disobedience might appear?\+it* Thus S. Augustin sheweth, that disobedience is a sin because it is against a precept, though otherwise the thing that is done were not evil. And among other good notes, ⁸ teacheth that true obedience inquireth not wherefore a thing is commanded, but leaving that to the Superior, promptly doth what is appointed. \fq ¹ \ft Why a particular positive law, besides the general laws of God & nature, was given to man. \fq ² \ft \+it lib. 8. de Gen. ad lit. c. 11. Psal. 15.\+it* \fq ³ \ft First reason. \fq ⁴ \ft 2. reason. \fq ⁵ \ft The sin of disobedience. \fq ⁶ \ft Joined with damage to him that disobeyeth. \fq ⁷ \ft Joined with damage of him that forbiddeth. \fq ⁸ \ft True obedience is blind and prompt.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.17 \fq Of the tree eat thou not. \ft This example of our first parent's transgression sheweth, ¹ ² how frivolous an answer it is to say, that breaking of commanded fasts, or eating meats forbidden cannot hurt us, the meat being good & wholesome: for so the fruit of the tree was good, and should have hurt no man, if it had not been forbidden. Even so all meats of their own nature are good, yet the precept of fasting (foretold by our Saviour in general, & determined by his Church in particular) & so of any other like law, though it be in things otherwise indifferent, ³ proceeding from lawful Superiors, bindeth the subjects in conscience. And the transgression is properly disobedience, what other sin soever may also be mixed therewith. \fq ¹ \ft Not meat, but the disobedience hurteth him that transgresseth the precept of abstinence. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. 9, Luc. 10. S. Epiph. in compen. fidei Cat. S. Aug. ep. 80.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Laws in things indifferent bind in conscience.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.17 \fq Thou shalt die the death. \ft Against the new doctrine, ¹ denying that after sin is remitted, ² any temporal punishment remaineth for the same, this place declareth that death (whereof God forewarned Adam, if he should eat of the fruit forbidden) remained due, and was at last inflicted upon him for his sin, which was presently remitted upon his repentance. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Calvin l. 3. inst. c. 4. parag. 31. & 32.\+it* \fq ² \ft Temporal punishment due after sin is remitted.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.17 \ft Again, for so much as we are all subject to death, ¹ ² it proveth that we were all guilty of this sin, by which death came upon all men, as S. Paul teacheth. Else God should punish us without our fault, which is unpossible that his goodness should do Especially it appeareth in infants, ³ ⁴ who dying before they come to use of reason, can never commit other sin: for though they were circumcised, or had sacrifice offered, or other remedy used for them before Christ, or baptised since Christ, ⁵ yet they suffer (as S. Augustin noteth) both death and many other penalties, of sickness, cold, heat, hunger, and the like, which can neither be to them matter of merit (as to others it may be) nor profit them for avoiding of other sins, seeing they die in their infancy. Yea moreover, ⁶ if they died without circumcision, or other remedy of those former times, their souls perished from their people; & now without Baptism can never enter into the Kingdom of heaven, which could not stand with God's justice, if they were not guilty of sin. \fq ¹ \ft Death due to all for Original sin. \fq ² \ft \+it Rom. 5.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Yea to infants who have no other sin. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Li. 13. de civit. c. 6. l. 2. de pec. mer. & remis. c. 34.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Also other penalties inflicted upon infants. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Gen. 17. Ioan. 3. S. Greg. l. 4. Moral. c. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 18 Our Lord God also said: It is not good for man to be alone: let us make him a help like unto himself.
\v 19 Our Lord God therefore having formed of clay all beasts of the earth, and fowls of the air; brought them to Adam, that he might see what to call them: for all that Adam called any living creature, the same is his name.
\v 20 And Adam called all beasts by their names, and all fowls of the air, and all cattle of the field: but unto Adam there was not found an helper like himself.
\v 21 Our Lord God therefore cast a deep sleep upon Adam: and when he was fast asleep, he took one of his ribs, & filled up flesh for it.
\v 22 And our Lord God \f c \fr 2.22 \ft As we say brick is made of earth, and a house is built of brick: so Adam was made of earth, & Eve built of a rib of Adam. And that of one rib, as if God should build a house of one brick, or as indeed he fed 5000. men with five loaves. \+it Chris. ho. 15. S. Aug. Tract. 24. in Joan. S. Tho. p. 1. q. 92. a. 3.\+it*\f*\f 1 \fr 2.22 \ft Mat. 19, 5. Mat. 10, 7. 1. Cor. 6, 16. Eph 5, 31.\f* built the rib which he took of Adam into a woman, & brought her to Adam.
\v 23 And Adam said: This now is bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called woman, because she was taken out of man.
\v 24 Wherefore man shall leave his father & mother, & shall cleave to his wife, & they shall be \f d \fr 2.24 \ft Not three, nor four, nor more, for then two were changed to another number. S. Jer. l. 1. cont. Iovi.\f* two in one flesh.
\v 25 And they were both naked, to wit Adam and his wife; and were not ashamed.
\c 3
\cd By the craft of the Devil speaking in a serpent, our first parents transgressed God's commandment. 7. Who being ashamed would hide themselves: 9. but are reproved by God. 14. And besides other particular punishments (yet with promise of a Redeemer) are cast out of Paradise. \f - \ft The second part. Of the fall of man, & propagation of man & of sin.\f*
\p
\v 1 But \f a \fr 3.1 \ft Serpents most crafty to escape harm when they hurt men: so is the devil.\f* the serpent also was more subtile than all the beasts of the earth, which our Lord God had made. Which said to the woman: Why hath God commanded you, that you should not eat of every tree of Paradise? \ef - \fr 3.1 \fq Why hath God? \ft ¹ ² Here we may see how sin came first among men. For the devil envying man's happy state tempted Eve the weaker person, beginning with a question, thereby to allure her into conference, and by such a question as might bring her into suspicion of God's affection towards man, saying: \+it Why hath God commanded you, that you should not eat of every tree of Paradise?\+it* insinuating by these words, and withal internally suggesting, that God dealt hardly with them, abridging their liberty without cause. ³ And when he had got so much of her, that she was displeased with the precept, which she shewed by adding of her own (to make it seem more grievous) that they were forbidden \+it to touch the tree\+it*, & again by reporting the punishment as doubtful, saying, Lest perhaps we die; then the tempter avouched boldly, and falsely, that they should not die, and charged God to be envious of the benefit they should get by eating of that tree, saying \+it their eyes should be opened, and they should be as Gods, knowing good and evil\+it*. Upon which persuasion, and liking also she had to the fruit, she did take and eat, and persuaded Adam also to eat. ⁴ ⁵ And forthwith they saw that they would not have seen, knew evil which they had better not to have known, were ashamed, and endeavoured to cover and hide themselves. Even thus the devil dealeth with men ever since, assualting the weaker persons, and weaker part, as the flesh and sensuality, and by them setteth upon the stronger and superior part, to get consent of freewill, without which there is no sin; according to that famous saying of Augustin: ⁶ \+it Peccatum adeo est voluntarium, ut nullo modo dic peccatum, si non voluntarium. Sin is so voluntary, that in nowise it can be sin, if it be not voluntary.\+it* Wherefore it was no sin in Eve to be tempted by the serpent, which she could not avoid, nor in Adam to be tempted by Eve, but they sinned when they consented to the evil suggestions. ⁷ ⁸ And now in the regenerate, though concupiscence remain, which is the effect of sin past, & occasion of sin in those that yield again to tentations, yet it is not sin, but punishment of sin, and matter of exercise in the just, and if we resist, of merit: and therefore S. Paul exhorteth us, ⁹ \+it to walk in the spirit, and the lusts of the flesh we shall not accomplish\+it*. And in another place sheweth, ¹⁰ \+it that he which fighteth lawfully shall be crowned\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Sin entered among men by the envy & craft of the devil, man consenting to his suggestions. \fq ² \ft \+it Sap. 2. 24. Ioan. 8, 44. S. Aug. lib. 14. de civit. c. 11. Rupert. li. de Trinit. & operibus suis c. 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Eve first sinned in thought, then in words, last in deeds. \fq ⁴ \ft Bad sequels of sin. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Lib. de vera Religione c. 14. Lib. 1. Retract. c. 13.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft No sin can be without freewill. \fq ⁷ \ft Concupiscence no sin, but the effect, and occasion of sin. Also occasion of merit. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it S. Aug. lib. 1. de nupt. & con. c. 23.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Gal. 5.\+it* \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it 2. Tim. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 To whom the woman answered: Of the fruit of the trees that are in Paradise we do eat:
\v 3 but of the fruit of the tree which is in the midst of Paradise, God hath commanded us that we should not eat: and that we should not touch it, lest perhaps we die.
\v 4 \x - \xt 2. Cor. 11, 3.\x* And the serpent said to the woman: No, you shall not die the death.
\v 5 For God doth know that in what day soever you shall eat thereof, your eyes shall be opened: and you shall be as gods, knowing good and evil.
\v 6 The woman therefore saw that the tree was good to eat, and fair to the eyes, and delectable to behold: and she took of the fruit thereof, & did eat, & gave to her husband, who did eat. \x - \xt Eccl. 25. 1. Tim. 2, 14.\x*
\v 7 And the eyes of them both were opened: and when they \f b \fr 3.7 \ft After sin they were ashamed, not before. \+it S. Chrisos.\+it*\f* perceived themselves to be naked, they sowed together leaves of a fig tree, and made themselves aprons.
\v 8 And hearing the voice of our Lord God walking in Paradise at the afternoon air, Adam hid himself, and so did his wife from the face of our Lord God, amidst the trees of Paradise.
\v 9 And our Lord God called Adam, and said to him: Where art thou?
\v 10 Who said: I heard thy voice in Paradise: and I feared, because I was naked, and I hid me.
\v 11 To whom he said: And who hath told thee that thou wast naked, but that thou hast eaten of the tree, whereof I commanded thee that thou shouldst not eat?
\v 12 And Adam said: The woman which thou gavest me to be my fellow-companion, gave me of the tree, & I did eat.
\v 13 And our Lord God said to the woman: Why hast thou done this? Who answered: The serpent deceived me, and I did eat.
\v 14 And our Lord God said to the serpent: Because thou hast done this thing, \f c \fr 3.14 \ft All this curse pertaineth to the devil that spake in the serpent. \+it S. Aug. l. 2. de Gen. ad lit. c. 36. S. Beda in hunc locum.\+it*\f* accursed art thou among all cattle, and beasts of the earth: upon thy breast shalt thou go, & \f d \fr 3.14 \ft Earthly or worldly and carnal men S. Greg. in\f* earth shalt thou eat all the days of thy life.
\v 15 I will put enmity between thee & the woman, & thy seed and the seed of her: she shall bruise thy head in pieces, and thou shalt lie in wait \f e \fr 3.15 \ft Though good men resist tentations at the first assaults, and so bruise the serpent's head, yet he endeavoureth still to deceive especially in the end of man's life, signified by the heel. S. Gre. in cap. 1. Iob.\f* of her heel. \ef - \fr 3.15 \fq She shall bruise. \ft ¹ Protestants will not admit this reading, \+it ipsa conteret\+it*, she shall bruise, lest our Blessed Lady should be said any way to bruise the serpent's head. And Kemnisius amongst others saith, that all ancient Fathers read, \+it ipsum\+it*, not, \+it ipsa\+it*. But he is convinced of lying by Claudius Marius Victor, \+it lib. 1. in Gen.\+it* Alcimus Avitus \+it lib. 3. cam. c. 6.\+it* S. Chrysostom \+it hom. 17. in Genes.\+it* S. Ambrose \+it lib. de fuga sæculi cap. 7.\+it* S. Augustin \+it l. 2. de Genesi contra Manichæos, cap. 18. & lib. 11. de Genesi ad literam chap. 26.\+it* S. Gregory \+it lib. 1. Moralium cap. 38.\+it* And after them S. Bede, Eucherius, Rabanus, Rupertus, Strabus, and Lyra upon this place, S. Bernard \+it ser. 2. super Missus est\+it*, and many others, who read \+it ipsa\+it*, as the Latin text now hath. ² \fq ¹ \ft The Latin text defended against Kemnisius and other Protestants. \fq ² \ft See Card. Bellarmin. li. 2. c. 12. de verbo Dei.\ef* \ef - \fr 3.15 \ft ¹ But whether we read, \+it She shall bruise,\+it* or, \+it her seed\+it*, that is her son Christ, \+it shall bruise\+it* the serpent's head, we attribute no more, nor no less to Christ, nor to our Lady by the one reading, than by the other: for by the text, \+it I will put enmities between thee and the woman, between thy seed, and her seed\+it*, it is clear, that this enmity and battle pertained to the woman & her seed on the one party, and to this devil, that spake by the serpent, & all the wicked, on the other party, & that the victory should happen to mankind. ² Which being captive by Adam's sin, occasioned by a woman, should be redeemed, both sexes, though in far different sort, concurring thereto. And so it is most true, that Christ by his own proper power, and his Blessed Mother by her most immediate cooperating to his Incarnation (& consequently to other Mysteries) did bruise the serpent's head, break & vanquish his power. ³ (a) As many ancient Fathers do excellently discourse: namely S. Bernard, writing upon these words in the Apocalyps. c. 12. \+it A great sign appeared in heaven, a woman clothed with the sun:\+it* Albeit (saith he) by one man and one woman, we were greatly damaged, yet (God be thanked) by one man & one woman all losses are repaired, and that not without great increase of graces. For the benefit doth far exceed the loss. Our merciful father giving us for a terrestrial Adam Christ our Redeemer, & for old Eve God's own Mother. Moreover as the same S. Bernard sheweth, this Blessed Virgin in singular sort bruised the serpent's head, ⁴ ⁵ in that she quite vanquished all manner suggestions of the wicked serpent, never yielding to, nor taking delight in any evil moved by him. \fq ¹ \ft Both readings yield the same sense. \fq ² \ft As Adam was the cause, and Eve an occasion of man's captivity: so Christ is the true cause and his mother an occasion of our restoration. \fq ³ \ft \+it (a) S. Ireneus li. 3. c. 33. & lib. 5. circa med. S. Epiph. Hær. 78. S. Ierom. ep. 22. ad Eustoch. S. Aug. (or S. Fulgent.) ser. 18 de Sanctis. de fide & Symb. de Agone Christiano.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Our B. Lady resisted all evil suggestions. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it (b) Ser. 2. super Missus est.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 To the woman also he said: I will multiply thy travails and thy child-bearings: in travail shalt thou bring forth children, and thou shalt be under thy husband's power, and he shall have dominion over thee. \x - \xt 1. Cor. 14.\x*
\v 17 And to Adam he said: Because thou hast heard the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree wherefore I commanded thee that thou shouldst not eat, cursed is the earth in thy work: with \f f \fr 3.17 \ft All men travail one way or other: & such as suffer weeds to overgrow (in their souls) shall after this life either sustain the fire of Purgatory, or eternal pain. \+it S. Aug. l. 2. c.\+it* 20. Gen. con. Man.\f* much toiling shalt thou eat thereof all the days of thy life.
\v 18 Thorns and thistles shall it bring forth to thee, and thou shalt eat the herbs of the earth.
\v 19 In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return to earth, of which thou wast taken: because dust thou art, and into dust thou shalt return. \ef - \fr 3.19 \fq Dust thou art. \ft By these words Adam was admonished to humble himself, considering the matter whereof his body was made, & into which he should be resolved again ¹ ² (c) whereupon it came to be a ceremony amongst penitents, to cast ashes on their heads, as appeareth in holy Scriptures. For which cause the Church now also useth this ceremony the first day of Lent, putting ashes on her children's heads: willing them to remember, that dust they are, & to dust they shall return, to move us by this meditation to more serious penance. \fq ¹ \ft The ceremony of ashes, on Ashwednesday \fq ² \ft \+it (c) Job 42. Esai. 58. Jere. 6. Ionæ. 3. Mat. 11.\+it*\ef*
\v 20 And Adam called the name of his wife, Eve: for because \f g \fr 3.20 \ft She was mother rather of all the dying: but in figure of our B. Lady who is mother of Christ, life itself, she is called mother of the living. S. Epiph. her. 78.\f* she was mother of all the living.
\v 21 Our Lord God also made for Adam and his wife garments of skins, and clothed them:
\v 22 And said: Lo Adam is become as it were one of us, knowing good & evil: now therefore lest perhaps he reach forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever. \ef - \fr 3.22 \fq Lest perhaps \ft ¹ Notwithstanding God's eternal decree in disposing all things, and his omnipotency which nothing can resist, yet he produceth good, and either avoideth or disposeth of evil which he suffereth, by ordinary means, as appeareth Act. 27, v. 31. and that because man hath freewill, with which God concurreth, & destroyeth not nor forceth, as S. Augustin teacheth. ² \fq ¹ \ft God's providence concurreth with man's free will \fq ² \ft \+it de grat. & l. arb. c. 6. de corrept. & grat. ad art. falso impos.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 3.22 \ft ¹ ² These Angels also hinder the devil, that he cannot enter Paradise, lest he should take of the fruit of the tree, and give it to men to prolong their lives, and thereby draw them to his service. \fq ¹ \ft Good Angels hinder devils of their desires. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Aug. lib. 11. de Gen. ad lit. c. 40.\+it*\ef*
\v 23 And our Lord God sent him out of the Paradise of pleasure, to work the earth of which he was taken.
\v 24 And he cast out Adam, and placed before the Paradise of pleasure Cherubins, and a flaming and a turning sword, for to keep the way of the tree of life. \ef - \fr 3.24 \fq Placed Cherubins. \ft Man being cast out of Paradise, the same is defended with double guard: with Angels, that are watchful, wise, & potent; and with fire & sword, most terrible armour to man. ¹ Whereby again we see that God useth ordinary means in his providence, as the ministry of Angels & human terror, & would neither destroy the tree, ² nor deprive it of the virtue to prolong life, nor bereave man of freewill, by which he might desire to return: but conserving nature in all creatures, preventeth inconveniences otherwise. \fq ¹ \ft Paradise defended by Angels and by fire & sword. \fq ² \ft God destroyeth not nature.\ef*
\c 4
\cd Wicked Cain killeth holy Abel: 9. whose blood cryeth for revenge. 11. Cain a cursed vagabond, 17. hath much issue. 25. Adam also hath Seth, and Seth Enos.
\p
\v 1 And Adam knew Eve his wife, who conceived and brought forth Cain, saying: I have gotten a man through God.
\v 2 And again she brought forth his brother Abel. And Abel was a shepherd, & Cain a husbandman.
\v 3 And it befell after many days that Cain \x - \xt Heb. 11.\x* offered of the fruits of the earth gifts to our Lord. \ef - \fr 4.3 \fq Offered gifts. \ft Either God himself taught Adam, and he his children, or else they knew by instinct of nature, that Sacrifice must be offered to God, to acknowledge thereby his supreme dominion over man, and man's due subjection to his divine Majesty. And that not only in internal affection, which (as S. Augustin, ¹ and all Catholic Doctors teach) is principally required, but also in external things, ² because we consist of body, and not only of soul, and have, by God's goodness, the use of corporal things. As here we see example in the law of nature: and the same was ordained by written precept in the law of Moyses: ³ the Prophets also foretold, that external Sacrifice should be offered in the law of grace and new Testament, to wit, the same which Christ instituted, and left in his Church, to continue to the end of the world. Moreover this homage of offering Sacrifice is so peculiar to God only, ⁴ that albeit many other exterior rites and services are used both to God & men, as to be bare-head, to bow, to kneel, & the like before them, \+it either of great humility\+it* (saith S. Augustin) ⁵ \+it or of pestiferous flattery,\+it* to such as are \+it homines colendi, venerandi, si autem eis multum additur, & adorandi: men to be worshipped, reverenced, and if much be given them, adored;\+it* (for this term of \+it adoring\+it* is also applied to men in holy Scriptures, Gen. 23. v. 7. 17. v. 29) yet Sacrifice is due to God only, and to no creature how excellent so ever. In so much (saith the same doctor) that as all nations found it necessary to offer Sacrifice, so none durst sacrifice to any, \+it nisi ei quem Deum aut scivit, aut putavit, aut finxit;\+it* but to him whom they either knew, or thought, or feigned to be God. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Lib. 10. de civit. c. 5.\+it* \fq ² \ft External Sacrifice due to God in every Law. \fq ³ \ft \+it Levit. 1. Dan. 12. Mal. 1. Luc. 22.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Sacrifice due to God only and to no creature. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Lib. 10. civit. cap. 4. Aristot. li. 2. Metaphis. Ethic. 9. Polit. 7. c. 8.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 Abel also \f a \fr 4.4 \ft A figure of the Lamb that was slain from the beginning of the world. \+it Apoc. 13. v. 8.\+it*\f* offered of the first-begotten of his flock, and of their fat: and our Lord had respect of Abel, & to his gifts. \ef - \fr 4.4 \fq Had respect to Abel. \ft ¹ Both Cain and Abel did well in offering external Sacrifice, but they differed much in sincerity and manner of choosing or dividing their oblations, touching God's part and their own, as S. Justinus Martyr, S. Hierom, S. Augustin and others teach. ² For Abel offered of the best things, of \+it the first-begotten of his flock, and of their fat.\+it* And therefore God respected & approved it. But to Cain and to his gifts he had not respect, because he wanted sincere devotion. Which difference of God's acceptance appeared doubtless, as S. Hierom and S. Augustin supposed, by some external sign, otherwise Cain had not understood it. Most like it was by fire sent from God, which inflamed and consumed Abel's Sacrifice, & not Cain's. As we read of diverse other Sacrifices in holy Scriptures. \fq ¹ \ft Abel's Sacrifice declared acceptable, & not Cain's, by some external sign. \fq ² \ft \+it To. 3. q. 4. Quæst. Hebraic. Lib. 15. civit. c. 7. Mala. 1. Hebr. 11. Levit. 9. Iudic. 6. 2. Par. 7. 3. Reg. 18. 2. Mac. 1.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 But to Cain, and to his gifts he had not respect: And Cain was exceeding angry, and his countenance abated.
\v 6 And our Lord said to him: Why art thou angry? and why is thy countenance fallen?
\v 7 If thou do well, shall thou not receive again: but if thou doest ill, shall not thy sin forthwith be present at the door? But the lust thereof shall be under thee, and thou shalt have dominion over it. \ef - \fr 4.7 \fq Shalt not thou receive. \ft ¹ Reward of good works and punishment of evil are clearly proved by this place, God saying to Cain: \+it If thou doest well, shalt thou not receive again?\+it* What else but well for well doing, as Abel received consolation of his Sacrifice well offered? \+it But if thou doest ill, shall not thy sin be present forthwith at the door\+it*, afflicting thy conscience, and not suffering thy mind to be in quiet, for remorse of thy wicked fact, and fear of just judgement? For hence it came that Cain's countenance fell, and his stomach boiled with anger: punishment so beginning even in this life, & much more in the next world our Saviour will \+it render\+it* (as himself saith) ² to every man according to his works: which the Apostle expresseth more distinctly, \+it eternal life, or wrath & indignation\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Reward and punishment according to our works. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. 16. Rom. 2.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 4.7 \fq under thee. \ft This Text so plainly sheweth freewill in man, ¹ also after his fall, that the English Protestants to avoid so clear a truth, for these words, \+it the lust thereof\+it* (to wit of sin) \+it shall be under thee, and thou shalt have dominion over it,\+it* corruptly translate in some of their Bibles thus: ² ³ Unto thee \+it his\+it* desire shall be subject, and thou shalt rule over \+it him.\+it* As if God had said, that Abel should be under Cain. As the phantastical Manichees perverted the sense, whose absurdity S. Augustin controlleth, ⁴ maintaining the true construction of the words, \+it Tu dominaberis illius; nunquid fratris? absit. Cuius igitur, nisi peccati? Thou shalt rule over; what, over thy brother? Not so. Over what then, but sin?\+it* In other English Editions, namely in the last, which we suppose they will stand to, it is better, but yet obscure, thus, \+it Unto thee shall be the desire thereof, and thou shalt have rule of it.\+it* Let us therefore examine the sense, and if S. Hierome, ⁵ the great scripture Doctor did rightly understand it, God did speak to this effect to Cain: \+it Because thou hast freewill, I warn thee, that sin have not dominion over thee, but thou over sin.\+it* ⁶ The Hebrew hath thus: \+it ad te appetitus eius, & tu dominaberis in eum,\+it* or, \+it ei. Unto thee the appetite thereof, and thou shalt rule over it.\+it* Thargum Hierosolimitanum concludeth God's speech to Cain thus: \+it Into thy hand I have given power of thy concupiscence, and have thou dominion thereof: whether thou wilt to good or to evil\+it*. The Greek hath thus: \+it To thee is the conversion thereof, and thou shalt bear rule over it\+it*: to wit, appetite, lust, concupiscence is under thy will. Finally, ⁷ ⁸ all antiquity, universality, and uniform consent of Christian Doctors, and other learned Philosophers, and reasonable men hold it for certain and an evident truth, that man, yea a sinner, hath freewill. Yet Luther, the father of Protestants, ⁹ so abhorred this truth, that he could not abide the very word, nor vouchsafe (when he writ against it) to title his beastly book, \+it Contra liberum arbitrium, Against freewill\+it* but, \+it De servo arbitrio, Of servile arbitrament.\+it* And denieth that man is in any wise free to choose, to resolve, or determine, but in all things servile, tied, constrained, and compelled to what soever he doth, saith, or thinketh. Further, that man in all his actions is like to a hackney, that is, forced to go whither the rider will have him. And knowing the whole world against him, shameth not to confess, that he setteth them all at naught in respect of himself, concluding thus: \+it I have not\+it* (saith he) \+it conferred with any in this book, but I have affirmed, and I do affirm. Neither will I that any man judge hereof, but I counsel all to obey, or yield to my opinion.\+it* ¹⁰ ¹¹ Calvin also for his part conspireth in this heresy with Luther, but more faintly, rather wisheth than imagineth that men be so mad as to fly from the name of freewill. \+it I\+it* (Saith Calvin) ¹² \+it neither myself would use this word, and would wish others, if they ask me counsel, to abstain from it.\+it* But we will be bold to oppose S. Hierome's reason against Luther, Calvin, all Manichees, and others that deny freewill. \+it God made us\+it* (saith he) ¹³ \+it with freewill, neither are we drawn by necessity to virtues nor to vices; otherwise where is necessity, there is neither damnation nor crown\+it*. ¹⁴ \fq ¹ \ft Freewill in man also after his fall. \fq ² \ft Heretical translation. \fq ³ \ft \+it Bible 1579.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it lib. 15. c. 7. civit.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Quæst Hebraic. in Gen.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft The Hebrew also & Greek text prove freewill in Cain. \fq ⁷ \ft Freewill testified by antiquity, universality, and consent of learned & reasonable persons. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it S. Aug. l. de vera Rel. c. 14.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft Luther abhorred the name of freewill. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it li. de servo arbitrio.\+it* \fq ¹¹ \ft Calvin also misliketh the word freewill. \fq ¹² \ft \+it lib. 2. c. 2. par. 8.\+it* \fq ¹³ \ft \+it l. 2. adv. Iovinian.\+it* \fq ¹⁴ \ft Where is necessity there is neither reward nor punishment due.\ef*
\v 8 And Cain said to Abel his brother: Let us go forth abroad. And when they were in the field, Cain rose up against his brother Abel, and slew him. \x - \xt Sap. 10.\x*
\v 9 And our Lord said to Cain: Where is Abel thy brother? Who answered: I know not, am I my brother's keeper? \x - \xt 1. Io. 3.\x*
\v 10 And he said to him: what hast thou done? \f b \fr 4.10 \ft Willful murder is one of the sins that cry to God for revenge.\f* The voice of thy brother's blood cryeth to me out of the earth.
\v 11 Now therefore cursed shalt thou be upon the earth, which hath opened her mouth, & received the blood of thy brother at thy hand.
\v 12 When thou shalt till it, it shall not yield to thee her fruit: a rogue and vagabond shalt thou be upon the earth.
\v 13 And Cain said to our Lord: Mine iniquity is greater than that I may deserve pardon.
\v 14 Lo thou dost cast me out this day from the face of the earth, and from thy face shall I be hid, and I shall be a vagabond and fugitive on the earth: every one therefore that findeth me shall kill me.
\v 15 And our Lord said to him: No, it shall not so be: but whosoever shall kill Cain, shall be punished seven-fold. And our Lord put a mark on Cain, that whosoever found him should not kill him.
\v 16 And Cain went forth from the face of our Lord, and dwelt as a fugitive on the earth at the east side of Eden. \ef - \fr 4.16 \fq Cain went forth. \ft It is a mark of Heretics to make breach, ¹ and go forth of the Church. And commonly it cometh of envy. \+it Some run into heresies and schisms\+it* (saith S. Cyprian) ² \+it when they envy Bishops, whilst one either complaineth that himself was not rather ordained, or disdaineth to suffer another above him. Hereupon he kicketh, hereupon he rebelleth. Envy moved Cain to kill his brother, because his own works were wicked\+it* and rejected: \+it and his brother's just,\+it* ³ and esteemed. So going forth became obstinate, obdurate, and desperate in his sin, and being reprobate of God, began a wicked City, opposite to the City of God. Wherefore Moyses, as S. Augustin noteth, ⁴ intending to describe and shew the perpetual continuance of God's City, the true Church, from Adam, which he doth by the line of Seth to Noe, & so forward to his own time, would not omit to tell also the progeny of Cain, even to the flood, wherein all his offspring was finally drowned & destroyed, that the true City of God might appear more distinct, more conspicuous, and more renowned. And that indeed the same only (and not any broken and interrupted companies or conventicles) might be known to be the true Church of God. \fq ¹ \ft Going forth of the Church a mark of Heretics. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Ioan. 2. Tract. de Zelo & livore.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it 1. Ioan. 3.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it lib. de Pastore. c. 8. & c. 20.\+it*\ef*
\v 17 And Cain knew his wife, who conceived and brought forth Enoch: And \f c \fr 4.17 \ft By the increase of Abraham's seed (by the line only of Isaac and Jacob, besides themselves of Ismael and Esau) in little more than 400. years to above six hundred thousand men able to bear arms \+it (Num. 1.)\+it* it appeareth that Cain's progeny in as many years might suffice to people a city, yea a whole country. S. Aug. l. civit. c. 8.\f* he built a city, and called the name thereof by the name of his son, Enoch.
\v 18 Moreover Enoch begat Irad, and Irad begat Maviael, and Maviael begat Mathusael, and Mathusael begat Lamech.
\v 19 Who took \f d \fr 4.19 \ft This Lamech of Cain's issue, is the first that is noted in Scripture to have taken two wives.\f* two wives, the name of the one was Ada, and the name of the other Sella.
\v 20 And Ada brought forth Iabel, who was the father of them that dwell in tents, and of herdsmen.
\v 21 And his brother's name was Jubal: he was the father of them that sing on harp & organs.
\v 22 Sella also brought forth Tubalcain, who was a hammerer and worker in all work of brass and iron. And the sister of Tubalcain was Noema.
\v 23 And Lamech said to his wives Ada and Sella: Hear my voice ye wives of Lamech, harken to my talk: for I have slain a man to the wounding of myself, and a stripling to mine own dry blow bruising. \ef - \fr 4.23 \fq I have slain. \ft ¹ So hard and obscure is this place, that S. Hierom required by S. Damasus Pope to expound it, ² dareth not affirm any one sense for certain, but proposing diverse which the text may seem to bear, wisheth the Pope who was also very learned) to examine all more at large: putting him in mind that Origen writ his twelfth and thirteenth books upon this only place. ³ The most probable exposition seemeth to be gathered out of the Hebrew's Tradition, that this Lamech of the issue of Cain (for there was another Lamech of Seth's progeny) much addicted to hunting, & his eyes decaying, used in that exercise the direction of a young man his nephew, the son of Tubalcain. Who seeing something move in the bushes, supposing it to be a wild beast, willed his grand-father to shoot at the same: which he did, & struck the mark with a deadly wound, & approaching to take the prey, found it to be old Cain. Whereupon sore amazed, afflicted, & moved with great passion, did so beat the young man for his ill direction, that he also died of the dry blows. After both which mishaps, & his passion at last calmed, Lamech lamenteth, as the text saith, that he had killed a man & stripling, to wit, the one with a wound, the other with dry blows, for which he feared sevenfold punishment more than Cain suffered for killing Abel. Nevertheless S. Hierom & other ⁴ Fathers think it probable, that Lamech killing the one of ignorance, the other in passion, was not so severely punished as he feared. ⁵ And so they understand the rest of this passage, that sevenfold vengeance was taken of Cain, by prolongation of his miserable life till his seventh generation, when one of his own issue slew him, & another of the same linage with him. And Lamech was punished seventy sevenfold when his seventy seven children (for so many he had, as Josephus writeth) & all their offspring perished in the flood. ⁶ Mystically by seventy seven may be signified, that the sin of mankind should be punished & expiated in Christ our Redeemer, who was born in the seventy seventh generation from Adam. \fq ¹ \ft Scripture hard. \fq ² \ft \+it Tom 3. ad 1. quest. Damasi.\+it* \fq ³ \ft A probable sense according to the Hebrews' Tradition. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it S. Chris. ho. 20. in Gen.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Lib. 1. Antiq. ca. 2. Origen in Gen. Luc. 3.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft Mystical sense.\ef*
\v 24 Seven-fold vengeance shall be taken of Cain: but of Lamech seventy times seven-fold.
\v 25 Adam also knew his wife again: and she brought forth a son, & called his name Seth, saying: God hath given me other seed for Abel, whom Cain slew.
\v 26 But to Seth also was born a son, whom he called Enos, this man began to invocate the name of our Lord. \ef - \fr 4.26 \fq Began to invocate. \ft ¹ Seth was a most holy man, and so brought up his children, that they were called the sons of God, Gen. 6. Adam also and Eve were penitent, & became great Confessors, and are now Saints. And so it cannot be doubted but amongst other spiritual exercises they prayed & invocated God. And therefore that which is here said: He (to wit Enos) \+it began\+it* or (as the Hebrew hath) then was begun, \+it to invocate the name of our Lord,\+it* cannot be understood of private, ² but of some public prayer of many meeting together, & observing some rites and set form in peculiar place dedicated to divine Service, the Church being now grown to a competent multitude; and that besides Sacrifice, which was also before, as appeareth both by Cain and Abel. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Suidas vocabulo Seth. Joseph. l. 1. Anti. S. Aug. epist. 99. ad Evod.\+it* \fq ² \ft Public prayer besides Sacrifice in the Church of God.\ef*
\c 5
\cd The progeny of Adam, & number of their years (with the death of the rest, & translation of Enoch) in the line of Seth, to Noe and his three sons.
\p
\v 1 This \x - \xt Sap. 2, 24. Eccl. 17, 1.\x* is the book of the generation of Adam. In the day, when God created man, to the likeness of God made he him.
\v 2 Male and female created he them; and blessed them: and called their name Adam, in the day when they were created.
\v 3 And Adam lived an hundred and thirty years; & begat to his own image and likeness, and called his name Seth.
\v 4 And the days of Adam, after he begat Seth, came to eight hundred years: and he begat sons and daughters. \ef - \fr 5.4 \fq Begat sons and daughters. \ft Moyses in this genealogy reciteth not always the first-begotten, nor the whole progeny by their names for then he should have repeated Cain and Abel, and have named many others, but those only by whom the Church of God continued, ¹ ² signifying the rest in general, whose succession was cut off by the flood. \fq ¹ \ft The continual succession of God's Church, & interruption of other communities. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Aug. l. 15. civ. c. 20.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 And all the time that Adam lived, came to nine hundred & thirty years, and he died. \ef - \fr 5.5 \fq And he died \ft By this God's word is verified saying, that Adam should die, if he should eat of the forbidden tree. And the devil is proved a liar, saying, they should not die. ¹ It is also most true that Adam died that day in which he did eat. For he began that very day to decline to death; and so doth all mankind ever since as truly said the woman of Thecua to King David: ² \+it we do all die, and as waters that return not, we fall down on the earth. And what else\+it* (saith S. Gregory) \+it is this daily decaying of our corruption, but a lingering death?\+it* And none of all these that lived longest reaching to a thousand years (which with God is as one day) man died in that day in which he transgressed. Morally ancient Fathers here note, that albeit the life of the Patriarchs seemeth long to us, ³ yet if we compare the same to eternity it is nothing. Neither by the judgement of Philosophers may any thing be counted long, that hath an end: as Tullie bringing Cato wisely disputing, sheweth the longest life to be but a short moment. Whereby again we may see what loss we sustain by sin: seeing if sin had not been, we should all have been translated from earth to heaven, and never have died. \fq ¹ \ft How man died the day that he sinned. \fq ² \ft \+it 2. Reg. 14. ho. 37. in Evang. Psa. 89. S. Ireneus li. 5. adver. Heret. Cicero li. de Senec. & q. 1. Tuscul.\+it* \fq ³ \ft All time is short in respect of eternity.\ef*
\v 6 Set also lived an hundred five years, and begat Enos.
\v 7 And Seth lived after he begat Enos, eight hundred and seven years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 8 And all the days of Seth came to nine hundred & twelve years, and he died.
\v 9 And Enos lived ninety years, and begat Cainan.
\v 10 After whose birth he lived eight hundred and fifteen years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 11 And all the days of Enos came to nine hundred and five years, and he died.
\v 12 Cainan also lived seventy years, & begat Malaleel.
\v 13 And Cainan lived after he begat Malaleel, eight hundred & forty years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 14 And all the days of Cainan came to nine hundred and ten years, and he died.
\v 15 And Malaleel lived sixty five years and begat Jared.
\v 16 And Malaleel lived after he begat Jared, eight hundred & thirty years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 17 And all the days of Malaleel came to eight hundred ninety five years, and he died.
\v 18 And Jared lived an hundred sixty two years, and begat Enoch.
\v 19 And Jared lived after he begat Enoch eight hundred years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 20 And all the days of Jared came to nine hundred sixty two years, and he died.
\v 21 Moreover Enoch lived sixty five years, and begat Mathusala.
\v 22 And Enoch \f a \fr 5.22 \ft This Hebrew phrase \+it walked with God,\+it* signifieth that he lived well & pleased God.\f* walked with God: and lived after he begat Mathusala, three hundred years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 23 And all the days of Enoch came to three hundred sixty five years.
\v 24 And he walked with God, and was seen no more: because God \f b \fr 5.24 \ft The seventy two Interpreters say, God translated him, and so doth S. Paul. \+it Heb. 11.\+it*\f* took him. \ef - \fr 5.24 \fq Was seen no more. \ft ¹ ² That Enoch and Elias are yet alive is a constant known truth in the hearts and mouths of the faithful, saith S. Augustin in his first book, \+it de peccat. merit. & remiss. c. 3.\+it* and confirmeth the same in diverse other place. And it is testified by very many both Greek and Latin Doctors: S. Ireneus li. 5. S. Justinus Martyr, q. 85 ad Orthodoxos, S. Hyppolytus li. de Antichristo, S. Damascen. li. 4. de Orthodoxa fide, S. Hierom. epist. 61. ad Pamach. c. 11. S. Ambrose in Psalm. 45. D. Chrysostom. ho. 21. in Gen. ho. 58. in Mat. ho. 4. in Epist. 2. ad. Thess. ho. 22. in ep. ad Heb. S. Greg. li. 14. Moral c. 21, ho. 12, in Ezech. S. Prosp. li. ult. de promis. S. Bede in c. 9. Marc. Theophilact and Oecumenius in cap. 17. Mat. and others innumerable. Touching Elias it is manifest in Scriptures, ³ ⁴ that he shall come, and preach, & be slain with another witness of Christ, before the terrible day of Jugdement. ⁵ ⁶ Of Enoch Moyses here maketh the matter more than probable, saying of every one of the rest, \+it he died\+it*, only of Enoch saith not so, but that \+it he appeared or was seen no more.\+it* For which the seventy two Interpreters say, \+it And he was not found, for God translated him.\+it* Which cannot signify death, but transporting or removing to another place. Whereto agreeth the Author of Ecclesiasticus, saying: ⁷ \+it Enoch pleased God, and was translated.\+it* But most clearly S. Paul saith: ⁸ \+it Enoch was translated, that he should not see death, and he was not found, for God translated him.\+it* With what plainer words can any man declare, that a special person were not dead, than to say: \+it He was translated,\+it* or conveyed away, \+it that he should not see death?\+it* ⁹ Neither is it a reasonable citation to interpret this of spiritual death. For so Adam being eternally saved (as S. Ireneus li. 3. c. 34 Epiphan. con. heresim 46. S. Augustin epist. 99. ad Evodium, and others teach, and the whole Church believeth) was preserved from that death, and so undoubtedly were Seth and Enos, being most holy; and the rest here recounted, as is most probable. Nevertheless for further confutation of the contrary opinion of Protestants, the reader may also observe the judgement of S. Chrysostom, ¹⁰ ¹¹ who affirmeth that \+it Though it be not a matter of faith, whether Enoch be now in Paradise from whence Adam and Eve were expelled, or in some other pleasant place: Dicunt tamen sacræ Scripturæ quod Deus transtulit tum, & quod viventem transtulit eum, quod mortem ipse non sit expertus. The holy Scriptures say that God translated him, & that he translated him alive, that he felt not (or hath not experienced) death.\+it* And S. Augustin as expressly saith, \+it Non mortuus, sed vivus translatus est. He\+it* (to wit Enoch) \+it is translated, not dead but alive.\+it* Yea he teacheth how his life is sustained thus many thousand years upon earth. And sheweth moreover that both Enoch and Elias shall die. \+it For seeing Enoch and Elias\+it* (saith he) \+it are dead in Adam, and carrying the offspring of death in their flesh, to pay that debt, are to return to this life\+it* (of common conversation) \+it and to pay this debt which so long is deferred.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Enoch & Elias yet living in body. \fq ² \ft \+it l. 20. civit. c. 20. li. 2. de gratia Christi c. 23. tract. 4. in Joan.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Manifest Scripture that Elias yet liveth, and shall be slain & another with him. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Mala. 4. Apo. 11.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Likewise that Enoch did not see death. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Καὶ ὀυχ ἑυρίσκετο ὅτι μετατέθηκεν ἀυτὸν ὁ Θεὸς.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Eccl. 44.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Heb. 11.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft These Scriptures speak of temporal not of spiritual death. \fq ¹⁰ \ft The fathers prove by the scriptures that Enoch is not dead. \fq ¹¹ \ft \+it ho. 21. in Gen. lib. 1. de pec. mer. c. 2. & 3. l. 9 de Gen. ad lit. c. 6. S. Tho. in c. 11. ad Hebreos.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 5.24 \ft ¹ ² Diverse reasons are also alleged, why God would reserve these two alive. ³ First, to shew by example, that as their mortal bodies are long conserved from corrupting or decaying, in like sort Adam & Eve and all others not sinning, should have been conserved, and according to God's promise never have died, but after some good time translated to heaven, and endued with immortality. ⁴ Secondly, to give us an argument of immortality, which is promised after the general Resurrection. For seeing God doth preserve some mortal so long from all infirmity, we may assuredly believe that he will give immortal and eternal life of body and soul to his Saints, after they have payed the debt of death, and are risen again. ⁵ Thirdly, these two (one of the law of nature, the other of the law of Moyses) are preserved alive, to come amongst men again towards the end of the world, to teach, testify, and defend the true faith and doctrine of Christ against Antichrist, when he shall most violently oppugn and persecute the Church. ⁶ Of Enoch it is said in the book of Ecclesiasticus, that he was translated, \+it ut det gentibus poenitentiam, that he give repentance to the nations,\+it* by his preaching, and reducing the deceived from Antichrist. And of Elias Malachie prophesieth, that he \+it shall come before the great and terrible day of our Lord, and shall turn the heart of the fathers\+it* (that is the people of the Jews) \+it to the sons\+it* (the Christians) \+it and of the sons\+it* (the deceived Christians) \+it to the fathers,\+it* the ancient true Catholics. \fq ¹ \ft Causes why Enoch & Elias are reserved alive. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Chris. ho. 21. in Gen. & in 2. Thes. 2. Theodo. q. 45. in Gen. Aretas in 11. Apoc. S. Greg. lib. 14. Moral c. ult. & ho. 12. in Ezech. Eccl. 44. Mala. 4. Eccl. 48.\+it* \fq ³ \ft 1 \fq ⁴ \ft 2 \fq ⁵ \ft 3 \fq ⁶ \ft See D. Sand. lib. 8. c. 31. de Monarch. Eccl. And F. Pererius in c. 12. Danielis.\ef*
\v 25 Mathusala also lived an hundred eighty seven years, and begat Lamech.
\v 26 And Mathusala lived, after he begat Lamech, seven hundred eighty two years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 27 And all the days of Mathusala came to \f c \fr 5.27 \ft This is the longest life of all here recited. But if we consider that Adam was as strong of body the first day he was created, as these others were at the age of 60. years (before which, none are said to have begot children & so subtract 60. years from Mathusala, then Adam lived in man's state longer than he by 21. years.\f* nine hundred sixty nine years, and he died.
\v 28 And Lamech lived an hundred eighty two years, & begat a son:
\v 29 and he called his name Noe, saying: This son shall comfort us from the works & labours of our hands on the earth, which our Lord cursed.
\v 30 And Lamech lived, after he begat Noe, five hundred ninety five years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 31 And all the days of Lamech came to seven hundred seventy seven years, and he died. And \f d \fr 5.31 \ft The second prophecy before Mass on Easter Eve.\f* Noe when he was five hundred years old, begat Sem, Cham, and Japhet.
\c 6
\cd Man's sins cause of the deluge. 4. Giants were then upon the earth. 8. Noe being just was commanded to build the Ark, 18. wherein he with seven persons more, and the seed of other living things were saved.
\p
\v 1 And after that men began to be multiplied upon the earth, and had procreation of daughters,
\v 2 the \f a \fr 6.2 \ft The professors of true religion were called the sons of God; the followers of errors the sons of men.\f* sons of God seeing the daughters of men that they were fair, took to themselves wives out of all which they had chosen. \ef - \fr 6.2 \fq Sons of God. \ft ¹ The progeny of Seth, professing true faith & Religion, were called the sons of God; and those of Cain's issue and congregation, following erroneous and wicked opinions, were called the sons of men. Which were then the distinctive terms of true and false Religion, as afterwards were the terms of Jews and Gentiles: after Christ, Christians and Pagans: and lastly true and false Christians are distinguished, by the names of Catholics and Heretics, as S. Augustin teacheth in his questions upon Genesis, ² & other places. Which is confirmed by the like judgement of S. Cyril Alexandrinus li. 9. advers. Iulianum, S. Ambrose li. de Noe & arca. c. 4. S. Pacianus epist. ad Symphorianum, Theodoret, & many others upon this place. \fq ¹ \ft Sons of God & sons of men was then such a distinction, as now Catholics & Heretics. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. 15. c. 23. civit. De vera relig. c. 7. con. ep. fund. c. 4. Trac. 32. in Ioan.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 And God said: My spirit shall not remain in man for ever, because he is flesh: and his days shall be an hundred and twenty years. \ef - \fr 6.3 \fq An hundred and twenty years. \ft ¹ ² Man's life was not here shortened to an hundred and twenty years, as some have misunderstood this place. For after this diverse lived much longer, as appeareth in the genealogy of Sem to Abram in the 11. chapter of Genesis. And Abraham lived 175. years (c. 25.) Isaac 180. (c. 35.) Jacob 147. (c. 47) and Joiadas born 1500. years after, lived 130. years (2. Par. 24.) But 120. years were granted before the flood for that generation to repent in, as the Chaldee Edition expresseth more plainly: \+it Terminus dabitur ei centum, viginti annorum, si forte convertatur.\+it* The term of an hundred and twenty years, shall be given them, if perhaps they may convert. And so S. Chrysostom, S. Hierom, and S. Augustin expound this Scripture ³. Yet whether God cut off 20. of these years, and brought the flood after a 100 (for Noe had his sons when he was 500. years old, & the flood came in the 500. year of his age) or that this warning was given twenty years before any of his sons were born, is not so easily decided by the holy Doctors, how easy soever Protestants say all Scriptures are ⁴. Though under correction of better judgement, it seemeth more probable, that Moyses by anticipation joineth the birth of Noe's sons (when he was 500. years old) to the rest of the genealogy of the first Patriarchs, in the former chapter, and then telleth of this admonition, given 20. years before their birth. And so God expected the people's repentance the whole time of 120. years prescribed. \fq ¹ \ft This warning & expectation of repentance sheweth free-will in man. \fq ² \ft \+it Philo. Iosephus. Lactant. Rupert. Tostatus.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it ho. 27. in Gen. Tradit. Heb. lib. 15. c. 24. civit.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Scriptures not easy.\ef*
\v 4 And Giants were upon the earth in those days. For after the sons of God did company with the daughters of men, & they brought forth children, these be the mighty of the old world, famous men. \ef - \fr 6.4 \fq Giants were upon the earth. \ft ¹ Some have thought that these giants were not men, nor begotten by men, but that either devils, which fell at first from heaven, or other Angels allured with concupiscence begat them of the daughters of Cain. Philo Iudeus in his book de Gigantibus writeth, that those who in Moyses here called \+it Angels,\+it* the Philosophers called \+it Genios. Qui sunt animalia ærea, which are living creatures with airy bodies:\+it* Josephus (li. 1. Antiq.) saith that Angels begat these giants. Tertullian also (li. de habitu muliebri) holdeth the same error, and diverse more otherwise good Authors. But. S. Cyril of Alexandria (li. 9. adver, Iulian) S. Chrysostom (homil. 22. in Gen.) S. Ambrose (de Noe & arca. c. 4.) S. Augustin (li. 15. c. 23. de civit.) S. Hierome (Tradit. Hebraic.) and other most principal Doctors teach it to be untrue, ² yea unpossible, that these giants should have been begotten by any other creatures than by men. ³ For that Angels & devils are mere spirits without all natural bodies. And if they had airy bodies (as they have not) yet they could not have such generation. ⁴ For the power or force to engender belongeth to the vegetative soul, whose proper operations are to turn nutriment into the substance of the subject wherein it is, & to engender new issue or offspring from the same, as Aristotle sheweth (li. 2. de anima, textu. 24.) And in what bodies soever there is begetative soul, it must needs be, that the same was engendered, and must sometimes decay and die, and so devils should be mortal. ⁵ Moreover, if they could have generation together with mankind, then such issue should be a distinct species both from man and devil, as a male differeth both from horse and ass. ⁶ Again, if spirits have abused women in assumpted bodies, and shape of men, yet they did not take them to wives, as the Scripture saith they did who begat these giants. ⁷ Finally, the holy Scripture here expressly calleth the giants men: \+it These be the\+it* mighty ones, famous \+it men\+it*; the modesty of Scripture terming them famous, whom our common phrase would call infamous, being more monstrous in wickedness of mind, ⁸ than in hugeness of body. For they were most insolent, lascivious, covetous, cruel, and in all kind of vices most impious. \fq ¹ \ft Erroneous opinions concerning these giants. \fq ² \ft The Principal doctors prove that they were men, and begotten of men. \fq ³ \ft First reason. \fq ⁴ \ft 2 \fq ⁵ \ft 3 \fq ⁶ \ft 4 \fq ⁷ \ft 5 \fq ⁸ \ft Giants most monstrous in body and in mind.\ef*
\v 5 And God seeing the malice of men was much on the earth, & that all the cogitation of their heart was bent to evil at all times, \ef - \fr 6.5 \fq All the cogitation bent to evil. \ft ¹ Luther (in his 21. article condemned by Leo the tenth) would prove by these words, and the like following, \+it All flesh had corrupted his way upon earth,\+it* that all works of men are sins. For (saith he) seeing the hearts of all men are bent always to evil, and all human actions proceed from the heart, it must needs be that the heart as the fountain being corrupt, the streams also issuing from the same must be corrupted. Again, all flesh having corrupted his way upon earth, there is not any just man (saith he) nor any man without sin: and with Protestants all sins are mortal. ² But Heretics' arguments are like to that the Poets feign of Sisyphus labouring to carry a great stone to the top of an high hill, which when he hath brought almost to the height, it still falleth from him, & tumbleth again to the bottom. Even so their arguments that make greatest shew of proving their opinions, are nothing but vain travailing, when they come to be tried by the true sense of holy Scripture. In this place Moyses describeth the enormity of sin that reigned in the world before the flood, for which God sent that destruction. For it was heinous indeed, and that especially in four respects. ³ ⁴ First, the malice of wickedness was general, which is signified by those words, \+it all flesh hath corrupted his way upon earth.\+it* ⁵ Secondly, it was great malice, signified by the words \+it much, and, all the cogitations of their heart is bent to evil.\+it* For they committed all manner of wickedness, in haughtiness of pride, in all lasciviousness of the flesh, in all cruelty of robbing, sacking, and murdering, in all impiety against God & man. ⁶ Thirdly, it was of long continuance, and daily iterated. For Cain once fallen into damnable sin never repented, and all his progeny was exceeding wicked, and after that Adam and Seth were dead, and Enoch translated, many of the faithful fell to the wicked sort, and became worse and worse \+it omni tempore,\+it* all ways, or \+it every day.\+it* ⁷ Fourthly, they were obstinate and obdurate, not repenting when \+it Noe\+it* built the ark, and \+it preached justice\+it* (as S. Peter testified) ⁸ & therefore \+it God saved him and his family, bringing in the deluge upon the world of the impious\+it*. All which maketh nothing at all for Luther. ⁹ For although the malice of man and corruption of flesh was then very general, great, of long continuance, and obstinate, yet was it not so universal, but that God himself excepted Noe, saying to him: \+it I have found thee just in my sight in this generation,\+it* whereby it is clear that these general terms, \+it all cogitation\+it* and \+it all flesh\+it*, have exceptions. As likewise other as general propositions in this same chapter, concerning the punishment threatened, comprehend not absolutely all and every one, but almost all, very few excepted. I \+it will clean take away,\+it* or destroy \+it man whom I have created, from the face of the earth. The end of all flesh is come before me.\+it* Again, \+it that I may destroy all flesh wherein is breath of life under heaven.\+it* These are very general speeches, that all should be destroyed, and yet eight persons of mankind that had the same natural flesh, and amongst other living creatures that had breath, diverse pairs were saved alive. So that this place (nor any other in holy Scripture) will not prove that Protestants' paradox, that all men's actions are mortal sins, or that no man in this life is or can be just: but many scriptures tell us plainly that some men were just, ¹⁰ as Noe, Job, Daniel, Zacharias, Elizabeth, Simeon, and others. Of Noe see more in the next annotation. \fq ¹ \ft Luther's argument that all men's works are sins. \fq ² \ft Heretics like to Sisyphus. \fq ³ \ft The sins before the flood very grievous in four respects. \fq ⁴ \ft 1 \fq ⁵ \ft 2 \fq ⁶ \ft 3 \fq ⁷ \ft 4 \fq ⁸ \ft \+it 2. Pet. 2.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft Luther's argument answered. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it Ezech. 1. Luc. 1. & 2. Apoc. 22.\+it*\ef*
\v 6 it \f b \fr 6.6 \ft God who is immutable, & subject to no passion, yet by the enormity of sins seemeth provoked to wrath, and to repent that he had made man. \+it S. Amb. li. de Noe & arca. c. 4.\+it*\f* repented him that he had made man on the earth. And touched inwardly with sorrow of heart,
\v 7 I will, saith he, clean take away man, whom I have created, from the face of the earth, from man even to beasts, from that which creepeth even unto the fowls of the air. For it repenteth me that I have made them.
\v 8 But Noe found grace before our Lord.
\v 9 These are the generations of Noe: Noe was a just and perfect man in \f c \fr 6.9 \ft In all generations God reserved some just. Much more in the law of Grace.\f*\f 1 \fr 6.9 \ft Eccl. 44. 17.\f* his generations, he did walk with God. \ef - \fr 6.9 \fq Noe was a just and perfect man. \ft ¹ Here Noe is not only called just, but also perfect. The Hebrew word \+it tamim\+it* of the verb \+it tamam\+it* (which signifieth to finish or accomplish) sheweth that Noe was a perfect or complete man doing all that he was commanded, and performing the offices of all virtues that pertained to him, and that not in a vulgar and mean sort, but in a high degree, & heroical manner, as sundry ancient Fathers have gathered upon this place. We shall cite some few of their sayings for example. S. Hierom (Tradit. Hebraic. in Gen.) distinguishing between consummate justice (of the next life) & justice of this generation (or transitory life) saith: \+it Noe the just man was perfect in his generations: Noe did walk with God: that is, did follow his steps.\+it* S. August. (li. 15. civit. c. 26.) saith the like, that \+it Noe was called just in his generation, to wit, not as the citizens of God's city are to be perfected in that immortality, in which they shall be equal to Angels, but as they may be perfect in this pilgrimage.\+it* And in his book de perfectione contra Cælestium, he describeth him to be ² \+it a perfect man, that runneth without blame towards perfection, void of damnable sins, and is not negligent to cleanse venial sins by alms, prayers,\+it* and other good works. S. Ambrose also testifieth, (li. de Noe & arca c. 4.) that albeit the world was very wicked, yet some were just, saying: \+it By the grace (or favour) which Noe found, it shewed that other men's offence doth not obscure the just man, who is praised, not by the nobility of his birth, but by the merit of his justice & perfection.\+it* S. Chrisost. most largely (ho. 23. in Gen.) setteth forth the justice and perfection of Noe. Where after he hath shewed that Noe deserved indeed the name of a man, because he by flying vices and following virtues conserved the image of man, when others like beasts were led away and ruled by their wicked lusts, proceedeth thus in his commendation: \+it Behold\+it* (saith he) \+it another kind of praise: Noe is called, just, which denomination comprehendeth all virtue. For this name, just, we use to pronounce of them, that exercise all manner of virtue. And that you may learn how he arrived to the very top which was then also required of our nature, the Scripture saith, he was just, being perfect in his generation. He performed what things soever it behoveth one to do that embraceth virtue, for such an one is perfect. He intermitted nothing, he halted in nothing, he did not well in this thing and sinned in that thing, but was perfect in every virtue, which was requisite for him to have. Moreover to make also this just man more conspicuous to us in regard of the time, and by comparing him with others, the Scripture saith, he was perfect in his generation: in that time, in that perverse generation, which declined unto evil, which would not so much as pretend any resemblance of virtue. In that generation therefore, in those times, that just man not only pretended, but arrived to that height of virtue, that he became perfect, and in all things absolute. And that which I said before, to do well amongst the enemies of virtue, amongst them that forbid virtue, doth always testify a greater poise of virtue, so by this occasion the just man got greater praises. Neither doth divine scripture here make an end of praising him, but further sheweth the excellency of his virtue, and that he was approved by God's own censure,\+it* ³ \+it for besides saying, he was perfect in his generation, it addeth, that Noe pleased God. So great was the renown of his virtue, that he deserved to be praised of God. For Noe pleased God, saith the Scripture, that you may know that he was approved of God: He pleased that eye, that cannot be deceived, by his good works.\+it* Thus far S. Chrysostom, and much more to the same effect. S. Gregory the Great in his fifth book of Morals, and 36. chapter upon the third chapter of Job, recounting certain principal Patriarchs, among the rest saith: \+it Noe for that he pleased God's examination was saved alive in the unclean world.\+it* And after a large catalogue of other just men in confirmation of this doctrine, that some were just in the law of nature, concludeth thus: \+it Neither is it to be believed\+it* (saith he) \+it that only so many were just before the law was received as Moyses contracteth in his most brief description.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Noe just and perfect. \fq ² \ft Who is perfect in this life. \fq ³ \ft \+it Divino calculo.\+it*\ef*
\v 10 And he begat three sons, Sem, Cham, and Japheth.
\v 11 And the earth was corrupted before God, & was replenished with iniquity.
\v 12 And when God had perceived that the earth was corrupted (for all flesh had corrupted his way upon the earth)
\v 13 He said to Noe: The end of all flesh is come before me, the earth is replenished with iniquity from the face of them, and I will destroy them with the earth.
\v 14 Make thee an ark of timber plank: cabinets shalt thou make in the ark, and shalt pitch it within and without with bitume.
\v 15 And thus shalt thou make it. The length of the Ark shall be three hundred cubits: fifty cubits the breadth, and thirty cubits the height of it. \ef - \fr 6.15 \fq Three hundred cubits. \ft ¹ Apelles an old heretic, scholar of Marcian, but after leaving him, and amongst other new coined heresies, rejecting the Law of the Prophets, would by this place impugn Moyses, saying it was unpossible that in so small room as was the ark by this description, the designed pairs of all kinds of beasts, fowl, and serpents, should be contained, with the eight persons, and all their provision of meat for a whole year. Whereupon he concludeth that this narration (which he calleth a fable) hath no probability nor possibility to be true. ² To whom and all such calumniators it may be answered, that Moyses even in an heretic's own conceit, if malice obscured not his sense, must needs be thought wise enough, if he had been disposed to feign fables, to frame them probable, or possible, especially when he pretended not to signify a miracle in the smallness of the room to receive so much as he reporteth. ³ Origen to answer him, supposeth a cubit here mentioned to have contained six ordinary cubits: and so doubtless the ark might easily contain all things that are here spoken of, for so it were like to a great city. ⁴ But this opinion neither hath good warrant, that ever the Ægyptians (of whom he supposeth Moyses might have learned it) or any other nation used such long cubits, neither can this measure of a cubit be agreeable to Moyses meaning, ⁵ who no doubt speaketh of the like cubits here, as he doth in other places. ⁶ And in Exodus he describeth an Altar to be made five cubits long, five broad, and three in height. Which would be by Origen's measure every cubit containing six ordinary cubits, that is nine foot at least) in length, & likewise in breadth 45. foot, and 27. foot in height. Again (Deut. 3.) Moyses telleth of an iron bed of Og King of Basan, that was nine cubits long, and four broad. Which make according to Origen's measure of a cubit, fourscore and one foot in length, and in breadth 36. foot: which indeed have no probability And therefore ⁷ S. Augustin and other Doctors, supposing that Moyses in all these books, written for instruction of the same people, whom he brought forth of Ægypt, speaketh of one sort of cubits, do likewise judge that he meaneth ordinary and known cubits, which contain a foot and a half every cubit, as Vitruvius Agricola and others do prove, or a foot and three quarters of a foot, which is the greatest cubit that seemeth to be mentioned in holy Scripture, called a man's cubit, or \+it cubit of a man's hand.\+it* ⁸ And so the Ark was at least in length 450. foot, in breadth 75. in height 45. or at most in length 525. foot, in breadth 87. and a half: in height 52. and a half. And either of these capacities was sufficient to receive all the things here mentioned, considering the lofts and partitions that were in the whole ark. \fq ¹ \ft Appelles an old Heretic, that denied Christ to have true flesh. \fq ² \ft A general answer to all calumniators of wise & learned men. \fq ³ \ft \+it ho. 2. in 6. Gen.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Origen's opinion of long cubits not probable. \fq ⁵ \ft Moyses in other places cannot be understood to speak of so long cubits. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Exod. 27.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft \+it l. 15. civ. c. 27.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Deu. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 Thou shalt make a window in the ark, & in a cubit finish the top of it: & the door of the ark thou shalt set at the side below, middle chambers & third lofts shalt thou make in it.
\v 17 Behold I will bring the waters of a great flood upon the earth, that I may destroy all flesh wherein there is breath of life under heaven. All things that are in the earth shall be consumed.
\v 18 And I will establish my covenant with thee: and thou shalt enter into the ark, thou and thy sons, and thy wife, and the wives of thy sons with thee.
\v 19 And of all living creatures of all flesh, thou shalt bring pairs into the ark, that they may live with thee: of the male sex and the female.
\v 20 Of fowls according to their kind, and beasts in their kind, and of all that creepeth on the earth, according to their kind: pairs of all sorts shall enter in with thee, that they may live.
\v 21 Thou shalt take therefore with thee of all meats, that may be eaten, and thou shalt lay them up with thee: & they shall be meat for thee and them.
\v 22 Noe therefore \f d \fr 6.22 \ft A right example of a just man.\f*\f 1 \fr 6.22 \ft Heb. 11.\f* did all things which God commanded him.
\c 7
\cd Noe with his family, and pairs of all kinds of beasts and fowls, being entered into the ark, 12. it raineth forty nights. 21. All men and other living creatures on the earth, without the ark, are destroyed.
\p
\v 1 And our Lord said to him: Get thee in, thou and all thy house into the ark; for I have seen thee just \f a \fr 7.1 \ft Noe was just not only by the estimation of men, but indeed and before God.\f* in my sight in this generation.
\v 2 Of all beasts that are \f b \fr 7.2 \ft Observation of clean and unclean beasts by tradition, before the law of Moyses.\f* clean, thou shalt take seven and seven, male and female:
\v 3 but of the beasts that are unclean two and two, male and female. Yea and of the fowls also of the air seven and seven, male and female: that seed may be saved upon the face of the whole earth.
\v 4 For yet a while, and after seven days I will rain upon the earth forty days and forty nights: and I will clean destroy all substance that I have made, from the face of the earth.
\v 5 Noe therefore did all things which our Lord had commanded him.
\v 6 And he was six hundred years old, when the waters of the flood flowed over the earth.
\v 7 \x - \xt Mat. 24. Luc. 17.\x* And Noe entered and his sons, his wife and the wives of his sons with him into the ark, because of the waters of the flood.
\v 8 Of beasts also the clean and the unclean, and of fowls, and of all that moveth upon the earth.
\v 9 two and two went to Noe into the ark, male and female, as our Lord had commanded Noe.
\v 10 And after the seven days were passed, the waters of the flood flowed over the earth.
\v 11 In the six hundred year of the life of Noe, in the second month, in the seventeenth day of the month, all the fountains of the \f c \fr 7.11 \ft The Hebrew word \+it Thehom\+it* signifieth a gulf of water, from whence new fountains sprang, more abundantly than ever since or before.\f* great depth were broken up, and \f d \fr 7.11 \ft \+it Arubbah\+it* signifieth great pipes or windows, by which water fell down in great abundance from the air, here called heaven. \+it S. Hier. quest. Heb. S. Epiph, ad Io. Hierosolym. S. Chr. ho. 25. in Gen.\+it*\f* the flood-gates of heaven were opened:
\v 12 and the rain fell upon the earth forty days and forty nights.
\v 13 In the very point of that day entered Noe, and Sem, and Cham, and Japheth his sons; and his wife, and the three wives of his sons with them into the ark.
\v 14 they and every beast according to their kind, and all cattle in their kind, and all that moveth upon the earth according to their kind, and all fowl according to their kind, all birds, and all that fly.
\v 15 went to Noe into the ark, two and two of all flesh, wherein there was breath of life.
\v 16 And such as entered in, male and female of all flesh did enter in, as God had commanded him: and our Lord shut him in on the out side. \ef - \fr 7.16 \fq Shut him in. \ft God who by his only will could in a moment have drowned all the rest of the world, saving whom he pleased, not needing in any thing the help of his creatures, ¹ yet would use both natural and supernatural means, as the labour of Noe to build the ark, new fountains springing, and the heavens, pouring down water forty days together, afterwards the wind to dry up the earth, and because the door being great (for Elephants to enter in) and was to be firmed without (as S. Ambrose noteth) ² for better enduring the forcible waters, could not commodiously be closed by Noe, \+it Our Lord\+it* by the ministry of Angels) \+it shut him in on the out side,\+it* to teach us by all this and the like disposition of things, that albeit his Divine omnipotency can do what he will all alone, yet he will have his creatures to concur and cooperate as secondary causes, sometimes naturally, sometimes supernaturally, or miraculously, as it pleaseth his goodness to impart to them power and virtue. \fq ¹ \ft God useth both natural and supernatural means, as secondary causes, in producing, conserving, governing, punishing, & in rewarding his creatures. \fq ² \ft \+it l. de Noe & arca, c. 15.\+it*\ef*
\v 17 And the flood grew forty days upon the earth: and the waters increased, and lifted up the ark on high from the earth.
\v 18 For they overflowed exceedingly; and filled all on the face of the earth: moreover the ark fleeted upon the waters.
\v 19 And the waters prevailed out of measure upon the earth; and all the high mountains under the whole heaven were covered.
\v 20 Fifteen cubits higher was the water above the mountains, which it covered.
\v 21 And all flesh was consumed that moved upon the earth, of fowl, of cattle, of beasts, and of all creepers, that creep upon the earth: all men,
\v 22 and all things, wherein there is breath of life on the earth, died. \x - \xt Sap. 10, 4.\x*
\v 23 \x - \xt Eccl. 39.\x* And he clean destroyed all substance that was upon the earth, from man even to beast, as well it that creepeth, as the fowls of the air: and they were destroyed from off the earth: but only Noe remained, and they that were with him in the ark. \ef - \fr 7.23 \fq But only Noe. \ft As there is not any thing in all the old Testament, from the creation of the world till the coming of Christ more notable, more admirable, or of greater importance, than this history of the general flood; so was there nothing (though all or most chanced to them in figure) ¹ ² that ever more aptly, more lively, or more exactly prefigured Christ and his Church, with the rest of all mankind, than did Noe & the ark, & the drowning of the rest of the world in that deluge. Which S. Augustin declareth in many places, ³ but most especially & of purpose in his twelfth book against Faustus the Manichee, from the 14. chapter to the 22. and in his fifteenth book of the city of God, in the two last chapters: where he sheweth at large both the certainty of the history, & that as certainly it was a figure of things in the new Testament, & withal the great congruity between the figure & the things figured. ⁴ The same did Origen explicate (hom. 2. in Gen.) S. Gregory (hom. 12. in Ezech.) Rupertus (li. 4. comment. in Gen. c. 71. & sequent.) & diverse other ancient Doctors, confirming their expositions by S. Peter's testimony, saying: In the ark \+it a few, that is eight souls\+it* (or persons) \+it were saved\+it* (from drowning) \+it by water, whereunto Baptism being of the like form now saveth you also.\+it* And by our Saviour's words saying: ⁵ \+it As in the days of Noe, so shall also the coming of the Son of man be.\+it* In sum the Doctors teach, that Noe, signifying \+it rest\+it*, was a figure of Christ, ⁶ the very \+it rest\+it* of man's soul. Whom whosoever followeth shall find rest for their souls. The ark signifieth the Church, ⁷ the form thereof being six times so long as broad, and ten times so long as high, resembleth the proportion of man's body, lying prone or prostrate. The door in the side representeth the wound in Christ's side, from whence flowed the holy Sacraments, by which the faithful enter into the Church, ⁸ & are sanctified. The timber whereof the ark is made, & the water bearing it up, signified the Cross of Christ & Baptism. \+it For as Noe\+it* (saith S. Augustin) ⁹ \+it with his, was delivered by the water and the wood, so the family of Christ, by Baptism signed with Christ's Passion on the Cross.\+it* ¹⁰ Likewise the squareness of the timber which both sustained the burden of all contained in the ark, and resisted the boisterous waves of the flood beating without, did signify such men in the Church, as be constant and stand firmly in all sorts of tentations: especially godly and learned Doctors and Pastors, ¹¹ who by word and example uphold and confirm the faithful people in all afflictions within, and withstand & convince all Heretics and other Infidels that oppugn the Church without. Again, the higher and lower rooms with the middle chambers and third lofts, and other distinctions of cabinets, and partitions, and all sorts of living creatures clean and unclean, received therein, did signify the varieties of all states of functions, ¹² and diversity of manners and merits in the Church, in which are persons of all degrees, Clergy and Laity, Potentates, Princes, subjects, good and evil. ¹³ The most strong kind of glue called \+it bitumen\+it*, signified the permanent or everlasting stability, ¹⁴ and unseparable connexion of the Church, by the grace and continual assistance of the Holy Ghost conserving the same. The consummation of the ark in one cubit signified the unity of the same Church, which is one in all times and places ¹⁵. Neither would God Almighty have many arks for Noe and his sons or other creatures, not many chief Rulers (though he would that of them should come many Nation:) but one only ark, and one chief Governor thereof, ¹⁶ and that all without the same should corporally die, to signify that all which die without the Church do perish, and are eternally damned. ¹⁷ Whereupon S. Hierom, ¹⁸ amongst other Fathers, sheweth that all within the Church, that communicate with the See Apostolic (wherein S. Damasus sat then Governor) are as those in the ark of Noe, and all Schismatics, Heretics, and other Infidels are in like case with the rest of the world, that were drowned with the flood. \fq ¹ \ft \+it 1 Cor. 10.\+it* \fq ² \ft All or most things in the old Testament be in figure of the new, & no figure more exact than the flood of Noe. \fq ³ \ft \+it Ep. 99. l. 5. de Bap. c. 28. de unitate Eccles. ca. 5. In Psal. 103. & 131. Ser. 69. de tempore.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft How the Doctors apply the figure to the things figured. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it 1. Pet. 3. Mat. 24. Luc. 17.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft Noe signified Christ. \fq ⁷ \ft The ark the Church. \fq ⁸ \ft Entrance into the Church by Baptism. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it li. 12. de Baptis. c. 14.\+it* \fq ¹⁰ \ft Virtue of Sacraments cometh from Christ's Passion. \fq ¹¹ \ft Doctors and Pastors in the Church. \fq ¹² \ft Variety of states and orders in the Church \fq ¹³ \ft Good and evil in the Church. \fq ¹⁴ \ft Perpetuity of the Church. \fq ¹⁵ \ft Unity of the Church. \fq ¹⁶ \ft One chief Governor in the Church. \fq ¹⁷ \ft No salvation out of the Church. \fq ¹⁸ \ft \+it Ep. 57. ad Damasum.\+it*\ef*
\v 24 \x - \xt 1. Pet. 3.\x* And the waters held on above the earth an hundred fifty days.
\c 8
\cd The waters diminishing by little & little, 5. Noe sendeth forth a crow, 8. after him a dove, thrice: 18. lastly goeth forth with all that were with him in the ark, 20. erecteth an Altar, and offereth Sacrifice. \f - \ft The second Age of the world.\f*
\s1 A BRIEF REMONSTRANCE OF THE STATE OF THE CHURCH and face of Religion in the first Age of the world, from the creation to Noe's flood, the space of 1656. years.
\ip Here, according to our purpose mentioned before, we will briefly recite certain principal points of Religion, taught and observed in the first Age: In which the foundations of the true manner of serving God (that should be continued to the end of the world) were laid, and prospered in some, as appeareth in these seven first chapters of Genesis. But first of all, we shall in two words repeat (as it is clearly gathered in the same holy Scripture) the state of man before and immediately after his fall, being the subject to whom all this pertaineth.
\ip After therefore that God had created other things, both in heaven and earth, last of all he made \f - \ft Man made to God's image, and in happy state.\f* Man, to his own image and likeness, with understanding & freewill, therein like to Angels, and superior to all other creatures, and so made him Lord and Master of all earthly things. Neither were these the greatest benefits which God bestowed on man: for his divine goodness endued also this his reasonable creature with innocency & original justice, whereby all things were most rightly ordered within him and about him. His mind, will, and reason were obedient to God; \f - \ft Man obeyed God, and all earthly creatures obeyed man.\f* his senses & inferior part of his soul were subject to reason; his flesh and body obeyed the spirit; and all earthly creatures obeyed him. God also adorned man with excellent knowledge, both natural and supernatural. And albeit his body was of corruptible substance, yet the same, and all his posterity, if they had not sinned, should have been conserved, and without dying have been translated to everlasting life. Thus man was placed in Paradise, and Eve there made of a rib of \f - \ft Man placed in Paradise.\f* his side, to be his mate and unseparable companion, as man and wife joined in Marriage, with God's blessing for increase and multiplication. As appeareth in the two first chapters of this book.
\ip But \it God having made man right, he entangled himself\it* (as holy Scripture speaketh) \it with\it* \f - \ft Man fell by yielding to tentations.\f* \f - \ft \+it Eccl. 7. 30. Sap. 2. 24.\+it*\f* \it infinite questions\it*. For the devil envying man's felicity inveigled our mother Eve with questions and lies, and then by her, first seduced and deceived, allured also Adam to the transgression of God's commandment. And so they lost original justice, which Adam had received for himself and all mankind: and all proceeding from them by natural propagation are born the children of wrath, in original sin contracted from Adam, slaves of the devil, not only subject to temporal death, \f - \ft Original sin.\f* but also are excluded for ever from heavenly bliss and glory: except by Christ's redemption particularly applied, they be restored to grace and justice in this life.
\ip And touching Adam and Eve, whose sin was not original but actual, directly committed by \f - \ft Adam and Eve were penitent.\f* themselves, God's mercy so reclaimed them by new grace, that they despaired not (as Cain, and some others did afterwards) but with hope of remission were sorry and penitent, and accordingly received penance and redemption. For God \it brought Adam from his sin\it* (as holy writ testifieth) \f - \ft \+it Sap. 10.\+it*\f* and the same is collected of Eve, God shewing the like signs of his provident mercy towards them both, of which we shall by and by note some for example.
\ip Now let us see the more principal points of faith and Religion professed and observed by the Church of God before Noe's flood. First, they believed in one Eternal and Omnipotent God, who \f - \ft Faith in one God.\f* made the whole world and all things therein of nothing. Which is easily confessed of all that are not plain Atheists, and may be proved against them by reason. And therefore Adam and other Patriarchs could not err in this Article, nor others be ignorant thereof, except they were very wicked.
\ip The Mystery also of the Blessed Trinity, three Divine Persons in one God, though far above \f - \ft The Blessed Trinity.\f* the reach of man's reason, yet was believed more expressly by some, more implied by others, and conserved from Age to Age by tradition, at least amongst the chief heads and leaders. Whereupon Moyses afterwards insinuated the same great Mystery by diverse words and phrases, writing of God and his works. The two words \it God created\it* if they be rightly considered import so much. For the word \it Elohim, God\it*, in the plural number, signifieth plurality of Persons (for many Gods it cannot signify, seeing there is but one God) and the verb \it bara, created\it*, in the singular number signifieth one God in nature and substance, albeit three Persons. For whatsoever God doth in creatures, is the work of the whole Trinity: though holy Scriptures do oftentimes appropriate some work to one Divine Person, some to another; which also proveth distinction of Persons in God. \f - \ft Though the B. Trinity work jointly in all creatures, yet diverse works are attributed to distinct Persons.\f* So the words \it God created heaven and earth\it* signify the \it Father\it*, to whom \it power\it* is attributed: \it In\it* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 1.\+it*\f* \it the beginning\it*, signify \it the son\it*, to whom \it wisdom\it* is appropriated: and the words, \it The Spirit of God moved over the waters\it*, signify \it the Holy Ghost\it*, by whose bountiful \it goodness\it*, the waters were made fruitful. Likewise God's own words: \it Let us make man\it* signify the plurality of Persons, and \it Image and likeness\it* in the singular number, signify one God.
\ip Men also knew by faith many things pertaining to themselves. As that the body was made of the \f - \ft By faith the state of man past & present was known.\f* slime of the earth: the soul not produced of any thing formerly existing, but created immediately of nothing, and naturally immortal: that the soul of Adam was endued with grace and justice: that he fell from the happy state by yielding to tentation, and breaking God's commandment of abstinence: that for the same sin Adam and Eve were cast forth of Paradise, and all mankind subject to death, and other calamities.
\ip For remedy against sin, & restoration to grace, they believed in \it Christ\it* promised to be born of \f - \ft Belief in Christ to come.\f* the woman's seed, who by his death should conquer the wicked serpent, deliver man from captivity, and restore him to spiritual life. And this is the cause of the perpetual enmity between the woman (especially the most Blessed Virgin-Mother, of whom Christ took flesh) and the serpent, and between her seed, the spiritual children of Christ, and the serpent's seed, the whole company of the wicked. Of this battle and conquest Thargum Hierosolimitanum thus speaketh: \it There shall be remedy and health to the children of women, but to thee, o serpent, there shall be\it* \f - \ft Remedy for men, but none for Angels that fell. Heb. 2. 16.\f* \it no medicine, yea they shall tread thee under their feet, in the latter days, by the power of Christ their King\it*. Likewise God's familiar conversation with diverse men in man's shape (Gen. 2. 3. 4. 6. and 7.) was a sign of Christ's incarnation. And the Sacrifices immolated did prefigurate his death, in respect whereof it is said in the Apocalypse, \it The Lamb was slain from the beginning of the\it* \f - \ft \+it Apo. 13, 8.\+it*\f* \it world\it*. But more expressly S. Paul testifieth, that Abel, Enoch, and Noe believed in Christ, naming them for example of the first Age, and others of other times, and in the end concludeth, that many more \it being approved by the same faith, received not the promise\it* (to wit in their lifetime) \it God providing that they without others\it* (of the new Law) \it should not be consummate\it*, that is, not \f - \ft None admitted into heaven before Christ.\f* \f - \ft \+it Heb. 11.\+it*\f* admitted into heavenly joys & fruition of God, until the way of eternal glory were opened by our Lord's Passion and Ascension.
\ip Neither did the true servants of God in those first days only believe in heart, but they also \f - \ft External Sacrifice.\f* professed their faith & Religion by external Rites, namely in offering of Sacrifice (the most special homage & service to God) which is clearly testified, chap. 4. as well bloody in figure of Christ's Passion, as unbloody in figure of the holy Eucharist. Also the accepting of the one rightly offered by Abel, & rejecting the other not done sincerely by Cain, was declared by external signs, which Cain disdaining and envying his brother's good work, knowing his own to be nought, of mere malice killed his brother.
\ip Besides Sacrifice they had also other Rites in public Assemblies, praying and invocating the name \f - \ft Public prayer with other Rites.\f* of our Lord, in more solemn manner, from Enos' time and so forward, according to that is recorded of him in the end of the fourth chapter. For doubtless Adam, Abel, and Seth did also pray and call upon God, and therefore it was some addition or increase of solemnity in the service of God, which is referred to Enos.
\ip They had moreover other ceremonies: of the seventh day particularly blessed and \f - \ft Ceremonial observations.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 2, 3.\+it*\f* sanctified by God, kept holy by Adam and other Patriarchs, as Abben Ezra witnesseth in his \f - \ft Feasts.\f* commentaries upon the ten commandments: of abstaining from meats, for it seemeth the \f - \ft Abstinence.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 20. Gen. 9.\+it*\f* more godly sort did eat no flesh before the flood, which was after permitted: observation \f - \ft Clean & unclean.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 7, 2.\+it*\f* of clean and unclean beasts for Sacrifice: of peculiar places dedicated to religious uses, \f - \ft Places dedicated to prayer.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4, 26.\+it*\f* where people met together to pray. Likewise diverse other things in the first Age were figures of Christ's Sacraments : the Spirit of God giving power to the waters (as Tertullian, S. \f - \ft Figures of Christ's Sacraments.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 1.\+it*\f*
\ip Hierom, and others expound it) and the flood of Noe, by S. Peter's testimony, were figures of Baptism . Marriage instituted in Paradise, is the very pattern of holy \f - \ft Baptism.\f* \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 3.\+it*\f* \f - \ft Marriage.\f* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 19.\+it*\f* Matrimony, a Sacrament in the Church of Christ, where one man and one wife are only lawful, and not more at once in any wise, Christ reforming that which in Moyses' law was tolerated (for hardness of men's hearts, and for avoiding murder, to put away one wife, and take another) to this first institution as it was in the beginning, \it two in one flesh\it*, not three nor more. The repentance of Adam and Eve was a perfect and examplar figure of the Sacrament of Penance . First, \f - \ft Penance.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 2, 24.\+it*\f* they were ashamed, covering their nakedness, and hiding themselves, which shewed their grief and sorrow for the sin committed . Secondly they confessed their fault , and by \f - \ft Contrition. conserved.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 3.\+it*\f* \f - \ft confession.\f* what means it happened. For God examining Adam, he answered truly and simply saying: \it The woman which thou gavest me, to be my companion, gave me of the tree, and I did eat\it*. Likewise Eve confessed sincerely, saying: \it The serpent deceived me, and I did eat\it*. Thirdly, God gave them \f - \ft Satisfaction.\f* penance (besides death before threatened and other penalties annexed) that Eve \it should in pain and travail bring forth her children\it*; and \it Adam should eat his bread in the sweat of his face\it*, and withal cast them forth of Paradise, but not forth of his favour, as appeared by his making them garments of skins, granting them & their posterity the rest of the earth to live and labour in, especially to serve him & do penance, with admonition to remember, that of dust man was made, and into dust he shall return . All which were signs of love, and that finally he would bring them and many \f - \ft From hence is taken the ceremony of ashes, on Ashwednesday.\f* more to eternal salvation.
\ip The first-born and heads of families were Priests at the time of the law of nature, until the law \f - \ft Priesthood.\f* being changed, God took Priests only of the stock of Aaron, and the rest of the Levites to assist them in that function: \it Aaron & his sons thou shalt appoint, saith our Lord, over the service of\it* \f - \ft \+it Num. 3. 10, 12. 45.\+it*\f* \it Priesthood, for I have taken the Levites of the children of Israel for every first-born\it*. And S. Paul teacheth, that changing of Priesthood and changing of the law go always together, shewing \f - \ft Priesthood & Law stand & change together.\f* evidently that every lawful community or common-wealth under God, hath external Priesthood. So that if there had been no distinct Order of external Priesthood in the law of nature, or now were none in the law of grace (as Protestants say there is not) there were no law at all. See more of \f - \ft \+it Luther lib. de abroganda Missa.\+it*\f* this point in the Annot. chap. 7. ad Hebre. Here we only observe that Abel, Seth, Enos, and other Patriarchs were Priests, & exercised priestly functions: yea Cain also was a Priest (though a bad one) and offered Sacrifice.
\ip But external offices or ministry, without a well disposed mind and sincere virtues producing \it Good works\it*, did never justify any man. And therefore Cain's Sacrifice , offered with a perverse \f - \ft Good works necessary.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4.\+it*\f* mind, was not respected by God, as Abel's was: whereupon he becoming worse and more malicious, God sharply reproved his anger and envy, conceived without just cause, saying: \it If thou doest well, shalt thou not receive again: but if thou doest ill, shall not thy sin forthwith be present at the door?\it* clearly shewing that every one shall receive according to his works.
\ip This place also evidently sheweth \it Freewill\it*, yea in a wicked man. For this expostulation had \f - \ft Freewill.\f* never been uttered by our most reasonable Lord and Master, if Cain had been deprived of freewill. For he might have excused himself, and must needs have been holden excused, if he had been forced to do as he did. But God charged him as inexcusable, and as one that knew, or ought to know, that he had freewill. And doth further inculcate that he had, and should have power and freewill over his concupiscence, to correct the same if he would, saying: \it The lust thereof shall be under thee, and thou shalt have dominion over it\it*. So that no sinner, be he never so wicked, much less a just man, lacketh freewill. Yet Luther abhorreth the very word, and Calvin wished it out \f - \ft \+it Li. de servo arbit. li. 2. Instit. ca. 2. par. 8. Gen. 3.\+it*\f* of the world.
\ip \it Temporal punishment\it* is proved to be due for sin remitted, by that both death and other penalties \f - \ft Temporal pain due for sin remitted.\f* are inflicted by God's justice upon men, after justification, and by the particular punishments laid upon Adam and Eve, confessing their faults.
\ip \it Purgatory\it* is also proved by the same justice of God. For when any dieth penitent, and yet have \f - \ft Purgatory.\f* not made full satisfaction, they must suffer for that remaineth after death, and be purged, before they can enter into rest. Which remnant of debt our B. Saviour calleth \it The last farthing\it*, and \f - \ft \+it Mat. 5.\+it*\f* saith, it must be payed. The Jews also at this day hold the doctrine of Purgatory by tradition. And consequently they \it Pray for souls departed\it*, not only to God, but also to the ancient Patriarchs \f - \ft Prayer for the dead.\f* (which likewise sheweth \it Invocation of Saints\it* ) in these words: \it Ye fathers which sleep in\it* \f - \ft And to Saints.\f* \f - \ft \+it Officio pro defunctis.\+it*\f* \it Hebron, open to him the gates of Eden\it*, that is of Paradise, which was planted in Eden. And Hebron is the place where Adam was buried, and his sepulcher religiously conserved in the time of \f - \ft Sepulchers of Patriarchs religiously\f* Iosue , above 1500 years after his death. The same is the place which Abraham bought, and \f - \ft \+it Iosue. 14. Gen. 23.\+it*\f* there buried Sara: where also himself, and Isaac, and Jacob were buried: and to which finally the bodies of the twelve sons of Jacob were translated from Sichem, as Iosephus writeth . And \f - \ft \+it l. 2. Antiquit.\+it*\f* Sichem also was specially honoured, because such persons had been buried there, as S. Hierome witnesseth of his own knowledge in his time. \f - \ft \+it Epist. ad Pamach.\+it*\f*
\ip Again by religious care of burying the dead in this first age, \it Enoch\it* was more certainly known \f - \ft Enoch translated alive.\f* to be \it Translated\it* alive, and not to be dead. For the seventy Interpreters, and S. Paul say \it He was not found\it*, which importeth that they sought diligently for him, and that his body could not be \f - \ft \+it Gen. 5. Heb. 11.\+it*\f* found, for God translated him.
\ip By all which we see mutual offices, and communion of good works amongst good men alive and dead, which is called \it Communion of Saints\it*. And herein \it Angels\it* lacked not their offices. \f - \ft Communion of Saints.\f* \f - \ft Ministry of Angels.\f*
\ip For God set Cherubins to keep the gate of Paradise, that neither man should enter, being justly \f - \ft \+it Gen. 3. 24.\+it*\f* expelled for sin, nor devils, as S. Augustin noteth , lest they should take fruit of the tree of life, \f - \ft \+it li. 11. de Gen. ad lit. c. 40.\+it*\f* and giving it to men, allure them to more sin. And now \it Saints\it* being exalted to Angels' glory, have like honourable offices towards other men , as Angels have. Yea the blood of Abel unjustly \f - \ft Honour of Saints.\f* shed by Cain , and justly to be revenged by God, sheweth the peculiar honour, which God \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4.\+it*\f* bestoweth upon his Saints, for their virtues and merits in this life, & especially in their death. For \it Precious in the sight of our Lord, is the death of his Saints\it*. \f - \ft \+it Psal. 115.\+it*\f*
\ip Hence also is proved, that seeing in this life the good are afflicted, and the bad oftentimes prosper temporally, there must needs be another \it Court\it* of exact Justice, and another \it Reckoning day\it*, \f - \ft General Judgement.\f* wherein every one shall receive, according as they have done good or evil. Which was sufficiently intimated by God's discussing, and manifesting Abel's and Cain's deserts, which were hidden before, and in part rewarding them accordingly, yet reserving the full reward of the one, and punishment of the other to the next world. Of the \it Judge\it* and his sentence Enoch (alleged by S. \f - \ft Judge of the world.\f* Jude the Apostle) prophesied clearly, saying: \it Behold our Lord cometh in his holy thousands, to\it* \f - \ft \+it Epis Iudae. v. 14.\+it*\f* \it do judgement against all, and to reprove all the impious of all the works of their impiety, whereby they have done impiously, and of all the hard things which impious sinners have spoken against him\it*. Thus holy Enoch preached touching the wicked, which thought there was no Judgement to come, nor Judge to be feared.
\ip At this Judgment all shall appear in body and soul returning to life. For that \it All men shall rise\it* \f - \ft Resurrection.\f* from death is proved by the immortality of man's soul, which God did not make nor produce of corruptible matter, but immediately \it breathed into his face the breath of life, and man became a living soul\it*. So the soul being immortal, and having a natural inclination to the body, man's natural \f - \ft \+it Gen. 2, 7.\+it*\f* perfection requireth the conjunction of body and soul. For neither soul nor body separated is a man, but both joined in one subsistence are a man, in so much that mankind should perish, except the bodies shall rise again, and live with the soul. And then shall the bodies be qualified according to the state of the souls, happy or miserable for ever.
\ip Of \it Eternal life\it* the translation of Enoch is a figure. For seeing God preserveth his \f - \ft Everlasting life.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 5.\+it*\f* corruptible body so long from death and infirmity, it is a token and manifest sign, that by the same power of God the bodies of men shall at the last day, after that all men are once dead, rise again, and remain with the souls forever: The good in \it Eternal joy\it*, the wicked in \it Eternal pain\it*. \f - \ft The blessed in eternal joy.\f* \f - \ft The wicked in endless pain.\f*
\ip Both signified by the custody of the gate of Paradise by Angels ; who for ever keep out those, \f - \ft \+it Gen. 3, 24.\+it*\f* that are still defiled with sin, and so they depart into fire everlasting, and admit the innocent and just into the Kingdom of heaven, which is everlasting joy and perfect felicity.
\ip Thus we see the face and brief sum of Religion in the beginning of the world, till the flood: and the state of the Church, which was always \it Visible\it*, consisting of men good and bad, with a \f - \ft Church ever visible.\f* continual \it Succession\it* of Rulers , as well spiritual as temporal. For the first-born were both \f - \ft Succession of Patriarchs.\f* Priests and Princes in every family. And amongst the same one ever chief of all . From which \f - \ft One supreme head of the Church.\f* rank Cain was excluded, or rather excluded himself, by \it Going forth from the face of our Lord\it*. \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4, 16.\+it*\f* Whereupon holy Moyses reciteth this Monarchical succession of one chief and \it Supreme Head\it*, \f - \ft \+it Gen. 5.\+it*\f* from \it Adam\it* by the line of \it Seth, Enos, Cainan, Malaleel, Jared, Enoch, Mathusala, Lamech\it*, and \it Noe\it*. Nevertheless he setteth down also the progeny of Cain , the first beginner of a worldly, \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4, 17.\+it*\f* schismatical, and heretical conventicle , opposite to the City of God. He denied God's \f - \ft Cain's negative doctrine.\f* \f - \ft \+it S. Aug. l. de Pastore. c. 8. & 20.\+it*\f* providence (as Thargum Hierosolimitanum testifieth) protesting to Abel, \it That there was no Justice nor Judge, nor other world than this, no reward for virtue, nor punishment for sin, and so desperately he killed Abel\it*. Of these negative principles proceeded other like detestable opinions, and most wicked life, savage and barbarous cruelty, and all kind of impiety. And in process of time albeit many remained in true faith, and unity of the Church , yet by conversation with such \f - \ft True faith still remained in many.\f* miscreants, especially by occasion of Marriages between the faithful and infidels, almost the whole world was corrupted in manners. But Noe was just and perfect . In punishment \f - \ft Some also just and perfect.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 6.\+it*\f* therefore of so great and enormous sins, God sent the general flood, whereby all Cain's progeny, and all other infidels were wholly destroyed and extinguished , and the true Church notably \f - \ft Interruption of heretical Synagogues.\f* purged; only just Noe and his family reserved. By whom the same true Church was continued , and the world again replenished with men. \f - \ft Continuance of the Church.\f*
\p
\v 1 And \f 1 \fr 8.1 \ft The third part of this book. Of the new increase & multiplication of the world.\f* God remembered Noe, and all the beasts, and all the cattle which were with him in the ark, and brought a wind upon the earth, and the waters decreased.
\v 2 And the fountains of the depth and the flood-gates of heaven were shut up: and the rain from heaven was stayed.
\v 3 And the waters returned from the earth going & coming: and they began to decrease after an hundred fifty days.
\v 4 And the ark rested the seventh month, the seven and twentieth day of the month, upon the mountains of Armenia.
\v 5 But the waters for all that were going and decreasing until the tenth month: for in the tenth month, the first day of the month, the tops of the mountains appeared.
\v 6 And after that forty days were passed, Noe opening the window of the ark, which he had made, let forth a crow:
\v 7 which went forth, and did \f a \fr 8.7 \ft The crow returned not into the ark, but (as appeareth by the Hebrew text) going and returning rested upon the ark.\f* not return, till the waters were dried upon the earth.
\v 8 He sent forth also a dove after him, to see if the waters were ceased yet upon the face of the earth.
\v 9 Which finding not where her foot might rest, returned to him into the ark: for the waters were upon the whole earth: and he stretched forth his hand and caught her, and brought her into the ark.
\v 10 And having expected yet seven more days again he let forth a dove out of the ark.
\v 11 But she came to him at eventide, carrying a bough of an olive tree that had green leaves, in her mouth. Noe therefore understood that the waters were ceased upon the earth.
\v 12 And he expected yet nevertheless other seven days: and he sent forth a dove, which returned not any more unto him.
\v 13 Therefore in the sixth hundred and one year, the first month, the first day of the month, the waters were clean diminished upon the earth: & Noe opening the roof of the ark, looked, and saw that the face of the earth was dried.
\v 14 In \f b \fr 8.14 \ft They entered into the ark the 17. day, the second month of the other year: so they remained there a whole year & ten days.\f* the second month, the seven and twentieth day of the month the earth was dried.
\v 15 And God spake to Noe, saying:
\v 16 Go forth of the ark, thou and thy wife, thy sons and the wives of thy sons with thee.
\v 17 All cattle that are with thee, of all flesh, as well in fowls, as in beasts, and all creepers that creep upon the earth, bring out with thee, and go ye upon the earth: increase and multiply upon it.
\v 18 Noe therefore went forth, and his sons: his wife, and the wives of his sons with him.
\v 19 Yea and all cattle, beasts, & creepers that creep upon the earth, according to their kind, went forth out of the ark.
\v 20 And Noe built an Altar to our Lord: and taking of all cattle and fowls that were clean, offered Holocausts upon the Altar. \ef - \fr 8.20 \fq Built an Altar. \ft ¹ Noe without express commandment, and without delay, offereth Sacrifice to God, for the benefit received in his and his families conservation, with the other living creatures, in that general deluge of the world, \+it Well knowing\+it* (saith S. Ambrose) ² \+it that to be true thanks-giving, which is presented, not commanded\+it* ³ \+it : therefore he made no delay\+it* ⁴ \+it . For the virtue of a grateful mind excludeth doubtful deliberation, and he that expecteth till the debt of thanks be exacted, is an ungrateful person.\+it* ⁵ For more solemnity, he dedicated an apt and permanent place, for this peculiar divine service, \+it building an Altar to our Lord.\+it* The Hebrew word \+it Mizbeach\+it* (of the verb \+it Zabach\+it*, to kill, or make sacrifice) and the Greek \+it Thysiasterion\+it*, signify an Altar to sacrifice on ⁶, not a common table for meat. He offered of the ⁷ clean and best things: because pure and devout Sacrifice is due to God. Moreover, it was large and bountiful ⁸, for he offered of all the kinds of clean beasts and fowls. Finally, he offered them in \+it Holocausts\+it* ⁹ \+it ,\+it* where all was burned and consumed in the honour of God. ¹⁰ How grateful all this was to God, Moyses signifieth saying: \+it Our Lord smelled a sweet savour:\+it* not that either any sweet corporal savour could of it-self delight God, who is the most spiritual substance, or that the burning of flesh, bones, and bowels of beasts could yield sweet savour; but the devout mind declared by such external duty greatly pleased God. For God requireth both, but specially a sincere heart. As not only divine Scriptures and holy Fathers ¹¹, but also moral Philosophers teach us. \+it It were a grievous thing\+it* (saith Plato writing of sacrifices) \+it If God had respect rather to the gifts and sacrifices of men, than to their mind.\+it* lib. peri tes proseuches. \fq ¹ \ft Noe's sacrifice many ways commendable. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. de Noe & Arca c. 22.\+it* \fq ³ \ft 1. Voluntary. \fq ⁴ \ft 2. Speedy. \fq ⁵ \ft 3. Solemn. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Heb. 13, 10.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft 4. Pure. \fq ⁸ \ft 5. Bountiful. \fq ⁹ \ft 6. Holocaust. \fq ¹⁰ \ft Sacrifice is pleasant to God: not for the external things but for the sincere mind. \fq ¹¹ \ft \+it Levit. 1. Psal. 50. Isai. 1. Amos. 5. Mal. 1. Iustin. Mar. li. 3. q. 4. S. Hiero. Q. Heb.\+it*\ef*
\v 21 And our Lord smelled a sweet savour, and said: I will no more curse the earth for men: for the sense and cogitation of man's heart are prone to evil from their youth: I will no more therefore strike every living soul as I have done.
\v 22 All the days of the earth, \f c \fr 8.22 \ft In the whole year of the flood was no sowing nor reaping, nor pleasant variety of times, but all desolate and miserable: hence forth God promiseth more seasonable times. S. Amb. li. de Noe & Arca. c. 33.\f* seed-time and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter, night and day shall not rest.
\c 9
\cd God reneweth the blessing of multiplication, 3. alloweth the eating of flesh, but not of blood. 8. promiseth never again to destroy the world by water. 22. Cham saw and reported his father's nakedness, which Sem and Japheth covered. 24. For which he is cursed, and they are blessed.
\p
\v 1 And God blessed Noe and his sons, And he said to them: \f a \fr 9.1 \ft Of this commandment, or rather blessing see the Annotations chap. 1. v. 28.\f* Increase & multiply, and replenish the earth.
\v 2 And your terror and dread be it upon all the beasts of the earth, and upon all the fowls of the air, with all that move upon the earth: all the fishes of the sea are delivered to your hand.
\v 3 And all that moveth and liveth shall be yours for meat: even as the green herbs have I delivered all to you. \ef - \fr 9.3 \fq All that moveth. \ft ¹ ² S. Justinus Martyr, S. Chrysostom, and other ancient Doctors prove, that flesh was lawful to be eaten before the flood: but being not necessary, because men were stronger, and other things also of more force, the better sort which were of Seth's race abstained from it. But after the flood flesh being more necessary, God altereth that custom of abstinence, with this limitation and commandment, that they shall not eat blood. \fq ¹ \ft Voluntary abstinence without commandment. \fq ² \ft \+it Ad. q. 119. Ho. 27. in. Gen.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 Saving that flesh with blood you shall not eat. \x - \xt Levi. 17.\x* \ef - \fr 9.4 \fq Flesh with blood. \ft ¹ Though this positive precept, of not eating blood, served well to make men more abhor man-slaughter (which is forbid by the law of nature, and the revenge thereof here and in other places severely threatened) yet it was specially given both immediately after the flood, and in the law of Moyses ² (with many the like) to exercise men in obedience. And the same was renewed, for a time, by the Apostles ³, to appease a controversy in the primitive Church. For that the Jews converted to Christ, having been long accustomed to this observation, could not endure to see it broken by themselves or other Christians, and being no great burden for the Gentiles, it was decreed that all should keep it. And so peace was made. Nevertheless it was abolished when the cause ceased, as S. Augustin ⁴ declareth against Faustus the Manichee. Such is the authority of the Church to decree, and again to disannul an observation of a thing of it-self indifferent. \fq ¹ \ft Abstinence from blood sometimes commanded, not always. \fq ² \ft \+it Levi. 17.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 15.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it li. 32. c. 13.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 For I will require the blood of your souls at the hands of all beasts: and at the hand of man, at the hand of each man, and of his brother, will I require the soul of man.
\v 6 Whosoever shall shed man's blood, his blood shall be shed: for to the image of God man was made.
\v 7 But increase you and multiply, and go upon the earth, and fill it.
\v 8 Thus also said God to Noe, and his sons with him:
\v 9 Behold I will establish my covenant with you, and with your seed after you:
\v 10 and with every living soul that is with you, as well in all fowls as in cattle & beasts of the earth that are come forth out of the ark, and in all beasts of the earth.
\v 11 I will establish my covenant with you, and all flesh shall be no more destroyed with the waters of a flood, neither shall there be from hence-forth a flood to waste the earth.
\v 12 And God said: This is the sign of the covenant which I give between me and you, and between every living soul, that is with you, for perpetual generations:
\v 13 \f b \fr 9.13 \ft The rainbow was before, but was not a sign, as God saith hence forth it should be, for men to remember his promise. \+it Severianus in Gen. S. Tho. Quodlib. 3. a. 30.\+it*\f* my bow will I set in the clouds, and it shall be the sign of a covenant between me and between the earth.
\v 14 And when I shall cover the element with clouds, my bow shall appear in the clouds:
\v 15 and I shall remember my covenant with you, and with every living soul that beareth flesh: and there shall no more be waters of a flood, to destroy all flesh.
\v 16 And my bow shall be in the clouds, and I shall see it, and I shall remember the everlasting covenant, that was made between God and every living soul of all flesh which is upon the earth.
\v 17 And God said to Noe: This shall be the sign of the covenant, which I established between me & all flesh of the earth.
\v 18 The sons therefore of Noe, that came out of the ark, were Sem, Cham, and Japheth: and Cham he is the father of Chanaan.
\v 19 These three are the sons of Noe: and \f c \fr 9.19 \ft By this it is clear that Noe had no more children after the flood. \+it S. Chrysost. ho. 29. in Gen.\+it*\f* of these was all mankind spread over the whole earth.
\v 20 And Noe a husbandman began to till the ground, and planted a vineyard.
\v 21 And drinking of the wine was made drunk, and naked in his tabernacle. \ef - \fr 9.21 \fq Drunk. \ft ¹ Noe sinned not, by the common judgement of ancient Fathers ², in that he was overcome with wine, because he knew not the force thereof, having drunk only water all his life before. But this external rebuke and worldly disgrace happened to Noe, in figure of Christ naked on the Cross ³, as S. Cyprian Epist. 63 ad Coecilium, S. Augustin li. 16. de civit. c. 2. & li. 12. contra Faustum Manicheum. c. 23. & 24. Eucherius, Rupertus, and others upon this place do teach. ⁴ And likewise that Sem and Japheth were a figure of the Church consisting of Jews and Gentiles, and Cham of Heretics, and other Infidels, that deride the infirmities, which our Saviour sustained. \+it Go to now\+it* (ye Manichees, saith S. Augustine) ⁵ \+it object calumnies to the ancient holy Scriptures, do so, ye children of Cham, to whom naked flesh seemeth vile, by which yourselves were begotten. For neither could ye by any means have been called Christians, except Christ, as he was foretold by the Prophets, had come into the world, had drunk that cup of his vineyard, which could not pass from him, had slept in his passion as in drunkenness of folly (which is wise than men) and so the infirmity of mortal flesh (which is stronger than men) had become naked, by the secret counsel of God, which infirmity unless the \+sc Word Of God\+sc* had taken upon him, the very name Christian, whereof ye also glory, had not been at all in the earth.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Noe sinned not in drunkenness. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Chrisost. ho. 29. S. Theo. q. 65. S. Am. c. 30. de Noe & Arca.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Noe a figure of Christ. \fq ⁴ \ft Sem & Japheth a figure of the Church, Cham of Infidels. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Li. 12. c. 24. cont. Faust.\+it*\ef*
\v 22 Which when Cham the father of Chanaan had seen, to wit that his father's privities were bare, he told it to his two brethren abroad.
\v 23 But indeed Sem and Japhet put a cloak upon their shoulders, and going backward, covered the privities of their father: and their faces were turned away, and they saw not their father's privities.
\v 24 And Noe awaking from the wine, when he had learned what his younger son had done to him,
\v 25 he said: Cursed be Chanaan, a servant of servants shall he be unto his brethren. \ef - \fr 9.25 \fq cursed be Chanaan. \ft ¹ Why Chanaan the son is cursed, and not his father Cham, diverse yield diverse reasons. ² S. Theodoretus reporteth out of the Hebrew Doctors, that Chanaan a boy first saw his grand-father's nakedness, and told the same to his father, and so they both derided that they should have covered, Cham's other sons not offending: and therefore not his whole progeny but only Chanaan & his posterity were here cursed by Noe. ³ S. Chrysostome supposeth that for so much as God had blessed Noe & his three sons coming forth of the Ark, he could not presume to curse any of those, whom God had blessed, therefore cursed Chanaan who in wickedness was like to his father. S. Gregory bringeth this for example of wicked men, escaping punishment in this life, and are punished in the next, and in their posterity following their vices ⁴. \+it What meaneth it\+it* (saith he) ⁵ \+it that Cham sinning, Chanaan his son had sentence of revenge, but that the sins of the reprobate prosper here in this life unrevenged, and are punished afterwards?\+it* And clear it is that Chanaan's most wicked posterity were subdued in the end, and most of them destroyed by the children of Israel ⁶ (who were of Sem) under the conduct of Josue, according to Noe's prophetical blessing of Sem, and cursing of Chanaan. \fq ¹ \ft Why Chanaan is cursed rather than Cham. \fq ² \ft \+it q. 57. in Gen.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it ho. 29.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Sins punished in the posterity. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Li. 25. Moral. c. 18.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft The effect of blessing and cursing.\ef*
\v 26 And he saith: Blessed be the Lord God of Sem, Chanaan be his servant. \x - \xt Apoc. 13, 8.\x*
\v 27 God enlarge Japheth, and dwell he in the tabernacles of Sem, and Chanaan be his servant. \ef - \fr 9.27 \fq God enlarge. \ft ¹ This blessing of Japheth was ² literally fulfilled, when (according to his name, which signifieth latitude or enlargement) his plentiful issue possessed most ample countries, both in the Continent and Islands. ³ But mystically it had effect (as S. Hierom, S. Augustine, Rupertus, and others expound it) when the Apostles, being Jews of the race of Sem, first builded the Christian Church, wherein the most part of that nation refused to dwell, contemning Christ's Gospel & grace, and the fullness of Gentiles entered in, and were made inheriters. Finally, Chanaan is servant to both Sem and Japhet, in that Heretics being under the Jurisdiction of the Church, gathered of Jews and Gentiles, ⁴ ⁵ serve to stir up Catholics' diligence to more exact knowledge of all truth; and their patience to more merit and glory. \fq ¹ \ft Japheth's blessing. \fq ² \ft Literal. \fq ³ \ft Mystical. \fq ⁴ \ft How Heretics serve Catholics. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Act. 13. Rom. 11. Ephes. 2. Tradit. Hebra. Li. 16. civit. c. 2. 24. cont. Faust. Li. 4. c. 38. in Gen.\+it*\ef*
\v 28 And Noe lived after the flood three hundred fifty years. \x - \xt Heb. 11.\x*
\v 29 And all his days were in the whole nine hundred fifty years: and he died.
\c 10
\cd The genealogy of Noe's children, by whom the world was increased again after the flood.
\p
\v 1 These are the generations of the sons of Noe, Sem, Cham, and Japheth: and children were born to them after the flood.
\v 2 The children of Japheth: Gomer, & Magog, & Madai, & Javan, & Tubal, and Mosoch, and Thiras.
\v 3 Moreover the children of Gomer: Ascenez, and Riphath, and Thogorma.
\v 4 And the sons of Javan: Elisa, and Tharsis, the Cetims and the Dodanims.
\v 5 Of these were divided the Isles of Nations in their countries, each one according to his tongue, & their families in their nations.
\v 6 And the sons of Cham: Chus, & Mesraim, & Phut, and Chanaan.
\v 7 And the sons of Chus: Saba, Hevila, and Sabatha, & Regma, & Sabathaca. The sons of Regma: Saba, and Dadan.
\v 8 Moreover Chus begat Nemrod: he began to be mighty in the earth. \ef - \fr 10.8 \fq Nemrod. \ft To this Nemrod the son of Chus, first builder and King of Babylon, Josephus, S. Epiphanius, S. Hierom, S. Augustin, and generally all ancient writers ascribe the first tyranny ¹ ², and first setting up of an earthly city opposite to the City of God after the flood ³. He was a \+it Valiant\+it*, or rather a \+it Violent hunter,\+it* and a giant hunter (saith S. Augustin, according to the seventy Interpreters) who by falsehood and force brought many under his dominion. For he stirred up pride (saith Josephus) and contempt of God in men, avouching that they were not beholding to God for present felicity, but to their own virtue; and so supposing men would fall from God to him, if he offered himself a leader and helper against a new flood, by little and little drew all to tyranny. He was otherwise called Saturnus, and was at length amongst others accounted a God. After him his son Belus Jupiter (as most authors affirm, reigned 65 years. And then succeeded Ninus the first King of the Assirians. \fq ¹ \ft Nemrod King of Babylon, a subtle & cruel giant. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. 1. c. 4. Antiquit. l. Antiq. Chalda. Li. 1. c. 2. in Osee. li. 16 c. 3. 4. & 17. civit. Berosus Annian li. 4. c. 5. Eusebius in chron. & alij.\+it* \fq ³ \ft An Arch-heretic.\ef*
\v 9 and he was a valiant hunter \f a \fr 10.9 \ft That is, in his sight who cannot be deceived.\f* before our Lord. Thereof rose a proverb: As it were Nemrod the valiant hunter before our Lord.
\v 10 And the beginning of his kingdom was Babylon, and Arach, and Achad, and Chalanne in the land of Sennaar.
\v 11 Out of that land came forth Assur, & builded Ninive, & the streets of the city, and Chale. \ef - \fr 10.11 \fq Assur builded Ninive. \ft ¹ Here is great difficulty and much dispute amongst writers, who this Assur is. Briefly, we may either say with ² Josephus and S. Augustin, that Assur the son of Sem built a city, which afterwards Ninus of Cham's race enlarged, enriched, and changing the name called it Ninum: or else that this word. \+it Assur\+it* here signifieth (as 4. Keg. 15. Psal. 82. Esaie 10. & 31.) the King of Assirians, to wit Ninus the son of Belus, who as all histories Greek and barbarous report (saith S. Hierom) was the first that reigned over all Asia, and among the Assyrians built Ninum a city of his own name, which the Hebrews call Ninive. He set up the Monarchy of the Assyrians, called the golden Kingdom, which stood 1240. years: and made his father Belus to be honoured for a God. To whom the Babylonians, as Plinie testifieth, first erected statuas, altars, and temples. ³ Of this Belus or Bel of Babylon were also derived other false gods, as Belial the god of Libertines, or without yoke, Beelphegor god of the Moabites, Beelzebub of the Acharonites, Baal in Samaria, Baalim amongst the Philistims, and the like in other nations ⁴. \fq ¹ \ft Assur, son of Sem, or Ninus King of Assirians. \fq ² \ft \+it (a) li. 1. Antiq. c. 4. Li. 16. c. 3. civit. Euseb. in chron.\+it* \fq ³ \ft First false gods. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Iud. 19. Num. 25. 4. Reg. 1. 3. Reg. 16.\+it*\ef*
\v 12 Resen also between Ninive, & Chale: this is the great city.
\v 13 But Mesraim also begat the Ludims, & the Anamims, & the Laabims, the Nephthuims,
\v 14 and the Phetrusims, & the Casluims: of whom came forth the Philistims & the Caphtorims.
\v 15 And Chanaan begat Sidon his first-begotten, Hethæus,
\v 16 and Iebusæus, and Amorrhæus, and Gergesæus.
\v 17 Hevæus and Aracæus, Sinæus,
\v 18 and A radius, Samaræus, and Hamathæus: and afterwards were spread the people of the Chananites.
\v 19 And the limits of Chanaan were from Sidon as we come to Gerara even to Gaza, until thou enter to Sodoma and Gomorrha, and Adama, and Seboim, even to Lesa.
\v 20 These are the children of Cham in their kindreds, and tongues, and generations, and lands, and nations.
\v 21 Of Sem also \f b \fr 10.21 \ft Hence S. Augustin gathereth that the people of Israel were called Hebrews of this Heber. \+it l. 16. c. 3. civit.\+it*\f* father of all the children of Heber, the elder brother of Japheth, were born:
\v 22 The children of Sem: Ælam and Assur, and Arphaxad, and Lud, and Aram.
\v 23 The children of Aram: Us, and Hul, and Gether, and Mes.
\v 24 And Arphaxad also begat Sale, of whom was born Heber.
\v 25 And to Heber were born two sons: the name of the one was Phaleg, \f c \fr 10.25 \ft Heber having a son born when the tongues were divided called him Phaleg, which signifieth division. \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 11. civit.\+it*\f* because that in his days was the earth divided: and his brother's name was Jectan.
\v 26 The which Jectan begat Elmodad, and Saleph, and Asarmoth, Jare;
\v 27 and Aduram, and Uzal, and Decla,
\v 28 and Ebal, and Abimael, Saba,
\v 29 and Ophir, and Hevila, and Jobab. All these were the children of Jectan.
\v 30 And their dwelling was from Messa as we go on as far as Sephar a mountain in the east.
\v 31 These are the children of Sem according to their kindred, and tongues, and countries in their nations.
\v 32 These are the families of Noe, according to their peoples and nations. Of these were the nations divided on the earth after the flood. \ef - \fr 10.32 \fq The Nations. \ft How many Nations & tongues were in the world immediately after the tower of Babylon, is more commonly supposed then clearly shewed by old or late writers. Only it seemeth certain and evident, that there were just as many tongues as Nations. But to find precisely (as the common opinion holdeth) ¹ 72, is hard. For in this chapter (where they would count this number) are not mentioned so many. Nevertheless, if we add certain that began distinct Nations afterwards, till Jacob with his children went into Ægypt (in whom only the Hebrew Nation and tongue continued to Christ's time) ² ³ the number will come right. Of Japheth were born 7. sons chief of Nations. Again of Gomer (besides his supposed successor, who cannot be counted beginner of another distinct nation) came 2. other heads. Likewise of Iavan (besides his first son) rose 3. more nations. Of Cham by his first son Chus were 6. nephews Princes of Nations. Again of Regma (besides his successor) came one more. And Nemrod, besides his Kingdom of Babylon, raised up other 6. By his second son Mesraim came 8. nations, Cham's third son Phur made only one nation. And Chanaan his sons made 11. more. Of Sem last mentioned for better connecting the many History and succession of the Church) came the chief and principal Nation the Hebrews ⁴, descending from him by Arphaxad, Sale, Heber, and so directly to Jacob, Of Sem also were born 4. other sons beginners of nations. Again of Aram (besides his first son) were 3. fathers of nations. Likewise of Heber (besides the Hebrews descending by Phaleg) were born to his other son Iectan 13. heads of nations. These are all that are named in this place: to wit, of Japheth, 12. of Cham 33. and of Sem 21. which make in all 66. whereunto if we adjoin ⁵ Nachor (Abraham's brother) Moab, and Ammon (Lot's sons) also Ismael (Abraham's eldest son) and his issue by Cetura, and finally Esau (Jacob's brother) who made 6. more distinct nations, the whole number is 72. This probable collection, with the rest, we submit to better judgement. \fq ¹ \ft The common opinion of 72. tongues is not clear in Scripture. \fq ² \ft Yet this number of nations & tongues may be probably gathered in this and other places of Genesis. \fq ³ \ft \+it S. Aug. 16. c. 3. civit.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft The Hebrews chief of these nations. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Gen. 24. 19. 17. 25, 6. 25, 23.\+it*\ef*
\c 11
\cd God hindreth the vain purpose of building a high tower, 7. by confounding men's tongues: 9. whereof it is called Babel. 10. The genealogy of Sem to Abram. \f - \ft The fourth part of this book. Of the division of tongues and nations.\f*
\p
\v 1 And the earth was of one tongue, and all one speech.
\v 2 And when they removed from the east, they found a plain in the land of Sennaar, and dwelt in it.
\v 3 And each one said to his neighbour: Come, let us make brick, and bake them with fire. And they had brick instead of stone, and bitume instead of mortar:
\v 4 and they said, Come, let us make us a city and a tower, the top whereof may reach to heaven: and let us renown our name before we be dispersed into all lands. \ef - \fr 11.4 \fq Let us make. \ft ¹ Here we may see in Nemrod the common causes of heresies, and the manner of Heretics proceeding. For he having a subtile, proud, and aspiring mind ², first detracted from God, persuadng men (as is noted before) not to depend upon God's providence, and finding some others of like humour, they conspired together, and drew more followers, by bearing the simpler sort in hand ³ (for it was unpossible wise man should believe it) that they would make a tower of defence against a new flood, if God should think to drown the world again. But their principal intention was to make themselves great and strong for the present, and famous to posterity. ⁴ All which God overthrew, neither suffering them to build up their imagined castle of strength, nor to be praised for their work, but made them infamous to the world's end. \fq ¹ \ft Pride is cause of schism and heresy. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Chri. ho. 30. in Gen. Cassian. collat. 4. c. 12.\+it* \fq ³ \ft False pretences deceive the simple. \fq ⁴ \ft Heretics prosper for a time, but are confounded in the end.\ef*
\v 5 And our Lord descended to see the city and the tower, which the children of Adam builded,
\v 6 and he said: Behold, it is one people, and one tongue is to all: and they have begun to do this, neither will they leave off from their determinations, till they accomplish them indeed.
\v 7 Come ye therefore, let us go down, and there confound their tongue, that none may hear his neighbour's voice. \ef - \fr 11.7 \fq Let us confound. \ft God in dissipating this vain work of men, would use the ministry of Angels ¹. As not only Philo Judæus, and Origen, ² but also S. Augustin. S. Gregory, and other fathers expound these words, \+it Come, let us descend, and confound their tongue.\+it* Where they also note God's singular wisdom, mercy, and justice, so punishing the offence, ³ that he turneth it to his own glory, and the profit of all men: shewing his power and sovereign Majesty by two great miracles ⁴. First, by so suddenly and utterly depriving all those builders of their usual tongue, that presently they could neither speak it, nor understand it. Secondly, by giving distinct languages to several sorts of families, which they immediately understood, and spoke most promptly, as if they had long before learned and used the same. But to no man was given more than one language. And so, to the more commodity of all mankind, they were forced to part into sundry coasts of the earth, which they inhabited and replenished with distinct Nations, having the same Angels their spiritual Patrons and Protectors, which had severally changed their language. In particular, ⁵ ⁶ it was profitable to the good, who being before oppressed by the united power of many wicked, were relieved (as S. Gregory teacheth) when their persecutors were divided ⁷. These good were the family of Heber, as S. Chrysostom and S. Augustin prove. For seeing the change of tongues was inflicted for punishment, ⁸ it appeareth that Heber and his family were innocent of the vain attempt, whose tongue was not changed, but remained the same, and of him was called the Hebrew tongue for distinction sake after there were many tongues, which before had no distinct name, being the only tongue of all men. Again, touching the offenders (who were punished in their tongues, that they could not be understood commanding one another, because they would not understand God justly commanding them all) they also reaped this profit, that they were forced to leave off that bad work, and withal to seek more ample habitations, who if \+it they had there more increased in number and strength, would without doubt\+it* (saith S. Chrysostom) \+it have attempted worse things.\+it* And infinite man-slaughter would have been committed amongst so many, for possession of that one city & tower. Finally the fathers note ⁹, that as God wrought here much good by division of tongues, so he wrought much more by communion of tongues given to the Apostles ¹⁰, thereby enabling them to gather one Church of all tongues and nations. \fq ¹ \ft Ministry of Angels. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. de confus. ling. ho. 11. in Numer. li. 16. c. 6. civit. l. 2. Moral. in c. 1. Iob.\+it* \fq ³ \ft God turneth the offence of men to good. \fq ⁴ \ft Two Miracles in privation of one tongue, & giving a new. \fq ⁵ \ft Division among evil men is profitable. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Li. 34. Moral. in c. 41. Iob. ho. 11. in Gen. li. 16. c. 11. & li. 18. c. 39. ci. S. Aug. li. 6. c. 4. civit.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft The member offending is punished. \fq ⁸ \ft Heber and his family consented not to the building of Babel. \fq ⁹ \ft Others conjecture Cainan should not be in the text of the 70. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it Editio quædam vaticanæ.\+it*\ef*
\v 8 And so our Lord dispersed them from that place into all lands, and they ceased to build the city.
\v 9 And therefore the name thereof was called Babel, \f a \fr 11.9 \ft He that speaketh so confusedly that he is not understood is said to babble.\f* because there the tongue of the whole earth was confounded: and from thence our Lord dispersed them upon the face of all countries.
\v 10 These are \f b \fr 11.10 \ft Moyses here sheweth the succession of Patriarchs, from Sem to Abraham, as he did before from Adam to Noe. \+it S. Aug. l. 16. c. 10. civit.\+it*\f* the generations of Sem: Sem was an hundred years old when he begat Arphaxad, two years after the flood.
\v 11 And Sem lived after he begat Arphaxad, five hundred years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 12 Moreover Arphaxad lived thirty five years, and begat Sale. \ef - \fr 11.12 \fq Begat Sale. \ft ¹ Here is an intricate difficulty: For the Hebrew and Latin text, both here and in Paralipomenon ² saying Aphaxad begat Sale, the 72. Interpreters and S. Luke ³ place Cainan between them, as son of Arphaxad, and father of Sale. Eusebius also in his Chronicle, with most Greek Doctors, and S. Augustine ⁴, count Cainan in this Genealogy of Sem. Whereupon many do number him in this rank, and suppose that Moyses omitted him for some Mystery ⁵, and yet writeth truly, that Arphaxad begat Sale, not his proper son, but his son's son: as S. Matthew saith ⁶, Joram begat Ozias, who was his nephew's nephew. But against this solution it is replied, that then Arphaxad should have been a grandfather at 35. years of age: which were strange in those days, however it is now. ⁷ And a greater difficulty, or rather absurdity must also be granted, that Arphaxad begat both Cainan at the age of 35. years, according to the 72. Interpreters, and that Sale was also begotten the same year, according to the Hebrew, being both true. Which inconvenience is not in the Genealogy written by S. Matthew. Others therefore according to the Hebrew and Latin text, with most Latin Doctors, omit Cainan in this place and Paralipomenon, namely with S. Hierom, who diligently examining and reconciling varieties between the Hebrew ⁸ and the Greek, maketh no mention at all of this difference. ⁹ Which maketh some to conjecture, that in S. Hierom's time Cainan was not in the Greek copies, at least not in those that he had, and held for the best. And at this day some have him not. Which may be admitted for a probable answer touching the Hebrew and Greek of the old Testament. But for so much as all copies, both Greek & Latin ¹⁰, also S. Hierom's Edition of S. Luke's Gospel have Cainan ¹¹, the difficulty still remaineth between Moyses and S. Luke. How then shall this doubt be solved? We cannot solve it. And no marvel. For Venerable Bede could not, whose words are these: ¹² \+it S. Luke useth rather the Greek testimonies than the Hebrew's: whereof happeneth that I much marvel at, and for dullness of wit, being strucken with great admiration, I cannot throughly scan, seeing in the Hebrew verity are found only ten generations from the flood unto Abraham, by what means\+it* S. Luke, \+it who (the Holy Ghost governing his pen) could in no sort write false, would rather set down eleven generations in the Gospel, Cainan adjoined according to the seventy Interpreters.\+it* ¹³ Thus writeth S. Bede, reverently admiring that he could not understand. For being assured that the Holy Ghost governed the pens both of Moyses and S. Luke, and that he is not contrary to himself, it must needs be true which each of them writeth, though other learned men cannot reach the profoundness of some difficulties that occur. And therefore Beza was extreme saucy to dash Cainan out of S. Luke's Gospel ¹⁴, and that wittingly and most impudently, saying in his Annotations: \+it Non dubitavimus expungere, We doubted not to put it out.\+it* ¹⁵ ¹⁶ The former English Editions, otherwise corrupt in many places, have Cainan in the text of S. Luke Gospel, but their latter translaters are in this point pure Bezites. \fq ¹ \ft Scriptures hard. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Par. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Luc. 3. v. 36.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Li. 16. c. 10. civit.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Some think Moyses omitted Cainan for a mystery. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Mat. 1.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Refutation. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Quest. Hebræ.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Ho. 30. in Gen. S. Greg. ho. 30. in Evan.\+it* \fq ¹⁰ \ft Gift of tongues most profitable to the Church. \fq ¹¹ \ft But never any Catholic (nor heretic before Beza) put Cainan out of S. Luke's Gospel. \fq ¹² \ft \+it Prefat. comment. in Actæ Apost.\+it* \fq ¹³ \ft A memorable sentence of S. Bede. \fq ¹⁴ \ft Beza sacrilegiously proud. \fq ¹⁵ \ft The heretical English Editions differ in this point. \fq ¹⁶ \ft \+it 1552. 1577.\+it*\ef*
\v 13 And Arphaxad lived after he begat Sale, three hundred three years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 14 Sale also lived thirty years, and begat Heber.
\v 15 And Sale lived after he begat Heber, four hundred three years, & begat sons & daughters.
\v 16 And Heber lived thirty four years, and begat Phaleg.
\v 17 And Heber lived after he begat Phaleg, four hundred thirty years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 18 Phaleg also lived thirty years, and begat Reu.
\v 19 And Phaleg lived after he begat Reu, two hundred nine years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 20 And Reu lived thirty two years, and begat Sarug.
\v 21 Reu lived also after he begat Sarug, two hundred seven years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 22 And Sarug lived thirty years, and begat Nachor.
\v 23 And Sarug lived after he begat Nachor, two hundred years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 24 And Nachor lived nine and twenty years, and begat Thare.
\v 25 And Nachor lived after he begat Thare, and hundred and nineteen years, and begat sons and daughters.
\v 26 And Thare lived seventy years, and begat Abram, and Nachor, and Aran.
\v 27 And these are the generations of Thare: Thare begat Abram, Nachor, and Aran. Moreover Aran begat Lot.
\v 28 And Aran died before Thare his father, in the land of his nativity, in \f c \fr 11.28 \ft Ur a city, or territory of Chaldea. \+it 70. Interpret. and Josephus l. 1. Antiq.\+it*\f* Ur of the Chaldees.
\v 29 And Abram and Nachor married wives: the name of Abram his wife was Sarai: and the name of Nachor his wife, Melcha, the daughter of Aran the father of Melcha, and the father of Jescha.
\v 30 And Sarai was barren, neither had she children.
\v 31 Thare therefore \f d \fr 11.31 \ft Abram was commanded to go forth of Chaldea, as appeareth Act. 7. v. 4. though this journey is here ascribed to Thare as the principal person.\f* took Abram his son, and Lot the son of Aran, his son's son, and Sarai his daughter in law, the wife of Abram his son, and brought them out of Ur of the Chaldees, for to go into the land of Chanaan: and they came as far as Haran, and dwelled there.
\v 32 And the days of Thare came to two hundred five years, and he died in Haran.
\c 12
\cd Abram commanded by God to leave his country, with promise to be blessed in his seed, 5. taking his wife Sarai, and his nephew Lot, 6. Wandereth in the land of Chanaan, 7. erecteth an Altar in Sichem, 8. another in Bethel. 10. Thence by occasion of famine passeth into Ægypt: 14. where his wife (called his sister) is taken into the King's house, 19. but untouched is restored to him. \f - \ft The beginning of the third Age.\f*
\s1 THE CONTINUANCE OF THE CHURCH AND RELIGION in the second age of the world. From Noe's flood to Abraham's going forth of his country. The space of 368. years.
\ip No man can well doubt nor will deny, that the same Church continued all the second Age \f - \ft Counting Cainan, this Age continued 398. years.\f* which was in the first, considering that Noe lived above 50. years after the birth of Abraham , \f - \ft Noe and Sem lived in Abraham's time.\f* and Sem 150. more: and that these three, and some others of that time are renowned in holy Scripture, for sincere professors of true Religion . But for more manifestation of their faith, and \f - \ft Articles of Religion professed in the second Age.\f* that the Church was then very conspicuous, we shall repeat certain principal points of Religion professed and practised all that time, by a continual known visible company united in one mystical body: though in the mean while, the wicked sprung and grew in number and worldly force, much oppressing yet never suppressing the good.
\ip First, just Noe coming forth of the ark with his family , professed his religious mind to \it One\it* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 8.\+it*\f* \f - \ft One God.\f* \it God\it* almighty, supreme Lord of all, by \it Offering external\it* voluntary, speedy, pure, solemn, and \f - \ft External Sacrifice.\f* bountiful \it Sacrifice\it* of Holocausts, as a \it Priest\it* upon an \it Altar\it*. After which most grateful \f - \ft Priesthood.\f* \f - \ft Altars.\f* office, God making a covenant with him and his seed, never again to destroy the world by water , confirmed the same by the sign of the rainbow, which represented the second Person of \f - \ft \+it Gen. 9.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The B. Trinity.\f* the B. \it Trinity\it*, the \it Son of God, Christ\it* our Lord to be born \it The Son of man\it*, & extended upon the \f - \ft Christ.\f* \it Cross: in whom Noe believing was instituted heir of the justice, which is by faith in our Redeemer\it* \f - \ft Cross.\f*. \f - \ft Redeemer.\f* \f - \ft \+it Heb. 11.\+it*\f*
\ip We have here again \it God's operative blessing\it*, with the effect of increase and multiplication, \f - \ft God's blessing operative.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 9.\+it*\f* the issue of Noe by his three sons, in short time making many Nations. By the way also we have an example of \it Father's\it* solemn \it Blessing\it* and \it Cursing\it* their children. The effects whereof \f - \ft Fathers' blessing & cursing.\f* \f - \ft \+it c. 9. v. 25.\+it*\f* succeeded afterwards accordingly. Likewise in this Age was given a particular precept \it Not\it* \f - \ft Abstinence from blood.\f* \f - \ft \+it c. 9. v. 4.\+it*\f* \it to eat blood\it*. And Noe observed distinction of \it Clean\it* beasts, offering Sacrifice in them only, \f - \ft Clean, and unclean.\f* \f - \ft \+it c. 8. v. 20.\+it*\f* as before the flood he was commanded to take more of them into the ark than of the \it Unclean\it*.
\ip In that so ample mention is made of sin and wickedness, there is no doubt, but Noe, the \it preacher of justice\it*, admonished and exhorted sinners to \it Repentance\it*: yea he \it Punished\it* Cham & \f - \ft Penance preached and inflicted.\f* \f - \ft \+it c. 10, 10. c. 11. 2. Pet. 2.\+it*\f* Chanaan, by his curse in their posterity. And God himself \it Threatened\it* to exact the blood of man unjustly shed . In the mean time \it Inflicted\it* also some \it punishment\it* upon the builders of Babel, by \f - \ft \+it c. 9, 5. c. 11.\+it*\f* confounding their tongues. And that by the \it Ministry of Angels\it*. \f - \ft Ministry of Angels.\f*
\ip \it Which\it* punishment in part, and threats of more import a \it General Resurrection\it*, and \f - \ft Resurrection.\f* \f - \ft Judgement.\f* \it Judgement\it*, where all things \it shall be\it* exactly discussed and \it Judged\it*. And then will follow \it Eternal\it* \f - \ft Eternal joy and pain.\f* \it life\it* to the good: and \it Everlasting pain\it* to the damned.
\ip All these points of Religion (and others mentioned in the former age, and no doubt taught by Noe & his sons) shew clearly a \it Visible Church\it*, consisting of good and bad. \it Noe\it* remaining the \f - \ft Church visible. Good and bad in the Church.\f* \f - \ft \+it Cap. 8. & 9. c. 10, 21.\+it*\f* same man as before the flood; \it Sem\it* and \it Japheth\it* are commended and blessed for well doing; \it Cham\it* blamed, & cursed in his posterity: yet neither he nor any of his sons or daughters fell into heresy, or other infidelity, for any thing that appeareth in Scripture, or other authentical testimony. \f - \ft Always some good.\f* \it Heber also and his family are\it* particularly commended by Moyses, as the right followers, and the spiritual children of Sem (who had innumerable other carnal children) as those that were innocent touching the presumptuous building of others, who for the same fault lost their old tongue, which the family of Heber kept. As S. Chrysostom and S. Augustin do prove . Again, diverse of this \f - \ft \+it Ho. 39. in Gen. li. 16. c. 11. & li. 18. c. 39. civit.\+it*\f* family falling afterwards by little and little to other nations, the \it Family of Thare\it*, saith the same S. Augustin (li. 16. c. 12.) albeit not all, or not always, yet ever some of them, and Abraham continually, with Sem, Heber, Phaleg, & many others, not mentioned by Moyses in his brief description (as S. Gregory doubteth not to suppose) were just, and kept the true faith, and \f - \ft \+it L. 5. c. 35. Moral. in 3. Iob.\+it*\f* undefiled Religion.
\ip But \it Nemrod\it* Cham's nephew, and son of Chus described for a valiant hunter, a violent \f - \ft Nemrod an Arch-heretic\f* \f - \ft \+it Ioseph li. 1. c. 4. Antiq.\+it*\f* giant, and tyrant, was an Arch-heretic, a deviser and teacher of false doctrine, against God and true faith. By subtlety and tyranny he induced many of liking or of fear to follow him, and so in schism he maintained heresy, \it That men were not beholding to God, but to themselves, for temporal\it* \f - \ft His proud heresy.\f* \it prosperity\it*.
\ip Whereof began a new & cruel confederacy, against the City of God, & the second great Sect of Infidels. \it For Barbarism\it* being the first, begun by Cain and ended by the flood: \it The second\it* \f - \ft The third sect was Graecism.\f* \it mother\it* of all Sects beginning after the flood (as S. Epiphanius writeth) was \it Scythism\it*: so \f - \ft \+it L. de heresibus.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The second Scythism.\f* called of the Scythians a most cruel people . Who according to Nemrod's heresy (not thinking \f - \ft Cruelty.\f* themselves beholding to God for temporal happiness, but to their own forces) tyrannized over the weaker, and many wicked banding together extremely oppressed the more peaceable, especially the Church and true servants of God. And this was one special cause of building Babylon, besides their ambitious desire of perpetual fame, and their bearing the simple in hand of a defence against a new flood, to make it indeed, a strong hold for tyrants to offend others, and to defend themselves. Wherefore God (who before destroyed all Infidels by the flood) confounded these builders by dividing their tongues, and so forced them to break and part into many countries.
\ip Thus mankind being divided upon the earth, opinions also were multiplied concerning Religion. \f - \ft Untruths are unconstant.\f* For shortly the persuasion of men's trusting in themselves, and in other mortal men appeared absurd, even the strongest feeling adversities, or failing sometimes of their purposes, saw there was need of supernal help, and that earthly things depended much upon divine will and power. But having forsaken God Almighty, the only maker and conserver of all, they began to imagine and serve false gods, both famous dead men, which had prospered in this world, and diverse other things, by which they received commodity, or feared damage.
\ip Hence therefore rose the third principal Sect called \it Grecism\it*, beginning also in this second age, \f - \ft The first sect of Infidels was Barbarism before the flood.\f* as the same S. Epiphanius writeth. For Ninus the first king of the Assirian great Monarchy, brought to pass that his father \it Belus Jupiter\it* was esteemed and worshipped for the only great God by the Assirians . To him the Babylonians erected first Temples, Altars, and Statuas. Nemrod also by \f - \ft Idolatry.\f* the name of \it Saturnus\it*, as the progenitor of Belus, and first great King or Tyrant of Babylon, \f - \ft \+it Satur deorum.\+it*\f* was accounted a god, and the father of gods. About this time likewise began the Dynastae \f - \ft Dynastae were those that reigned in Aegypt by force and policy: & after were great gods, and little gods.\f* among the Aegyptians, and not sooner, as they vainly brag to have been before the flood, yea much longer than indeed the world hath been. Moreover the Chaldees worshipped the fire. Others the sun, the moon, and innumerable other feigned gods. Against all which (& likewise against all heresies) are two special arguments. First, that they were not from the beginning, as the true God, and all truths are known and received by continual Tradition, but brought in afterwards by men, and commonly by ill men. Secondly, they are not accepted and esteemed for gods, or truths in all places, but with great diversity and dissension, one sort allowing that others despise, as holy Athanasius notably writeth in his oration against Idols in these words: \it Quot sunt gentes\it* \f - \ft Idolatry and Heresy are confuted, by that they begin disorderly; & are at dissension in their imagined Religions.\f* \it totidem Deorum genera confinguntur &c. How many nations\it* (saith he) \it so many kinds of gods are feigned. Also the same country, the same city dissenteth within it-self in superstition of Idols. The Phenicians certes acknowledge not the Aegyptians' gods, neither do the Aegyptians adore the same Idols with the Phenicians: Nor the Scythians receive the gods of the Persians, nor the Persians of the Scythians. The Pelasgies refuse the Thrasian gods, the Thrasians know not the Thebians. The Indians are against the Arabians, the Arabians against the Aethiopians: and in like sort the Aethiopians differ in their religious affairs from the Arabians. The Syrians worship not the gods of the Cilicians, and the nations of Capadocia besides all these have gods of other names. The Bithynians also feigned diverse gods, the Armenians again diverse from them. What need we many words? Those that are in the continent honour other gods from the Island-people. In brief, each city and each village not knowing the gods of their neighbours, setteth forth their own, and esteemeth them only in place of gods\it*. Thus far S. Athanasius. Name we like countries, provinces, cities, and towns in these parts of Europe, where Luther's scholars have set their feet, consider the form of Religion and opinions which \f - \ft Luther's progenies differ as much in opinions of Religion, as Paynims do in their false gods.\f* they hold, and we shall see as unorderly beginnings, & as horrible dissensions in heresies (which S. Hierom calleth the Idols of the New Testament) as the ancient fathers have discryed in \f - \ft \+it in c. 11. Osee.\+it*\f* Paganism. For Lutherans or Protestants having no lawful generation, but proceeding of bastards' race, upstarts of unknown progeny, are no less at discord among themselves, only all agreeing against Catholic, like \it syncretisants\it* against their common enemies, or Herod, Pilate, & the Jews against Christ. And in England alone are diverse Sects without possible means to agree in one. \f - \ft Sects in England diverse from Luther, and each one from the rest.\f* For albeit the civil state endeavoureth prudently and seriously to bring all to uniformity, at least in public shew, yet they are but like many faces under one hood, every sort keeping their own opinions; yea almost every Preacher and mean scholer (to say nothing of artificers and common Ministers) arrogating to be his own Judge, contemneth to stand to Luther or Calvin, to Geneva or Parliament, to Convocation or Synod of their own, but to his own only understanding, and interpretation of holy Scripture. Nor yet to that always; for when he is pressed with that he once said, he will forget it, or eat his own word, if he have not written it, or that you have ready witness against him, so hard it is to make a deceived Protestant or Puritan confess that he is \f - \ft Proud & contentious spirits are hardly persuaded to the truth.\f* convinced, except by very pregnant means you can first cast out of him, or bind fast the spirit of presumption, dissension and contention: whereas the simplest Catholic in the world hath the self same faith in all points, with the whole Church , in which he remaineth, and upon whose \f - \ft Unlearned Catholics believe the same faith in all points with the learned.\f* judgement he dependeth.
\ip To return therefore, from whence we are not unnecessarily digressed, we conclude with S.
\ip Augustin ; When Moyses had shewed the beginning and progress of Nemrod's earthly city, \f - \ft \+it li. 16. c. 10. civit.\+it*\f* leaving it in Babylon, that is \it confusion\it*, as needless to prosecute it further, he returneth to declare the perpetual \it succession\it* of the City of God, the Church, as before the flood from Adam to Noe, by the line of Seth, so after the flood from the same Noe, by the line of \it Sem, Arphaxad, Sale,\it* \f - \ft The succession of Patriarchs from Noe to Abraham.\f* \it Heber, Phaleg, Reu, Sarug, Nachor, Thare\it*, and \it Abraham\it*. The rest of Sem's children, and all the progenies of Japheth and Cham, as not pertaining to this purpose, omitted, so connecting those in order of generations, by whom the succession is directly brought to Abraham, Prince of the elected people, a most special Patriarch , to whom new and great promises are made of multiplication \f - \ft Abraham a principal Patriarch.\f* of his seed, and possession of the land of Chanaan, but especially of Christ our Redeemer, and the same many ways confirmed, as will appear in the next Age.
\p
\v 1 And \x - \xt Act. 7.\x*\f 2 \fr 12.1 \ft The fifth part of this book. Of Abraham's leaving his country, God's blessing of his seed, and commandment of Circumcision.\f* our Lord said to Abram: Go forth of thy country, and out of thy kindred, and out of thy father's house, and come into a land which I will shew thee.
\v 2 And I will make thee into a great nation, and I will bless thee, and magnify thy name, and thou shalt be blessed.
\v 3 I will bless them that bless thee, and curse them that curse thee, and \f a \fr 12.3 \ft In Christ born of Abraham's seed all nations are blessed. Gal. 3.\f* IN \sc Thee\sc* shall all the kindreds of the earth be blessed. \x - \xt Heb. 11.\x*
\v 4 Abram therefore went out as our Lord had commanded him, and with him went Lot: seventy five years old was Abraham when he went forth out of Haran.
\v 5 And he took Sarai his wife, and Lot his brother's son, and all the substance which they had possessed, and the souls which they had gotten in Haran: and went forth to go into the land of Chanaan. And when they were come into it.
\v 6 Abram passed through the country unto the place Sichem, as far as the noble vale: and the Chananite was at that time in the country.
\v 7 And our Lord appeared to Abram, and said to him: To thy seed will I give this land. Who builded there \f b \fr 12.7 \ft Abram dedicated Altars to God especially in those places where he received promises, or benefits. \+it S. Chrisost. ho. 31. in Gen.\+it*\f* an altar to our Lord, that had appeared to him.
\v 8 And marching on from thence to a mountain, that was on the east side of Bethel, there he pitched his tent, having Bethel on the west, and Hai on the east: he builded there also an altar to our Lord, and called upon his name.
\v 9 And Abram went forward going, and proceeding on to the south.
\v 10 And there came a famine in the country: and Abram descended into Ægypt, to be as a pilgrim there: for the famine was very sore in the land.
\v 11 And when he was near to enter into Ægypt, he said to Sarai his wife: I know that thou art a fair woman,
\v 12 and that when the Ægyptians shall see thee, they will say: She is his wife; and they will kill me, and reserve thee.
\v 13 Say therefore, I pray thee, that thou art my sister: that I may be well used for thee, and that my soul may live for thy sake. \ef - \fr 12.13 \fq Say therefore. \ft Abraham concealed that Sarai was his wife, and lied not in saying she was his sister; as he also called Lot his brother ¹, being his brother's son, and she his brother's daughter. Whereby he prevented danger of his own life, using such lawful means as lay in him, committing his wife's chastity to God's protection, which himself could not provide for. ² In which case if he had not done his own endeavour, he had rather tempted God (saith S. Augustin) ³ than trusted in God. And so God preserved her, though she was in Pharao his house. v. 17. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Gen. 13.\+it* \fq ² \ft Men are bound to do their lawful endeavours, and to commit the rest to God. \fq ³ \ft \+it li. 16. c. 19. civit. & q. 26. in. Gen.\+it*\ef*
\v 14 When Abram therefore was entered into Ægypt, the Ægyptians, saw the woman that she was passing beautiful.
\v 15 And the Princes told Pharao, and praised her to him: and the woman was taken into the house of Pharao.
\v 16 And they used Abram well for her sake. And he had sheep and oxen and he asses, and men servants, and maid servants, and she asses, and Camels.
\v 17 And our Lord \f c \fr 12.17 \ft God by corporal affliction hindered Pharao & his men from doing violence to Sarai. \+it S. Chris. ho. 32. in Gen.\+it*\f* scourged Pharao with very sore plagues, and his house, for Sarai Abram's wife.
\v 18 And Pharao called Abram, and said to him: What is this that thou hast done to me? Why didst thou not tell me that she was thy wife?
\v 19 For what cause didst thou say she was thy sister, that I might take her to my wife? Now therefore there is thy wife, take her, and go thy ways.
\v 20 And Pharao gave certain men commandment in the behalf of Abram: and they conducted him, and his wife, and all that he had.
\c 13
\cd Abram and Lot return from Ægypt into Chanaan, 6. and being rich separate themselves, 10, Lot choosing the country about Iordan, Abram dwelleth in Chanaan. 14. Where again God promiseth him that land, and multiplication of his seed. 18. And he erected another Altar to God.
\p
\v 1 Abram therefore ascended out of Ægypt, he and his wife, and all that he had, and Lot with him to the south coast.
\v 2 And he was very rich in possession of gold and silver.
\v 3 And he returned by the way that he came, from the south unto Bethel, even to the place where before he had pitched a tabernacle between Bethel and Hai:
\v 4 in the place of the altar which he had made before, and there he called upon the name of our Lord.
\v 5 But Lot also that was with Abram had flocks of sheep, and herds of beasts, and tents.
\v 6 Neither was the land able to receive them for to dwell together: for their substance was much, and they could not dwell together.
\v 7 Whereupon also there arose strife amongst the herdsmen of Abram and of Lot. And that time the Chananite and the Pherisite dwelled in that country.
\v 8 Abram therefore said to Lot: Let there be no brawl, I beseech thee, between me and thee, and between my herdsmen, and thy herdsmen: for we be \f a \fr 13.8 \ft Four sorts of brethren in holy writ: german brothers, as Jacob and Esau: of kindred, as Abraham and Lot: of same nation, as the Jews & Samaritanes: in Religion as all Catholics.\f* brethren.
\v 9 Behold the whole land is before thee: go apart from me, I pray thee: if thou wilt go to the left hand, I will take the right: if thou choose the right hand, I will pass to the left.
\v 10 Lot therefore lifting up his eyes, saw all the country about Jordan, which was watered throughout before that our Lord subverted Sodome and Gomorre, as the Paradise of our Lord, and like as Ægypt as men come unto Segor.
\v 11 And Lot chose unto him the country about Jordan, and he departed from the East: and they were separated either brother from the other.
\v 12 Abram dwelt in the land of Chanaan: and Lot abode in the towns that were about Jordan, and dwelt in Sodome.
\v 13 And the men of Sodome were very wicked, and sinners before the face of our Lord out of measure.
\v 14 And our Lord said to Abram, after that Lot was separated from him: Lift up thine eyes, and look from the place wherein thou now art, to the north and south, to the east and west:
\v 15 All the land which thou seest will I give to thee, and to thy seed for ever.
\v 16 And I will make \f b \fr 13.16 \ft Not the children of the flesh, but the children of promise are the seed, Rom. 9. and are innumerable. Apoc. 7. v. 9.\f* thy seed as the dust of the earth: if any man be able to number the dust of the earth, thy seed also shall he be able to number.
\v 17 Arise and walk through the land in the length, and in the breadth thereof: for I will give it to thee.
\v 18 Abram therefore removing his tent, came, and dwelt beside the vale of Mambre, which is in Hebron: and he builded there an altar to our Lord.
\c 14
\cd The King of Sodom with other four Kings are overcome in battle by four others, 12. where Lot is taken. 14. But Abram with 318. persons prosecuting and overcoming the victorers, 16. rescued Lot, with all the captives and prey. 18. Melchisedech King and Priest blessed Abram, 20. Abram payed tithes to him, 21. and rendered the spoil to the King of Sodom.
\p
\v 1 And it came to pass in that time, that Amraphel the king of Sennaar, and Arioch the king of Pontus, and Chodorlahomor king of the Elamites, and Thadal the king of Nations.
\v 2 made war against Barra the king of Sodome, and against Bersa the king of Gomorra, and against Sennaab the king of Adama, and against Semebar the king of Seboim, and against the king of Bala, the same is Segor.
\v 3 All these came together into the wood-land vale, which now is the salt sea.
\v 4 For they had served Chodorlahomor twelve years, & the thirteenth year they revolted from him.
\v 5 Therefore in the fourteenth year came Chodorlahomor, and the kings that were with him, and they struck Raphaim in Astarothcarnaim, and Susim with them, and Emim in Savee of Cariathaim,
\v 6 and the Corrheans in the mountains of Seir, even to the Champion country of Pharan, which is in the wilderness.
\v 7 And they returned, and came as far as the fountain of Misphat, the same is Cades: and they struck all the country of the Amelechites, and of the Amorrheans, that dwell in Asasonthamar.
\v 8 And they went forth the king of Sodome, and the king of Gomorra, and the king of Adama, and the king of Seboim, moreover also the king of Bala, which is Segor: and they set themselves against them in battle array in the wood-land vale:
\v 9 to wit against Chodorlahomor king of the Elamites, and Thadal king of Nations, and Amraphel king of Sennaar, and Arioch king of Pontus: four kings against five.
\v 10 But the wood-land vale had many pits of bitume. Therefore the king of Sodome and of Gomorra turned their backs, and were overthrown there: and they that remained fled to the mountain.
\v 11 And they took all the substance of the Sodomites and Gomorrheans, and took all kind of victuals, and went their way:
\v 12 and Lot also and his substance, the son of Abrams brother, who dwelled in Sodom.
\v 13 And behold one that had escaped told Abram the Hebrew, that dwelt in the vale of Mambre, the Amorrean, brother of Eschol, and the brother of Aner: for these made a league with Abram.
\v 14 Which when Abram had heard, to wit, that his brother Lot was taken, he numbered of the servants born in his house, well appointed, three hundred and eighteen: and pursued them unto Dan.
\v 15 And dividing his company, he ran upon them in the night, and struck them, and pursued them unto Hoba, which is on the left hand of Damascus.
\v 16 And he brought back all the substance, and Lot his brother with his substance, the women also and the people.
\v 17 And the king of Sodom went forth to meet them, after he returned from the slaughter of Chodorlahomor, and of the kings that were with him in the vale Savee, which is the king's vale.
\v 18 But Melchisedech the king of Salem, bringing forth bread and wine, for he was the Priest of God most High, \ef - \fr 14.18 \fq Melchisedech. \ft S. Hierom being earnestly requested by Evagrius to give his judgement touching Melchisedech ¹ ², whom a nameless author had endeavoured to prove to be the Holy Ghost, plainly confuteth that error: as also another error of Origen and Didymus saying, he was an Angel. Likewise S. Epiphanius \+it (heresi 55. and 76.)\+it* relateth and condemneth a third error, of some that thought him to be the Son of God. These two Fathers, and S. Augustine \+it (li. de heresibus her. 34.)\+it* and diverse others whom S. Hierome allegeth, prove evidently, that he was a very man, a Priest and a King, yea the High Priest (at least of that country) Superior to Abraham, and a figure of Christ. Besides these heresies, the same S. Hierome relateth two probable opinions. ³ The Jews' Rabbins generally hold that Melchisedech was Sem the son of Noe, from whom Abraham and all the Hebrews descended. Which they seem rather to affirm, as loath to confess that any man of another Nation than their own, should have been greater and more excellent than Abraham in spiritual causes, than for any reason they do or can allege. Yet many, escpecially later writers, as Liranus, Tostatus, Genebrardus, and others, do embrace this opinion as most probable. Though S. Hierome seemeth only to have added the Hebrew's opinion (as he saith) because he would intimate all to his friend, when he had first cited graver Authors, S. Ireneus, Hyppolytus, Eusebius Cesariensis, Eusebius Emissenus, Apolinarius, and Eustathius, all agreeably affirming that Melchisedech was a Chananite ⁴, King of Salem, which was afterwards called Jerusalem. To this opinion agreeth Philo Judæus, continually speaking of him as of a stranger to the Jews' Nation. Josephus also a Jew writeth plainly \+it (li. 7. de bello Iudaico. c. 18.)\+it* that he was of Chanaan, and Prince of the Chananites. Also S. Dionysius Ariopagita (Cælest. Hier. c. 9.) S. Epiphanius (her. 55. & 67.) Theodoretus (q. 63. in Gen) and Suidas, are of the same mind, and many other Christian Doctors. Who confirm their assertion by that S. Paul saith to the Hebrews ⁵: \+it He whose generation is not numbered among them, took tithes of Abraham.\+it* ⁶ For what else can S. Paul mean, but that Melchisedech's kindred & people, was diverse from the kindred and people of the Jews? Which he could not say of Sem, from whom Abraham & all Jews descended: as it cannot be said, that Adam and Noe are of diverse generation from any people that now liveth, because we all come of them. Of this difficulty (not pertaining to any controversy of our time) the studious may see more in F. Pererius his commentary upon this 14. chap. of Genesis. disp. 3. \fq ¹ \ft Old heresies concerning Melchisedech. \fq ² \ft \+it Tomo 3. prope initium.\+it* \fq ³ \ft A probable opinion that Sem and Melchisedech was all one. \fq ⁴ \ft More probable that Melchisedech was a Chananite. \fq ⁵ \ft Proved by S. Paul's words. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Heb. 7. v. 6.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 14.18 \fq Bringing forth. \ft Seeing the Royal Prophet David, and S. Paul say Christ is a Priest for ever according to the Order of Melchisedech ¹ ², we demand of Protestants, if Christ fulfilled not Melchisedech's figurative Sacrifice offered in bread and wine, by offering his own body and blood at his last supper in forms of bread & wine, and by instituting the same to be offered by his Priests till the end of the world, what other figurative sacrifice of Melchisedech they can find performed by Christ, by which it may appear that he is a Priest for ever according to that Order? Calvin (li. 4. Instit. c. 18. para. 2.) Kemnisius (par. 2. exam. pag. 740. & 747.) Peter Martyr (in 1. Cor. 5.) and most English Protestants grant that Melchisedech was a Priest, ³ and that the peculiar function of a Priest if to offer Sacrifice; wherefore they having no sacrifice will have only Ministers and no Priests: but they deny that Melchisedech offered Sacrifice in bread and wine. Whereupon we join issue with them to prove that he did, and that by this place amongst others of holy Scripture. \fq ¹ \ft Christ is a Priest according to the Order of Melchisedech. \fq ² \ft \+it Ps. 109. Heb. 7.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Why the Protestants have Ministers and no Priests.\ef* \ef - \fr 14.18 \ft Kemnisius complaineth that the Latin text hath \+it Obtulit\+it*, for \+it Protulit\+it*, \+it Offered\+it*, for \+it Brought forth\+it*. And to disprove the same, he allegeth the Hebrew, Chaldee, Greek, and S. Cyprian. But Catholics more justly complain of him for lying. For all Latin Editions have Proferens, bringing forth. ¹ The question therefore in controversy is, to what end and use Melchisedech brought forth bread and wine? Calvin and Kemnisius say it was only to refresh or feed Abraham and his men, and not for sacrifice. Which their bare saying is without reason, for that there was store of victuals in the prey (v. 11.) and they had eaten thereof ². Moreover the Hebrew word \+it Hotsi, brought forth\+it*, is a word pertaining to Sacrifice, as in the 6. chap. of Judges. (v. 18. and 19) and importeth that the bread and wine were first offered in Sacrifice, and then doubtless they did participate: thought they wanted not other sufficient corporal food. Again, the words following \+it For he was the Priest of God most High\+it*, can have no other sense, ³ but that he did the function of a Priest in the bread and wine which he brought, otherwise if the only cause of bringing that provision had been to relieve the camp with victuals, the reason would rather have been yielded, because he was a bountiful King, a liberal Prince, a special friend to Abraham, as indeed he was: But none of these reasons or the like fitted this purpose so well, nor touched the cause of bringing forth bread and wine, as to signify that he was Priest, whose office is to offer Sacrifice. \fq ¹ \ft The state of the controversy. \fq ² \ft \+it v. 24. S. Chris. ho. 36. in Gen.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Melchisedech offered sacrifice in bread & wine.\ef* \ef - \fr 14.18 \ft Here again some Protestants take exception against the Latin text, that the causual conjunction \+it Enim, for\+it*, is not agreeable to the Hebrew, but should be the copulative \+it Et, and\+it*, which is a mere wrangling. ¹ For the learned know well enough that the Hebrew particle is better expressed in such places by \+it Enim,\+it* or \+it quia, for\+it*, or \+it because\+it*, than by Et. And so the English Bible printed in the year of our Lord 1552. readeth: \+it Melchisedech King of Salem brought forth bread and wine, for he was the Priest of the most Highest God.\+it* The latter Editions also in like places have not the copulative \+it And\+it*, but some other word as the sense requireth Gen. 20, v. 3. \+it Thou art but a dead man, for the woman's sake which thou hast taken; for she is a man's wife\+it*: Where the Hebrew phrase is, \+it And she is married to a husband,\+it* Gen. 30. v. 27. they read thus: \+it For I have proved that the Lord hath blessed men for thy sake,\+it* where precisely construing the Hebrew they should say, \+it I divined\+it* (or conjectured) \+it and the Lord blessed me for thy sake.\+it* Likewise Esay 64. v. 5. they read: \+it But Lo, thou hast been angry, for we offended,\+it* the Hebrew is thus: \+it Lo thou art angry, and we have sinned.\+it* In the same place they translate, \+it Yet shall we be saved,\+it* which the Hebrew expresseth by the copulative, \+it And\+it* we shall be saved. So when they think it convenient, they translate the Hebrew particle, \+it for, that, yet,\+it* which strictly signifieth \+it And\+it*. Now let us also see the original in this place. ² In the Hebrew it is thus: \+sc Vmalchi Tsedec Melec Salem Hotsi Lechem Vaiaiin. Vehv Cohen Leel Elion. Vaie Vare Chehv\+sc*, &c. In English word for word thus: \+it And Melchisedech King of Salem brought forth bread and wine. And he the Priest to God most High. And he blessed him, &c.\+it* Where albeit the causual word, \+it For,\+it* is not expressed, yet these words, \+it And he the Priest\+it*, further declaring that besides the office and dignity of a King (which was said before) Melchisedech was also a Priest, must needs signify that he did something about the bread and wine belonging to a Priest's office ³. And what that something was, perhaps the University of Cambridge will testify, whose late Professour of Divinity teacheth plainly, that Melchisedech offered Sacrifice, and was therein a figure of Christ ( \+it Pag. 6. Reprehen.)\+it* ⁴ \+it Sacerdotes\+it* (saith he) \+it ij vere & proprie sunt, qui sacrificia faciunt; qualis fuit. Aaron, & Aaronis filii, & Melchisedechus, & quem illi adumbrabant, Christus. Priests truly and properly are they, that offer sacrifices, such as was Aaron, and the sons of Aaron, and Melchisedech, and Christ, whom they prefigured.\+it* If then both Aaron & Melchisedech were truly & properly Priests, because they offered sacrifices (according to this Professor's definition) and both were figures of Christ, it must needs be granted that as Christ fulfilled the figure of Aaron's bloody sacrifices, in offering himself upon the Cross; so he also fulfilled the figure as well of unbloody sacrifices of Aaron, as escpecially of Melchisedech's Sacrifice, in some other besides that on the Cross, seeing the Prophet David and S. Paul say, Christ is a Priest (not according to Aaron's Order, for that was to have an end, but) \+it For ever\+it* according to the Order of Melchisedech ⁵. And what other Sacrifice did our Saviour offer to remain perpetual, but of his own body & blood in unbloody manner, under the forms of bread and wine, with commandment of his Apostles and Priests to do the same till the end of the world? Let the indifferent Reader weigh it well: And whosoever is not very proud will for his better instruction or confirmation, esteem, the uniform judgement of many, ancient, goodly, and learned Fathers, writing upon this place. We will only recite their words without other deduction, for brevity sake. \fq ¹ \ft The Latin text justified by other places, yea by Protestants' translations. \fq ² \ft The Hebrew text sheweth that Melchisedech did the office of a Priest in bread and wine. \fq ³ \ft The proper office of a Priest is to offer Sacrifice. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Guiliel. Whitak. contra Gregor. Martin.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Christ still exerciseth the office of Priesthood according to the Order of Melchisedech by the ministry of Priests.\ef* \ef - \fr 14.18 \ft S. Clemens Alexandrinus, li. 4. Strom. versus finem, writeth thus: Melchisedech King of Salem, Priest of God most High, gave wine & bread sanctified nutriment in type of the Eucharist. ¹ S. Cyprian Epist. 63. ad Cæcilium: Christ is Priest for ever according to the Order of Melchisedech, which Order is this coming from that Sacrifice, and thence descending, that Melchisedech was Priest of God most High, that he offered bread and wine, that he blessed Abraham. For who is more a Priest of God most High, than our Lord Jesus Christ, who offered Sacrifice to God the Father, and offered the same which Melchisedech had offered, bread and wine, to wit, his own body and blood. And a little after: That therefore in Genesis, the blessing might be rightly celebrated about Abraham by Melchisedech the Priest, the image of Christ's Sacrifice consisting in bread and wine went before, which thing our Lord perfecting and performing, offered bread and the chalice mixt with wine, and he that is the plentitude, fulfilled the verity of the prefigured image. \fq ¹ \ft The ancient fathers prove that Melchisedech offered Sacrifice in bread & wine, in figure of Christ, and of other Priests of the new Testament.\ef* \ef - \fr 14.18 \ft Eusebius Cesariensis li. 5. Demonst. Evang. c. 3. Even as he who was Priest of Nations was never seen to offer corporal sacrifices, but only bread & wine, when he blessed Abraham; so first our Lord & Saviour himself, then Priests that come from him, exercising the spiritual office of Priesthood in all Nations, after the Ecclesiastical ordinances, do represent the mysteries of his body and healthful blood, in bread and wine: which mysteries Melchisedech knew so long before by divine spirit, and used as representations of things to come. S. Ambrose li. 5. de Sacramen. c. 1. We know the figure of the Sacraments went before, in Abraham's time, when Melchisedech offered Sacrifice. Idem in cap. 5. Heb. It is clear that oblations of cattle are vanished, which were in Aaron's Order, but Melchisedech's institution remaineth, which is celebrated all the world over in administration of the Sacraments. S. Hierome Epist. ad Marcellam ut migret Bethleem: Have recourse to Genesis, and you shall find Melchisedech, King of Salem, Prince of this city, who even then in figure of Christ offered bread and wine, and dedicated the Christian mystery in our Saviour's body and blood. Idem Epist. ad Evagrium: Melchisedech offered not bloody victims, but dedicated the Sacrament of Christ in bread and wine, a simple and pure sacrifice. Idem Quest. in Gen. c. 14. Our mystery if signified in the word of Order, not by Aaron in immolating brute victims, but in offering bread and wine, that is, the body and blood of our Lord Jesus, Idem in c. 26. Matthei: Melchisedech the Priest of God most High, by offering bread and wine, prefigured the Mystery of the Eucharist. S. Chrysostom ho. 35. in Gen. Seeing the figure, think also I pray thee of the verity. Ho. 36. After that Melchisedech King of Salem brought bread and wine (for he was Priest of God most High) Abraham received his oblations. S. Augustine Epist. 95. Melchisedech bringing forth the Sacrament (or mystery) of our Lord's table, knew how to figurate his eternal Priesthood. Idem li. 16. c. 22. civit. There first appeared that Sacrifice, which is now offered to God by Christians in the whole world. Idem li. 17. c. 17. li. 18. c. 35. and upon the Psalm 109. li. 1. contra advers. Leg. & Prophet. c. 20. Ser. 4. de Sanctis Innocentibus. But it is bootless or needless to cite more places, or more Authors: for whosoever will not submit their judgements to these, would not, it is like, believe, if their own Masters should rise again and warn them, lest they be damned for their incredulity. ¹ \fq ¹ \ft Paying of tithes in the law of nature.\ef*
\v 19 blessed him, and said: Blessed be Abram to God the Highest, which created heaven and earth: \ef - \fr 14.19 \fq Blessed him. \ft Calvin (in cap. 7. v. 9. Heb.) Musculus (locis com. c. de Missa Papist.) and some other Protestants, to avoid the connexion of Melchisedech's Priesthood and bringing forth of bread and wine, will needs have these words, \+it He was a Priest\+it*, referred only to that which followeth, \+it he blessed Abraham\+it*. ¹ ² And some English translaters for this purpose have corrupted the text, by changing \+it And\+it* into \+it Therefore\+it*, saying thus: \+it And he was a Priest of the most High God, therefore he blessed him.\+it* Which is also a false gloss. For Melchisedech did not bless Abram because he was a Priest, for Abram was also a Priest, but because he was a greater Priest than Abram; which S. Paul urgeth saying: ³ ⁴ \+it Without all contradiction that which is less, is blessed of the better\+it*, concluding thereupon that Melchisedech was greater than Abraham. Again, other Superiors that are not Priests may bless their inferiors. As Josue and Salomon blessed the people, and parents bless their children. ⁵ \fq ¹ \ft Heretical translation. \fq ² \ft \+it Bible 1579.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The greater blesseth the less. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Heb. 7.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Iosue 8. & 22. 3. Reg. 8.\ef*
\v 20 and blessed be God the Highest, by whose protection the enemies are in thy hands. And he gave him the tithes of all. \ef - \fr 14.20 \fq Gave tithes. \ft This is another prerogative of Melchisedech, ¹ that Abram payed tithes to him, which S. Paul likwise explicateth (Heb. 7.) and proveth thereby that Christ's Priesthood is greater than the Levitical. Moreover this paying of tithes by Abraham sheweth the antiquity of this tradition, being practised in Abraham's time, that the spiritual Superiors received tithes of their inferiors. \fq ¹ \ft Calvin contemneth all the ancient fathers. in ca. 9. Heb.\ef*
\v 21 And the king of Sodom said to Abram: Give me the souls, and the rest take to thee.
\v 22 Who answered him: I lift up my hand to my Lord God most High, possessor of heaven and earth,
\v 23 that from the very woof-thread unto the shoe latchet, I will not take of all that are thine, \f a \fr 14.23 \ft Abraham enriched by God, would take no more of man, but his soldiers' sustenance, the proper hire of spiritual workmen. S. Chrisost. in Gen.\f* lest thou say, I have enriched Abram:
\v 24 except such things as the young men have eaten, and the shares of the men that came with me, Aner, Eschol, and Mambre: these shall take their shares.
\c 15
\cd Abram doubting and lamenting that he should have no child, God promiseth him much issue, 6. who believing is justified. 9. offereth sacrifice prescribed by God, 13. & is forewarned that his seed shall be in a strange land 400. years. 14. shall be delivered from servitude, 18. and possess Chanaan.
\p
\v 1 When these things therefore were done, the word of our Lord was made to Abram by a vision saying: fear not Abram, I am thy Protector, and thy reward exceeding great.
\v 2 And Abram said: Lord God, what wilt thou give me? I shall go without children: and the son of the steward of my house is this Damascus Eliezer.
\v 3 And Abram added: But to me thou hast not given seed: and Lo my servant born in my house shall be mine heir.
\v 4 And immediately the word of our Lord came to him saying: He shall not be thine heir: but he that shall come out of thy womb him shalt thou have thine heir.
\v 5 And he brought him forth abroad, and said to him: Look up to heaven, and number the stars, if thou canst. And he said to him: so shall thy seed be.
\v 6 \x - \xt Ro. 4. Gal. 3. Iac. 2.\x* Abram believed God, and it was reputed to him unto justice. \ef - \fr 15.6 \fq Believed God. \ft S. Hilary (li. 9. de Trin.) and S. Ambrose (li. 1. de Abraham. c. 3.) by this example teach us, what manner of faith is reputed to justice, to wit, ¹ such a faith as without tergiversation, or requiring of proof or reason, doth simply believe that which God once saith, because he is omnipotent, how far soever the thing that is said surmounteth our understanding. For so heroical was the act of Abraham's faith, promptly believing God's word in a matter most hard to his former conceit, that for the same he received singular praise; and for the like afterwards was called \+it The father of many Nations\+it* (c. 17. v. 5.) and by S. Paul \+it The father of all that believe.\+it* (Ro. 4. v. 11.) where the Apostle teacheth that Abraham had no justice, nor estimation of justice before God, until he believed in Christ (v. 18. 19, 20.) because all works before that faith are insufficient ². Neither was this a sole faith, but had other necessary virtues of hope, and charity, humility, reverence, obedience, and the like joined with it. Wherefore S. James testifieth that Abraham was justified by works (that is by works following faith, not going before faith.) ³ For \+it Faith\+it* (saith he) \+it if it have not works, is dead in it-self\+it*, (c. 2. v. 17.) \+it And by works Abraham's faith was consummate v. 22.\+it* And concludeth thus: Do ye see, that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only. v. 24. \fq ¹ \ft To believe God's word without staggering is an act of justice. \fq ² \ft Not works before faith but joined with faith are meritorious. \fq ³ \ft Only faith doth not justify.\ef*
\v 7 And he said to him: I am the Lord that brought thee out from Ur of the Chaldees for to give thee this land, and that thou mightest possess it.
\v 8 But he said: Lord God, how may I know that I shall possess it?
\v 9 And our Lord answered, and said: \f a \fr 15.9 \ft These three kinds of beasts & two of birds signify that the Israelites should be three generations in a strange land, the fourth in the desert, the fifth in possession of Chanaan. \+it Theod. q. 65. in Gen.\+it*\f* Take me a cow of three years old, and a she goat of three years, and a ram of three years, a turtle also, and a pigeon.
\v 10 Who taking all these, divided them by the midst, and laid each two pieces arow one against the other: but the birds he divided not.
\v 11 And the fowls lighted upon the carcasses, and Abram drove them away.
\v 12 And when the sun was setting, a deep sleep fell upon Abram, and a great and darksome horror invaded him.
\v 13 \x - \xt Act. 17.\x* And it was said unto him: Know and foreknow that a pilgrim shall thy seed be in a land not their own (and they shall bring them under bondage, and afflict them) \f b \fr 15.13 \ft Abraham & his seed were in a strange land 400. and odd years, but in servitude & affliction about 140.\f* four hundred years.
\v 14 \x - \xt Exod. 12.\x* But the Nation, whom they shall serve, I will judge: and after this they shall go forth with great substance.
\v 15 And thou shalt go to thy fathers in peace, buried in a good old age.
\v 16 But in the fourth generation they shall return hither: for \f c \fr 15.16 \ft God deferreth to punish, either that the wicked may amend, or the good be exercised by them. S. Aug. Psal. 54. or because the iniquity is not come to that great measure which his wisdom foreseeth, and will punish in the end, to his own more glory, & more good of others. S. Greg. ho. 11. in 3. Ezech.\f* as yet the iniquities of the Amorrheans are not at the full until this present time.
\v 17 Therefore when the sun was set, there arose a dark mist, and there appeared a furnace smoking, and a flake of fire passing between those divisions.
\v 18 That day God made a covenant with Abram, saying: To thy seed will I give this land from the river of Ægypt even to the great river Euphrates.
\v 19 the Cineans, and Cenezeites, the Cedmonites,
\v 20 and the Hethits, and the Pherezits, the Raphaims also,
\v 21 and the Amorreans and the Chananites, and Gargasites, and the Jebusites.
\c 16
\cd Sarai giveth her hand-maid Agar as a wife to Abram: 4. who conceiving despiseth her mistress, is therefore afflicted, and flyeth away. 7. But is warned by an Angel to return and humble herself, 15. which she doth, and beareth Ismael
\p
\v 1 Sarai therefore the wife of Abram had brought forth no children; but having an handmaid, an Ægyptian named Agar,
\v 2 she said to her husband: Behold, our Lord hath closed me, that I might not bear; Go in unto my handmaid, if happily of her at the least I may have children. And when he agreed to her in this request,
\v 3 she took Agar the Ægyptian her handmaid ten years after that they first dwelled in the land of Chanaan, and gave her unto her husband to wife. \ef - \fr 16.3 \fq To wife. \ft ¹ ² The Manichees did calumniate holy Abraham and other Patriarchs for having many wives, condemning them of incontinency and adultery for the same. Luther ³ ⁴ in the contrary extreme held it not unlawful, but indifferent now in the law of grace, for a man to have more wives than one at once. ⁵ And some English Protestants hold, that for adultery the innocent party may marry another, the first living But the Catholic doctrine distinguishing times and causes, sheweth how plurality of wives was lawful sometimes, and at other times, especially since Christ, altogether unlawful and undispensable. The sum of which verity is this. By the first institution of Marriage in the state of innocency, and law of nature, and by the law of Christ, it is unlawful for any man to have more wives, and for any woman to have more husbands than one. In the one part of which Law notwithstanding God sometimes dispensed. For there be two kinds of precepts pertaining to the law of nature ⁶ ⁷. One sort are as first principles of the law of nature, in which God never dispenseth, much less any man. As that one woman may not have more husbands than one, because the same would rather hinder procreation, and so were directly against the fruit of marriage. The other sort are as conclusions drawn from the first principles, in which God sometimes dispenseth, but never any man. As in this present example: seeing it is against natural procreation that one woman should have many husbands, it is convenient also, there being ordinarily as many men as women in the world, that every man likewise should be restrained to one wife, for so procreation may rather be increased, than if some men have many wives, and others by that occasion have none at all, except in some special case. As after the flood, when there was scarcity of people, God dispensed with such men as indeed were like to make greater procreation by plurality of wives ⁸. Which appeareth sufficiently by that Sarai persuaded her own husband to marry another wife, and he a true servant of God agreed thereto, not as a new thing, but as a lawful practice of those times. And Moyses ⁹ here and in other places still speaketh of it, as of a custom known to the people for lawful. \+it If a man\+it* (saith he) \+it have two wives, one beloved and the other hated, and they have children by him, and the son of the hated be first-born, he cannot prefer the son of the beloved.\+it* Whereby is clear that two wives were then lawful, and the children of both legitimate, and that the first-born must be preferred, without respect of first of last Marriage. Yet this dispensation either ceased before Christ's time, the cause ceasing when the world was replenished; or at least our Saviour took it away, restoring Matrimony to the first institution of two in one flesh ¹⁰ ¹¹. Who pleaseth to see the Doctors that understand and expound the Scriptures to this effect, may read S. Augustine li. 22. c. 30. & 47. con. Faust. Manich. li. 16. c. 25. & 38. civit. & li. 1. de adulter. coniugiis. S. Chrysostom. ho. 56. in Gen. S. Amb. li. de Abraham. c. 4. Also S. Chrysost. S. Hierom. and S. Bede in 19. Matthei. \fq ¹ \ft Manichees condemned plurality of wives in the Patriarchs. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Aug. l. 22. c. 47. cont. Faust.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Luther alloweth it in Christians. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Luther proposit. 62. 65. & 66.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Other Protestants in some case. \fq ⁶ \ft Two sorts of precepts in the law of nature. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it S. Aug. de bono coniugali. c. 17.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft Plurality of wives sometimes allowed. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Deut. 21.\+it* \fq ¹⁰ \ft By the law of Christ in no case lawful. \fq ¹¹ \ft \+it Mat. 19. Gen. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 Who did company with her, but she \f a \fr 16.4 \ft Some obey whilst they are rude, or in low state, but having got a little knowledge or advancement disdain their advancers. \+it S. Gregory. li. 21. in 1. Reg. 3.\+it*\f* perceiving that she was with child, despised her mistress.
\v 5 And Sarai said to Abram: Thou doest unjustly against me: I gave my handmaid into thy bosom, who perceiving herself to be with child, despiseth me. Our Lord judge between me and thee.
\v 6 To whom Abram making answer: Behold, saith he, thy handmaid is in thine own hand, use her as it pleaseth thee. When Sarai therefore did afflict her, she ran away.
\v 7 And an Angel of our Lord having found her beside a fountain of water in the wilderness, which is in the way to Sur in the desert,
\v 8 he said to her: Agar, the handmaid of Sarai, whence comest thou? And whither goest thou? Who answered: From the face of Sarai my mistress do I fly.
\v 9 And the Angel of our Lord said to her: Return to thy mistress, and humble thyself under her hand.
\v 10 And again: Multiplying, saith he, will I multiply thy seed, and it shall not be numbered for the multitude thereof.
\v 11 And again after that: Behold, saith he, thou art with child, and thou shalt bring forth a son; and thou shalt call his name Ismael, because the Lord hath heard thine affliction.
\v 12 He shall be a wild man: his hand shall be against all men, and all men's hands against him; and over against all his brethren shall he pitch his tents.
\v 13 And she called the name of our Lord that spake unto her: Thou the God which hast seen me. For she said: verily here have I seen the back parts of him that hast seen me.
\v 14 Therefore she called that well, the well of him that liveth and seeth me. The same is between Cadesse and Barad.
\v 15 And Agar brought forth a son to Abram: Who called his name Ismael.
\v 16 Eighty and six years old was Abram when Agar brought him forth Ismael.
\c 17
\cd God renewing his promises to Abram 5. changeth his name, 10. and commandeth Circumcision. 15. changeth also his wife's name, promiseth a son of her. 20. Likewise that Ismael shall prosper. 23. And the same day Abraham circumcised himself, and Ismael, and all the men of his house.
\p
\v 1 And after that he began to be ninety and nine years old, our Lord appeared unto him, and said unto him: I am the God almighty: walk before me, and be \f a \fr 17.1 \ft He is perfect in this life, that sincerely tendeth towards perfection of the next life. And this God here commanded to Abraham, & Christ to all Christians. \+it Mat. 5. S. Aug. l. de perfect. cont. Cælest.\+it* See Gen. 6. v. 9.\f* perfect.
\v 2 And I will make my covenant between me and thee: and I will multiply thee exceedingly.
\v 3 Abram fell flat on his face.
\v 4 And God said to him: I am, and my covenant is with thee, and thou shalt be a father of many Nations.
\v 5 Neither shall thy name be called any more Abram; but thou shalt be called Abraham: because a father of many Nations I have made thee. \ef - \fr 17.5 \fq Many nations. \ft Abram signifying \+it High\+it* or \+it Noble father\+it*, ¹ changed into Abraham, which signifieth \+it Father of many\+it* (Nations) and Sarai signifying \+it My Princess\+it*, changed into Sara, which absolutely signifieth \+it Princess\+it*, import great Mysteries intended by God. For by this changing of names was declared and confirmed, that Abraham by the issue that he should have of Sara, should be the father of many Nations, which S. Paul ² expressly applieth to his spiritual children, those especially that should believe in Christ, of the Gentiles, prefigured by \+it Isaac born of the freewoman, by the promise\+it*, as the Jews were prefigured by \+it Ismael born of the bondwoman according to the flesh\+it*, concluding with the prophet Esay ³, that \+it Many are the children of the desolate, that before was barren.\+it* ⁴ For albeit Abraham was natural father of four Nations, the Ismaelites, Madianites, Idumeans, and the Israelites, yet he was spiritual father of many more, to wit, of all Nations that believe in Christ from his own time to the end of the world, of which some are Jews, but the greater part Gentiles, as the same Apostle sheweth Ro. 4. Gal. 4. Ephes. 3. Colos. 1. and in other places. Whereby it is clear, that the Church of Christ doth always consist of many Nations ⁵, not as Donatists and Protestants absurdly say, sometimes of few invisible or unknown persons, for so Abraham should sometimes be father of few or no Nations, which derogateth either from God's omnipotency if he could not, or from his fidelity if he should not maintain his promise made to Abraham for ever. \fq ¹ \ft Changing of names is mystical. \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 28. and 39. civit. S. Hierom. Tradit. Hebraicis.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Gal. 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Isa 54.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Abraham natural father of four Nations: spiritual father of all that do believe in Christ. \+it Rom. 4, 11.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft The Church of Christ ever consisteth of many Nations.\ef*
\v 6 And I will make thee increase exceedingly, and I will make thee into Nations, and kings shall come forth of thee.
\v 7 And I will establish my covenant between me and thee, and between thy seed after thee in their generations by a perpetual covenant: to be thy God, and thy seeds after thee.
\v 8 And I will give to thee, and to thy seed the land of thy peregrination, all the land of Chanaan for a perpetual possession, and I will be their God.
\v 9 Again God said to Abraham: And thou therefore shalt keep my covenant, and thy seed after thee in their generations.
\v 10 This is my covenant which you shall observe between me and you, and thy seed after thee: All the male kind of you shall be circumcised: \ef - \fr 17.10 \fq Shall be circumcised. \ft The Sacrament of Circumcision here first instituted, about 400. years before the Law of Moyses, ¹ is a perfect figure of Baptism, resembling it in four things: First, by both these Sacraments the faithful are distinguished from infidels. Secondly, profession of faith is made in them both, either by those that receive the same, if they be of discretion, or by others for them, if they be infants. Thirdly, by both these Sacraments entrance is made into the Church and to the participation of other Sacraments and spiritual rites. Fourthly, both these Sacraments induce subjection to the jurisdiction and laws of the Church. ² But Baptism doth far excel Circumcision in that it is more easy, or less painful, more universal, for it pertaineth to all Nations, and both sexes; and especially in virtue and efficacy, for Baptism as an instrumental cause remitteth sin and justifieth, Circumcision was only a sign that grace was given & sin remitted. Again, Baptism imprinteth a character in the soul, the other leaveth a mark only in the flesh. Finally, Baptism openeth the gate of heaven, in virtue of Christ's passion now past, which circumcision could not before Christ suffered death. Of which both resemblance and difference S. Augustine treateth in many places, especially li. 3 de doct. Christ. c. 9. Epist. 118. ad Ianuarium. li. 19. c. 13. cont. Faust. and in Psal. 73. where he also maketh like comparison between other Sacraments of the old and new Testament. \fq ¹ \ft Circumcision a figure of Baptism. \fq ² \ft Baptism excelleth Circumcision.\ef*
\v 11 and you shall circumcise the flesh of your prepuce, that it may be for a sign of the covenant between me and you.
\v 12 An infant of \f b \fr 17.12 \ft Circumcision and name received the eighth day signified the association of Saints in heaven after the seven days travail of this world. \+it Ser. de circum. apud S. Cyprian.\+it*\f* eight days shall be circumcised among you, all male-kind in your generations: as well the homebred shall be circumcised, as the bought servant of whosoever he is, not of your stock:
\v 13 and my covenant shall be in your flesh for a perpetual covenant.
\v 14 The male, whose flesh of his prepuce shall not be circumcised, that soul shall be destroyed out of his people: because he hath broken my covenant. \ef - \fr 17.14 \fq Shall be destroyed. \ft ¹ Here occur two difficulties about the true sense of this hard place. First, whether this punishment belonged to them only, by whose fault circumcision was omitted; or to infants also that should be circumcised and were not. Secondly, whether temporal punishment or eternal was here threatened for transgression of this precept. ² Touching the first doubt, it seemeth probable, that this punishment pertained only to those, by whose negligence themselves, or others of their charge, were not circumcised: for that the reason why such a one is punished is alleged, \+it Because he hath broken God's covenant.\+it* Which is only in their power to keep or break, that are of discretion, and not in the power of infants. The indifferency also of the Hebrew and Greek text favoureth this sense, for where according to the Latin we read, \+it The male whose flesh of prepuce is not circumcised\+it*, The Hebrew and Greek may be translated, \+it The male that doth not circumcise the flesh of his prepuce,\+it* which cannot be meant of an infant of eight days old. this exposition is likewise confirmed by example, Exod. 4. where Moyses was in danger to be slain by an Angel, because his son was not circumcised. Nevertheless S. Augustin (li. 3. c. 18 cont. Iulian. li. 16. c. 27. civit. and in other places) proveth that this commination pertained also to infants. Whose judgement is confirmed by the 70 Interpreters, adding for explication these words, \+it the eighth day\+it*: which necessarily include infants, as subject to this punishment; not for that they could transgress this precept, or commit a new sin, but for lack of circumcision. The reason whereof is, for that God now determined this only remedy for original sin, in the male sex of Abraham's seed, in place of sacrifice, or other profession of faith used before, and that in case it could conveniently be applied, for otherwise the former remedies were still available. \fq ¹ \ft Two difficulties. \fq ² \ft To whom the punishment pertained when circumcision was omitted.\ef* \ef - \fr 17.14 \ft ¹ Concerning the other difficulty many ancient fathers expound these words, \+it Shall be destroyed out of his people\+it*, of only temporal punishment; either death, as the like phrase signifieth, Exod. 12. \+it Whosoever shall eat leavened bread, his soul shall perish out of Israel;\+it* or temporal separation from the people of God, as Num. 19. \+it Every one that toucheth the dead corpse, shall perish out of Israel.\+it* But S. Augustine (li. 3. c. 18. cont. Iulian. li. 5. hypog. & alibi) S. Gregory (li. 4. Moral. c. 2. & 3.) S. Prosper (li. de promiss. Dei p. 1. c. 14.) S. Beda (li. 2. in Lucam c. 8.) and S. Bernard (ser. 3. de circum.) understand this threatening not only of temporal punishment, but also of eternal separation from God, and the society of Saints. And that also infants are so secluded only for lack of this remedy, as now children which die without Baptism, committing no new fault are deprived of the vision of God, for their original sin not remitted. Whereupon is gathered that albeit Circumcision was principally instituted to distinguish the people of God ², which should come of Abraham's seed, from other Nations, yet it was also for remission of original sin: not in all, but in those to whom God appointed this particular remedy. \fq ¹ \ft What punishment was threatened. \fq ² \ft Circumcision instituted to distinguish the people of God and for remedy of original sin in some persons, but not in all.\ef*
\v 15 God said also to Abraham: Sarai thy wife thou shalt not call Sarai, but Sara.
\v 16 And I will bless her, and of her I will give thee a son, whom I will bless, and he shall be into Nations, and kings of peoples shall spring of him.
\v 17 Abraham fell upon his face, and \f c \fr 17.17 \ft Abraham laughed not doubting but rejoicing. \+it S. Ambro. li. de Abraham c. 4. S. Aug. l. 16. c. 26. de civit.\+it*\f* laughed, saying in his heart: Shall trowest thou to him that is an hundred year old a son be born? And Sara that is ninety years old shall she bear?
\v 18 And he said to God: I would that Ismael may live before thee.
\v 19 And God said to Abraham: Sara thy wife shall bear thee a son, and thou shalt call his name Isaac, and I will establish my covenant to him for a perpetual covenant, and to his seed after him.
\v 20 Concerning Ismael also I have heard thee. Behold, I will bless him, and increase and multiply him exceedingly: twelve Dukes shall he beget, and I will make him into a great Nation.
\v 21 But my covenant I will establish with \f d \fr 17.21 \ft Temporal blessings were common to Ismael, but spiritual pertained properly to Isaac and Israel, & their successors.\f* Isaac, whom Sara shall bring forth to thee at this time another year.
\v 22 And when he had left off speaking with him, God ascended from Abraham.
\v 23 And Abraham took Ismael his son, and all the homebred of his house: and all whom he had bought, all the males of all the men of his house: and he circumcised the flesh of their prepuce forthwith the very same day, as God had commanded him.
\v 24 Abraham was ninety and nine years old, when he circumcised the flesh of his prepuce.
\v 25 And Ismael his son was full thirteen years old at the time of his circumcision.
\v 26 The self-same day was Abraham circumcised and Ismael his son.
\v 27 And \f e \fr 17.27 \ft Conformity in Religion conserveth peace in every family. \+it Tho. Anglus in hunc locum.\+it*\f* all the men of his house, as well the homebred as the bought servants and strangers were circumcised together.
\c 18
\cd Angels entertained as guests by Abraham 10. tell when Sara shall bear a son, whereat she laughing, they confirm that they had said. 16. They also foretell the destruction of Sodome, 22. for which Abraham prayed six times.
\p
\v 1 And God appeared to him in the vale of Mambre as he sat in the door of his tent, in the very heat of the day.
\v 2 And when he had lifted up his eyes, there appeared to him three men standing near unto him: whom after he had seen, he ran to meet them from the door of his tent, and adored to the ground.
\v 3 And He said \f a \fr 18.3 \ft Abraham saw three, and adored one, professing three divine Persons and one God. \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 29. civit.\+it*\f* Lord, if I have found grace in thy sight, go not past thy servant: \x - \xt Heb. 13.\x*
\v 4 but I will fetch a little water, and wash ye your feet, and rest ye under the tree.
\v 5 And I will set a morsel of bread, and strengthen your heart, afterward you shall pass: for therefore are you come aside to your servant. Who said: Do as thou hast spoken.
\v 6 Abraham made haste into the tent to Sara, and said to her: Make haste, temper together three measures of flour, and make hearth cakes.
\v 7 But himself ran to the herd, and took from thence a calf very tender and very good, and gave it to a young man: who made haste and boiled it.
\v 8 He took also butter and milk, and the calf which he had boiled, and set before them: but himself did stand beside them under the tree.
\v 9 And when they had eaten, they said to him: Where is Sara thy wife? He answered: Lo she is in the tent.
\v 10 To whom he said: Returning I will come to thee at this time, life accompanying, and Sara thy wife shall have a son \x - \xt Rom. 9.\x*. Which when Sara heard, she laughed behind the door of the tent.
\v 11 And they were both aged, and far entered in years, and it ceased to be with Sara after the manner of women.
\v 12 Who laughed secretly saying: After I am waxen old, and my Lord is an old one, shall I give myself to pleasure?
\v 13 And our Lord said to Abraham: Why did Sara laugh, saying: Shall I an old woman bear a child indeed?
\v 14 Is there any thing hard to God? According to appointment I will return to thee this very self-same time, life accompanying, & Sara shall have a son.
\v 15 Sara denied, saying: I laughed not; being much afraid. But our Lord: \f b \fr 18.15 \ft Abraham laughing with admiration for joy was not reprehended, but Sara laughing of diffidence was reprehended, by him that seeth the heart. \+it S. Aug. q. 36. in Gen. & li. 16. c. 31. civit.\+it*\f* It is not so, saith he, but thou didst laugh.
\v 16 When the men therefore were risen up from thence, they turned their eyes against Sodome: and Abraham did go with them, bringing them on the way.
\v 17 And our Lord said: Can I conceal from Abraham the things which I will do,
\v 18 whereas he shall be into a Nation great, and very strong, and in him are TO BE \sc Blessed All The Nations of The Earth\sc*?
\v 19 For I know that he will command his children, and his house after him that they keep the way of the Lord, & do judgement and justice: that for Abraham's sake the Lord may bring to effect all the things that he hath spoken unto him. Therefore said our Lord:
\v 20 The cry of Sodome and Gomorre is multiplied, and their sin is aggravated exceedingly.
\v 21 I will descend, and see whether they have in act accomplished the cry that is come to me: or whether it be not so, that I may know.
\v 22 And they turned themselves from thence, and went their way to Sodome: but Abraham as yet stood before our Lord.
\v 23 And approaching he said: What! Wilt thou destroy the just with the wicked?
\v 24 \f c \fr 18.24 \ft what a wall are just men to their country? Their faith saveth us, their justice defendeth us from destruction. \+it S. Amb. li. 1. de Abrah. c. 6.\+it*\f* If there shall be fifty just persons in the city, shall they perish withal? And wilt thou spare that place for fifty just, if they be therein?
\v 25 Be it far from thee, that thou do this thing, and that thou kill the just with the wicked, and that the just be in like case as the wicked, this is not beseeming thee, which judgest all the earth: no, thou wilt not do this judgement.
\v 26 And our Lord said to him: If I shall find in Sodome fifty just persons within the city, I will spare the whole place for their sake.
\v 27 And Abraham answered, and said: Because I have once begun, I will speak to my Lord, whereas I am dust and ashes.
\v 28 What if there shall be five less than fifty just persons? Wilt thou for forty five destroy the whole city? And he said: I will not destroy it, if I shall find five and forty.
\v 29 And again he said unto him: But if forty shall be found there, what wilt thou do? He said: I will not strike it for forty's sake.
\v 30 Lord, saith he, be not angry I beseech thee, if I speak: What if thirty shall be found there? He answered: I will not do it, if I shall find thirty there.
\v 31 Because, saith he, I have once begun, I will speak to my Lord: What if twenty shall be found there? He said: I will not destroy it for twenty's sake.
\v 32 I beseech thee, saith he, be not angry Lord, if I speak yet once more: What if ten shall be found there? And he said: I will not destroy it for ten's sake.
\v 33 And our Lord departed after that he ceased to speak unto Abraham: and Abraham returned into his place.
\c 19
\cd Lot receiving Angels in his house is abused by the Sodomites. 12. He with his wife (26. who for looking back is turned into a statua of salt) and his two daughters, are delivered. 24. Sodome and Gomorre are burned. 31. Lot lieth unwitting with both his daughters, & begat of them Moab and Ammon, of whom came the Moabites and Ammonites.
\p
\v 1 And the two \f a \fr 19.1 \ft Abraham and Lot by hospitality merited to receive Angels instead of men. Heb. 13.\f* Angels came to Sodome at even, and Lot sitting in the gates of the city. Who when he had seen them, rose up & went to meet them: and adored prostrate unto the ground,
\v 2 and said: I beseech you, my Lords, turn into the house of your servant, and lodge there: Wash your feet, and in the morning you shall go forth on your way. Who said: No, but we will abide in the street.
\v 3 He compelled them earnestly to turn in unto him: and when they were entered into his house, he made them a banquet, and baked unleavened bread, and they did eat.
\v 4 And before they went to bed, the men of the city beset the house from young to old, all the people together.
\v 5 And they called Lot, and said to him: Where are the men that came in to thee at night? Bring them forth hither that we may know them.
\v 6 Lot going forth to them, and shutting the door after him, said:
\v 7 Do not so, I beseech you, my brethren, do not commit this evil.
\v 8 I have two daughters, which as yet have not known man: I will bring them forth to you, and abuse you them as it shall please you, so that you do no evil to these men, because they are entered under the shadow of my roof.
\v 9 But they said: Get thee back thither. And again: Thou camest in, said they, as a stranger, what to be a Judge? Thyself therefore we will afflict more than these. And they did violence to Lot exceedingly: and it was even now at the point that they would break the doors.
\v 10 And behold the men put forth their hand, and drew in Lot unto them, and shut the door:
\v 11 and them that were without they struck with blindness from the least to the greatest, so that they could not find the door.
\v 12 And they said to Lot: Hast thou here any of thine? Son in law, or sons, or daughters, all that are thine, bring them out of this city:
\v 13 for we will destroy this place, for that \f b \fr 19.13 \ft This sin cryeth to heaven for revenge.\f* their cry is waxen loud before our Lord, who hath sent us to destroy them.
\v 14 Therefore Lot went forth, and spake to his sons in law that were to take his daughters, and said: Arise, get you forth out of this place, because our Lord will destroy this city. And he seemed unto them to speak as it were in jest.
\v 15 And when it was morning, the Angels urged him, saying: Arise, take thy wife, and the two daughters which thou hast: lest thou also perish withal in the wickedness of the city.
\v 16 He lingering, they took his hand, and the hand of his wife, and of his two daughters, because our Lord spared him.
\v 17 And they led him forth, and set him without the city; and there they spake to him, saying: Save thy life; look not back, neither stay thou in all the country about: but save thyself in the mountain, lest thou also perish withal.
\v 18 And Lot said to them: I beseech thee my Lord,
\v 19 because thy servant hath found grace before thee, and thou hast magnified thy mercy, which thou hast wrought with me, in that thou wouldest save my life, and safe I cannot be in the mountain, lest perhaps the evil catch me, and I die.
\v 20 There is this city here-by at hand, whereunto I may fly, a little one, and I shall be safe in it: is it not a little one, and my life shall be saved?
\v 21 And he said to him: Behold also in this point I have heard thy prayers, not to overthrow the city for which thou hast spoken.
\v 22 Make haste, and be saved there: because I cannot do any thing till thou enter in thither. Therefore the name of that city was called \f 1 \fr 19.22 \ft \+it a little one.\+it*\f* Segor.
\v 23 The sun was risen upon the earth, and Lot entered into Segor.
\v 24 Therefore our Lord rained upon Sodome and Gomorre brimstone & fire from our Lord out of heaven:
\v 25 and he subverted these cities, and all the country about, all the inhabitants of the cities, and all things that spring of the earth.
\v 26 And his wife \f c \fr 19.26 \ft Lot's wife turned into salt, admonisheth the servants of God to proceed in virtue, & not to look back to vice. \+it Luc. 17. S. Aug. li. 16. c. 30. civit.\+it*\f* looking behind her, was turned into a statua of salt.
\v 27 And Abraham getting up early in the morning, there where before he had stood with our Lord,
\v 28 beheld Sodome and Gomorre, and the whole land of that country: and he saw the cinders rise up from the earth as it were the smoke of a furnace.
\v 29 For when God subverted the cities of that country, he \f d \fr 19.29 \ft Lot neither perfect nor very wicked was delivered for Abraham's sake. \+it S. Aug. q. 45. in Gen.\+it*\f* remembering Abraham, delivered Lot out of the subversion of the cities wherein he had dwelt.
\v 30 And Lot ascended out of Segor, and abode in the mountain, his two daughters also with him (for he was afraid to abide in Segor) and he abode in a cave himself, and his two daughters with him.
\v 31 And the elder said to the younger: Our father is old, and there is no man left on the earth, that may company with us after the manner of the whole earth.
\v 32 Come, let us make him drunk with wine, and let us lie with him, that we may preserve seed of our father.
\v 33 They therefore made their father to drink wine that night: and the elder went in, and lay with her father: but he perceived not, neither when his daughter lay down, nor when she rose up.
\v 34 The next day also the elder said to the younger: Behold I lay yesternight with my father, let us make him drink wine also this night, and thou shalt lie with him, that we may save seed of our father.
\v 35 They made their father drink wine that night also, and the younger daughter went in, and lay with him: and neither then truly did he perceive when she lay down, or when she rose up.
\v 36 The two daughters therefore of Lot were with child by their father.
\v 37 And the elder bare a son and she called his name Moab: he is the \f e \fr 19.37 \ft Moabites & Ammonites were two distinct Nations, perhaps of the 72. See. p. 41.\f* father of the Moabites even to this present day.
\v 38 The younger also bare a son, and she called his name Ammon, that is the son of my people: he is the father of the Ammonites even to this day.
\c 20
\cd Isaac is born, 4. circumcised, 8. and weaned. 9. Agar and Ismael are put forth of Abraham's house, 15. but after desolation are nourished, and prosper in the desert. 22. King Abimelech and Abraham make a league confirming it with oath. \f - \ft The sixth part of this book.\f*
\p
\v 1 Abraham removed from thence into the south country and dwelt between Cades, and Sur: and he lived as a pilgrim in Gerara.
\v 2 And he said of Sara his wife: She is \f a \fr 20.2 \ft See pag. 50.\f* my sister. Abimelech therefore, the king of Gerara, sent and took her.
\v 3 And God came to Abimelech in a dream by night, and said to him: Lo thou shalt die for the woman that thou hast taken: for she hath a husband.
\v 4 But Abimelech had not touched her, and he said: Lord wilt thou kill a Nation that is ignorant & just?
\v 5 Did not he say to me: She is my sister? And she say, He is my brother? In simplicity of my heart and cleanness of my hands have I done this.
\v 6 And God said to him: And I do know that thou didst it with a sincere heart: and therefore I kept thee that thou shouldest not sin against me, and I permitted not that thou shouldest touch her.
\v 7 Now therefore restore the wife to her husband, because he is a Prophet: and he shall pray for thee, and thou shalt live. But if thou wilt not restore her, know thou that dying thou shalt die, thou and all things that are thine.
\v 8 And Abimelech forthwith rising up in the night, called all his servants: and he spake all these words to their ears, and all the men were sore afraid.
\v 9 And Abimelech called also for Abraham, & said to him: What hast thou done to us? What have we offended against thee, that thou hast brought upon me and upon my kingdom a great sin? That which thou oughtest not to do, thou hast done to us.
\v 10 And again expostulating, he said: What sawest thou, that thou didest this?
\v 11 Abraham answered: I thought with myself, saying: Perhaps there is not the fear of God in this place, & they will kill me for my wife:
\v 12 howbeit otherwise also in very deed she is my sister, \f b \fr 20.12 \ft Not his father's own daughter, but of his progeny. \+it S. Aug. l. 22. cont. Faust. c. 35. & l. 16. c. 19. & 30. de civit.\+it*\f* the daughter of my father, & not the daughter of my mother, & I took her to wife.
\v 13 And after that God brought me out of my father's house, I said to her: This mercy thou shalt do with me: In every place which we shall come unto, thou shalt say that I am thy brother.
\v 14 Therefore Abimelech \f c \fr 20.14 \ft Kings of the earth esteeming the Church only for a laudable people of God, seek to subject her to themselves: but knowing he to be the inviolable spouse of Christ, subject themselves to her, & offer to her most honourable gifts. \+it S. Aug. li. 22. cont. Faust. c. 38.\+it*\f* took sheep and oxen, and servants, and handmaids, and gave to Abraham: and restored to him Sara his wife,
\v 15 and said: The land is before you, dwell wheresoever it shalt please thee.
\v 16 And to Sara he said: Behold I have given thy brother a thousand pieces of silver, this shall serve thee for a veil of thine eyes to all that are with thee, and whithersoever thou shalt go, remember also thou wast taken.
\v 17 And Abraham praying, God healed Abimelech and his wife, and his handmaids, and they bare children:
\v 18 for our Lord had closed up every matrice of the house of Abimelech for Sara Abraham's wife.
\c 21
\p
\v 1 And \f 1 \fr 21.1 \ft Of the progeny and other blessings of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.\f* our Lord visited Sara, as he had promised: and fulfilled the things which he spake.
\v 2 And she conceived & bare a son in her old age, at the time that God had foretold her.
\v 3 And Abraham called the name of his son, which Sara bare him, \f a \fr 21.3 \ft Abraham & Sara laughed (he admiring, she doubting) at the joyful promise of a son, & therefore he is called Isaac, which signifieth laughter. \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 31. civit.\+it*\f* Isaac:
\v 4 and he circumcised him the eighth day, as God had commanded him,
\v 5 when he was an hundred years old: for at this age of his father, was Isaac born.
\v 6 And Sara said: Laughter God hath made to me: whosoever shall hear of it, will laugh with me.
\v 7 And again she said: Who that shall hear of it would believe Abraham, that Sara gave suck to a son, which she bare him now being an old man?
\v 8 The child therefore grew and was weaned: and Abraham made a great feast in the day of his weaning.
\v 9 And when Sara had seen the son of Agar the Ægyptian playing with Isaac her son, she said to Abraham:
\v 10 Cast out this handmaid, & her son: for the son of the handmaid shall not be heir with my son Isaac.
\v 11 Abraham took this grievously for his son.
\v 12 To whom God said: Let it not seem grievous to thee for the boy, and for thy handmaid: all things that Sara shall say to thee, hear her voice: because \f b \fr 21.12 \ft See c. 17. v. 21.\f*\f 1 \fr 21.12 \ft Rom. 9. Heb. 11.\f* in Isaac shall seed be called to thee. \ef - \fr 21.12 \fq Let it not. \ft ¹ It seemed so hard, inhuman, and unjust to cast Agar and Ismael out of the house, that Abraham would not have done it, unless God had commanded him in this to hear the voice of Sara. But when he knew God's will therein, not respecting flesh and blood, nor disputing further of the lawfulness of the fact, sent them both away, commending them to God's protection: and so by this separation the family was made quiet. ² It represented also a notable Mystery of the Synagogue of the Jews and Church of Christ. As S. Augustine ³ doth excellently expound the same, according to S. Paul (Gal. 4.) That Ismael's playing with Isaac was persecution, because it tended to pervert him, shewing it to be a greater injury to delude and deceive any, in drawing them to new and particular companies, than corporally to persecute them: ⁴ and that the Church and Catholic Princes punish heretics for their good ⁵, to make them return to the truth, or finally to cease from seducing others. ⁶ \+it Ecce libera afflixit ancillam, & non illam vocat persecutionem. Apostolus, ludit servus cum domino, & persecutionem vocat. Afflictio ista non vocatur persecutio, & lusio illa vocatur persecutio.\+it* Behold the freewoman afflicted the handmaid, and the Apostle calleth it not persecution, the servant playeth with the Master, and he calleth it persecution ⁷. This affliction is not called persecution, and that play is called persecution. Again ⁸, he saith of Sara, \+it Illusionem vidit in illo lusu, animadvertit servi superbiam, displicuit illi, eiecit foras.\+it* She saw foul play in that play, she noted the servant's pride, it displeased her, she cast him forth of the doors. S. Hierom ⁹ also teacheth that the spiritual never persecuteth the carnal: but spareth him as his rustical brother, knowing that he may in time be profitable. \fq ¹ \ft Separation to be made in families, when just cause requireth. \fq ² \ft Mystical sense. \fq ³ \ft \+it li. 15. c. 2. civit.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Epist. 48. & 50.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft The true Church doth not persecute but justly punisheth offenders. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Tract. 11. in Ioan.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Heretics and other infidels do persecute, when either by word or sword they impugn the truth. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Tract. 12.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft \+it in 4. Gal\+it*\ef*
\v 13 But the son also of the handmaid I will make into a great nation, because he is thy seed.
\v 14 Abraham therefore rose up in the morning, and taking bread and a bottle of water, put it upon her shoulder, and delivered the boy, and dismissed her. Who went away, and wandered in the wilderness of Bersabee.
\v 15 And when the water in the bottle was spent, she cast the boy under one of the trees that were there.
\v 16 And she went her way, and sat over against a great way off as far as a bow can cast, for she said: I will no see the child dying. And sitting over against, she lifted up her voice and wept.
\v 17 And God heard the voice of the boy: and an Angel of God called Agar from heaven, saying: What doest thou Agar? Fear not; for God hath heard the voice of the boy, from the place wherein he is.
\v 18 Arise, take up the boy, & hold his hand; for into a great nation will I make him.
\v 19 And God opened her eyes: who seeing a well of water, went, and filled the bottle, and gave the boy to drink.
\v 20 And God was with him: who grew, and abode in the wilderness, and became a young man archer.
\v 21 And he dwelt in the wilderness of Pharan, and his mother took a wife for him out of the land of Ægypt.
\v 22 The same time said Abimelech, and Phicol the General of his army, to Abraham: God is with thee in all things which thou doest.
\v 23 Swear therefore by God, not to hurt me, and my posterity, and my stock: but according to the mercy, that I have done thee, thou shalt do to me, and to the land wherein thou hast lived a stranger.
\v 24 And Abraham said: I will swear.
\v 25 And he rebuked Abimelech for the well of water, which his servants had taken away by force.
\v 26 And Abimelech answered: I knew not who did this thing: yea and thyself didst not tell me, and I heard not of it but today.
\v 27 Abraham therefore took sheep and oxen, and gave to Abimelech: and both of them made a league.
\v 28 And Abraham set seven ewe lambs of the flock apart.
\v 29 To whom Abimelech said: What mean these seven ewe lambs, which thou hast made to stand apart?
\v 30 But he said: Seven ewe lambs shalt thou take at my hand: that they may be a testimony for me, that I digged this well.
\v 31 Therefore was that place called \f 1 \fr 21.31 \ft \+it well of oath\+it*\f* Bersabee: because there both did swear.
\v 32 And they made a league for the well of oath.
\v 33 And Abimelech arose, and Phicol the General of his army, and they returned to the land of the Palestines. But Abraham planted a grove in Bersabee, and called there upon the name of our Lord God eternal.
\v 34 And he was a sojourner in the land of the Palestines many days.
\c 22
\cd The faith and obedience of Abraham is proved in his readiness to sacrifice his son Isaac. 11. He is stayed from the act by an Angel. 16. Former promises are renewed to him. 20. And his brother Nachor hath also much issue.
\p
\v 1 Which \x - \xt Judith. 8.\x* things being done, \f 2 \fr 22.1 \ft The third prophecy in the Office before Mass on Easter eve. And the first on Whitsun-eve.\f* God tempted Abraham, and said to him: Abraham, Abraham. But he answered: Here I am. \ef - \fr 22.1 \fq Tempted. \ft ¹ ² God tempteth none to evil (as S. James teacheth) but by trial and experiment maketh men known to the world, as here Abraham's faith and singular obedience were manifested, when he doubted not \+it To offer his only-begotten in Sacrifice, accounting that God is able to raise up even from the dead.\+it* ³ Whereupon he received his son again \+it in parable\+it*, that is, in figure and mystery of Christ dead and alive again. ⁴ Isaac also in this action signified the divinity of Christ which suffered not, and the ram among the briars figured his humanity, that should be offered in Sacrifice to God. \fq ¹ \ft God tempteth not to evil, but by experience maketh known what virtue is in men. \fq ² \ft \+it Iac. 1. 13. Heb. 11. 19.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 32. civit. Theod. q. 72. in Gen.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Isaac figured Christ's divinity, the Ram his humanity.\ef*
\v 2 \x - \xt Heb. 11\x* He said to him: \f a \fr 22.2 \ft This history is clear, and a most notorious example of perfect obedience.\f* Take thy only begotten son, whom thou lovest, Isaac, and go into the Land of vision: and there thou shalt offer him for an holocaust upon one of the mountains which I will shew thee.
\v 3 Therefore Abraham rising up in the night, saddled his ass: taking with him two young men, and Isaac his son: and when he had cut wood for the holocaust, he went his way to the place which God had commanded him.
\v 4 And the third day, lifting up his eyes, he saw the place a far off:
\v 5 and he said to his young men: Tarry you here with the ass: I and the boy going with speed as far as yonder, after we have adored will return to you.
\v 6 He took also the wood of the holocaust, and laid it upon Isaac his son: and himself carried in his hand fire and a sword. And as they went on together,
\v 7 Isaac said to his father: My father. And he answered: What wilt thou son? Behold, saith he, fire & wood; where is the victim of the holocaust?
\v 8 And Abraham said: God will provide unto himself the victim of the holocaust, my son. They went on therefore together:
\v 9 and they came to the place which God had shewed him, wherein he builded an altar, and laid the wood in order upon it: and when he had bound Isaac his son, he laid him on the altar upon the pile of wood.
\v 10 And he stretched forth his hand, and caught the sword, for to sacrifice his son.
\v 11 And behold an Angel of our Lord from heaven cried, saying: Abraham, Abraham. Who answered: Here I am.
\v 12 And he said to him: Stretch not forth thy hand upon the boy, neither do thou any thing to him: now have I known that thou fearest God, and hast not spared thine only begotten son for my sake.
\v 13 Abraham lifted up his eyes, and saw behind his back a ram amongst the briers sticking fast by the horns, which he took & offered an holocaust instead of his son.
\v 14 And he called the name of that place, \f b \fr 22.14 \ft It is a grateful and religious thing, by naming of places to conserve the memory of God's benefits, that posterity may know them. \+it S. Chrisost. ho. 48. in Gen.\+it*\f* Our Lord seeth. Whereupon even to this day it is said, In the mountain our Lord will see.
\v 15 And the Angel of our Lord called Abraham the second time from heaven, saying:
\v 16 By my own self have I sworn, saith the Lord: because thou hast done this thing, and hast not spared thine only begotten son for my sake;
\v 17 I will bless thee, and I will multiply thy seed as the stars of heaven, and as the sand that is by the sea shore: thy seed shall possess the gates of his enemies,
\v 18 and IN \sc Thy Seed Shall be Blessed All The Nations of The Earth\sc*, because thou hast obeyed my voice.
\v 19 Abraham returned to his young men, and they went to Bersabee together, and he dwelt there.
\v 20 These things so being done, it was \f c \fr 22.20 \ft Nachor's progeny is here mentioned to shew whence Rebecca came, whom Isaac married.\f* told Abraham that Melcha also had born children to Nachor his brother,
\v 21 Hus the first-begotten, and Buz his brother, and Camuel the father of the Sirians,
\v 22 and Cased, and Azau, Pheldas also and Iedlaph,
\v 23 and Bathuel, of whom was born Rebecca: these eight did Melcha bear to Nachor Abraham's brother.
\v 24 And his concubine, named Roma, bare Tabee, and Gaham, and Tahas, and Maacha.
\c 23
\cd Sara dying Abraham solemnizeth her funeral: 4. buyeth a field with a double cave of Ephron, 15. for four hundred sicles. 19. and there buryeth her.
\p
\v 1 And Sara lived an hundred twenty seven years.
\v 2 And she died in the city of Arbee which is Hebron, in the land of Chanaan: And Abraham came to mourn and weep for her.
\v 3 And after that he was risen up from \f a \fr 23.3 \ft A clear example of religious Office in burying the dead.\f* the funeral obsequies, he spake to the children of Heth, saying:
\v 4 I am a stranger and pilgrim among you: give me the right of a sepulchre with you, that I may bury my dead.
\v 5 The children of Heth answered, saying:
\v 6 My lord hear us, thou art a prince of God among us: in our principal sepulchres bury thou thy dead: and no man can let thee but that in his own monument thou mayest bury thy dead.
\v 7 Abraham rose up and \f b \fr 23.7 \ft Adoration used for reverence done to men. \+it See also c. 27. v. 29. c. 33. v. 3. 7. and S. Aug. q. 61. in Gen.\+it*\f* adored the people of the land, to wit the children of Heth;
\v 8 and he said to them: If it please your soul that I bury my dead, hear me, and be intercessors for me to Ephron the son of Seor:
\v 9 that he give me the double cave, which he hath in the uttermost part of his field: for money to the worth therefore let him deliver it to me before you for possession of a sepulchre.
\v 10 And Ephron dwelt in the midst of the children of Heth. And Ephron made answer to Abraham in the hearing of all that went in at the gate of the city, saying:
\v 11 No, it shall not be so, my lord, but thou rather hearken to that which I do say: The field I deliver to thee, and the cave that is therein, in the presence of the children of my people, bury thy dead.
\v 12 Abraham adored before the people of the land.
\v 13 And he spoke to Ephron, his people standing round about: I beseech thee to hear me: I will give money for the field: take it, and so I will bury my dead in it.
\v 14 And Ephron answered:
\v 15 My lord hear me. The ground which thou desirest is worth four hundred sicles of silver: this is the price between me and thee: but how much is this? Bury thy dead.
\v 16 Which when Abraham had heard, he weighed the money that Ephron had asked, in the hearing of the children of Heth, four hundred sicles of silver of common current money.
\v 17 And the field that before time was Ephron's, wherein was the double cave, looking towards Mambre, as well it, as the cave, and all the trees thereof in all the limits thereof round about,
\v 18 was made sure to Abraham for a possession, in the sight of the children of Heth, and of all that went in at the gate of his city.
\v 19 And so Abraham buried Sara his wife in the double cave of the field, that looked towards Mambre, this is Hebron in the land of Chanaan.
\v 20 And the field was made sure to Abraham, and the cave that was in it, for a possession to bury in, of the Children of Heth.
\c 24
\cd Abraham's servant adjured and sent by him into Mesopotamia, to seek a wife for Isaac, 12. prayeth to God for a sign, findeth Rebecca, 34. and demanding her for this purpose, 50. with her parents, 58. and her own consent, she goeth with him, 67. is married to Isaac: who thereby is comforted for the death of his mother.
\p
\v 1 And Abraham was old, and of many days, and our Lord had blessed him in all things.
\v 2 And he said to the elder servant of his house, which was ruler over all that he had: Put thy hand under my thigh,
\v 3 that I may adjure thee by our Lord God of heaven and earth, that thou \f a \fr 24.3 \ft In choosing a wife a virtuous stock and family, especially true faith and religion, are before all other things to be considered and preferred. \+it S. Amb. li. 1. c. 9. de Abrah. S. Chrisost. ho. 48. in Gen.\+it*\f* take not a wife for my son of the daughters of the Chananites, among whom I dwell:
\v 4 but that thou go unto mine own country and kindred, and thence take a wife for my son Isaac.
\v 5 The servant answered: If the woman will not come with me into this land, whether must I bring thy son back again to the place, from whence thou didst come forth?
\v 6 And Abraham said: Beware thou never bring my son back again thither.
\v 7 Our Lord God of heaven, which took me out of my father's house, and out of my native country, which spake to me, and sware to me, saying: To thy seed will I give this land; he shall send his Angel before thee, and thou shall take from thence a wife for my son:
\v 8 but if the woman will not follow thee, thou shalt not be bound by the oath: only bring not my son thither again.
\v 9 The servant therefore put his hand under the thigh of Abraham his lord, and sware to him upon this word.
\v 10 And he took ten camels of his lord's herd, and departed, of all his goods carrying something with him, and setting forward went on into Mesopotamia to the city of Nachor.
\v 11 And when he had made the camels lie down without the town beside a well of water at even, at the time when women are wont to come forth to draw water, he said:
\v 12 O Lord God of my lord Abraham, meet me today, I beseech thee, and do mercifully with my master Abraham.
\v 13 Behold I stand nigh to the fountain of water and the daughters of the inhabiters of this city will come forth to draw water.
\v 14 Therefore the maid, to whom I shall say: Bow down thy tankard that I may drink; and she shall answer: Drink, yea to thy camels also will I give drink; she it is, whom thou hast provided for thy servant Isaac: and by this I shall understand, that thou hast dealt mercifully with my master. \ef - \fr 24.14 \fq The maid to whom I shall say. \ft Observation of speeches called ominous, which are interpreted to signify good or evil luck, ¹ are sometimes superstitious & suggested by evil spirits, who now & then telling or insinuating some truth, get credit, & so allure men to attend to such vain, uncertain, and unlawful signs, as S. Augustine testifieth (li. 2. de Gen. ad lit. c. 17. & li. 12. c. 22.) ² Nevertheless such signs are sometimes lawfully observed, & desired from God, as the same Doctor disputeth (li. quest. super Gen. q. 53) & S. Chrysostom teacheth more clearly (ho. 45. in Gen.) likewise S. Theodoret (q. 73. in Gen.) But whosoever will not err in particular cases, must follow either express Scripture, or the judgement of the Church ³ ⁴, which is always directed by the spirit of truth. And touching this prayer of Abraham's servant, & his desire of this determinate sign to know the maid, whom God had provided to be Isaac's wife, the fathers generally hold that it was religious, devout, and discreet ⁵. For he being careful of his Master's business, and not trusting his own judgement, but relying upon that Abraham had said, \+it Our Lord shall send his Angel before thee\+it*, commended so weighty a cause to God by prayer, the Angel suggesting both to him to desire, & to the maid to perform (as the event sheweth) such qualities and virtues in her, as were most agreeable to the great charity and hospitality daily practised in Abraham's house, most convenient and necessary (as he discreetly considered) for that family, and good of many. The like observations were approved in Gedeon ⁶, and Ionathas ⁷. And to pray for such signs in some case, or for manifest miracles is also approved by the Apostle's example praying God \+it To shew by lot which of the two he had chosen to the Apostleship, in place of Judas.\+it* ⁸ And that he would extend his hand to cures, and signs, and wonders, to be done by the name of his holy son \+sc Jesus\+sc*. \fq ¹ \ft Ominous speeches sometimes superstitious. \fq ² \ft Sometimes lawful. \fq ³ \ft Holy Scripture and the Church are judges of doubtful observations. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ioan. 14.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Eliezer's prayer, for a particular sign, was lawful, devout, and discreet. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it 1. Reg. 14.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Act. 1. & 4.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Iudic. 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 Neither had he yet ended these words within himself, & behold Rebecca came forth, the daughter of Bathuel, the son of Melcha, wife to Nachor the brother of Abraham, having a tankard on her shoulder;
\v 16 a passing comely maid, & most beautiful virgin, & not known to man: and she was gone down to the fountain, and had filled her tankard, and came back.
\v 17 And the servant ran to meet her, and said: Give me a little water to drink of thy tankard.
\v 18 Who answered: Drink my lord. And quickly she let down the tankard upon her arm, and gave him drink.
\v 19 And when he had drunk, she added: but for thy camels also I will draw water, till all do drink.
\v 20 And pouring out the tankard into the troughs, she ran back to the well to draw water: and being drawn gave it to all the camels.
\v 21 But he musing beheld her with silence, desirous to know whether our Lord had made his journey prosperous or not.
\v 22 And after that the camels had drunk, the man plucked forth golden earlets, weighing two sicles, and as many bracelets of ten sicles weight.
\v 23 And he said to her: Whose daughter art thou? Shew me; is there any place in thy father's house to lodge?
\v 24 Who answered: I am the daughter of Bathuel, the son of Melcha, whom she bare to Nachor.
\v 25 And she added, saying: Of straw also and hay we have good store, and a large place to lodge in.
\v 26 The man bowed himself, and adored our Lord,
\v 27 saying: blessed be the Lord God of my lord Abraham, that hath not taken away his mercy & truth from my lord, and hath brought me the straight way into the house of my lord's brother.
\v 28 The maid therefore ran, and reported into \f b \fr 24.28 \ft Her father having perhaps many wives & every one a several house, she went to her mother's house.\f* her mother's house all things that she had heard.
\v 29 And Rebecca had a brother named Laban, who in all haste went forth to the man, where the fountain was.
\v 30 And when he had seen the earlets and bracelets in his sister's hands, and hand heard all her words reporting: These words spake the man unto me; he came to the man which stood beside the camels, and nigh to the fountain of water:
\v 31 and said to him: Come in, thou blessed of our Lord: Why standest thou without? I have prepared the house, and a place for the camels.
\v 32 And he brought him into his lodging: and he unharnessed the camels, and gave straw and hay, and water to wash his feet, and of the men that were come with him.
\v 33 And bread was set before him. Who said: I will not eat till I speak my message. He answered him: Speak.
\v 34 And he said: I am the servant of Abraham:
\v 35 And our Lord hath blessed my lord wonderfully, and he is magnified: and he hath given him sheep, & oxen, silver and gold, men servants & women servants, camels, and asses.
\v 36 And Sara my lord's wife hath born my lord a son in her old age, and he hath given him all things that he had.
\v 37 And my lord adjured me saying: Thou shalt not take a wife for my son of the Chananites, in whose land I dwell:
\v 38 but thou shalt go to my father's house, and of mine own kindred shalt thou take a wife for my son:
\v 39 but I answered my lord: What if the woman will not come with me?
\v 40 Our Lord, saith he, in whose sight I walk, will send his Angel with thee, & will direct thy way: and thou shalt take a wife for my son of mine own kindred, and of my father's house.
\v 41 Thou shalt be innocent from my curse, when thou shalt come to my kin, and they will not give her thee.
\v 42 I came therefore today to the well of water, and said: O Lord God of my lord Abraham, if thou hast directed my way, wherein I now walk,
\v 43 behold I stand besides the well of water, and the virgin, that shall come forth to draw water, when she shall hear me say: Give me a little water to drink of thy tankard;
\v 44 and she shall say to me: Drink both thou and for thy camels I will draw also; that is the woman, which our Lord hath prepared for my master's son.
\v 45 And whilst I pondered these things secretly with myself, Rebecca appeared coming with a tankard, which she carried upon her shoulder: & she went down to the fountain, & drew water.
\v 46 And I said to her: Give me a little to drink. Who speedily let down the tankard from her shoulder, and said to me: Drink Both thou, and to thy camels I will give drink. I drank, and she watered the camels.
\v 47 And I asked her, and said: Whose daughter art thou? Who answered: I am the daughter of Bathuel, the son of Nachor, whom Melcha bare him. I hung therefore earlets to adorn her face, and I put bracelets upon her hands.
\v 48 And prostrate I adored our Lord, blessing the Lord God of my lord Abraham, who hath brought me the straight way to take the daughter of my lord's brother for his son.
\v 49 Wherefore if you do according to mercy and truth with my lord, shew me: but if it please you otherwise, that also tell me, that I may go to the right hand or to the left.
\v 50 And Laban and Bathuel answered: From our Lord the word hath proceeded: we cannot speak any other thing with thee besides his pleasure.
\v 51 Behold Rebecca is before thee, take her and go thy ways, & let her be the wife of thy lord's son, as our Lord hath spoken.
\v 52 Which when Abraham's servant heard, falling down he adored our Lord to the ground.
\v 53 And taking forth vessels of silver, and gold, and garments, gave them to Rebecca for a present. To her brothers also, and to her mother he offered gifts.
\v 54 A banquet was made, and eating and drinking together they lodged there. And in the morning the servant arose, and said: Dismiss me, that I may go to my lord.
\v 55 And her brother and mother answered: Let the maid tarry at the least ten days with us, & after she shall depart.
\v 56 Stay me not, said he, because our Lord hath directed my way: dismiss me that I may go on to my lord.
\v 57 And they said: Let us call the maid, and \f c \fr 24.57 \ft As children ought not to marry without their parent's good liking; so the parties' own consent is most necessary. S. Amb. Epist. 43.\f* ask her will.
\v 58 And being called, when she was come, they asked: Wilt thou go with this man? Who said: I will go.
\v 59 They dismissed her therefore, and her nurse, and Abraham's servant, and his company,
\v 60 wishing prosperity to their sister, and saying: Thou art our sister, increase thou into thousand thousands, and thy seed possess the gates of their enemies.
\v 61 Therefore Rebecca and her maids being set upon camels, followed the man: who with speed returned unto his lord; and
\v 62 the same time Isaac walked along the way, that leadeth to the well of the Living and the seeing, so called; for he dwelt in the south country:
\v 63 and he was gone forth to \f d \fr 24.63 \ft Suach signifieth to speak considerately with heart or mouth. Here S. Ambrose (li. 1. c. 1. de Isaac) and S. Aug. (q. 69. in Gen.) understand it of mental prayer.\f* meditate in the field, the day now being well spent: and when he head cast up his eyes, he saw camels coming a far off.
\v 64 Rebecca also, when she saw Isaac, lighted off the camel,
\v 65 and said to the servant: Who is that man which cometh towards us along the field? And he said to her: The same is my lord. But she quickly taking her cloak, covered herself.
\v 66 And the servant told Isaac all things that he had done.
\v 67 Who brought her into the tent of Sara his mother, & took her to wife: and he loved her so much, that it did moderate the sorrow which was chanced of his mother's death.
\c 25
\cd Abraham having many children by his wife Cetura, died at the age of 175. years: 12. Ismael also having twelve sons Dukes, died. 19. Isaac praying for his barren wife, she hath Esau and Jacob twins. 30. Esau selleth his first birth right to Jacob for a mess of potage.
\p
\v 1 And Abraham married another wife named Cetura:
\v 2 Which bare him Zamran, and Jecsan, and Madan, and Madian, and Jesboc, and Sue.
\v 3 Jecsan also begat Saba & Dadan. The Children of Dadan were Assurim, and Latusim, and Loomim.
\v 4 But also of Madian was born Epha, and Opher, and Henoch, and Abida, and Eldaa: all these were the children of Cetura.
\v 5 And Abraham gave all his possessions to Isaac:
\v 6 and to the children of his concubines he gave gifts, and separated them from Isaac his son, whilst himself yet lived, to the east country. \ef - \fr 25.6 \fq To the children of his concubines. \ft ¹ S. Augustin (li. 16. c. 34. de civit.) sheweth that both Agar and Cetura, being Abraham's lawful wives (for so they are called in holy Scripture) are also called concubines, because they had not like privileges to Sara, whose son was sole heir to his father, and the children of the others had only gifts (or moveable goods) not attaining to the promised Kingdom. And all this for mystery sake. For Ismael signified the carnal people before Christ, the children of Cetura prefigured Heretics ², who suppose themselves to pertain to the new Testament, but are separated no less than the Jews from Christ's Kingdom. And albeit there was also another particular reason why Agar was called concubine, because she was a second wife the first then living, yet this learned father saith, he did not see why Cetura, being married after the death of Sara, should be called concubine, but only for this Mystery. \fq ¹ \ft Why Agar and Cetura being lawful wives are called concubines. \fq ² \ft Their children signified Pagans and Heretics.\ef*
\v 7 And the days of Abraham's life were an hundred seventy and five years.
\v 8 And decaying died in \f a \fr 25.8 \ft The life of the just hath fullness of days though it be otherwise short; the days of the wicked are void of fruit, be they many or few. \+it S. Ambros. li. de Abraham.\+it*\f* a good old age, and having lived a great time, and being full of days and was gathered to his people.
\v 9 And there buried him Isaac and Ismael his sons in the double cave, which was situated in the field of Ephron the son of Seor the Hethite, over-against Mambre,
\v 10 which he had bought of the children of Heth: there was he buried, and Sara his wife.
\v 11 And after his death God blessed Isaac his son, who dwelled beside the well of the Living & Seeing, so named.
\v 12 These are the generations of Ismael the son of Abraham, whom Agar the Ægyptian bare him, Sara's servant: &
\v 13 These are the names of his children according to their calling and generations. The first begotten of Ishmael Nabajoth, then Cedar, and Adbeel, and Mabsam,
\v 14 Masma also, and Duma, and Massa,
\v 15 Hadar, and Thema, and Iethur, and Naphis, and Cedma.
\v 16 These are the sons of Ismael: and these are their names by their castles and towns, twelve Princes of their tribes.
\v 17 And the years of Ismael's life came to an hundred thirty seven, and decaying died, and was put unto his people.
\v 18 And he dwelt from Hevila even to Sur, which looketh towards Ægypt, as they enter to the Assyrians, before the face of all his brethren died he.
\v 19 These also are the generations of Isaac the son of Abraham: Abraham begat Isaac:
\v 20 Who when he was forty years old, took to wife Rebecca the daughter of Bathuel the Syrian of Mesopotamia, sister to Laban.
\v 21 And Isaac besought our Lord for his wife, because she was barren: who heard him, and made Rebecca to conceive. \ef - \fr 25.21 \fq Heard him. \ft Notwithstanding God's assured promise, that Isaac should have issue (Gen. 21. v. 12.) yet he prayeth instantly for the same. And Moyses here attributeth Rebecca's conceiving to Isaac's prayer. ¹ Whereby we see that God's foreseeing, predestinating, and promising, exclude not, but indeed include secondary causes, and ordinary means, by which his eternal will and pleasure is fulfilled. For as God did foresee that Rebecca should have children, so he did foresee that Isaac should pray for it, and obtain it; and the one was as sure to come to pass as the other. And the same consequence is true concerning eternal life as S. Gregory teacheth li. 1. c. 8. Dialog. \fq ¹ \ft God's predestination & foreseeing include, & not exclude the means by which his will is done.\ef*
\v 22 But the little ones struggled in her womb; who said: If it should be so with me, what need was there to conceive? And she \f b \fr 25.22 \ft S. Augustin (q. 72. in Gen.) disputeth, but could not decide, whether Rebecca went to some Priest, or Prophet, or Altar, or whither else, or only retired to private prayer.\f* went to consult our Lord.
\v 23 Who answering said: Two nations are in thy womb, and two peoples shall be divided out of thy womb, and one people shall overcome the other, and the elder shall serve the younger. \ef - \fr 25.23 \fq The elder shall serve the younger. \ft ¹ As before (c. 17. v. 21. and c. 21. v. 12.) the covenant and great promises made to Abraham's seed, are declared to pertain only to Isaac, and not to Ismael, nor to the other brothers; so the same belong not to Esau the elder, but only to Jacob the younger son of Isaac, the Holy Ghost saying, \+it The elder shall serve the younger.\+it* And withal signifieth (saith S. Augustin) ² that the elder people of the Jews shall serve the younger Christian people. For although it may be understood literally to be fulfilled, in that the Idumeans coming of Esau were subdued by King David coming of Jacob ³; yet it is more conveniently believed, that this prophecy tended to a greater thing. And what is this, but that which is evidently fulfilled in the Jews and Christians? \fq ¹ \ft The covenant made to Abraham pertained only to Isaac and Jacob, not to the rest of his issue. \fq ² \ft \+it li. 16. c. 35. civit.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it 2. Reg. 8. Psal. 59.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 25.23 \ft Another great document of grace S. Paul gathereth upon this Mystery: that the twins being not yet born, nor having done any thing good or evil, without any good merits the younger is elected, the elder reprobate ¹ ². For doubtless (saith S. Augustin) touching original sin they were both equal, and concerning proper sin, neither of them had any at all. By which example he sheweth God's mere mercy in the elect, and justice in the reprobate, as is more largely noted in the English New Testament, upon the ninth chapter to the Romans. \fq ¹ \ft God's mere mercy in electing any, his justice to the reprobate \fq ² \ft \+it Rom. 9.\+it*\ef*
\v 24 Now her time was come to be delivered, and behold twins were found in her womb.
\v 25 He that came forth first, was red, and all hairy in manner of a skin: and his name was called Esau. Immediately the other coming forth, held his brother's plant in his hand: and therefore he called him Jacob.
\v 26 Threescore years old was Isaac, when the little ones were born unto him.
\v 27 Who being grown up, Esau became a man cunning in hunting, & a husband man: but Jacob \f c \fr 25.27 \ft Holy Scripture premonisheth Jacob's sincerity, lest in the Mysteries following he might be suspected of false dealing. S. Aug. li. 16. c. 37. civit.\f* a plain man dwelled in tents:
\v 28 Isaac loved Esau, because he did eat of his hunting: and Rebecca loved Jacob.
\v 29 & Jacob boiled broth: to whom Esau being come faint out of the field,
\v 30 said: Give me of this red broth, because I am exceeding faint. For which cause his name was called Edom.
\v 31 To whom Jacob said: sell me thy first-birth-right. \ef - \fr 25.31 \fq Sell me thy first-birth right. \ft Jacob instructed by his mother, that God had chosen him in place of his brother Esau (for to her God had revealed that the elder should serve the younger) ¹ did lawfully use this opportunity to get Esau's grant of the right pertaining to the first-born, but Esau in selling it sinned, shewing himself an intemperate profane man. Heb. 12. \fq ¹ \ft Jacob lawfully bought, but Esau sinned in selling the first-birth-right.\ef*
\v 32 He answered, Lo I die, what will the first-birth-right avail me?
\v 33 Jacob said: Swear therefore to me. Esau sware to him, and sold his first-birth-right.
\v 34 And so taking bread and the rice broth, did eat, and drink, and went his way; little esteeming that he had sold his first-birth-right.
\c 26
\cd Isaac by reason of famine goeth into Gerara, 3. where God reneweth to him the promises made to Abraham. 9. King Abimelech blameth him for calling his wife his sister. 15. The people envying his wealth, quarreleth for his wells. 26. At last Abimelech maketh league with Isaac.
\p
\v 1 And when a famine was risen in the land, after that sterility that had chanced in the days of Abraham, Isaac went to Abimelech king of the Palestines into Gerara.
\v 2 And our Lord appeared to him, and said: \f a \fr 26.2 \ft God by Abraham's exemplar life invited the Ægyptians to true religion: now commandeth Isaac to stay in Gerara to the like end. \+it S. Theod. q. 76. in Gen.\+it*\f* Go not down into Ægypt, but rest in the land which I shall tell thee.
\v 3 And sojourn in it, and I will be with thee, and will bless thee: for to thee and to thy seed I will give all these countries, accomplishing the oath which I sware to Abraham thy father.
\v 4 And I will multiply thy seed as the stars of heaven: and I will give to thy posterity all these countries: and in thy seed \sc Shall be Blessed All The Nations of The Earth\sc*,
\v 5 for because Abraham obeyed my voice, & kept my precepts and commandments, and observed my ceremonies and laws. \ef - \fr 26.5 \fq My ceremonies. \ft ¹ These were not the same ceremonies and laws which were afterwards prescribed by God, and delivered by Moyses, but other observances by which Abraham and other holy Patriarchs before him, served God with certain external worship, differing from the rites of the Gentiles, especially from Enos' time (Gen. 4.) and so forward. \fq ¹ \ft External ceremonies in the law of nature.\ef*
\v 6 Therefore Isaac abode in Gerara.
\v 7 Who when he was asked by the men of that place concerning his wife, answered: She is \f b \fr 26.7 \ft See pag. 50.\f* my sister; for he was afraid to confess that she was married to him, thinking lest peradventure they would kill him because of her beauty.
\v 8 And when very many days were passed, & he abode there, Abimelech the king of the Palestines looking forth through a window saw him sporting with Rebecca his wife.
\v 9 And calling for him, he said: it is evident that she is thy wife: why didst thou feign her to be thy sister? He answered: I feared lest I should die for her.
\v 10 And Abimelech said: Why hast thou deceived us? Some man of the people might have lien with thy wife, and thou hadst brought upon us \f c \fr 26.10 \ft Adultery a great sin also among Paynims.\f* a great sin. And he commanded all the people, saying:
\v 11 He that shall touch this man's wife, dying shall die.
\v 12 And Isaac sowed in that land, and he found that same year an hundred fold: and our Lord blessed him. \ef - \fr 26.12 \fq An hundred fold. \ft For this increase of wealth the King and people at first envied Isaac, but afterwards perceiving that God Almighty, whom he served, so blessed him, the rest of the land remaining barren, they sought to make league with him (v. 28.) ¹ Even so the Kings and nations of the world, first envying and persecuting Christ's Church, at length seeing it still prosperous, became with all humility children of the same Church, and servants of Christ, being overcome not by force of arms but by patience, & peaceable endeavours of those whom they most hated. Whereof excellently saith S. Leo (Ser. 1. in Natali Apost.) Although Rome renowned by many victories, dilated her Empire by land and by sea, yet was it less, that martial travail subdued, than that which Christian peace hath obtained. The Bishops of Rome having larger Jurisdiction spiritual, than ever the Roman Cæsars had temporal Dominion. \fq ¹ \ft Christian fortitude prevaileth more by suffering, than by forcible resisting.\ef*
\v 13 And the man was made rich, and he went prospering and increasing, till he was made exceeding great:
\v 14 and he had also possessions of sheep and of herds, and a very great family. For this the Palestines envying him,
\v 15 stopped at that time all the wells, that the servants of his father Abraham had digged, filling them up with earth:
\v 16 in so much that Abimelech himself said to Isaac: Depart from us, because thou art become mightier than we a great deal.
\v 17 And departing, to come to the Torrent of Gerara, and to dwell there,
\v 18 again he digged other wells, which the servants of his father Abraham had digged, and which after his death the Philistines had stopped up of old: & he called them by the same names which his father before had called them.
\v 19 And they digged in the \f 1 \fr 26.19 \ft * The channel where sometimes a vehement stream runneth, sometimes none at all.\f* Torrent, and found living water:
\v 20 but there also the pastors of Gerara made a brawl against the pastors of Isaac, saying: it is our water. For which cause he called the name of the well, by occasion of that which had happened, \f 1 \fr 26.20 \ft \+it Wrangling.\+it*\f* Calumny.
\v 21 And they digged also another; and for that they brawled likewise, and he called the name of it, Enmity.
\v 22 Going forward from thence he digged another well, for which they contended not: therefore he called the name thereof, Latitude, saying: Now hath our Lord dilated us, and made us to increase upon the earth.
\v 23 And he went up from that place unto Bersabee,
\v 24 Where our Lord appeared to him that same night, saying: I am the God of Abraham thy father, do not fear, because I am with thee: I will bless thee, and multiply thy seed for my servant Abraham's sake.
\v 25 Therefore he builded there an altar: and having called upon the name of our Lord, he pitched his tent: & commanded his servants that they should dig a well.
\v 26 To the which place when there were come from Gerara Abimelech, and Ocozath his friend, & Phicol chief Captain of his soldiers,
\v 27 Isaac spake to them: Why are ye come to me a man whom you hated, and have thrust out from you?
\v 28 Who answered: We saw that the Lord is with thee, and therefore we said: Let there be an oath between us, and \f d \fr 26.28 \ft So nations of the world first envied the Church of Christ, but after made peace with it.\f* let us make a league,
\v 29 that thou do us no harm, as we also have touched nothing of thine, neither have we done that which might hurt thee: but with peace have we dismissed thee increased with the blessing of the Lord.
\v 30 Therefore he made them a feast, and after they had eaten and drunken,
\v 31 A rising in the morning, they sware one to another: and Isaac dismissed them peaceably into their place.
\v 32 And behold the same day came the servants of Isaac telling him of a well, which they had digged, and saying: We have found water.
\v 33 Whereupon he called it Abundance: and the name of the city was given Bersabee, even unto this present day.
\v 34 But Esau being forty years old married wives, Judith the daughter of Beeri the Hethite, and Basemath the daughter of Elon of the same place:
\v 35 Both which had \f e \fr 26.35 \ft Esau by marrying against his parents' will, made breach from them.\f* offended the mind of Isaac and Rebecca.
\c 27
\cd Jacob by his mother's counsel getteth his father's blessing in place of Esau. 42. And by her is advised (for avoiding Esau's wrath, who threatened to kill him) to fly to his uncle Laban, in Haran of Mesopotamia.
\p
\v 1 And \f 1 \fr 27.1 \ft The Epistle on Saturday the second week in Lent.\f* Isaac was old, and his eyes were dim, and he could not see: and he called Esau his elder son, and said to him: My son? Who answered: Here I am.
\v 2 To whom his father: Thou seest, quoth he, that I am old, and know not the day of my death.
\v 3 Take thy instruments, thy quiver, and bow, and go abroad: and when thou hast taken any thing by hunting,
\v 4 make me broth thereof, as thou knowest I like, and bring that I may eat, and my soul may bless thee before I die.
\v 5 Which when Rebecca had heard, and he was gone into the field to fulfill his father's commandment,
\v 6 she said to her son Jacob: I heard thy father talking with Esau thy brother, and saying to him:
\v 7 Bring me of thy hunting, and make me meats that I may eat, and bless thee in the sight of our Lord before I die.
\v 8 Now therefore, my son, assent to my counsel:
\v 9 and go thy way to the flock, bring me two kids of the best, that I may make of them meat for thy father, such as he gladly eateth:
\v 10 Which when thou hast brought in, and he hath eaten, he may bless thee before he die.
\v 11 To whom he answered: Thou knowest that Esau my brother is an hairy man, and I am smooth:
\v 12 if my father shall feel me, and perceive it, \f a \fr 27.12 \ft Jacob secure in conscience that the right of first-birth belonged to him, yet feared to give occasion of offence to his father.\f* I fear lest he will think I would have deluded him, and I shall bring upon me a curse for a blessing.
\v 13 To whom his mother said: This curse, my son, light upon me, only hear thou my voice, and go, fetch me the things which I have said.
\v 14 He went, and brought, and gave them to his mother. She dressed meats, even as she knew his father liked.
\v 15 And she did on him the garments of Esau very good, which she had at home with her:
\v 16 and the little skins of the kids she put about his hands, and covered the bare of his neck.
\v 17 And she gave him the broth, and delivered him bread that she had baked.
\v 18 Which when he had carried in, he said: My father? But he answered: I hear. Who art thou my son?
\v 19 And Jacob said: I am thy first begotten Esau: I have done as thou didst command me: arise, sit, and eat of my hunting, that thy soul may bless me. \ef - \fr 27.19 \fq I am thy first-begotten Esau. \ft Jacob was not by nature the first-begotten, but by God's ordinance, & by covenant made with Esau ¹, had right to the preeminence and privileges belonging to the first-born. So he did not lie, but spake a truth, meaning that he was that son, to whom by divine election the first-birth-right was due, which his father supposed to pertain to Esau. But because some scorners of Christian doctrine (like to the old Manichees) use to say, that Catholic Doctors and Schoolmen excuse and condemn whom they list by such glosses, let such reprovers understand, that both modern & ancient Catholic writers avow this defence of the holy Patriarch Jacob, not by private spirit, but by the most true and proper sense of holy Scripture it-self. Where it may appear, if they will examine the text ², that Jacob in all this procurement of his father's blessing, neither did any thing unjustly nor said any thing falsely. ³ First, it was revealed to his mother (chap. 25. v. 23.) \+it That the elder\+it* (of her twins) \+it should serve the younger.\+it* ⁴ Secondly, holy Scripture testifieth in the same chapter (v. 27.) \+it That Jacob was a plain\+it* (or sincere) \+it Man,\+it* void of unjust dealing. ⁵ Thirdly, for more quiet enjoying that right, which God had ordained for him, he procured his brother's consent and confirmation (v. 33.) ⁶ Fourthly, though he was secure in conscience that the blessing was due to him, yet he feared (v. 12.) lest he might give occasion of offence to his father, to whom this mystery was not yet revealed. ⁷ Fifthly, Isaac perceiving at last God's will, that Jacob should be preferred, was neither offended with him, nor revoked his blessing as unlawfully surprised, but condescending thereto, ratified that he had done, saying (v. 33.) \+it I have blessed him, and he shall be blessed.\+it* ⁸ Sixthly, God himself from this time forwards often appeared to Jacob, and with great promises and many temporal and spiritual benefits, declared his singular love to him. ⁹ Seventhly, these three Patriarchs, Abraham, Isaac, & Jacob, are special renowned Saints of the old Testament: yea the Lord and Creator of all would peculiarly be called (Exodi. 3.) the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. Moyses praying instantly for God's mercy and clemency towards the people (Exodi. 32.) besought him to remember Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, his servants: and so in both old and new Testament these three are often mentioned as chief Princes in the Kingdom of Heaven. All which shew the great virtues and holiness of them all. \fq ¹ \ft Jacob did not lie, nor sin in saying he was Esau &c. \fq ² \ft He is proved innocent by the text. \fq ³ \ft 1. \fq ⁴ \ft 2. \fq ⁵ \ft 3. \fq ⁶ \ft 4. \fq ⁷ \ft 5. \fq ⁸ \ft 6. \fq ⁹ \ft 7.\ef* \ef - \fr 27.19 \ft ¹ And touching this fact of Jacob, where (if ever any-where) might seem to be some great sin, S. Augustin at large proveth that he did not herein sin at all: \+it That which Jacob did\+it* (saith he li. cont. mendacium c. 10.) \+it by his mother's instruction to deceive his father, if it be diligently considered, was no lie, but a mystery, and therefore for the familiar counsel of the Holy Ghost, which his mother had received, he is excused from sin\+it*. The same he confirmeth q. 74. in Gen. 1. 16. c. 32. de civit. & l. 22. c. 34 cont. Faust. The same also teach S. Chris. ho. 51. in Gen. S. Hiero. Epist. 125. S. Theodoret. q. 79. &. 80. in Gen. S. Gregory, ho. 6. in Ezechielem, S. Bede, Isidorus, Innocentius 3. Rupertus, & others upon this place, all agreeing absolutely that every lie is a sin ², declare that Jacob lied not, but still spake the truth, confirming their exposition by other like places of Scripture. As when our Saviour said of S. John Baptist (Matt. 11.) \+it He is Elias,\+it* meaning that he was Elias in spirit not in person. So Jacob said truly that he was Esau, not meaning in person but in right of the first-born, by God's ordinance: Esau also having condescended thereto by covenant and oath. In that also he deceived his father, was no sin. For it was a lawful and good deceit ³, such a one (saith S. Chrysostom ⁴ ) as Hieremie speaketh of, ⁵ \+it Lord thou hast deceived men, and I am deceived,\+it* so Isaac was deceived not as we commonly call deceit, but to his own and other's good, by God's disposition. \fq ¹ \ft The Fathers prove his innocency in this fact. \fq ² \ft Every lie is a sin. \fq ³ \ft Some deceit good. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ho. 6. ad Col.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Hier. 20.\+it*\ef*
\v 20 And again Isaac to his son: How couldest thou, said he, find it so quickly, my son? Who answered: \f b \fr 27.20 \ft It was truly God's will, but not in that sense as Isaac understood it.\f* It was the will of God that that which I would came quickly in my way.
\v 21 And Isaac said: Come hither, that I may feel thee my son, and may prove whether thou be my son Esau, or not.
\v 22 He came near to his father, and when he had felt him, Isaac said: The voice verily, is the voice of Jacob: but the hands are the hands of Esau.
\v 23 And he knew him not, because his hairy hands had made him like unto the elder. Blessing him therefore, \ef - \fr 27.23 \fq Knew him not. \ft S. Damasus demanding of S. Hierom ¹, what might be the reason why God would suffer his holy servant Isaac not to know Jacob, but to be deceived, and through ignorance to bless whom he would not, declareth that it happened not only to Jacob, but also to many other like holy men, to be ignorant of many things, and to be deceived in error of opinion: ² and that this error was profitable to Isaac and his house. For if he had given this blessing (which was a spiritual Jurisdiction) to Esau, as he purposed, he had committed a noxious error indeed, by preferring a bloody man, one that was ready, if he could, to have killed his brother (v. 41.) omitting him that was sincere, and very virtuous, and had done his own will, not God's will therein. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Epist. 125.\+it* \fq ² \ft It was good that Isaac knew not Jacob when he blessed him.\ef* \ef - \fr 27.23 \ft But why would not God reveal his will to Isaac (as he had commanded a far greater thing to Abraham, to sacrifice the same Isaac) that he might wittingly have blessed Jacob by God's commandment? The Fathers do probably allege this for one reason, that if Esau ¹, being a fierce and cruel man, had perceived that his father had willingly preferred Jacob, he would have been incensed against his father, conceived and attempted evil against him. Another reason S. Chrysostom ² and Theodoret do yield, that by this strange manner of imparting this blessing, ³ it might more manifestly appear to be God's will and ordinance, and not to proceed from man's affection, that Jacob should be preferred. \fq ¹ \ft Good in respect of Esau. \fq ² \ft \+it Ho. 53. q. 79. in Gen.\+it* \fq ³ \ft More to God's glory and Jacob's commendation.\ef*
\v 24 he said: Art thou my son Esau? He answered: I am.
\v 25 But he said: Bring me the meats of thy hunting, my son, that my soul may bless thee. Which when they were brought and he had eaten, he offered him wine also, which after he had drunk,
\v 26 he said to him: Come near me, and give me a kiss, my son.
\v 27 He came near, and kissed him. And immediately as he felt the fragrant savour of his garments, blessing him, he said: Behold the savour of my son is as the savour of a plentiful field, which our Lord hath blessed.
\v 28 God give thee of the dew of heaven, & of the fatness of the earth abundance of corn and wine.
\v 29 And let peoples serve thee, and tribes adore thee: be thou lord of thy brethren, and thy mother's children bow they before thee. He that shall curse thee, be he cursed: and he that shall bless thee, be he replenished with blessings.
\v 30 Isaac had scarce ended his words, and Jacob now gone forth abroad, but Esau came,
\v 31 and brought in to his father meats made of his hunting, saying: Arise my father, and eat of thy son's hunting; that thy soul may bless me.
\v 32 And Isaac said to him: Why! Who art thou? Who answered: I am thy first begotten son Esau.
\v 33 Isaac was amazed and astonished exceedingly, and marveling more than a man can believe, said: Who is he then that even now brought me venison that he had taken, and I did eat of all things before thou camest, and I have blessed him, \f c \fr 27.33 \ft Isaac now knowing it to be God's will, ratified that he had done.\f* and he shall be blessed.
\v 34 Esau having heard his father's words, roared out with a great cry; and being dismayed, said: Bless me also, my father.
\v 35 Who said: Thy brother came deceitfully and took thy blessing.
\v 36 But he said again: Rightly is his name called Jacob; for he hath supplanted me lo the second time: my first-birth-right he took before, and now the second time he hath stolen my blessing. And again to his father he said: Hast thou not reserved me also a blessing?
\v 37 Isaac answered: I have appointed him thy lord, and all his brethren I have made subject to his service: with corn and wine I have established him, and for thee, my son, what shall I do more after this?
\v 38 To whom Esau said: Hast thou one only blessing, father? I beseech thee bless me also. And when he wept that he howled again,
\v 39 Isaac being moved, said to him: In \f d \fr 27.39 \ft worldlings' blessing consisteth in transitory wealth.\f* the fat of the earth, and in the dew of heaven from above,
\v 40 shall thy blessing be. Thou shalt live by the sword, and shalt serve thy brother: and \f e \fr 27.40 \ft The Idumeans being subdued by King David (2. Reg. 8.) revolted from King Joram, & had a King of their own (4. Reg. 8.) they were again subdued by Hircanus (teste Iosepho. li. 13. Antiq.) but again Herod an Idumean reigned in Jewry Math. 2. Luc. 1.\f* the time shall come, when thou shalt shake off and loose his yoke from thy neck.
\v 41 Esau therefore always hated Jacob for the blessing wherewith his father had blessed him; and he said in his heart: The days will come of the mourning of my father, and I will kill Jacob my brother.
\v 42 These things were told to Rebecca: who sending and calling Jacob her son, said to him: Behold Esau thy brother threateneth to kill thee.
\v 43 Now therefore, my son, hear my voice, and get thee up and fly to Laban my brother into Haran:
\v 44 and thou shalt dwell with him a few days, till the fury of thy brother be assuaged,
\v 45 and his indignation cease, and he forget those things which thou hast done to him: afterward I will send, and bring thee from thence hither. Why shall I be deprived of both sons in one day?
\v 46 And Rebecca said to Isaac: I am weary of my life for the daughters of Heth: if Jacob take a wife of the stock of this land, I list not live.
\c 28
\cd Jacob with his father's blessing, and admonition not to take a wife of Chanaan, but of the daughters of his uncle Laban, goeth into Mesopotamia. 6. Esau in the mean time marrieth a third wife, his uncle Ismael's daughter. 11. Jacob seeth in sleep a ladder reaching to heaven, Angels ascending and descending, and our Lord leaning thereon renewed the promises made to Abraham and Isaac. 16. And he awaking maketh a vow.
\p
\v 1 Isaac therefore called Jacob, and blessed him, and commanded him saying: Take not a wife of the stock of Chanaan;
\v 2 but go and make a journey into Mesopotamia of Syria; to the house of Bathuel thy mother's father, and take thee a wife thence of the daughters of Laban thine uncle.
\v 3 And God Almighty bless thee, and make thee increase, and multiply thee: that thou mayest be into multitudes of peoples.
\v 4 And \f a \fr 28.4 \ft Isaac again confirmeth the blessings of Abraham to Jacob and his seed, omitting Esau; yea and God repeateth the same v. 13.\f* give he thee the blessings of Abraham, and to thy seed after thee: that thou mayest possess the land of thy peregrination, which he promised to thy grandfather.
\v 5 And when Isaac had dismissed him, taking his journey he came to Mesopotamia of Syria to Laban, the son of Bathuel the Syrian, brother to Rebecca his mother.
\v 6 And Esau seeing that his father had blessed Jacob, and had sent him into Mesopotamia of Syria, to marry a wife thence; and that after the blessing he had commanded him, saying: Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Chanaan;
\v 7 and that Jacob obeying his parents was gone into Syria:
\v 8 having trial also that his father did not willingly see the daughters of Chanaan,
\v 9 he went to Ismael, and took to wife besides them which he had before, Maheleth the daughter of Ismael Abraham's son, sister to Nabajoth.
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 28.10 \ft The Epistle in a votive Mass for travellers.\f* Therefore Jacob being departed from Bersabee, went on to Haran.
\v 11 And when he was come to a certain place, and would rest in it after sun set, he took one of the stones that lay there, and putting it under his head, slept in the same place. \ef - \fr 28.11 \fq Took of the stones. \ft ¹ Jacob travelling into a strange country went in such poor estate, the better to hide his departure from Esau, who otherwise might have killed him by the way. It was also thus disposed by God, that Jacob's faith and confidence might, to his greater merit, be exercised; and that God's providence might more manifestly appear, as it did in his return saying (Gen. 32:) With my staff I passed over this Jordan, and now with two troops I do return. \fq ¹ \ft Why Jacob travelled in poor estate.\ef*
\v 12 \x - \xt Sap. 10.\x* And he saw in his sleep a ladder standing upon the earth, & the top thereof touching heaven: the Angels also of God ascending & descending by it, \ef - \fr 28.12 \fq A ladder. \ft He that was in temporal distress was marvelously comforted spiritually ¹, by seeing a ladder that reached from the earth to heaven; Angels passing up and down the same, and the Son of God leaning upon it, as he that reigneth both in heaven and earth, who in particular promised him and his seed that whole land, that he and his seed should be blessed, yea that in \+it His Seed\+it* all nations should be blessed ², and that he would keep and protect him where-soever he went. How all this was performed is briefly rehearsed in the book of wisdom chap. 10. \fq ¹ \ft A notable example of God's comfort to the afflicted. \fq ² \ft All nations believing in Christ are blessed in him.\ef*
\v 13 and our Lord leaning upon the ladder saying to him: I am the Lord God of Abraham thy father, & the God of Isaac: the Land wherein thou sleepest, I will give to thee and to thy seed.
\v 14 And thy seed shall be as the dust of the earth: thou shalt be dilated to the West, and to the East, and to the North, and to the South: and IN \sc Thee And Thy Seed All The Tribes of The Earth Shall be Blessed\sc*.
\v 15 And Iwill be thy keeper whither soever thou goest, and will bring thee back into this land: neither will I leave thee, till I shall have accomplished all things which I have said.
\v 16 And when Jacob was awaked out of sleep, he said: Indeed our Lord is in this place, and I wist not.
\v 17 And trembling he said: How terrible is this place! This is none other but the house of God, and the gate of heaven.
\v 18 And Jacob arising in the morning took the stone, which he had laid under his head, and erected it for a title, pouring oil upon the top. \ef - \fr 28.18 \fq Erected it, pouring oil. \ft To erect a stone, and pour oil upon it, was nowise superstitious in Jacob. Neither did he learn it of Idolaters: for he abhorred and detested all idolatrical observances. But as S. Iustinus Martyr S. Clement of Alexandria, Origen, Eusebius, and others testify, idolatrical superstition did rather imitate true religious ¹ ceremonies. For the devil always affecting that honour which he seeth done to God, persuaded those whom he seduced and blinded with errors, to serve him in such manner of external rites, as God was served ², that thereby he might either have like worship with God, as it happened among Paynim Idolaters: or else deprive God of this kind of honour, as now we see Protestants reject and pull down consecrated Altars pretending them to be superstitious. Wherein they shew most gross ignorance, if indeed they so judge of ignorance, and not of mere malice. For who is so simple, but he may see that the chief ³ difference between Religion and Superstition in external things, consisteth in the persons to whom they are done, and in the intention of the doers, and by the same difference of person's civil honours is also distinguished, from both religious & superstitious. As he that kneeleth to God, religiously honoureth God. Kneeling to the sun; moon, or other false-Gods, superstitiously honoureth the devil, and kneeling to the King civilly honoureth the King. Jacob without doubt did all to God's only honour. And that which he did in this place, is now used in the Catholic Church. For so Rabunus, a diligent observer and writer of Ecclesiastical Rites, Ceremonies, and Custom, touching the use of holy oil witnesseth (li. 1. c. 45. Institut. cleric.) that the Altar being first sprinkled with water, is anointed with Chrism, to the example of the Patriarch Jacob, who after that dreadful vision, erected a stone for a title (or monument) pouring oil thereon, and calling that place \+it The house of God\+it* S. Cyprian also writing of Chrism mentioneth the two sorts of holy oil used in the Church ⁴; one of simple oil consecrated by a Bishop, which is used for Catechumens before Baptism, persons possessed, and the sick; the other is made of oil and balm, also consecrated by a Bishop, and this is used in Baptism, Confirmation, and in consecrating Altars, Kings, and Priests. \fq ¹ \ft Erecting and anointing of Altars is a religious office being done to God's honour. \fq ² \ft The Church learneth not rites of Idolaters, but they of the Church. \fq ³ \ft Difference in religious, and civil honour, consisteth in the persons, & intentions. \fq ⁴ \ft Two sorts of holy oil.\ef*
\v 19 And he called the name of the city \f 1 \fr 28.19 \ft \+it House of God.\+it*\f* Bethel, which before was called Luza.
\v 20 And he vowed a vow, saying: If God shall be with me, and shall keep me in the way, by the which I walk, and shall give me \f b \fr 28.20 \ft To whom enough is not enough, to him nothing is enough. Aulus Gell.\f* bread to eat, and raiment to put on, \ef - \fr 28.20 \fq Vowed. \ft It cannot be understood that Jacob here vowed or promised only to serve God, as the Sovereign Lord of all creatures, for to that he was bound, whether he should prosper temporally or no; but that he vowed particular godly works, ¹ to which he was not otherwise obliged. As here he expresseth two things. Presupposing before all, that the Lord Omnipotent shall be his God, he addeth, First, \+it And this stone, which I have erected for a title, shall be called the house of God.\+it* Whereby he promised the building of a Church, performed at his return (chap. 35.) Secondly, he added, \+it And of all things which thou shalt give me I will offer tithes to thee\+it*. And this Likewise was of free devotion. ² For tithes also in the law of nature were due to Priests, and by inferior Priests to the chief Priest, as Abraham gave tithes to Melchisedech. And so all his tithes were due to his father, and after his father himself was chief: yet he promised them to God, that is, to offer them in Sacrifice, and bestow them in other uses pertaining to God's service. \fq ¹ \ft Vows are properly of things which are not otherwise commanded. \fq ² \ft \+it Gen. 14.\+it*\ef*
\v 21 and I shall be returned prosperously to my father's house, the Lord shall be my God,
\v 22 and this stone, which I have erected for a title, shall be called the House of God: and of all things that thou shalt give to me, I will offer tithes to thee.
\c 29
\cd Jacob entertained by Laban, 15. serveth him seven years for Rachel, 23. but first receiving Lia, 27. seven days after receiveth also Rachel, and serveth for her seven years more. 31. She remaining barren, Lia beareth four sons.
\p
\v 1 Jacob therefore going on his journey, came into the East country.
\v 2 And he saw a well in the field, and three flocks of sheep lying beside it: for of it the beasts were watered, and the mouth thereof was closed with a great stone.
\v 3 And the manner was when all the sheep were gathered together they did roll off the stone, and after the sheep were refreshed they put it on the mouth of the well again.
\v 4 And he said to the shepherds: Brethren, whence are you? Who answered: Of Haran.
\v 5 And he asked them, saying: Know you Laban the son of Nachor? They said: We do know him.
\v 6 Is he in health? Quoth he: He is in health, say they: And behold Rachel his daughter cometh with his flock.
\v 7 And Jacob said: There is yet much day remaining, neither is it time to bring the flocks into the folds again: first give the sheep drink, and so bring them back to feed.
\v 8 Who answered: we cannot, till all the cattle be gathered together: and we remove the stone from the well's mouth, that we may water the flocks.
\v 9 They were yet speaking, and behold Rachel came with her father's sheep: for she fed the flock.
\v 10 Whom when Jacob had seen, & knew her to be his cousin-german, & that they were the sheep of Laban his uncle, he removed the stone, wherewith the well was closed.
\v 11 And having watered the flock, he \f a \fr 29.11 \ft S. Augustine (q. 87. in Gen.) commendeth familiar kissing of kinsfolk and friends as a laudable custom in some countries. It is no-where more civil & modest than in England.\f* kissed her: and lifting up his voice wept,
\v 12 and he told her that he was her father's brother, and the son of Rebecca: but she in haste went & told her father.
\v 13 Who when he heard that Jacob his sister's son was come, he ran forth to meet him, & embracing him & heartily kissing him, brought him into his house. And when he had heard the causes of his journey,
\v 14 he answered: Thou art my bone and my flesh. And after the days of one month were expired,
\v 15 he said to him: because thou art my brother, shalt thou serve me \f 1 \fr 29.15 \ft \+it Without wages?\+it*\f* gratis? Tell me, what wages wilt thou take?
\v 16 He had indeed two daughters, the name of the elder was Lia; & the younger was called Rachel.
\v 17 But Lia was blear-eyed, Rachel well favoured, & of a beautiful countenance.
\v 18 Whom Jacob loving, said: I will serve thee for Rachel thy younger daughter seven years.
\v 19 Laban answered: It is better that I give her to thee than to another man, tarry with me.
\v 20 Jacob therefore served for Rachel seven years: and they seemed a few days because of the greatness of his love.
\v 21 And he said to Laban: give me my wife: because now the time is complete, that I may company with her.
\v 22 Who having bid a great number of his friends to the feast, made the marriage.
\v 23 And at night \f b \fr 29.23 \ft Laban grievously offended, neither could Lia be excused, but Jacob was innocent in this fact.\f* he brought in Lia his daughter to him,
\v 24 giving his daughter a handmaid, named Zelpha. With whom when Jacob had companied after the manner, when morning was come he saw Lia:
\v 25 and he said to his father in law: What is it that thou didst mean to do? Did not I serve thee for Rachel? Why hast thou deceived me?
\v 26 Laban answered: It is not the custom in this place, that we bestow the younger in marriage first.
\v 27 Make up the \f c \fr 29.27 \ft After seven days he had Rachel who was his first spouse. \+it S. Hierom. Tradit. Heb. S. Aug. q. 89. in Gen.\+it*\f* week of days of this match: and I will give thee this same also, for the work that thou shalt serve me other seven years.
\v 28 He yielded to his pleasure; and after the week was past, he married Rachel to wife:
\v 29 to whom her father had delivered Bala for to be her servant.
\v 30 And having at length obtained the marriage that he wished, he preferred the love of the later before the former, serving with him other seven years.
\v 31 And our Lord seeing that he despised Lia, opened her womb, her sister remaining barren.
\v 32 Who conceived and bare a son, and called his name Ruben, saying: Our Lord saw mine affliction: now my husband will love me.
\v 33 And again she conceived and bare a son, and said: For because our Lord heard that I was contemned, he hath given this also to me: and she called his name Simeon.
\v 34 And she conceived the third time, and bare another son: and said: Now also my husband will be joined to me, for because I have born him three sons: and therefore she called his name, Levi.
\v 35 The fourth time she conceived and bare a son, and said: Now will I confess to our Lord. And for this she called him Juda: And she left bearing.
\c 30
\cd Rachel yet barren delivereth her handmaid to Jacob, who beareth two sons. 9. Lia ceasing to bear giveth her handmaid also, and she beareth two more. 17 Then Lia beareth other two sons, and one daughter. 22. Rachel beareth Joseph. 25. Jacob desirous to return home, is hired to stay for a certain part of the stock's increase. 43. Whereby he becometh exceeding rich.
\p
\v 1 And Rachel seeing she was unfruitful, \f a \fr 30.1 \ft Not properly envy, but grief & lawful emulation. \+it S.\+it* Aug. li. 22. c. 54. cont. Faust.\f* envied her sister, and said to her husband: Give me children, otherwise I shall die.
\v 2 With whom Jacob being angry answered: Am I as God, who hath deprived thee of the fruit of thy womb?
\v 3 But she said: I have here my servant Bala: Company with her, that she may bear upon my knees, and I may have children of her.
\v 4 And she gave him Bala unto \f b \fr 30.4 \ft Of plurality of wives see pag. 60.\f* marriage: who,
\v 5 When her husband had companied with her, conceived and bare a son.
\v 6 And Rachel said: Our Lord hath judged for me and hath heard my voice, giving me a son, and therefore she called his name, Dan.
\v 7 And again Bala conceiving bare another,
\v 8 for whom Rachel said: God hath compared me with my sister, and I have prevailed: and she called him Nephthali.
\v 9 Lia perceiving that she had left bearing, delivered Zelpha her handmaid to her husband.
\v 10 Who conceiving and bringing forth a son,
\v 11 she said: Happily. And therefore called his name Gad.
\v 12 Zelpha also bare another.
\v 13 And Lia said: This is for my blessedness: for women will call me blessed. Therefore she called him Aser.
\v 14 And Ruben, going forth in the time of wheat harvest into the field, found mandragoras: which he brought to his mother Lia. And Rachel said: Give me part of thy son's \f c \fr 30.14 \ft Holy Scripture (saith S. Augustine) would never have mentioned such womanly desires, but to admonish us to seek great mysteries therein. li. 22. c. 56. cont. Faustum.\f* mandragoras.
\v 15 She answered: Dost thou think it a small matter that thou hast taken my husband from me, unless thou take also my son's mandragoras? Rachel said: For thy son's mandragoras let him sleep with thee this night.
\v 16 And when Jacob returned at even from the field, Lia went out to meet him, and said: Company with me, because with wages I have hired thee for my son's mandragoras. And he slept with her that night.
\v 17 And God heard her prayers: and she conceived and bare the fifth son,
\v 18 And said: God hath given me a reward, because I gave my handmaid to my husband. And she called his name Issachar.
\v 19 Again Lia conceiving, bare the sixth son,
\v 20 & said: God hath endowed me with a good dowry: this turn also my husband will be with me, for because I have born him six sons, and therefore she called his name, Zabulon.
\v 21 After whom she bare a daughter named Dina.
\v 22 Our Lord also remembering Rachel, heard her, and opened her womb.
\v 23 Who conceived and bare a son, saying: God hath taken away my reproach.
\v 24 And she called his name, Joseph, saying: Our Lord add to me another son.
\v 25 And when Joseph was born, Jacob said to his father in law: Dismiss me that I may return into my country, and to my land.
\v 26 Give me my wives, and my children, for whom I have served thee, that I may depart: thou knowest the service that I have served thee.
\v 27 Laban said to him: Let me find grace in thy sight: I have learned by experience that God hath blessed me for thy sake:
\v 28 appoint thee wages which I shall give thee.
\v 29 But he answered: Thou knowest how I have served thee, and how great thy possession hath been in my hands.
\v 30 Thou hadest a small thing before I came to thee, and now thou art made rich: and our Lord hath blessed thee at my coming in. It is reason therefore that once I provide also for mine own house.
\v 31 And Laban said: What shall I give thee? But he said: I will nothing: but if thou wilt do that which I demand, I will feed and keep thy sheep again.
\v 32 Go round about all the flocks, and separate all the sheep of diverse colours, of speckled fleece: and what-soever shall be russet and spotted, and of diverse colours, as well in the sheep as in the goats, shall be my wages.
\v 33 And my justice shall answer for me tomorrow before thee, when the time of the bargain shall come: and all that shall not be of diverse colours, and spotted, and russet, as well in the sheep as in the goats, shall accuse me of theft.
\v 34 And Laban said: I like well that thou demandest.
\v 35 And he separated the same day the she goats, and the sheep, and the he goats, and the rams of diverse colours, and spotted: and all the flock of one colour, that is of white and black fleece, he delivered in the hand of his sons.
\v 36 And he put a space of three days' journey betwixt him and his son in law, who fed the rest of his flock.
\v 37 Jacob therefore \f d \fr 30.37 \ft Jacob did justly use this means to recover that which Laban withheld from him, being due for the dowry of his wives, and recompence for his service. \+it Rupert. l. 7. c. 39. in Gen.\+it*\f* taking green rods of the poplar, and of the almond, and of the plane-trees, in part pilled them: and when the barks were taken off, in the parts that were pilled there appeared whiteness: but the parts that were whole remained green: and by this means the colour was made diverse.
\v 38 And he put them in the troughs, where the water was poured out: that when the flocks should come to drink, they might have the rods before their eyes, and in the sight of them conceive.
\v 39 And it came to pass that in the very heat of the ramming, the sheep beheld the rods, and brought forth spotted, and of diverse colours, and speckled.
\v 40 And Jacob divided the flock, and put the rods in the troughs before the eyes of the rams: and all the white and the black were Laban's, and the rest, Jacob's, when the flocks were separated one from the other.
\v 41 Therefore when the ewes went to ram, in the prime time Jacob put the rods in the troughs of water before the eyes of the rams, and of the ewes, that in looking upon them they might conceive:
\v 42 but when the later coming was, and the last conceiving, he did not put them. And those that were lateward became Laban's: and they of the prime time, Jacob's.
\v 43 And the man was enriched beyond measure, and he had many flock, women servants and men servants, camels and asses.
\c 31
\cd Jacob by God's commandment parteth secretly with all he hath towards his father. 21. Laban pursueth him. 26. expostulating why he went in secret manner, 30. especially chargeth him with stealing his gods. 31. Jacob excuseth himself, not knowing that Rachel had taken away the Idols. 34. And she deludeth his diligent searching for them. 36. Then Jacob expostulateth with Laban for this unkindness. 43. Finally, they make a league and depart each to his own country.
\p
\v 1 After he heard the words of Laban's sons saying: Jacob hath taken all that was our father's, and being enriched of his substance, is become great:
\v 2 and perceiving also Laban's countenance, that it was not towards him as yesterday and the other day,
\v 3 especially our Lord saying to him: Return into the land of thy fathers, and to thy kindred, and I will be with thee;
\v 4 he sent, and called Rachel and Lia into the field where he fed the flocks,
\v 5 and said to them: I see your father's countenance that it is not towards me as yesterday and the other day: and the God of my father hath been with me.
\v 6 And yourselves know that I have served your father to the uttermost of my power.
\v 7 Yea your father also hath circumvented me, and hath changed my wages ten times: and yet God hath not suffered him to hurt me.
\v 8 If at any time he said: They of diverse colours shall be thy wages; all the sheep brought forth young of diverse colours. But when he said contrary: Thou shalt take all the white ones for thy wages; all the flocks brought forth white ones.
\v 9 And God hath taken your father's substance, and given it to me.
\v 10 For after the time came of the ewes conceiving, I lifted up mine eyes, and saw in my sleep the males ascending upon the females of diverse colours, and the spotted, and the speckled.
\v 11 And the Angel of God said to me in sleep: Jacob? And I answered: Here I am.
\v 12 Who said: Lift up thine eyes, and see all the males ascending upon the females, them of diverse colours, the spotted and the speckled. For I have seen all things that Laban hath done to thee.
\v 13 I am the God of Bethel, where thou didst \f e \fr 31.13 \ft Anointing of Altars, and free vows are grateful offices to God. See chap. 28.\f* anoint the stone, and didst vow the vow unto me. Now therefore arise, and go out of this land, returning into the land of thy nativity.
\v 14 And Rachel and Lia answered: Have we any thing left in the goods and heritage of our father's house?
\v 15 Hath he not reputed us as strangers, and sold us, and eaten up the price of us?
\v 16 But God hath taken our father's riches, and delivered them to us, and to our children: wherefore do all things that God hath commanded thee.
\v 17 Jacob therefore rose up, and setting his children and wives upon camels, went his way.
\v 18 And he took all his substance, and flocks, and whatsoever he had gotten in Mesopotamia, and went forward to Isaac his father into the land of Chanaan.
\v 19 At that time Laban was gone to shear his sheep, and Rachel stole the \f 1 \fr 31.19 \ft \+it Teraphim.\+it*\f* idols of her father. \ef - \fr 31.19 \fq Idols. \ft ¹ Images of false gods (as there were) are most properly called idols. And so the Hebrew word \+it Teraphim\+it* is here rightly translated idols: which in other places signifieth other things. As the statua which Michol put in David's bed ², covering the head thereof with a hairy goat's skin, to deceive Saul's servants who sought David's death, is called \+it Teraphim\+it*, and may there be translated a statua, image, or similitude, but not an idol ³. Again, Osee the Prophet foretelling the lamentable state of the Israelites ⁴, saith, they shall be long without King, Prince, Sacrifice, Altar, Ephod, and \+it Teraphim\+it* ⁵, which last word in the Protestants' English Bibles remaineth untranslated. Where if they had translated \+it Images\+it* (as here they do) it would prove, that some images pertain to true religion, the want thereof is lamented among other principal things. \fq ¹ \ft Images of false gods are idols. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Reg. 19.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Some images are neither religious nor superstitious. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Osee. 3.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Some are religious.\ef* \ef - \fr 31.19 \ft ¹ These idols Rachel stole from her father, to withdraw him from idolatry, as S. Basil (in li. proverb.) S. Gregory Nazianzen (orat. de Paschate) and Theodoret (q. 89. in Gen.) expound it. And in this, saith Theodoret, she was a right figure of the Catholic Church, which depriveth idolaters of their idols. It is probable also by her base using of them, that she held them not for gods, when she put them under the camel's litter, and sat upon them. Finally, that she reserved them, and did not cast them away, nor burn, nor bury them, argueth that they were perhaps of precious metal, or other matter, which she might turn to profit: ² and that lawfully in part of recompence, that she and her sister had no other dowry, but rather were sold to Jacob. Who also had suffered much injury at their father's hands. \fq ¹ \ft Rachel took away her father's Idols for his good. \fq ² \ft She kept them in recompence of wrongs.\ef*
\v 20 And Jacob would not confess to his father in law that he fled.
\v 21 And when he was gone as well himself as all things that were his right, & having passed the river was marching on to the Mount Galaad,
\v 22 it was told Laban the third day that Jacob fled.
\v 23 Who, taking his brethren unto him, pursued him seven days: and he overtook him in the Mount Galaad.
\v 24 And he saw in his sleep God saying unto him: Take heed thou speak not roughly any thing against Jacob.
\v 25 And Jacob had now pitched his tent in the mountain: and when he with his brethren had overtaken him, he pitched his tent in the same Mount Galaad.
\v 26 And he said to Jacob: Why didst thou so, that unwitting to me thou wouldst carry away my daughters as captives with the sword?
\v 27 Why wouldst thou fly without my knowledge, and not tell me, that I might have brought thee on the way with joy, and songs, and timbrels, and citterns?
\v 28 Thou hast not suffered me to kiss my sons and daughters: thou hast done foolishly: now also indeed,
\v 29 my hand is able to requite thee evil: but the God of your father said unto me yesterday: Take heed thou speak not any thing against Jacob roughly.
\v 30 Suppose thou didst desire to go to thy friends, and hadst a longing to thy father's house, why didst thou steal my gods?
\v 31 Jacob answered: In that I departed unwitting to thee, I feared lest thou wouldst take away thy daughters by force.
\v 32 But whereas thou chargest me with theft; with whom soever thou shalt find thy gods, let him be slain before our brethren: search whatsoever of thy things thou shalt find with me, and take away. Saying this, he knew not that Rachel had stolen the idols.
\v 33 Laban therefore having gone into the tent of Jacob, and of Lia, and of both the handmaids, found them not. And when he was entered into Rachel's tent,
\v 34 she in haste hid the idols under the camel's litter, and sat thereupon: and when he had sought all the tent, and found nothing,
\v 35 she said: Let not my lord be angry that I cannot rise up before thee, because according to the custom of women it is now chanced to me. So his carefulness in seeking was deluded.
\v 36 And Jacob being \f f \fr 31.36 \ft Jacob in this just expostulation was angry and sinned not. Psal. 4.\f* angry said in chiding manner: For what fault of mine, and for what offence of my part hast thou so chased after me,
\v 37 and searched all my household-stuff? What hast thou found of all the substance of thy house? Lay it here before my brethren, and thy brethren, and let them judge between me and thee.
\v 38 Have I therefore been with thee twenty years? Thy ewes and goats were not barren, the wethers of thy flock I did not eat:
\v 39 neither that which the beast had caught did I shew to thee, I made good all the damage: whatsoever perished by theft thou didst exact it of me:
\v 40 day and night was I parched with heat, and with frost, & sleep did fly from mine eyes.
\v 41 And in this sort have I served thee in thy house twenty years, fourteen for thy daughters, and six for thy flocks: thou hast changed also my wages ten times.
\v 42 Unless the God of my father Abraham, and the fear of Isaac had holpen me, peradventure now thou hadst sent me away naked: God beheld my affliction and the labour of my hands, and rebuked thee yesterday.
\v 43 Laban answered him: The daughters are mine and the children, and thy flocks, and all things that thou seest are mine: what can I do to my daughters, and nephews?
\v 44 Come therefore, let us enter in league: that it may be for a testimony between me and thee.
\v 45 Jacob therefore took a stone, and erected it for a title:
\v 46 and he said to his brethren: Bring hither stones. Who gathering them together made a heap, and they did eat upon it:
\v 47 Which Laban called the witness-heap: and Jacob called the hillock of testimony, either of men according to the property of his language.
\v 48 And Laban said: This heap shall be a witness between me and thee this day, and therefore the name thereof was called Galaad, that is, The witness-heap.
\v 49 Our Lord behold and judge us when we shall be departed one from the other,
\v 50 if thou shalt afflict my daughters, and if thou bring in other wives over them; none is witness of our talk but God, who is present and beholdeth.
\v 51 And he said again to Jacob: Behold this heap, and the stone which I have erected between me and thee,
\v 52 shall be a witness: this heap, I say, and the stone, be they for a testimony, if either I shall pass beyond it going towards thee, or thou shalt pass beyond it, thinking harm to me.
\v 53 The God of Abraham, and the God of Nachor judge between us, the God of their father, Jacob therefore sware by the fear of his father Isaac:
\v 54 and after he had offered victims in the mountain, he called his brethren to eat bread. Who when they had eaten, lodged there.
\v 55 but Laban arising in the night, kissed his sons, and daughters, and blessed them: and returned unto his place.
\c 32
\cd Angels meet Jacob by the way. 3. He sendeth messengers and gifts to pacify his brother Esau. 24. Wrestling with an Angel is not overcome, in fine the Angel benumbeth his thigh, blesseth him, and foretelleth that he shall be called Israel.
\p
\v 1 Jacob also went on his journey that he had begun: and the Angels of God met him.
\v 2 Whom when he had seen, he said: These are the Camps of God, and he called the name of that place Mahanaim, that is, Camps.
\v 3 And he sent also messengers before him to Esau his brother into the land of Seir, into the country of Edom:
\v 4 and he commanded them, saying: Thus speak ye unto my lord Esau: This saith thy brother Jacob: I have sojourned, and have been with Laban until this present day.
\v 5 I have oxen, & asses, & sheep, & men servants: & women servants: & now I send a legacy to my lord, that I may find grace in thy sight.
\v 6 And the messengers returned to Jacob, saying: We came to Esau thy brother, & behold he cometh with speed to meet thee with four hundred men.
\v 7 Jacob feared exceedingly: and being sore afraid divided the people that was with him, the flocks also and the sheep, and the oxen, and the camels, into two troops, \ef - \fr 32.7 \fq Feared exceedingly. \ft ¹ ² Justly may we marvel, why Jacob, so often assured by God's promises, confirmed by his many blessings, protected in all former dangers, accompanied the night before with armies of Angels, endued also with all virtues, and namely with perfect charity (which expelleth fear) was for all this so vehemently afraid! S. Augustin answereth ³, that he neither distrusted in God, nor did any unlawful thing: but did his own endeavour wisely and confidently, lest by presuming or despairing he should rather have tempted God than trusted in him. ⁴ The causes of his fear were in respect of himself and his brother. For considering God's former promises, benefits, & protections, were not to be presumed as absolute signs of his perpetual love, but conditional, if himself persevered sincerely in God's service; and seeing \+it The just man knoweth not whether he be worthy of love or of hatred\+it* ⁵ ⁶, he might doubt, lest by his twenty years conversation among Infidels in Mesopotamia, he had contracted some sins, for which God might suffer him to fall into calamity and affliction. And though he was in deed still more and more virtuous, and consequently in God's more favour and protection; yea so much the more, by how much less he presumed of his own good state and merits: yet by the vehement apprehending of his brother's inclination to revenge ⁷, the greatness of the occasion by procuring the first-birth-right and his father's blessing from him, the news of his speedy coming towards him with four hundred men, the natural situation of the place, where Esau might easily environ him, and (as he humbly though) his own unworthiness, he was possessed with natural fear (such as happeneth to constant men) and was sore afflicted for the tender care of his family. But reflecting upon God's goodness, he prudently disposed of his people and stocks, and besought God to protect him and his, by prayer qualified with requisite conditions, to wit, with ⁸ humility, not asking for his own but for \+it Abraham and Isaac's\+it* sake, and for God's own promise, acknowledging himself to be \+it less than God's mercies\+it* towards him, with ⁹ gratitude recounting great benefits received, saying, \+it With my staff I passed over this Jordan, and now with two troops I do return\+it*, with ¹⁰ confidence in that God had said he \+it Would dilate his seed as the sand of the sea\+it*, and with ¹¹ meekness in \+it sending gifts and good words\+it* to Esau. Thus finally he pacified him, and so his own fear was turned into joy. \fq ¹ \ft Jacob's fear was just, and without fault. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Ioan. 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it q. 102. in Gen.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft The causes of his fear. \fq ⁵ \ft The humble conceit of himself. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Eccl. 9.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Esau's inclination & means to revenge. \fq ⁸ \ft Jacob's prayer qualified with Humility, \fq ⁹ \ft Gratitude, \fq ¹⁰ \ft Confidence, \fq ¹¹ \ft Meekness.\ef*
\v 8 saying: If Esau come to one troop, and strike it, the other troop that remaineth shall be saved.
\v 9 And Jacob said: O God of my father Abraham, and God of my father Isaac; O Lord that didst say to me: Return into thy land, and into the place of thy nativity, and I will do thee good:
\v 10 I am inferior to all thy mercies, and thy truth that thou hast fulfilled to thy servant. With my staff I passed over this Jordan: and now with two troops I do return.
\v 11 Deliver me from the hand of my brother Esau, because I am sore afraid of him: lest perhaps he come, and strike the mother with the children.
\v 12 Thou didst say that thou wouldst do good to me, and dilate my seed as the sand of the sea, which for multitude cannot be numbered.
\v 13 And when he had slept there that night, he separated of those things which he had, gifts to his brother Esau,
\v 14 she goats two hundred, he goats twenty, ewes two hundred, and rams twenty,
\v 15 thirty milch camels with their colts, forty kine, and twenty bulls, twenty she asses, and their foals ten.
\v 16 And he sent by the hands of his servants every flock by it-self, and he said to his servants: Go before me, and let there be a space between flock and flock.
\v 17 And he commanded the former, saying: If thou meet my brother Esau, and he ask thee, whose art thou? Or whither goest thou? Or whose are these that thou doest follow?
\v 18 Thou shalt answer: Jacob's thy servant, he hath sent them for gifts to my lord Esau: himself also cometh after us.
\v 19 In like manner he gave commandments to the second, and the third, and to all that followed the flocks, saying: With the self-same words speak ye to Esau, when you shall find him.
\v 20 And ye shall add: Jacob also thy servant himself followeth on after us; for he said: I will pacify him with the gifts that go before, and afterward I will see him, perhaps he will be gracious unto me.
\v 21 The gifts therefore went before him, but himself lodged that night in the camp.
\v 22 And when he was risen early, he took his two wives, and his handmaids as many, with his eleven sons, and passed over the ford Iaboc.
\v 23 And when he had set over all things that appertained to him,
\v 24 he tarried alone: and behold a man wrestled with him till morning. \ef - \fr 32.24 \fq A man wrestled. \ft ¹ This wrestling with an Angel assumpting a body in form of a man was corporal, as the effect shewed in Jacob's sinew shrunk up, which made him to halt. v. 25. & 31. ² It was also spiritual, as appeareth by his earnest prayer, urging and at last obtaining the Angel's blessing. S. Lyonis. C. 4. cel. Hierer. S. Greg. prefat. in Job. Theodoret q. 91. in Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Jacob wrestled with an Angel corporally & spiritually. \fq ² \ft \+it Osee 12.\+it*\ef*
\v 25 Who when he saw that he could not overcome him, he touched the sinew of his thigh, and forthwith it shrank.
\v 26 And he said to him: Let me go for it is break of day. He answered: I will not let thee go, unless thou bless me.
\v 27 He therefore said: What is thy name? He answered: Jacob.
\v 28 But he, no, thy name, quoth he, \f a \fr 32.28 \ft The changing of his name here promised, is performed chap. 35. S. Hierom Tradit. Heb.\f* shall not be called Jacob. But Israel: for if thou hast been strong against God, how much more shalt thou prevail against men?
\v 29 Jacob asked him: Tell me by what name art thou called? He answered: Why dost thou ask my name? and blessed him in the same place.
\v 30 And Jacob called the name of the place Phanuel, saying: I have seen God face to face, and my soul was made safe.
\v 31 And immediately the sun rose to him, after that he was past Phanuel; but he halted on his foot.
\v 32 For which cause the children of Israel eat not the sinew, that shrunk in Jacob's thigh, unto this present day: because he touched the sinew of his thigh, and it shrunk.
\c 33
\cd Jacob seeing Esau come with a great troop of men, feareth harm, but is most courteously entertained by him. 10. He hardly persuaded Esau to take gifts, 13. and to return home. 17. So Jacob coming by Socoth to Salem, there buyeth a field, pitcheth his tents, and erecteth an Altar.
\p
\v 1 And Jacob lifting up his eyes, saw Esau coming, and with him four hundred men: and he divided the children of Lia, and of Rachel, and of the two handmaids:
\v 2 and he put both the handmaids and their children foremost: and Lia and her children in the second place: and Rachel and Joseph last.
\v 3 And himself going forward adored prostrate to the ground seven times, until his brother came near.
\v 4 Esau therefore running to meet his brother, embraced him: and clasping him fast about the neck, and kissing him wept.
\v 5 And casting up his eyes he saw the women and their little ones, and said: What mean these? And do they pertain to thee? He answered: They are the little ones which God hath given to me thy servant.
\v 6 And the handmaids and their children coming near bowed themselves.
\v 7 Lia also with her children came near: and when they had adored in like manner, last Joseph and Rachel adored.
\v 8 And Esau said: What are the troops that I did meet? He answered: That I might find grace before my lord.
\v 9 But he said: I have plenty, my brother, be thy things to thyself,
\v 10 And Jacob said: Do not so, I beseech thee, but if I have found grace in thine eyes, take a little present at my hands: for so have I seen thy face as if I should have seen \f a \fr 33.10 \ft Jacob seeing God's hand in this change of his brother's mind, not of flattery, but sincerely acknowledged his benignity, as God's countenance towards him.\f* the countenance of God: be gracious to me,
\v 11 and take the blessing which I have brought thee, and which God hath given me, who giveth all things. Scarce at his brother's great instance taking it,
\v 12 he said: let us march on together, and I will accompany thee in thy journey.
\v 13 And Jacob said: My lord thou knowest that I have with me little ones, and sheep, and kine with young: which if I cause to overlabour themselves in going, in one day all the flocks will die.
\v 14 It may please my lord to go before his servant: and I will follow softly after him, as I shall see my little ones to be able, until I come to my lord in Seir.
\v 15 Esau answered: I beseech thee, that of my people at the leastwise, which is with me, there may remain some to accompany thee in the way. It is not needful, said he, this only I have need of, that I may find grace (my lord) in thy sight.
\v 16 Esau therefore returned that day the same way that he came, into Seir.
\v 17 And Jacob cometh into Socoth: where having built a house, and pitched his tents, he called the name of that place Socoth, that is, Tabernacles.
\v 18 and he passed into Salem a city of the Sichimites, which is in the land of Chanaan, after he returned from Mesopotamia of Syria: and he dwelt beside the town.
\v 19 And he bought that part of the field, wherein he had pitched his tents, of the children of Hemor, the father of Sichem, for an hundred lambs.
\v 20 And erecting an altar there, on it he called upon the most Mighty God of Israel.
\c 34
\cd For ravishing Dina the Sichimites (being first circumcised) are slain by Simeon & Levi her brothers. 27. The rest of Jacob's sons spoil the city. 30. Jacob blameth them, fearing harm may come by this fact.
\p
\v 1 And \f 1 \fr 34.1 \ft O Dina (saith S. Bernard) what need was there to see women of a strange country! \+it Tract. de gradib. humilitatis.\+it*\f* Dina the daughter of Lia went forth to see the women of that country.
\v 2 Whom when Sichem had seen, the son of Hemor the Hevite, the Prince of that land, he was in love with her: and he took her away, and lay with her, by force ravishing the virgin.
\v 3 And his soul was fast knit unto her, and whereas she was sad, he comforted her with sweet words.
\v 4 And going to Hemor his father, he said: Take me this wench to be my wife.
\v 5 Which when Jacob had heard, his sons being absent and occupied in feeding of the cattle, he held his peace till they returned.
\v 6 And when Hemor, Sichem's father was come forth to speak unto Jacob,
\v 7 behold his sons came out of the field; and hearing what had passed, they were passing wrath, because he had done a foul thing in Israel, and committed an unlawful fact in ravishing Jacob's daughter.
\v 8 Hemor therefore spake to them: The soul of my son Sichem is fastened to your daughter: Give her unto him to wife:
\v 9 and let us contract marriages one with another: give us your daughters, and take you our daughters.
\v 10 And dwell with us: the land is at your commandment, till, occupy, and possess it.
\v 11 Yea and Sichem also said to her father and to her brethren: Let me find grace in your sight: and what soever you shall appoint I will give:
\v 12 raise the dowry, and require gifts, and I shall gladly give what you shall demand: only give me this wench to wife.
\v 13 Jacob's sons answered Sichem & his father \f a \fr 34.13 \ft They offended by falsely pretending religion, and by excess in revenge, & therefore are reproved by their father. v. 30. & cap. 49. v. 5. Otherwise their zeal was just to punish so foul a fault \+it Judith. 9.\+it*\f* in guile, being wrath for the deflowering of their sister:
\v 14 We cannot do that which you demand, nor give our sister to an uncircumcised person; which with us is an unlawful & abominable thing.
\v 15 But in this order we may be considerate, if you will be like to us, and all the man sex among you be circumcised:
\v 16 then will we give and take mutually your daughters, and ours: and we will dwell with you, and will be one people:
\v 17 but if you will not be circumcised, we will take our daughter, and depart.
\v 18 The offer pleased Hemor and Sichem his son:
\v 19 neither did the young man make delay, but forthwith fulfilled that which was demanded: for he loved the wench exceedingly, and he was the greatest man in all his father's house.
\v 20 And going into the gate of the city, they spake to the people:
\v 21 These men are men of peace, and are willing to dwell with us: let them occupy in the land, and till it, which being large and wide doth lack men to till it: their daughters we shall take to wife, and ours we will give to them.
\v 22 One thing there is for the which so great a good is deferred: If we circumcise our men sex, following the rite of the nation.
\v 23 And their substance, and cattle, and all things that they possess, shall be ours: only in this let us condescend, and dwelling together we shall make one people.
\v 24 And they all assented, and circumcised all the man sex.
\v 25 And behold the third day, when the grief of the wounds is most painful: Jacob's two sons Simeon and Levi, the brothers of Dina, taking their swords, entered into the city boldly: and killing all the man sex,
\v 26 murdered withal Hemor and Sichem, taking away Dina their sister out of Sichem's house.
\v 27 When they were gone forth, the other sons of Jacob ran in upon them that were slain, and spoiled the city in revenge of the rape.
\v 28 And wasting all things that were in their houses and fields, their sheep and herds, and asses,
\v 29 their little ones also, and their wives they led away captive.
\v 30 Which things when they had boldly achieved, Jacob said to Simeon and Levi: You have troubled me, and made me odious to the Chananites, and Pherezites, the inhabiters of this land. We are few: they being gathered together will strike me; and I and my house shall be destroyed.
\v 31 They answered: What? Should they abuse our sister as a strumpet?
\c 35
\cd Jacob purging his whole family of idols, goeth by God's commandment into Bethel, 7. There buildeth an Altar. 8. Debora dieth. 9. God appearing again to Jacob blesseth him, and changeth his name into Israel 16. Rachel bearing Benjamin dieth, and is buried in Bethleem, 22. Ruben lieth with Bala. 23. Israel's twelve sons are recited. 28. Isaac dieth at the age of 180. years: and his sons Esau and Jacob bury him.
\p
\v 1 In the mean time God spake to Jacob: Arise, and go up to Bethel, and dwell there, and make an altar to God that appeared to thee when thou didst fly from Esau thy brother.
\v 2 And Jacob having called together all his house, said: Cast away the strange gods that are among you, and be cleansed and change your garments. \ef - \fr 35.2 \fq Cast away the strange gods. \ft ¹ Jacob preparing to perform his vow of building a house and altar to God, first extirpateth all Idolatry from amongst his people; and then by Sacrifice appeaseth God's wrath, provoked howsoever, and specially by Simeon and Levi killing the Sichemites. Duly considering that what people or person desireth God's protection and blessings, must first be pure in Religion, and cleansed from sins: \+it Quia ei nulla nocebit adversitas, si nulla es dominetur iniquitas\+it*: because no adversity shall hurt him, if no iniquity have dominion over him. orat. ser. 6. post cineres. \fq ¹ \ft Cleansing from sin is the first office of the servants of God.\ef*
\v 3 Arise, and let us go up into Bethel, that we may make there an altar unto God: who herd me in the day of my tribulation, and accompanied me in my journey.
\v 4 They gave to him therefore all the strange gods that they had, and the earlets which were in their ears: but he buried them under the \f 1 \fr 35.4 \ft \+it An execrable tree.\+it*\f* terebinth that is behind the city of Sichem.
\v 5 And when they were departed, \f a \fr 35.5 \ft God (when it pleaseth him) maketh the weak stronger than the mighty, and few more terrible than many. \+it S. Chrisost. ho. 59. S. Aug. q. 112. in Gen.\+it*\f* the terror of God invaded all the cities round about, and they durst not pursue them going away.
\v 6 And Jacob came to Luza, which is in the land of Chanaan, surnamed Bethel: he and all the people that was with him.
\v 7 And he builded there an altar, and called the name of that place, The house of God: for there God appeared to him when he fled from his brother.
\v 8 The same time died Debora the nurse of Rebecca, and was buried at the foot of Bethel under an oak: and the name of that place was called The oak of weeping.
\v 9 And God appeared again to Jacob after he returned from Mesopotamia of Syria, and he blessed him,
\v 10 saying: Thou \f b \fr 35.10 \ft The name of supplanter not sufficiently expressing his valor he is also called Israel. See the Annotation.\f* shalt not be called any more Jacob, but Israel shall be thy name. And he called him Israel, \ef - \fr 35.10 \fq Called him Israel. \ft As the Patriarch now performeth his vow to God, so God fulfilleth his promise, giving him a new name. ¹ For \+it Jacob a supplanter\+it*, signifying too small force and praise for such a champion, God therefore honoured him with the name of \+it Israel\+it*. That is, \+it One that seeth and contemplateth God\+it*, as most ancient writers expound it. Also \+it A prince, or valiant with God\+it*, as S. Hierom sheweth Tradit. Heb. For \+it Isra\+it* in Hebrew signifieth \+it To domineer, or rule over\+it*, and \+it El\+it* signifieth God. And so this name given to him testifieth that he, by God's gift and grace, was valiant even against an Angel, representing God, much more against men, and other adversaries. Others interpret it, \+it The right one of God\+it*, as witnesseth the same S. Hierome both here, and in his commentaries in 44. Isaiæ. All do import a great excellency in this Patriarch. and the success of things confirmeth the same. Particularly in that not only some one of his sons (as in the issue of Abraham and Isaac, the rest being excluded) but his whole progeny of twelve sons ², making twelve Tribes, were participant of the peculiar blessings, in their offspring possessing the promised land, and exceedingly increasing became the most principal nation in the world, the selected people of God, called by the name and title of \+it the children of Israel\+it*. Of whom not only Moyses, but all the Old Testament most specially treateth, and of whom and in whom the promised Messias, the Redeemer of mankind, should be born. \fq ¹ \ft The name Israel signifieth special prerogatives in the Patriarch Jacob. \fq ² \ft All his twelve sons in their posterity were heirs of the promised land.\ef*
\v 11 and said to him: I am God Almighty, increase thou and multiply: Of thee shall be nations and peoples of nations, kings shall come forth of thy loins.
\v 12 And the land which I gave to Abraham and Isaac, I will give to thee, and to thy seed after thee.
\v 13 And he departed from him.
\v 14 But he erected a title of stone in the place where God had spoken unto him: offering upon it liquid offerings, and pouring oil on it,
\v 15 and calling the name of that place, Bethel.
\v 16 And being gone forth from thence, he came in the spring time to the land which leadeth to Ephrata: wherein when Rachel was in travail,
\v 17 because of difficulty in her travail, she began to be in danger, and the midwife said unto her: Fear not, for thou shalt have also this a son.
\v 18 And her soul departing for pain, and death now at hand, she called the name of her son Benoni, that is the son of my pain: but his father called him Benjamin, that is the son of the right hand.
\v 19 Rachel therefore died, and was buried in the high way that leadeth to Ephrata, this same is Bethleem. \x - \xt Math. 2.\x*
\v 20 And Jacob erected a title over her sepulchre: This is the title of Rachel's monument, until this present day.
\v 21 Departing thence, he pitched his tent beyond the Flock tower.
\v 22 And when he dwelt in that country, Ruben went, and \f c \fr 35.22 \ft For this fact Ruben was excluded from the chief dignity among his brethren. Gen. 49.\f* slept with Bala his father's concubine: which thing he was not ignorant of. And the sons of Jacob were twelve.
\v 23 The sons of Lia: Ruben the first begotten, and Simeon, and Levi, and Judas, and Issachar, and Zabulon.
\v 24 The sons of Rachel: Joseph and Benjamin.
\v 25 The sons of Bala Rachel's handmaid: Dan and Nephthali.
\v 26 The sons of Zelpha Lia's handmaid: Gad and Aser: these are the sons of Jacob, that were born to him in Mesopotamia of Syria.
\v 27 He came also to Isaac his father in Mambre, the city of Arbee, this is Hebron: wherein Abraham and Isaac sojourned.
\v 28 And the days of Isaac were complete an hundred eighty years.
\v 29 And spent with age he died, and was put to his people, being old and full of days, and Esau and Jacob his sons buried him.
\c 36
\cd Esau with his wives and children parteth from Jacob. 9. His genealogy is recited, with their habitations.
\p
\v 1 And these are the generations of Esau, the same is Edom. \ef - \fr 36.1 \fq The generations of Esau. \ft As before Moyses described ¹ the genealogies of Cain, of Japheth, and Cham, of Nachor, of Ismael, and other sons of Abraham; so here he recordeth another collateral progeny of Esau, that the difference and distinction of them and the selected people of God might be more conspicuous, because contraries opposed are seen more clearly. ² And so the Church's succession and perpetual light, compared with the interrupted and obscure companies, shineth the brighter. For albeit in those other generations there might be many faithful and just persons among the infidels and wicked, and of some we are assured (as of Lot and Job) yet faith and religion decayed, and was extinguished in their carnal children, and only continued in the right line from Adam to Jacob, whose twelve sons were fathers & beginners of twelve Tribes, and in them the same true Religion was still conserved and publicly professed, as in the only known visible Church, till the coming of Christ, as S. Augustin clearly sheweth in his excellent work of the City of God, especially in the 15. and 16. books, in many chapters. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Chap. 4, 10. 22. 25.\+it* \fq ² \ft By comparison of interrupted companies the continual succession of the Church is more glorious.\ef*
\v 2 Esau took wives of the daughters of Chanaan: Ada the daughter of Elon the Hetheite, and Oolibama the daughter of Ana daughter of Sebeon the Heveite: \ef - \fr 36.2 \fq Ada the daughter of Elon. \ft ¹ In the 26. chap. (v. 34.) Esau's two wives, which he took in Chanaan are called Judith the daughter of Beeri the Hetheite, and Basemath the daughter of Elon of the same place, and here the same two wives are named Ada the daughter of Elon the Hetheite, and Oolibama the daughter of Sebeon the Heveite. Which neither agree in names nor country. Again his third wife Ismael's daughter, here named Basemath, in 28. chap. v. 9. is called Maheleth. For reconciliation of which and other like difficulties, or seeming contradictions, albeit learned expositors say, that either these persons had diverse names, or one was true and natural father, another legal, or adoptive, for there were such also before the law of Moyses, as appeareth in the history of Thamar: ² yet it were hard to give a determinate solution of this difficulty. Which example, with many others (by us omitted in these brief annotations) convince the Protestants' presumptuous error, holding that Scriptures are easy to be understood. \fq ¹ \ft One place of Scripture seemeth contrary to another but is not. \fq ² \ft Holy Scriptures not easy to be understood.\ef*
\v 3 Basemath also the daughter of Ismael sister of Nabajoth.
\v 4 And Ada bare Eliphaz: Basemath bare Rahuel:
\v 5 Oolibama bare Jehus and Ihelon and Core, these are the sons of Esau, that were born to him in the land of Chanaan.
\v 6 And \f a \fr 36.6 \ft The separation of Esau from Jacob.\f* Esau took his wives and sons and daughters, and every soul of his house, and his substance, and cattle, and all that he could have in the land of Chanaan: and he went into another country, and departed from his brother Jacob. \ef - \fr 36.6 \fq Departed from his brother. 8. Dwelt in Mount Seir. \ft ¹ Here is another difficulty (though not so intricate as the former) how Esau now parted into Mount Seir, seeing he dwelled there, when his brother Jacob came from Mesopotamia. chap. 22. v. 4. Which S. Augustin (q 119. in Gen.) solveth saying: Esau first dwelt in Seir after he was disappointed of his Father's blessing, but dwelt again with his father after Jacob's return from Mesopotamia, and now went to Seir again after his father's Brothers easily envy each other: but the parents are glad of their children's advancement. \fq ¹ \ft Esau's last parting from Jacob. (c)\ef*
\v 7 For they were exceeding rich, and could not dwell together: neither was the land of their peregrination able to bear them, for the multitude of flocks.
\v 8 And Esau dwelt in Mount Seir, he is Edom.
\v 9 And these are the generations of Esau the father of Edom in mount Seir,
\v 10 and these are the names of his sons: Eliphaz the son of Ada the wife of Esau: Rahuel also the son of Basemath his wife.
\v 11 And Eliphas had sons: Theman, Omar, Sepho, and Gathan, and Cenes.
\v 12 And Thamna was the concubine of Eliphas the son of Esau: which bare to him Amalech. These are the sons of Ada the wife of Esau.
\v 13 And the sons of Rahuel were Nahath and Zara, Samma and Meza. These were the sons of Basemath the wife of Esau.
\v 14 These also were the sons of Oolibama, the daughter of Ana, the daughter of Sebeon, the wife of Esau, which she bare to him, Jehus, and Ihelon, and Core.
\v 15 These were Dukes of the sons of Esau: the sons of Eliphaz the first-begotten of Esau: Duke Theman, Duke Omar, Duke Sepho, Duke Cenes,
\v 16 Duke Core, Duke Gatham, Duke Amalech, these are the sons of Eliphaz in the land of Edom, & these are the sons of Ada.
\v 17 These also were the sons of Rahuel, the son of Esau: Duke Nahath, Duke Zara, Duke Samma, Duke Meza. And these be the Dukes of Rahuel, in the Land of Edom: these be the sons of Basemath the wife of Esau.
\v 18 And these were the sons of Oolibama the wife of Esau: Duke Jehus, Duke Ihelon, Duke Core. These be the Dukes of Oolibama, the daughter of Ana, and wife of Esau.
\v 19 These are the sons of Esau, and these are the Dukes of them: the same is Edom.
\v 20 These are the sons of Seir the Horreite, the inhabiters of the land: Lotan, and Sobal, and Sebeon, and Ana,
\v 21 and Dison, and Eser, and Disan. These are Dukes of the Horreite, the sons of Seir in the Land of Edom.
\v 22 And Lotan had sons: Hori and Heman. And the sister of Lotan was Thamna.
\v 23 And these were the sons of Sobal: Alvan and Manahat, and Ebal, and Sepho, and Onam.
\v 24 And these were the sons of Sebeon: Aia and Ana. This is Ana that found the hot waters in the wilderness, when he fed the asses of Sebeon his father:
\v 25 and he had a son Dison, and a daughter Oolibama.
\v 26 And these were the sons of Dison: Hamdan, and Eseban, and Iethram, and Charan.
\v 27 These also were the sons of Eser: Balaan, and Zavan, and Acan.
\v 28 And Disan had sons: Hus, and Aram.
\v 29 These were Dukes of the Horreites: Duke Lotan, Duke Sobal, Duke Sebeon, Duke Ana,
\v 30 Duke Dison, Duke Eser, Duke Disan: these were Dukes of the Horreites that ruled in the Land Seir.
\v 31 And the kings that ruled in the land of Edom, before that the children of Israel had a king, were these:
\v 32 Bela the son of Beor, and the name of his city Denaba.
\v 33 And Bela died, and \f b \fr 36.33 \ft By the common opinion of Latin and Greek fathers this was holy Job, as we shall discuss when we come to his book\f* Jobab the son of Zara of Bosra reigned in his stead.
\v 34 And when Jobab was dead, Husam of the land of the Themans reigned in his stead.
\v 35 He also being dead, there reigned in his stead Adad the son of Badad, that struck Madian in the country of Moab: and the name of his city was Avith.
\v 36 And when Adad was dead, there reigned for him Semla of Masreca.
\v 37 He also being dead, Saul of the river Rohoboth, reigned in his stead.
\v 38 And when he also was dead, Balanan the son of Achobor succeeded into the Kingdom.
\v 39 This men also being dead Adar reigned in his place, and the name of his city was Phau: and his wife was called Meetabel, the daughter of Matred, daughter of Mezaab.
\v 40 These therefore be the names of the Dukes of Esau in their kindreds, and places, and callings: Duke Thamna, Duke Alva, Duke Jetheth,
\v 41 Duke Oolibama, Duke Ela, Duke Phinon,
\v 42 Duke Cenez, Duke Theman, Duke Mabsar,
\v 43 Duke Magdiel, Duke Hiram: these are the Dukes of Edom dwelling in the land of their Empire, the same is Esau the father of the Idumeans.
\c 37
\cd Joseph informing his father of his brethren's faults, 5. and telling his dreams, is by them more hated. 13. being sent to visit them, 18. they first think to kill him, 26. but by Judas' counsel sell him to the Ismaelites, 29. unwitting to Ruben. 33. His father lamenteth supposing him to be slain by some wild beast. 36. He is sold again to Putiphar in Ægypt. \f - \ft The seventh part of this book. How Joseph was sold into Ægypt, and there advanced.\f*
\p
\v 1 And Jacob dwelt in the land of Chanaan, wherein his father sojourned.
\v 2 And \f a \fr 37.2 \ft These things following happened to Jacob, in his generations, that is in his children. \+it See S. Chrisost. ho. 23. in Gen.\+it*\f* these are his generations: Joseph when he was sixteen years old, fed the flock with his brethren being yet a boy: and he was with the sons of Bala and Zelpha his father's wives: and he accused his brethren to his father of \f b \fr 37.2 \ft That for ill life they were infamous, the Hebrew word \+it dibba\+it* signifieth \+it infamy.\+it*\f* a most wicked crime.
\v 3 And Israel loved Joseph above all his sons, because he had begotten him in his old age and he made him a coat of diverse colours. \ef - \fr 37.3 \fq In his old age. \ft ¹ This being one cause why Jacob loved Joseph above all his other sons, for that he was the youngest of the eleven (for Benjamin the twelfth was yet an infant) it is alleged in holy Scripture (saith S. Chrysostom Epist. ad Olympiam) as least offensive to his brethren. For a more special cause was for his mother Rachel's sake, but more principal cause of all was, for his great virtues and mature judgement; for which God also preferred him above them all, and now foreshewed the same by visions in sleep. Which they envying and meaning to prevent, did indeed unwitting cooperate thereto, God's providence turning their evil work to infinite good ². As the same holy Joseph truly interpreteth it to them after their father's death, when they justly feared revenge for so great and inhuman injuries done unto him, chap. 50. v. 20. \fq ¹ \ft The least offensive cause is alleged, why Jacob loved Joseph above his brethren. \fq ² \ft God turneth evil to good effect. \+it S. Aug. l. 14. c. 27. civit.\+it* Grave for hell corruptly translated.\ef*
\v 4 And his brethren seeing that he was loved of his father more than all his sons, they hated him, neither could they speak any thing to him peaceably.
\v 5 It chanced also that he reported to his brethren a dream that he had seen, which occasion was the seed of great hatred.
\v 6 And he said to them: hear my dream which I have seen:
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 37.7 \ft The Epistle on Friday, in the second week of Lent.\f* I thought we bound sheaves in the field: and my sheaf arose as it were, and stood, and your sheaves standing about did adore my sheaf.
\v 8 His brethren answered: What? Shalt thou be our king? Or shall we be subject to thy dominion? This occasion of his dreams and words ministered nourishment to the envy and hatred.
\v 9 He saw also another dream, which telling his brethren, he said: I saw in a dream, as it were the sun, and the moon, and eleven stars adore me.
\v 10 Which when he had reported to his father and brethren, his father rebuked him, and said: What meaneth this dream that thou hast seen? Why, shall I and thy mother, and thy brethren adore thee upon the earth?
\v 11 His brethren therefore envied him: but \f c \fr 37.11 \ft Brothers easily envy each other: but the parents are glad of their children's advancement.\f* his father considered the thing with himself.
\v 12 And when his brethren abode in Sichem feeding their father's flocks,
\v 13 Israel said to him: Thy brethren feed sheep in Sichem: come, I will send thee to them. Who answering,
\v 14 I am ready; he said to him: Go, and see if all things be well with thy brethren and the sheep, and bring me word again what they do. Being sent therefore from the Vale of Hebron, he came to Sichem:
\v 15 and a man found him there wandering in the field, and asked what he sought.
\v 16 But he answered: \f d \fr 37.16 \ft So Christ, & all good Pastors.\f* I seek my brethren, shew me where they feed the flocks.
\v 17 And the man said to him: They are departed from this place: for I heard them say: Let us go into Dothain. Joseph therefore went forward after his brethren, and found them in Dothain.
\v 18 Who when they had seen him a far off, before he came nigh them, they devised to kill him:
\v 19 and spake among themselves: Behold the dreamer cometh,
\v 20 come, let us kill him, and cast him into an old cistern: and we will say a naughty wild beast hath devoured him: \f e \fr 37.20 \ft So the Jews thinking to prevent Christ's exaltation cooperated unwitting thereto. \+it Prosper. li. de promiss. Dei.\+it*\f* and then it shall appear what his dreams do profit him.
\v 21 And Ruben hearing this, endeavoured to deliver him out of their hands, and said:
\v 22 Do not take away his life, neither shed ye blood: but cast him into this cistern that is in the wilderness, and keep your hands harmless: and he said this, desirous to deliver him out of their hands, and to restore him to his father.
\v 23 As soon therefore as he came unto his brethren, forthwith they stripped him out of his side coat, and of diverse colours,
\v 24 and cast him into the old cistern, that had not water.
\v 25 And sitting to eat bread, they saw Ismaelites wayfaring men coming from Galaad, and their camels carrying spices, and rosen, and myrrh into Ægypt.
\v 26 Judas therefore said to his brethren: What availeth it us if we kill our brother, and conceal his blood?
\v 27 It is better that he be sold to the Ismaelites, and that our hands be not polluted: for he is our brother and our flesh. His brethren assented to his words.
\v 28 And when the Madianite merchants passed by, they drawing him out of the cistern, sold him to the Ismaelites, for \f f \fr 37.28 \ft Some read \+it thirty:\+it* And as the reading is diverse, so Christ whom Joseph signified is more & less esteemed of\f* twenty pieces of silver, who brought him into Ægypt.
\v 29 And Ruben returning to the cistern, findeth not the boy:
\v 30 and renting his garments went to his brethren, and said: The boy doth not appear, and whither shall I go?
\v 31 And they took his coat, and dipped it in the blood of a kid which they had killed,
\v 32 sending some that should carry it to their father, and should say: This we have found: see whether it be thy son's coat, or no.
\v 33 Which when the father acknowledged, he said: It is my son's coat, a naughty wild beast hath eaten him, a beast hath devoured Joseph.
\v 34 And tearing his garments, did on sackcloth, mourning his son a great time.
\v 35 And all his children being gathered together to assuage their father's sorrow, he would not take comfort, but said: I will descend unto my son into hell, mourning. And whilst he persevered in weeping, \ef - \fr 37.35 \fq Into hell mourning. \ft Protestants denying more places for souls after this life, than Heaven for the just, & Hell for the wicked, translate the Hebrew word \+it Sheol, grave\+it*, for \+it hell\+it*. Because if they should grant that Jacob or other holy fathers of the old Testament descended into hell, they must confess some other hell, than where the damned are tormented, whither no Christian will say that those fathers went. If they contended only about the sense and meaning of the text, it were more tolerable, for therein they speak according to their erroneous opinion, as they think. ¹ But knowing as some of them do, that \+it Hell\+it* is true word of the text ², there is no sincerity nor moral honesty in putting \+it Grave\+it* in place thereof. And that they know it, the second table of the Bible, printed at London 1602. witnesseth, noting for a common place, that the 37. chap. of Genesis. v. 35. \+it Hell is taken for grave\+it*, thereby confessing, that the true English word of the holy Scripture in that place is \+it Hell\+it*, but that they would have it to signify grave. Whereupon any reasonable man would think to find the word \+it Hell\+it* in the text, with some gloss to shew that grave were to be understood. But in all their Editions, also in that which was printed the year next following, 1603. whereto the same table is adjoined, they read \+it grave\+it* & not \+it hell\+it* in that place, though in some ³ other places, they much disagree in translating the same word. \fq ¹ \ft Willful corruption. \fq ² \ft \+it See. S. Hiero. Ep. 119. S. Aug. li. 20. c. 15. civit.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Nu. 16. 2. Reg. 22. Job. 17. Psal. 15. 27. 85.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 37.35 \ft ¹ As for the sense, it cannot be that Jacob meant the grave: for when he said he would go to his son, he supposed him to be devoured by a wild beast, and not buried in a grave. And therefore must necessarily mean, that he would go where he thought the soul of his son to be. Which was neither in heaven, for then he would rather have ascended thither joyful, than descended to any place mourning; neither did he mean the hell of the damned, for that had been desperation; but to a low place, where the just souls then remained in rest, which was called Limbus Patrum, or Abraham's bosom ². That is, saith S. Augustin, in his answer to Bishop Evodius (Epist. 99.) \+it secreta cuiusdam quietis habitatio\+it*. The habitation of a certain secret rest. \fq ¹ \ft Jacob spake of hell not of grave. \fq ² \ft Abraham's bosom.\ef*
\v 36 the Madianites sold Joseph in Ægypt to Putiphar an Eunuch of Pharao's, master of the soldiers.
\c 38
\cd Judas having three sons by a Chananite, 6. marrieth the first, and after his death, the second to Thamar. 10. who also dying, he delayeth to match the third with her. 15. But himself begetteth of her (taking her for a harlot) two sons twins, Phares and Zara:
\p
\v 1 The \f a \fr 38.1 \ft Moyses inserteth here this history, because Christ should be born of the genealogy of Judas and Phares. Mat. 1.\f* same time Judas going down from his brethren, turned in to a man an Odolamite, named Hiras.
\v 2 And he saw there the daughter of a man of Chanaan, called Sue: and taking her to wife, he did company with her.
\v 3 Who conceived, and bare a son, and called his name Her.
\v 4 And conceiving a child again, she called her son after he was born, Onan.
\v 5 She bare also the third, whom she called Sela: after whose birth, she ceased to bear any more.
\v 6 And Judas gave a wife to Her his first-begotten, named Thamar.
\v 7 Also Her the first-begotten of Judas, was wicked in the sight of our Lord, and was slain of him.
\v 8 Judas therefore said to Onan his son: Company with thy brother's wife, and be joined to her, that thou mayest raise seed to thy brother. \ef - \fr 38.8 \fq Raise seed to thy brother. \ft ¹ By this it appeareth, that in the law of nature, when a married man died without issue, his brother might lawfully marry the widow; whose first son should be counted and called the son and heir of his uncle dead before. The same was established by the law of Moyses (Deut. 25) Which being now abrogated, it remaineth in the Church's power to constitute a law in this behalf ², and consequently to dispense in the same, so far as is agreeable with the law of nature. Whereof see more. Levit. 18. \fq ¹ \ft How a man might marry his brother's wife in the law of nature. \fq ² \ft The Church's decree is now our rule.\ef*
\v 9 He knowing that the children should not be born to himself, companying with his brother's wife, shed his seed upon the ground, lest children might be born in his brother's name.
\v 10 And therefore our Lord struck him, because he did a detestable thing.
\v 11 For the which cause Judas said to Thamar his daughter in law: Be a widow in thy father's house, till Sela my son grow up: for he feared lest he also might die, as his brethren. Who went her way and dwelt in her father's house.
\v 12 And after many days were come and gone, the daughter of Sue the wife of Judas died. Who after his mourning having received consolation, went up to the shearers of his sheep, himself and Hiras his shepherd of his flock, the Odolamite, into Thamnas.
\v 13 And it was told \f b \fr 38.13 \ft Thamar sinned desiring to be a mother without lawful marriage: and Judas sinned lying with a supposed harlot \+it S. Aug. li. 22. c. 61. 62. & 63. cont. Faust.\+it*\f* Thamar that her father in law came up into Thamnas to shear his sheep.
\v 14 Who putting off the garments of her widowhood, took a veil: and changing her habit, sat in the cross way that leadeth to Thamnas: because Sela was grown, and she had not taken him to her husband.
\v 15 Whom when Judas had seen, he supposed her to be an harlot: for she had covered her face, lest she should be known.
\v 16 And going unto her, he said: Suffer me to lie with thee: for he knew her not to be his daughter in law. Who answering: What wilt thou give me that thou mayest enjoy my company?
\v 17 He said: I will send thee a kid out of the flocks. And when she said again: I will suffer that thou wilt, if thou give me a pledge, till thou send that which thou dost promise;
\v 18 Judas said: What wilt thou to be given thee for a pledge? She answered: Thy ring, and bracelet, and staff which thou holdest in thy hand. The woman therefore by once companying conceived,
\v 19 and rising she went her way: and putting off the apparel which she had taken, put on the garments of her widowhood.
\v 20 And Judas sent a kid by his shepherd the Odolamite, that he might receive the pledge again, which he had given to the woman: who when he had not found her,
\v 21 he asked the men of that place: Where is the woman that sat in the cross way? All making answer: There was no harlot in this place,
\v 22 he returned to Judas, and said to him: I have not found her: yea the men also of that place said unto me, that there never sat harlot there.
\v 23 Judas said: Let her take it to her, surely she cannot charge us with a lie, I sent the kid which I promised: and thou didst not find her.
\v 24 And behold after three months they told Judas, saying: Thamar thy daughter in law hath played the harlot, and her belly seemeth to swell. And Judas said: Bring her forth \f c \fr 38.24 \ft Adultery punishable by death, in the law of nature.\f* that she may be burnt.
\v 25 Who when she was led to execution, she sent to her father in law, saying: By that man, whose these things are, have I conceived: look whose the ring is, and the bracelet, and the staff.
\v 26 Who acknowledging the gifts, said: She is juster than I: because I did not give her to Sela my son. But he knew her no more.
\v 27 And when she was ready to be brought to bed, there appeared twins in her belly: and in the very delivery of the infants, one put forth the hand, wherein the midwife tied a scarlet string, saying:
\v 28 This shall come forth the former.
\v 29 But he drawing back his hand, the other came forth: and the woman said: Why is the partition divided for thee? And for this cause called his name Phares.
\v 30 Afterward his brother came forth, in whose hand was the scarlet string: whom she called Zara.
\c 39
\cd Joseph being in great credit with his Master, hath the whole charge of his house. 7. Contemning his Mistress' solicitation to incontinence, 13. is falsely accused by her to his Master, 20. and cast into prison. 21. Where again he getteth credit, and hath the charge of all the prisoners.
\p
\v 1 Therefore Joseph was brought into Ægypt, and Putiphar an Eunuch of Pharao, Prince of his army, a man of Ægypt bought him, at the hand of the Ismaelites, by whom he was brought.
\v 2 And \f a \fr 39.2 \ft Joseph endowed with all virtues was a special mirror of chastity. \+it S. Amb. li. de Ioseph. c. 1.\+it*\f* our Lord was with him, and he was a man that in all things did prosperously: and he dwelt in his master's house,
\v 3 who knew very well that our Lord was with him, and that all things which he did were directed by him in his hand.
\v 4 And Joseph found grace before his master, and ministered to him: by whom being made ruler over all his things, he governed the house committed to him, and all things that were delivered unto him:
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 39.5 \ft The four cardinal virtues reigned in him.\f* and our Lord blessed the house of the Ægyptian for Joseph's sake, and multiplied as well in houses, as in lands all his substance.
\v 6 Neither knew he any other thing, but the bread which he did eat. And Joseph was of beautiful countenance, and comely favoured to behold.
\v 7 After many days therefore his Mistress cast her eyes on Joseph, and said: Sleep with me.
\v 8 Who \f b \fr 39.8 \ft Temperance.\f* in nowise assenting to that wicked act, said to her: Behold, my master having delivered all things unto me, knoweth not what he hath in his own house:
\v 9 Neither is there any thing which is not in my power, or that he hath not delivered to me, beside thee, that art his wife: \f c \fr 39.9 \ft Justice.\f* how therefore can I do this wicked thing, and sin against my God?
\v 10 With these kind of words \f d \fr 39.10 \ft Fortitude.\f* day by day both the woman was importune upon the young man, and he refused the adultery.
\v 11 And it chanced on a certain day, that Joseph went into the house, and did some business without any man with him:
\v 12 and she catching the skirt of his garment, said: Sleep with me. Who \f e \fr 39.12 \ft Prudence.\f* leaving the cloak in her hand, fled, and went forth abroad.
\v 13 And when the woman saw the garment in her hands, and herself to be contemned,
\v 14 she called to her the men of her house, and said to them: See he hath brought in an Hebrew to delude us: he came upon me for to lie with me; and when I had cried out,
\v 15 and he heard my voice, he left the cloak that I held, and fled forth.
\v 16 For an argument therefore of her credit she reserved the cloak, & shewed it to her husband returning home,
\v 17 and said: There came unto me the Hebrew servant, whom thou didst bring hither, for to delude me:
\v 18 and when he heard me cry, he left the cloak which I held, and fled forth.
\v 19 His master hearing these things, and giving over light credit to his wife's words, was very wrath:
\v 20 and delivered Joseph into prison, where the king's prisoners were kept, and he was there shut up.
\v 21 And \f f \fr 39.21 \ft God is more specially with his servants in affliction than in prosperity. \+it S. Amb. li. de Ioseph. c. 5.\+it*\f* our Lord was with Joseph, and having mercy upon him gave him grace in the sight of the chief of the prison.
\v 22 Who delivered in his hand all the prisoners that were kept in custody: and whatsoever was done, was under him.
\v 23 Neither did himself know anything, having committed all things to him: for our Lord was with him, and directed all his works.
\c 40
\cd Joseph interpreteth the dreams of two Eunuchs prisoners, 12. that the one should be restored to his office, 16. the other be hanged. 20. The third day the event declared the interpretations to be true, but Joseph is forgotten.
\p
\v 1 These things being so done, it chanced that two Eunuchs, the cup-bearer of the king of Ægypt, and his baker, offended against their lord.
\v 2 And Pharao being wrath against them (for the one was chief of the cup-bearers, the other chief baker)
\v 3 he sent them into the prison of the captain of the soldiers, in the which Joseph also was prisoner.
\v 4 But the keeper of the prison delivered them to Joseph, who also ministered to them: some little time was passed, and they were kept in custody.
\v 5 And they saw each of them both a dream in one night, according to an interpretation agreeing to themselves:
\v 6 to whom when Joseph was entered in the morning, and saw them sad,
\v 7 he asked them, saying: Why is your countenance sadder today than it was wont?
\v 8 Who answered: We have seen a dream, and there is nobody to interpret it to us. And Joseph said to them: Why doth not interpretation belong to God? Tell me what you have seen. \ef - \fr 40.8 \fq Doth not interpretation belong to God? \ft Dreams do come of diverse causes: ¹ some of natural complexion or disposition, whereby Philosophers or Physicians may probably judge of the state of man's body. Some are rather effects of things past, than signs of any things to come. Of which sort the wise man saith: \+it Dreams do follow many cares\+it* (Eccle. 5.) ² Some are suggested by evil spirits, either to flatter worldlings with great pretences, or to terrify weak minds with dangers and afflictions, or to vex and trouble those in sleep, whom they cannot easily move waking, as S. Gregory discourseth (li. 8. Moral. in cap. 7. Job.) ³ Some dreams are of God, as in Jacob, Joseph, these Eunuchs, Pharao, Nabuchodonosor ⁴, and others both good and evil men. But to discern and assuredly to judge of some dreams, whether they be from God, by holy Angels, or illusions of evil spirits, is a special gift of God, as also the interpretation thereof belongeth to God, as Joseph here testifieth. Whosoever therefore will be secure must rely either upon express Scripture, or judgement of the Church ⁵, as in ominous speeches was noted before (chap. 24.) Otherwise the general rule is, not to observe dreams, Deut. 18. \fq ¹ \ft Some dreams are natural. \fq ² \ft Some are illusions of evil spirits. \fq ³ \ft Some are from God. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Dan. 4.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Holy Scripture and the Church are judges of doubtful dreams.\ef*
\v 9 The chief of the cup-bearers first told his dream: I saw before me a vine,
\v 10 wherein were three branches, growing by little and little into buds, and after the blossoms, the grapes waxed ripe:
\v 11 and the cup of Pharao in my hand: and I took the grapes, and wrung them into the cup which I held, and I gave the cup to Pharao.
\v 12 Joseph answered: This is the interpretation of the dream: The three branches, are yet three days:
\v 13 after the which Pharao will remember thy service, & will restore thee to thy old degree: & thou shalt give him the cup according to thine office, as before thou hadst wont to do.
\v 14 Only remember me, when it shall be well with thee, and do me this mercy, to put Pharao in mind that he take me out of this prison:
\v 15 because I was taken away by stealth, out of the land of the Hebrews, and here an innocent was I cast into the lake.
\v 16 The master of the bakers seeing that he had wisely resolved the dream, he said: And I also saw a dream, that I had three baskets of meal upon my head:
\v 17 and that in one basket that was the higher, I carried all meats that are made by the art of baking, and that the birds did eat out of it.
\v 18 Joseph answered: This is the interpretation of the dream: The three baskets, are yet three days:
\v 19 after the which Pharao will take thy head from thee, and hang thee \f a \fr 40.19 \ft Death on the cross was most cruel, & most ignominious. Cicero 7. Ver. yet suffered by Christ and by him made glorious. \+it Sap. 2. Philip. 2.\+it*\f* on the cross, and the fowls shall tear thy flesh.
\v 20 The third day after this was the birth-day of Pharao: who making a great feast to his servants, at the banquet he remembered the master of the cup-bearers, and the chief of the bakers.
\v 21 And he restored the one into his place, to reach him the cup,
\v 22 the other he hanged on a gibbet, that the truth of the interpreter might be approved.
\v 23 And yet notwithstanding the chief of the cup-bearers, prosperous things succeeding, forgat his interpreter.
\c 41
\cd Pharao dreaming of fat & lean kine: 5. also of full and thin ears of corn, 8. no other being able to interpret, 9. Joseph is remembered. 25. Who interpreting the same, 38. is made ruler over all Ægypt, 50. marrieth, and hath two sons, Manasses and Ephraim.
\p
\v 1 After two years \f a \fr 41.1 \ft Pharao his dreams, and his Eunuchs' were prophetical. For by them God forshewed things to come v. 25. yet they were no Prophets, but Joseph: who had the gift to interpret them. \+it S. Aug. l. 12. c. 9. de Gen. ad lit. S. Greg. l. 11. Moral. in c. 13. Iob.\+it*\f* Pharao saw a dream. He thought he stood upon a river,
\v 2 out of the which came up seven kine, fair and fat exceedingly: and they fed in marish places.
\v 3 Other seven also came up out of the river, foul, and carrion lean: and they fed on the very bank of the river, in green places:
\v 4 and they devoured them that had the marvelous beauty and good state of bodies. Pharao after he waked,
\v 5 slept again, and saw another dream: Seven ears of corn grew forth upon one stalk full and fair:
\v 6 there sprang also other ears as many, thin and blasted with adustion,
\v 7 devouring all the beauty of the former. Pharao awaking up after his rest,
\v 8 and when morning was come being frighted with fear, he sent to all the interpreters of Ægypt, and to all the wise men: and they being called for, told them his dream, neither was there any that could interpret it.
\v 9 Then at length the master of the cup-bearers remembering himself, said: I confess my sin:
\v 10 The king being angry with his servants, commanded me and the chief of the bakers to be cast into the prison of the Captain of the soldiers:
\v 11 where in one night both of us saw a dream portending things to come.
\v 12 There was there a young man an Hebrew, servant to the same Captain of the soldiers, to whom telling our dreams,
\v 13 we heard whatsoever afterward the event of the thing proved to be so. For I was restored to my office, and he was hanged upon a gibbet.
\v 14 Forthwith at the king's commandment, Joseph being brought out of the prison they polled him: and changing his apparel, brought him unto him.
\v 15 To whom he said: I have seen dreams, and there is not any that can expound them; which I have heard, thou dost most wisely interpret.
\v 16 Joseph answered: Without me, God shall answer prosperous things to Pharao.
\v 17 Pharao therefore told that he had see: Me thought I stood upon the bank of the river,
\v 18 and seven kine came up out of the bank of the river, exceeding fair, and full of flesh: which grazed on green places in a marish pasture.
\v 19 And behold there followed these other seven kine, so passing ill favoured and lean, that I never saw the like in the land of Ægypt;
\v 20 which having devoured and consumed the former,
\v 21 gave no token of their fullness: but with the like leanness & deformity looked heavily. A waking, and fallen again into a deep sleep,
\v 22 Isaw a dream: Seven ears of corn grew forth upon one stalk, full and very fair.
\v 23 Other seven also thin and blasted with adustion, sprang of the stalk,
\v 24 which devoured the beauty of the former: I told the dream to the conjecturers, and there is no man that can declare it.
\v 25 Joseph answered: The king's dream is one: God hath shewed to Pharao \f b \fr 41.25 \ft These things came to pass by God's particular providence. \+it Psal. 4. God called\+it* (or caused) \+it a famine upon the land.\+it*\f* the things that he will do.
\v 26 The seven fair kine, and the seven full ears, be seven years of plentifulness and both contain the self-same meaning of the dream.
\v 27 Also the seven lean and thin kine, that came up after them, and the seven thin ears, and blasted with the burning wind, are seven years of famine to come.
\v 28 Which shall be fulfilled in this order.
\v 29 Behold there shall come seven years of great fertility in the whole Land of Ægypt:
\v 30 after which shall follow other seven years of so great sterility, that all the abundance before shall be forgotten: for the famine shall consume all the land,
\v 31 and the greatness of the scarcity shall destroy the greatness of the plenty.
\v 32 And in that thou didst see the second time a dream pertaining to the same thing, it is a token of the certainty for that the word of God shall come to pass, and be fulfilled speedily.
\v 33 Now therefore let the king provide a wise man and industrious, & make him Ruler over the Land of Ægypt:
\v 34 that he may appoint overseers over all countries: and gather into barns the fifth part of the fruits, during the seven years of the fertility,
\v 35 that now presently shall ensue: and let all the corn be laid up under Pharao's hands, and let it be reserved in the cities.
\v 36 And let it be in a readiness against the famine of seven years to come, which shall oppress Ægypt, and the land shall not be consumed with scarcity.
\v 37 The counsel pleased Pharao, and all his servants:
\v 38 and he spake to them: Can we find such another man, that is full of the spirit of God?
\v 39 He said therefore to Joseph: Because God hath shewed thee all things that thou hast spoken, can I find a wiser and one like unto thee?
\v 40 Thou shalt be over my house, & at the commandment of thy mouth all the people shall obey: only in the throne of the Kingdom I will go before thee.
\v 41 And again Pharao said to Joseph: Behold, I have appointed thee over the whole land of Ægypt.
\v 42 And he took his ring from his own hand, and gave it into his hand: and he put upon him a silk robe, and put a chain of gold about his neck.
\v 43 And he made him go up into his second chariot, the crier proclaiming that all should bow their knee before him, and that they should know he was made Governor over the whole Land of Ægypt. \ef - \fr 41.43 \fq Made Governor. \ft ¹ It is easy in the eyes of God suddenly to enrich the poor. For who would have thought (saith Philo) ² that in one day a bondman should be made a Lord, a poor prisoner the chief of the nobility, an under-gaoler the Viceroy or King's deputy, from a common prison to have a Kingly court of his own, from extreme ignominy, to ascend into so high a room of dignity? \fq ¹ \ft Holy Joseph suddenly advanced. \fq ² \ft \+it Eccl. 11. li. de Ioseph.\+it*\ef*
\v 44 And the king said to Joseph: I am Pharao: without thy commandment no man shall move hand or foot in all the land of Ægypt.
\v 45 And he turned his name, & called him in the Ægyptian tongue the Saviour of the world. And he gave him to wife Aseneth the daughter of Putiphar \f c \fr 41.45 \ft \+it Cohen\+it* signifieth Priest; as not only the Latin, but also the 70. & Philo and Iosephus here translate; though sometimes it signifieth \+it Prince,\+it* as the Chaldey paraphrasis interpreteth, whereby it is probable that this Putiphar was both a priest, and a prince.\f* Priest of Heliopolis. Joseph therefore went forth to the land of Ægypt \ef - \fr 41.45 \fq Saviour of the world. \ft In the original text the new name and title given by Pharao to Joseph is expressed by these two words, \+it Saphnath pahanaach\+it*: the former \+it saphnath\+it* in Hebrew signifieth a secret or hidden thing, of \+it saphan\+it* to hide: but the signification of the other word \+it pahanaach\+it*, is more uncertain, being found nowhere else in the holy Bible. The Rabbins do commonly interpret them both together, \+it The man to whom secrets are revealed\+it*, or \+it The revealer of secrets\+it*: ¹ and so this name agreeth well to Joseph, in respect of the gift of interpreting dreams. But besides his interpreting, he also gave most wise counsel, that tended to the safety of many, which, it is like, Pharao meant to express by this new name. And S. Hierom, who doubtless with great diligence, and no less judgement, searched the true signification thereof, saith that albeit this name in Hebrew soundeth \+it The finder out of secrets\+it*, yet seeing it was imposed by an Ægyptian (who knew no Hebrew) the reason thereof must be had of the same tongue; and these two words in the Ægyptian language are interpreted ² \+it The Saviour of the world\+it*; for that he delivered the world from the imminent ruin of famine. Thus saith S. Hierom. And so most aptly the figure answereth to Christ ³, the true Saviour of the world. \fq ¹ \ft Joseph truly called the revealer of secrets. \fq ² \ft But more honourably, the Saviour of the world. \fq ³ \ft Therein a figure of Christ.\ef*
\v 46 (and he was thirty years old when he stood in the sight of king Pharao) and did circuit all the countries of Ægypt.
\v 47 And the fertility of the seven years came: and the corn being bound up into sheaves was gathered into the barns of Ægypt.
\v 48 All the abundance also of grain was laid up in every city.
\v 49 And there was so great abundance of wheat, that it became equal to the sand of the sea, and the plenty exceeded measure.
\v 50 And there were born unto Joseph two sons before the famine came: whom Aseneth the daughter of Putiphar Priest of Heliopolis bare him.
\v 51 And he called the name of the first-begotten \f 1 \fr 41.51 \ft * Oblivion.\f* Manasses, saying: God made me to forget all my labours, and my father's house.
\v 52 The name also of the second he called \f 1 \fr 41.52 \ft * Fruitful or Growing.\f* Ephraim, saying: God hath made me to increase in the land of my poverty.
\v 53 Therefore when the seven years of the plentifulness, that had been in Ægypt were passed:
\v 54 the seven years of scarcity began to come, which Joseph foretold: and in the whole world the famine prevailed, but in all the land of Ægypt there was bread.
\v 55 The which being in hunger, the people cried to Pharao, desiring food. To whom he answered: Go ye to Joseph: & whatsoever he shall say to you, that do ye.
\v 56 And the famine daily increased in all the land: and Joseph opened all the barns, and sold to the Ægyptians: for them also the famine had oppressed.
\v 57 And all Provinces came into Ægypt, to buy victuals, and to moderate the misery of the scarcity.
\c 42
\cd Jacob sendeth ten of his sons to buy corn in Ægypt. 7. where Joseph knowing them, they not knowing him, with hard speeches putteth them in prison. 18. At last Simeon remaining in custody, till Benjamin be brought, the rest are dismissed, 25. with their money, unknown to them, in their sacks.
\p
\v 1 And Jacob hearing that victuals were sold in Ægypt, he said to his sons: Why neglect ye?
\v 2 I have heard that wheat is sold in Ægypt: Go ye down and buy us necessaries, that we may live and not be consumed with lack.
\v 3 Therefore the ten brethren of Joseph going down to buy corn in Ægypt,
\v 4 Benjamin being kept at home by Jacob, who said to his brethren: Lest perhaps he take any harm in the journey:
\v 5 entered into the land of Ægypt with others that went to buy. For the famine was in the land of Chanaan.
\v 6 And Joseph was Prince in the land of Ægypt, and at his pleasure corn was sold to the people. And when his brethren had adored him,
\v 7 and he knew them, he spake as it were to strangers somewhat roughly, asking them: From whence came you? Who answered: From the Land of Chanaan, that we may buy necessaries to live. \ef - \fr 42.7 \fq Somewhat roughly. \ft Joseph afflicted his brethren to bring them into consideration of their former faults, and to true contrition ¹. Without which, though injuries be remitted by men, yet the offenders are not absolved before God. Therefore he loving them and hating their sin, by affliction brought them to understanding. Who being at last truly penitent, he acknowledged and most lovingly embraced them, and provided for them in their necessity. \+it S. Aug. ser. 82. de temp.\+it* And this example S. Gregory (ho. 22. in Ezech.) applieth to the instruction of Pastors of souls: that they procure true repentance before absolution of sins ²: \+it Ne si inordinate culpa dimittitur, is qui est culpabilis in reatu gravius astringatur: Lest, if the fault be disorderly remitted, he that is faulty be more grievously entangled in guilt of conscience\+it*: therefore with much discretion severity in shew, and clemency in mind are required. \fq ¹ \ft Contrition necessary for the remission of sins. \fq ² \ft Disorderly remission is hurtful.\ef*
\v 8 And yet himself knowing his brethren, was not known of them.
\v 9 And remembering the dreams, which sometimes he had seen, he said to them: You are spies: to view the weaker parts of the land you are come. \ef - \fr 42.9 \fq You are spies. \ft ¹ To the same purpose he called them spies. After the manner of examiners calling suspected persons, as they may seem to be; thereby to try what they would answer. All for their wholesome penance, and withal to procure them afterwards more compassion among the Ægyptians. S. Aug. q. 139. super Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Joseph calleth his brothers spies for their good.\ef*
\v 10 Who said: It is not so, my Lord, but thy servants are come to buy victuals.
\v 11 We are all the sons of one man: we are come as men of peace, neither do thy servants go about any evil.
\v 12 To whom he answered: It is otherwise: you came to consider the undefensed parts of this land.
\v 13 But they: We thy servants, say they, are twelve brethren, the sons of one man in the Land of Chanaan: the youngest is with our father, the other is not living.
\v 14 This is it, quoth he, that I said: You are spies.
\v 15 Now presently I will take a trial of you: by the health of Pharao you shall not depart hence, until your youngest brother do come, \ef - \fr 42.15 \fq By the health of Pharao. \ft ¹ Joseph in swearing by Pharao's health, honoured God, the giver and conserver of health, life, power, dignity, and all that was in Pharao. Whereby we see that this manner of swearing was lawful. As now likewise Christians lawfully swear not only by God, but also by his creatures, saying: As God shall help them, and his holy Gospels: so it be with due circumstances and requisite conditions, namely with those which the Prophet Hieremie mentioneth ², \+it in truth, in judgement, and in justice\+it*: not false, rash, nor of an unjust thing: nor in frequent and common talk (a most bad custom) for so thou must neither swear by God himself, nor (as our Saviour teacheth) ³ by heaven, nor by the earth, nor by Hierusalem, nor by thy head, nor any thing else: signifying that in some case, and due manner we may swear by any of these. ⁴ And in this particular Joseph did rather name Pharao in his oath than God, as well to conceal himself as yet from his brethren, as to strike more terror in their hearts by naming his Master the King, in whom he had more interest than they. \fq ¹ \ft It is lawful to swear by creatures. \fq ² \ft \+it Hier. 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Mat. 5.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft In some case more convenient than to name God expressly.\ef*
\v 16 Send you one of you to bring him: and you shall be in prison till the things be proved which you have said, whether they be true or false: \f a \fr 42.16 \ft If these things which ye say be proved false, ye are to be held as spies for your lying. \+it S. Aug. q. 139. super Gen.\+it*\f* otherwise by the health of Pharao you are spies.
\v 17 Therefore he put them in prison three days.
\v 18 And the third day bringing them out of prison, he said: Do as I have said, and you shall live: for I fear God.
\v 19 If you be men of peace, let one of your brethren be bound in prison: and go ye your ways, and carry the corn that you have bought unto your houses,
\v 20 and bring your youngest brother to me, that I may prove your sayings to be true, and that you die not. They did as he had said,
\v 21 and they talked one to another: Worthily do we suffer these things, because we have sinned against our brother, seeing the distress of his soul whilst he besought us, and we heard not: therefore is this tribulation come upon us.
\v 22 Among whom Ruben one of them, said: Why, did not I say to you: Sin not against the boy; and you heard me not? Lo his blood is required.
\v 23 And they knew not that Joseph understood: because he spake to them by an interpreter.
\v 24 And he turned away himself a little while, and wept: and returning he spake to them.
\v 25 And taking Simeon, and binding him in their presence, he commanded his servants that they should fill up their sacks with wheat, and put every man's money again in their bags, giving them besides for to eat on the way: who did so.
\v 26 But they carrying their corn upon their asses, took their journey.
\v 27 And one of them opening his sack to give his beast provender in the Inn, beholding the money in the sack's mouth,
\v 28 he said to his brethren: My money is given me again, behold it is in the sack. And being astonished and troubled amongst themselves, they said: What is this, that God hath done unto us?
\v 29 And they came to Jacob their father into the land of Chanaan, & they told him all things that had chanced unto them, saying:
\v 30 The lord of the land spake to us roughly, & thought us to be spies of the province,
\v 31 to whom we answered: We are men of peace, neither do we attempt any treachery.
\v 32 We are twelve brethren born of one father: one is not living, the youngest is with our father in the Land of Chanaan.
\v 33 Who said to us: Thus shall I try that you be men of peace: Leave one of your brethren with me, and take ye provision necessary for your houses, and go your ways,
\v 34 and bring your youngest brother to me, that I may know you are not spies: and you may receive this fellow again, that is kept in prison: and afterwards may have license to buy what things you will.
\v 35 This being said when they poured out their corn, every man found his money tied in the mouth of the sacks: and all being astonished together,
\v 36 their father Jacob said: You have made me to be without children, Joseph is not living, Simeon is kept in bonds, and Benjamin you will take away: all these evils, are fallen upon me.
\v 37 To whom Ruben answered: Kill my two sons, if I bring him not again to thee: deliver him into my hand, and I will restore him unto thee.
\v 38 But he said: My son shall not go down with you: his brother is dead, and he alone is remaining: if any adversity shall chance to him in the land to the which you go, you shall bring down \f b \fr 42.38 \ft Mine old age, or, me an old man. S. Aug. q. 142.\f* my hoar hairs with sorrow unto hell. \ef - \fr 42.38 \fq Unto hell. \ft ¹ To that place where souls remain, as before chap. 37. v. 35. For this phrase, of \+it bringing unto hell\+it*, and \+it descending into hell\+it*, usually spoken in the old Testament, of all sorts of souls both just and wicked, signifieth that all went that time to hell, that is, to a low place, far distant from heaven: But some to rest, and some to pain. Whereupon S. Hierome saith: Hell is a place wherein souls are included, either in rest, or in pains, according to the quality of their deserts. \fq ¹ \ft Diverse mansions in hell.\ef*
\c 43
\cd The famine pressing the land, Jacob willeth his sons to go again into Ægypt to buy more corn: 3. Who refusing to go without Benjamin, 11. he is sent with them, and presents and double money, lest the former were brought back by error. 16. Joseph seeing Benjamin, 23. delivereth Simeon, and entertaineth them all at dinner.
\p
\v 1 In the mean time the famine did oppress all the land very sore.
\v 2 And the provision being spent, which they had brought out of Ægypt, Jacob said to his sons: Go again, and buy us a little victual.
\v 3 Judas answered: That Same man denounced unto us under attestation of an oath, saying: You shall not see my face, unless you bring your youngest brother with you.
\v 4 If therefore thou wilt send him with us, we will go forward together, and will buy necessaries for thee:
\v 5 but if thou wilt not, we will not go: for the man, as we have often said, denounced unto us saying: You shall not see my face without your youngest brother.
\v 6 Israel said to them: You have done this to my misery, in that you told him that you had another brother also.
\v 7 But they answered: The man asked us in order our progeny: if our father lived; if we had a brother: and we answered him consequently to that which he demanded: could we know that he would say: Bring hither your brother with you?
\v 8 Judas also said to his father: Send the boy with me, that we may set forward, and may live: lest we and our little ones die.
\v 9 I take upon me the boy: require him of my hand, unless I bring him again, and restore him to thee, I will be \f a \fr 43.9 \ft Guilt of sin is a greater bond than the life of Ruben's sons, which he offered, and Jacob yielded not thereto; & yet granted to this offer of Judas.\f* guilty of sin against thee for ever.
\v 10 If delay had not been made, we had been come now the second time.
\v 11 Therefore Israel their father said to them: If it must needs be so, do that you will: take of the best fruits of the land in your vessels, and carry to the man for presents, a courtesy of rosen, & of honey, & of incense, of myrrh, of terebinth, and of almonds.
\v 12 Double money also carry with you: and recarry that you found in your sacks, lest perhaps it was done by an error:
\v 13 but take also your brother, and go to the man.
\v 14 And my God almighty make him favourable unto you; and send back with you your brother, whom he keepeth, and this Benjamin: as for me I shall be desolate without children.
\v 15 The men therefore took the presents, & the double money, & Benjamin; & went down into Ægypt, & stood before Joseph.
\v 16 Whom when he had seen & Benjamin withal, he commanded the steward of his house, saying: Bring in the men into the house, and kill victims, and prepare a feast; because they shall eat with me at noon.
\v 17 He did that which was commanded him, and brought the men into the house.
\v 18 And there being sore afraid, they said one to another: because of the money which we carried back the first time in our sacks, we are brought in: that he may turn upon us \f 1 \fr 43.18 \ft \+it Calumniam.\+it*\f* a false accusation, and forcibly bring both us and our asses into bondage.
\v 19 Wherefore in the very door stepping to the steward of the house,
\v 20 They spake: Sir, we desire thee to hear us. Now once before we came down to buy provision:
\v 21 Which being bought, when we were come to the Inn, we opened our sacks, and found our money in the mouths of the sacks: which we have now brought again in the same weight.
\v 22 But we have brought other money besides, to buy the things that are necessary for us: our conscience is not privy, who put it in our bags.
\v 23 But he answered: Peace be with you, fear you not: your God, and the God of your father hath given you treasures in your sacks. For the money, which you gave me, I have for good. And he brought forth Simeon unto them.
\v 24 And being brought into the house, he fetched water, and they washed their feet, and he gave provender to their asses.
\v 25 But they made ready the presents, till Joseph should come in at noon: for they had heard that they should eat bread there.
\v 26 Therefore Joseph came in to his house, and they offered him presents holding them in their hands, and \f b \fr 43.26 \ft They now adore him, who they sold, lest they should adore him. S. Greg. ho. 22. in Ezech.\f* they adored prostrate to the ground.
\v 27 But he courteously resaluting them, asked them saying: Is the old man your father in health, of whom you told me? Is he yet living.
\v 28 Who answered: Thy servant our father is in health, he is yet living. And bowing themselves they adored him.
\v 29 And Joseph lifting up his eyes, saw Benjamin his brother of the same mother, and said: is this your young brother, of whom you told me? And again: God, saith he, be merciful unto thee my son.
\v 30 And he made haste because his heart was moved upon his brother, and tears brake forth: and entering into his chamber he wept.
\v 31 And when he had washed his face, coming forth again, he refrained himself, and said: Set bread on the table.
\v 32 Which being set down, to Joseph apart, and to his brethren apart, to the Ægyptians also that did eat with him apart (for it is unlawful for the Ægyptians to eat with the Hebrews, and \f c \fr 43.32 \ft See Exodi. 8. v. 26.\f* they think such a feast profane)
\v 33 they sat before him, the first begotten according to his first-birth, and the youngest according to his age. And they marveled out of measure,
\v 34 Taking the portions that they received of him: and the greater portion came to Benjamin, so that it exceeded \f d \fr 43.34 \ft Every one having five portions, Benjamin had double. Josephus li. 1. Antiq.\f* by five parts. And they drank and were inebriated with him. \ef - \fr 43.34 \fq Inebriated. \ft ¹ Not that they did eat or drink too much, or fell into excess, but competently. As the earth is said to be inebriated with rain (Psal. 64.) being sufficiently watered, and so made fruitful, not drowned, nor overflowed, for so it is unfruitful. S. Hierome Tradit. Heb. S. Aug. q. 144. super Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Moderation to be used in feasting.\ef*
\c 44
\cd Joseph causeth their sacks to be filled with corn, and their money to be put again therein, and in Benjamin's sack also a silver cup, 4. and when they were parted, sending after them, chargeth them with theft. 12. And the cup being found in Benjamin's sack, they all much afflicted return to Joseph. 17. who threatening to keep Benjamin, 18. Judas entreateth, 32. & finally offereth himself to servitude for him.
\p
\v 1 And Joseph commanded the steward of his house, saying: Fill their sacks with corn, as much as they can hold: and put the money of every one in the top of his sack.
\v 2 And in the sack's mouth \f a \fr 44.2 \ft By this Joseph tried his brethren's affection, whether they would entreat for Benjamin, or suffer him to be captive: as they had before sold himself to captivity. \+it Theo. q. 105. in Gen.\+it*\f* of the younger put my silver cup, and the price which he gave for the wheat. And it was so done.
\v 3 And when the morning rose, they were dismissed with their asses.
\v 4 And they were now departed out of the city, and had gone forward a little way: then Joseph sending for the steward of his house, said: Arise, quote he, and pursue the men: and overtaking them say to them: Why have you rendered evil for good?
\v 5 The cup which you have stolen is that wherein my lord doth drink, and wherein he is wont to divine: you have done a very evil thing.
\v 6 He did as he had commanded. And having overtaken them, he spake to them in the same order.
\v 7 Who answered: Why doth our lord speak so, as though thy servants had committed so heinous a fact?
\v 8 The money that we found in the top of our sacks, we recarried to thee from the land of Chanaan: & how followeth it, that we have stolen out of thy lord's house gold or silver?
\v 9 With whom soever of thy servants that shall be found which thou seekest, let him die, and we will be the bondmen of our lord.
\v 10 Who said to them: Be it done according to your sentence: with whom soever it shall be found, be he my bondman, and you shall be guiltless.
\v 11 Therefore in haste taking down their sacks upon the ground, every man opened.
\v 12 Which when he had searched, beginning from the elder even to the youngest, he found the cup in Benjamin's sack.
\v 13 But they \f b \fr 44.13 \ft Otorments of mercy! He vexeth whom he loveth. \+it S. Greg. ho. 22. in Ezech.\+it*\f* renting their garments, and loading their asses again, returned into the town.
\v 14 And Judas foremost with his brethren entered in to Joseph (for he was not yet gone out of the place) and they fell together before him on the ground.
\v 15 To whom he said: Why would you do so? Know you not that there is not the like to me in the science \f c \fr 44.15 \ft Joseph being indeed a Prophet, knowing more than all sorcerers in Ægypt spoke of himself as he was esteemed in that place. \+it S. Aug. q. 145. super Gen.\+it*\f* of divining.
\v 16 To whom Judas said: What shall we answer, my lord? Or what shall we speak, or be able justly to pretend? God hath found the iniquity of thy servants: lo we are all bondmen to my Lord, both we, & he with whom the cup was found.
\v 17 Joseph answered: God forbid that I should so do: he that stole the cup, the same be my bondman: and go you free unto your father.
\v 18 And Judas approaching nearer said boldly: I beseech thee my lord, let thy servant speak a word in thine ears, and be not angry with thy servant: for after Pharao thou art,
\v 19 my Lord. Thou didst ask thy servants the first time: Have you a father or a brother?
\v 20 and we answered thee my lord: We have a father an old man, and a little boy, that was born in his old age; whose brother by the mother is dead: and his mother hath him only, and his father loveth him tenderly.
\v 21 And thou saidst to thy servants: Bring him hither to me, and I will set mine eyes on him.
\v 22 We suggested to my lord: The boy cannot leave his father: for if he leave him, he will die.
\v 23 And thou saidst to thy servants: Unless your youngest brother come with you, you shall no more see my face.
\v 24 Therefore when we were gone up to thy servant our father, we told him all things that my lord did speak.
\v 25 And our father said: Go again, and buy us a little wheat.
\v 26 To whom we said: We cannot go: if our youngest brother shall go down with us, we will set forward together: otherwise he being absent, we dare not see the man's face.
\v 27 Whereunto he answered: You know that my wife bare me two.
\v 28 One went forth, and you said: A beast did devour him: and hitherto he appeareth not.
\v 29 If you take this also, and ought befall him in the way, you shall bring down \f d \fr 44.29 \ft See Gen. 42. v. 38.\f* my hoar hairs with sorrow unto hell.
\v 30 Therefore if I shall enter to thy servant our father, and the boy be wanting (whereas his life dependeth upon the life of him)
\v 31 and he shall see that he is not with us, he will die, and thy servants shall bring down his hoar hairs with sorrow unto hell.
\v 32 Let me be thy proper servant that did take him into my protection and promised, saying: Unless I bring him again I will be guilty of sin against my father for ever.
\v 33 I therefore thy servant will tarry instead of the child in the service of my lord, and let the child go up with his brethren.
\v 34 For I cannot return to my father, the child being absent; lest I stand by a witness of the calamity that shall oppress my father.
\c 45
\cd Joseph manifesteth himself to his brethren. Who being much terrified he comforteth them, and weeping embraceth every one. 16. The bruit whereof coming to Pharao, he congratulating commandeth Joseph to call his father with all his family into Ægypt. 21. So the eleven brothers are sent away with gifts & provision for Jacob's journey. 26. All which the father understanding is revived in spirit.
\p
\v 1 Joseph could no longer refrain many standing by in presence: whereupon he commanded that all should go forth, and no stranger should be present at their acknowledging one of another.
\v 2 And he lifted up his voice with weeping, which the Ægyptians heard, and all the house of Pharao.
\v 3 And he said to his Brethren: I am Joseph: is my father yet living? His brethren could not answer him being terribly astonished out of measure.
\v 4 To whom gently he said: Come hither to me. And when they were come near him, I am, quoth he, Joseph your brother, whom you sold into Ægypt.
\v 5 Be not afraid, neither let it seem to you a hard case that you did sell me into these countries: for \f a \fr 45.5 \ft God's providence turned their evil dealing to the good of the whole family, \+it chap. 50. v. 20.\+it*\f* God sent me before you into Ægypt for your preservation.
\v 6 For it is two years since the famine began to be upon the earth, and yet five years remain, wherein there can be neither earing nor reaping.
\v 7 And God sent me before, that you may be preserved upon the earth, and may have victuals to live.
\v 8 Not by your counsel, but by the will of God was I sent hither: who hath made me as it were a father to Pharao, and lord of his whole house, and Prince in all the land of Ægypt.
\v 9 Make haste and get ye up to my father, and you shall say to him: Thy son Joseph willeth thus: God hath made me lord of the whole land of Ægypt: come down to me, tarry not.
\v 10 And thou shalt dwell in the land of Gessen: and thou shalt be near me thou and thy sons, and thy son's children, thy sheep, and thy herds, and all things that thou dost possess.
\v 11 And there I will feed thee (for yet there are five years of famine remaining) lest both thou perish and thy house, and all things that thou dost possess.
\v 12 Behold, your eyes, and the eyes of my brother Benjamin do see, that it is my mouth that speaketh unto you.
\v 13 Report to my father my whole glory & all things that you have seen in Ægypt: make haste, and bring him to me.
\v 14 And falling upon the neck of his brother Benjamin, embracing him he wept: he also in like manner weeping upon his neck.
\v 15 And Joseph kissed all his brethren, and wept upon every one: after which things they were bold to speak unto him.
\v 16 And it was heard of, and very famously reported abroad in the king's court: The brethren of Joseph are come: and \f b \fr 45.16 \ft Joseph's prudent proceeding before he made himself known to his brethren and them to Pharao, procured all this joy and favour towards them in Ægypt.\f* Pharao was glad, and all his family.
\v 17 And he spake to Joseph that he should command his brethren, saying: Loading your beasts go into the Land of Chanaan,
\v 18 and take thence your father and kin, and come to me: and I will give you all the good things of Ægypt, that you may eat the marrow of the land.
\v 19 Give commandment also that they take wains out of the land of Ægypt, for the carriage of their little ones and wives: and say: Take up your father, and make haste to come with all speed.
\v 20 Neither do you leave any thing of your household stuff: for all the riches of Ægypt shall be yours.
\v 21 And the sons of Israel did as it was commanded them. To whom Joseph gave wains according to Pharao's commandment, and victuals for the way.
\v 22 He bade also to be brought up for every one two robes: but to Benjamin he gave three hundred pieces of silver with five robes of the best:
\v 23 sending to his father as much money & raiment, adding besides them he asses that should carry of all the riches of Ægypt, and as many she asses, carrying wheat for the journey and bread.
\v 24 Therefore he dismissed his brethren, and when they were departing he said to them: Be not angry in the way. \ef - \fr 45.24 \fq Be not angry in the way. \ft Travellers in journey are easily provoked to anger and brawling especially if they avoid not probable occasions ¹. Therefore Joseph admonisheth his brothers to beware thereof, lest in talking of him, and how they had sold him to strangers, some of them might accuse others, and excuse themselves, and so fall into new offences. S. Chrys. ho. 64. in Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Occasion of sin to be avoided.\ef*
\v 25 Who going up out of Ægypt, came into the land of Chanaan to their father Jacob.
\v 26 And they told him saying: Joseph thy son is living: and he ruleth in all the Land of Ægypt. Which when Jacob heard, awaking as it were out of a heavy sleep, notwithstanding did not believe them.
\v 27 They on the contrary side reported the whole order of the thing. And when he saw the wains and all things that he had sent, his spirit revived,
\v 28 and he said: It sufficeth me if Joseph my son be living yet: I will go, and see him before I die.
\c 46
\cd Israel warranted in a vision from God, goeth into Ægypt with all his family, 8. who are here recited. 28. Joseph meeting him in Gessen, adviseth him to tell Pharao that they are shepherds by their trade of life. \f - \ft The eighth and last part of this book.\f*
\p
\v 1 And \f 1 \fr 46.1 \ft Of Jacob and his progenies going into Ægypt. Of his and Joseph's death.\f* Israel taking his journey with all things that he had came to the well of the oath, and killing there victims to the God of his father Isaac, \ef - \fr 46.1 \fq Came to the well of oath. \ft ¹ In this holy place (called \+it Bersebee\+it*, that is, \+it well of oath\+it* ) where Abraham and Isaac had confirmed by oath their league with the Kings of the country, and erected Altars, Jacob also consulted God about his going into Ægypt, and was commanded to go with all that he had. \fq ¹ \ft God revealeth his will in holy places.\ef*
\v 2 he heard him by a vision of the night calling him, and saying unto him: Jacob, Jacob. To whom he answered: Lo here I am.
\v 3 God said to him: I am the most mighty God of thy father: fear not, go down into Ægypt, for into a great nation will I make thee there.
\v 4 I will go down with thee thither, and thence will I bring thee returning: Joseph also shall put his hands upon thine eyes.
\v 5 And Jacob rose up from the well of the oath: and his sons took him up, with their little ones and wives in the wains, which Pharao had sent to carry the old man,
\v 6 and all that he had possessed in the Land of Chanaan: and he came into Ægypt with all his seed,
\v 7 His sons, and nephews, daughters, and all his progeny together.
\v 8 And these are the names of the children of Israel, that entered into Ægypt, himself with his children. His first-begotten Ruben.
\v 9 The sons of Ruben: Henoch and Phallu and Hesron and Charmi.
\v 10 The sons of Simeon: Iamuel and Jamin and Ahod, and Jachin and Sohar, and Saul the Son of Chananitess.
\v 11 The sons of Levi: Gerson and Caath and Merari.
\v 12 The sons of Juda: Her & Onan and Sela and Phares and Zara. And Her and Onan died in the land of Chanaan. And there were sons born to Phares: Hesron and Hamul.
\v 13 The sons of Issachar: Thola and Phua and Job and Semron.
\v 14 The sons of Zabulon: Sared and Elon and Iahelel.
\v 15 These are the sons of Lia, which she bare \f a \fr 46.15 \ft That is, She bare their fathers in Mesopotamia. \+it S. Aug. q. 131. in Gen.\+it*\f* in Mesopotamia of Syria with Dina his daughter. All the souls of his sons and daughters are thirty three.
\v 16 The sons of Gad: Sephion and Haggi and Siuni & Esebon and Heri and Arodi and Areli.
\v 17 The sons of Aser: Jamne and Jesua and Jessui and Beria, Sara also their sister. The sons of Beria: Heber and Melchiel,
\v 18 these be the sons of Zelpha, whom Laban gave to Lia his daughter, and these she bare to Jacob sixteen souls.
\v 19 The sons of Rachel Jacob's wife: Joseph and Benjamin.
\v 20 And there were sons born to Joseph in the Land of Ægypt, which Aseneth the daughter of Putiphar Priest of Heliopolis bare to him: Manasses and Ephraim.
\v 21 The sons of Benjamin: Bela and Bechor and Asbel and Gera and Naaman and Echi and Ros and Mophim and Ophim and Ared.
\v 22 These be the sons of Rachel, which she bare to Jacob: all the souls, fourteen.
\v 23 The sons of Dan: Husim.
\v 24 The sons of Nephthali: Jaziel and Guni and Ieser and Sallem.
\v 25 These be the sons of Bala, whom Laban gave to Rachel his daughter: and these she bare to Jacob; all the souls, seven.
\v 26 All the souls, that entered with Jacob into Ægypt, and that came out of his thigh, besides his son's wives, sixty six. \ef - \fr 46.26 \fq Sixty six. 27. Seventy. \ft ¹ The difficulty in these two verses, concerning the number of Israelites that were at first in Ægypt with Jacob, is easily explicated, that just sixty six, of his own issue came in with him; and himself being counted maketh sixty seven, adding also Joseph (who was there before) and his two sons Manasses and Ephraim (born there) they were in all seventy. But a far greater difficulty remaineth: for besides these seventy persons, the Septuagint Greek Interpreters number and name five more; to wit, a son and a nephew of Manasses (the first called \+it Machir\+it*, the other \+it Galaad\+it*,) and two sons of Ephraim (called \+it Sutalaam\+it*, and \+it Taam\+it*,) and one nephew (named \+it Edem\+it* ) which number seventy five S. Steven also citeth ², following the vulgar known Scripture of the Septuagint, rather than the Hebrew text. Now in what sense these five could be said to have entered into Ægypt with Jacob, being not then born, nay some of them not born during Jacob's life, for Jacob lived but seventeen years in Ægypt (chap. 47. v. 28.) and Joseph being married but nine years before (for this was the second dear year after the seven plentiful years) his sons could not exceed seven or eight years, when Jacob came to Ægypt; and so being but 24. or 25. years old at his death, could not then be grand fathers: how therefore these five named by the Septuagint, and some others, not then born, of the lines of Phares and Benjamin, recited here by Moyses, could be said to come with Jacob into Ægypt, S. Augustin findeth so insoluble, that he doubteth not to affirm, some great hidden mystery to be understood by the Septuagint Interpreters, in these numbers ³, not otherwise perhaps explicable according to the letter. \fq ¹ \ft A difficulty how many Israelites came at first into Ægypt. \fq ² \ft \+it Act. 7.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Numbers mystical, sometimes not explicable in the literal sense.\ef*
\v 27 And the sons of Joseph, that were born to him in the land of Ægypt, two souls, All the souls of the house of Jacob, that entered into Ægypt, were seventy.
\v 28 And he sent Judas before him to Joseph, that he should tell him, and he should come into Gessen to meet him.
\v 29 Wither when he was come, Joseph addressing his chariot went up to meet his father, unto the same place: and seeing him fell upon his neck, and as they embraced he wept.
\v 30 And his father said to Joseph: Now will I die with joy, because I have seen thy face, and do leave thee alive.
\v 31 But he spake to his brethren, and to all his father's house: I will go up, & will tell Pharao, and will say to him: My brethren, and my father's house that were in the Land of Chanaan, are come to me:
\v 32 and the men are pastors of sheep, & their trade is to feed flocks: their cattle, & herds, and all that they could have, they have brought with them.
\v 33 And when he shall call you & shall say: What is your trade?
\v 34 You shall answer: We thy servants are pastors, from our infancy until this present, both we and our fathers. And this you shall say, that you may dwell in the Land of Gessen, because the Ægyptians \f b \fr 46.34 \ft Ægyptians honouring sheep, goats, and kine for gods, detested them that did govern, kill, or eat those cattle.\f* detest all pastors of sheep.
\c 47
\cd Jacob with his sons being come into Gessen, Pharao granteth them the same place to dwell in. 13. The famine forceth the Ægyptians to sell all their goods, lands, and possessions to the King, 22. except the Priest's part, to whom the King alloweth necessary food, without paying for it. 27. After seventeen years Jacob adjureth Joseph to bury him amongst his Ancestors.
\p
\v 1 Joseph therefore going in told Pharao, saying: My father and brethren, their sheep and herds, and all things that they possess, are come out of the Land of Chanaan; and behold they stay in the Land of Gessen.
\v 2 The utmost also of his brethren five persons he presented before the king:
\v 3 whom he asked: What trade have you? They answered: We thy servants are pastors of sheep, both we, and our fathers.
\v 4 We are come to sojourn in the land, because there is no grass for thy servant's flocks, the famine being very sore in the land of Chanaan: and we desire thee to command that we thy servants may be in the Land of Gessen.
\v 5 And the king therefore said to Joseph: Thy father and thy brethren are come to thee.
\v 6 The Land of Ægypt is in thy sight: make them to dwell in the best place, & deliver them the Land of Gessen. And if so be thou know that there are industrious men among them, appoint them masters of my cattle.
\v 7 After this Joseph brought in his father to the king, and set him before him: who blessed him,
\v 8 and being asked of him: How many be the days of the years of thy life?
\v 9 He answered: The days of the pilgrimage of my life are an hundred thirty years, \f a \fr 47.9 \ft Every man's life is short, & replenished with many miseries. \+it Job. 14.\+it*\f* few and evil, and they are not come to the days of my fathers, in which they were pilgrims.
\v 10 And blessing the king, he went forth.
\v 11 But Joseph gave possession to his father and his brethren in Ægypt, in the best place of the land, in Rhamesses, as Pharao had commanded.
\v 12 And he nourished them, and all his father's house, allowing victuals to every one.
\v 13 For in the whole world there wanted bread. & famine oppressed the land, especially of Ægypt and Chanaan.
\v 14 Out of which he gathered together all the money for the selling of corn, & brought it in unto the king's treasure.
\v 15 And when the buyers wanted money, all Ægypt came to Joseph, saying: Give us bread: why die we before thee, our money failing?
\v 16 To whom he answered: Bring your cattle, and for them I will give you victuals, if you have not to pay.
\v 17 Which when they had brought, he gave them sustenance for horses, and sheep, and oxen, and asses: and he sustained them that year for the exchange of the cattle.
\v 18 And they came the second year, and said to him: We will not conceal from our lord, that our money failing, our cattle withal have failed: neither art thou ignorant, that we have nothing besides our bodies and land.
\v 19 Why therefore shall we die in thy sight? Both we and our land will be thine: buy us to be the king's bondmen, & give us seed, lest for default of tillers the land be turned into a wilderness.
\v 20 Joseph therefore bought all the Land of Ægypt, every man selling his possessions for the greatness of the famine. And he brought it under Pharao's hands,
\v 21 and all the people thereof from the farthest ends of Ægypt, even to the uttermost coasts thereof,
\v 22 saving the land of the Priests, which the king had delivered them: \f b \fr 47.22 \ft The Priests of Ægypt being not forced to labour for their living, found out the Mathematics as witnesseth Aristotle \+it in princ. Metaph.\+it*\f* to whom also a certain allowance of victuals was given out of the common barns, and therefore they were not driven to sell their possessions. \ef - \fr 47.22 \fq Saving the land of the Priests. \ft ¹ Let them hear which now live (saith S. Chrysostom) ² what great care men had in times past of the Priests of idols; & let them learn at least to yield like honour to true Priests, to whom the ministry of all divine offices is committed. ³ For if the Ægyptians in their errors had so great care of Idols, thinking them to be more honoured if their Ministers were respected, how great condemnation do they deserve, that now diminish that which pertaineth to the provision of Priests? ⁴ Do ye not know that the honour pertaineth to God himself? Regard not therefore him to whom the honour is exhibited. For it is not for his cause to whom thou doest it, but for his sake whose Priest he is, that of him thou mayest abundantly receive rewards. Wherefore he said: \+it He that shall do it to one of these, hath done it for me;\+it* & \+it he that receiveth a Prophet in the name of a Prophet, shall receive the reward of a Prophet\+it* ⁵. Will our Lord reward thee according to the worthiness or means of his Ministers? According to thine own alacrity he either crowneth or condemneth &c. I say not this for the Priest's sakes; but for yours, desiring to gain you in all things. For in lieu of that little you give, you shall receive immortal rewards, and unspeakable good. Let us consider these things, & haste to serve them, not looking upon the cost, but upon God's Priests, he accounteth it as bestowed on himself. And he that so bestoweth, shall not only receive like retribution, but manifold greater; our merciful God always of the aboundance of his mercy exceeding the things which are done by us. Let us not therefore be worse than infidels, who for the error of idols gave so much to their servants; for how much error and truth do differ, so much difference is there between theirs and God's Priests. Thus much & something more writeth S. Chrysostom upon this place. \fq ¹ \ft The immunity and care of Priests in the law of nature. \fq ² \ft \+it Ho. 65. in Gen.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Yea amongst Infidels. \fq ⁴ \ft Much more amongst Christians, Priests ought to be respected. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Math. 25. & 10.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 47.22 \fq Priests. \ft The Hebrew word ¹ \+it Cohenim\+it* is here universally translated \+it Priests\+it* in all languages and Editions: which (chap. 40. v. 45.) some translate \+it Prince\+it*: and more probably (2. Reg. 8. v. ult.) where David's sons are called \+it Cohenim\+it*: who were indeed Princes, and not properly Priests. But in this present place it signifieth those, to whom Pharao allowed particular provision in the time of dearth, which all understand of Priests, and not of Princes. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Cohen\+it* in some places signifieth Prince, but is here translated \+it Priest\+it*, in all the English Bibles.\ef*
\v 23 Joseph therefore said to the people: Behold as you see, Pharao possesseth both you and your land: take seed, and sow the fields,
\v 24 that you may have corn. The fifth part you shall give to the king: the other four Iam content you shall have for seed, & for food to your families & your children.
\v 25 Who answered: Our life is in thy hand: only let our lord have a respect unto us, and we will gladly serve the king.
\v 26 From that time until this present day in the whole land of Ægypt, the fifth part is payed to the kings, and it became as it were a law, saving the land of the Priests, which was free from this condition.
\v 27 Israel therefore dwelt in Ægypt, that is, in the Land of Gessen, and possessed it: and was increased, & multiplied exceedingly.
\v 28 And he lived in it seventeen years: and all the days of his life came to an hundred forty seven years.
\v 29 And when he saw that the day of his death approached, he called his son Joseph, and said to him: If I have found grace in thy sight, put thy hand under my thigh: and thou shalt do me this mercy and truth. Not to bury me in Ægypt:
\v 30 but I will sleep with my fathers, and take me away out of this land, & bury me in the sepulchre of my ancestors. To whom Joseph answered: I will do that thou hast commanded. \ef - \fr 47.30 \fq I will sleep with my fathers. \ft ¹ Albeit neither the lack of burial, nor any cruelty nor contumely used against dead bodies, can annoy the just, for \+it those that kill men's bodies, can afterwards do them no more harm\+it* ²: Yet it is both a lawful natural desire, and a spiritual comfort and profit, to be buried in special places, where their own friends, or holy persons are buried, or where God is more specially served, Sacrifice and other prayers offered. And so both Jacob and Joseph desired to rest in the land of Chanaan, where their parents were buried, and where Christ should be born and redeem mankind. ³ But worldly pomp and honour of funerals are rather the comfort of the living, than the relief of the departed, as S. Augustin teacheth in Psal. 115. For in the sight of men, the troop of servants (saith the same S. Augustin lib. 1. c. 13. de civit.) made solemn & glorious exequies to the rich glutton, that was clothed in silk, & fared delicately in his life, but in the sight of God, the Angel's ministry made far more excellent to poor Lazarus, though they carried not his body into a marble tomb, but his soul into Abraham's bosom. \fq ¹ \ft Special place of burial lawfully desired, & spiritually profitable. \fq ² \ft \+it Luc. 12.\+it* \fq ³ \ft But pomp availeth not the dead.\ef*
\v 31 And he said: Swear then to me. Who swearing, Israel adored God, turning to the bed's head. \ef - \fr 47.31 \fq To the bed's head. \ft S. Paul alleging this place saith: \+it Jacob adored the top of\+it* (Joseph) \+it his rod\+it* ¹, following the ² Septuagint, who for the same Hebrew word (being without points, that is, without vowels) in this place say, \+it rod\+it*, and in the next chapter (v 2.) interpret \+it bed\+it*. For \+it Matteh\+it* signifieth \+it a rod\+it*, and \+it Mittah, a bed\+it*. The Latin therefore translating \+it bed\+it*, as the Hebrew is pointed, and the Septuagint, and S. Paul reading \+it rod\+it*, both are true, and both together express the whole action, that Jacob taking Joseph's \+it rod\+it* into his hand, and turning to \+it the bed's head\+it*, leaned on the top of the rod, and adored not only God, the Lord and giver of all good, but also his son Joseph now the chief ruler and Prince of Ægypt, as S. Augustin expoundeth q. 162. in Gen. And herein saith S. Chrysostom (ho. 66.) Joseph's dream was fulfilled, that the sun and moon should adore him. ³ The like saith Theodoret (q. 108. in Gen.) And Procopius addeth that Jacob adoring Joseph's rod, adored also Christ's Kingdom, prefigured by the same rod. But how adoration of creatures redoundeth to the honour of God, more is noted upon the said place of S. Paul Heb. 11. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Heb. 11.\+it* \fq ² \ft The Septuagint are not contrary to the Hebrew & Latin text, but supply that was omitted. \fq ³ \ft Adoration of God and creatures is not repugnant.\ef*
\c 48
\cd Joseph visiteth his father being sick. 5. Who adopteth his two son Manasses and Ephraim, 13. And blesseth them, preferring the younger before the elder, contrary to Joseph's mind. 22. And giveth a portion of land to Joseph, above his brethren.
\p
\v 1 These things being so done, it was told Joseph that his father was sick: who, taking his two sons Manasses and Ephraim, went forward.
\v 2 And it was told the old man: Behold thy son Joseph cometh to thee. Who being strengthened sat on his bed.
\v 3 And Joseph being entered in to him, he said: God Almighty appeared to me in Luza, which is in the Land of Chanaan: and he blessed me,
\v 4 and said: I will increase, and multiply thee, and make thee into multitudes of peoples: and I will give thee this land, and to thy seed after thee for an everlasting possession.
\v 5 Thy two sons therefore which were born to thee in the Land of Ægypt before I came hither to thee, shall be mine: Ephraim and Manasses, as Ruben and Simeon shall be reputed to me.
\v 6 But the rest begotten of thee after them, shall be thine, and shall be called by the name of their brethren in their possessions.
\v 7 For unto me, when I came out of Mesopotamia, Rachel died in the land of Chanaan in the very journey, and it was spring time: and I entered into Ephrata, and buried her by the way side to Ephrata, which by another name is called Bethleem. \x - \xt Mat. 2.\x*
\v 8 And seeing his sons he said to him: Who are these?
\v 9 He answered: They are my sons, whom God hath given me in this place. Bring them, quoth he, to me, that I may bless them.
\v 10 For Israel's eyes were dim by reason of very great age, and he could not see clearly. And when they were set beside him, kissing and embracing them,
\v 11 he said to his son: I am not defrauded of thy sight, moreover God hath shewed me thy seed.
\v 12 And when Joseph had taken them from his father's lap, he adored prostrate unto the ground.
\v 13 And he set Ephraim on his right hand, that is, on the left hand of Israel: but Manasses on his own left hand, to wit, on his father's right hand, and put them near to him.
\v 14 Who stretching forth his right hand, put it upon the head of Ephraim the younger brother: \f a \fr 48.14 \ft By this he made a cross prefiguring the Cross of Christ. Isidor. in hunc locum.\f* and the left upon the head of Manasses, that was the elder, changing hands. \ef - \fr 48.14 \fq Stretching forth his right hand. \ft ¹ ² As nature hath made the right hand readier to move, stronger to work and resist, and apter to frame and fashion any thing, so generally we use it more than the left. And when we use both hands at once, we ordinarily apply the right hand to the greater and more excellent effect, both in spiritual and corporal things. As in confirmation of fidelity or friendship, in blessing, writing, fighting, playing, and in most others things, we use the right hand, either only or chiefly. So the Patriarch Jacob laid his right hand upon Ephraim, knowing by prophetical spirit, ³ that he should be preferred before his elder brother Manasses. Literally fulfilled in Josue, Jeroboam, and other chief Princes of Ephraim's issue. And mystically in the Gentiles, being later called of God, and yet preferred before the Jews. \+it S. Cypri. li. 1. c. 21. adver. Iudeos. S. Amb. li. de Benedict. Patriarch. c. 1. S. Aug. li. 16. c. 42. de civit. &c.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The right hand also in spiritual things, preferred before the left. \fq ² \ft \+it Arist. li. 2. de cælo textu. 8. & de inces. animal. ca. 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The younger brother preferred signified the Gentiles before the Jews.\ef* \ef - \fr 48.14 \fq Changing hands. \ft The mystery, of the Gentiles excelling the Jews in time of grace, often prefigured by preferring the younger brother before the elder (Abel before Cain; Abraham before Nachor; Isaac before Ismael; Jacob himself before Esau; and now Ephraim before Manasses) is here further represented by Jacob's forming of a cross ¹ ², with his arms laid one over the other, when he blessed his two nephews: who otherwise might have laid his right hand first upon one, and then upon the other; or have caused them to change places; but he wittingly crossed his arms, and \+it changed his hands\+it*; or according to the Hebrew, \+it made his hands understand\+it*, that is, by his hands made it to be understood, not only that the younger should be in place of the elder, Ephraim before Manasses, and much more the Gentiles before the Jews, but also that this greater Mystery should be effected by Christ dying on a Cross. For what else could the very crossing of his arms, so wittingly and purposely done signify, but the form and figure of Christ's cross? As elsewhere the wood, which young Isaac carried on his back unto the mountain, prefigured the matter or substance of the same Cross. All accomplished when Christ was crucified: whereby the Jews were scandalized, and the Gentiles called and saved. Our Saviour himself foretelling, that \+it he being exalted\+it* (to wit upon the Cross) \+it would draw all unto himself\+it* ³. And S. Paul teaching that Christ \+it fastened the hand writing, that was against us, upon the Cross\+it* ⁴. \fq ¹ \ft The form of the Cross prefigured by Jacob crossing his arms. \fq ² \ft \+it Procop. Isidor. in Gen.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Joan. 12.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Collos. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 And Jacob blessed the sons of Joseph, and said: God, in whose sight my fathers Abraham & Isaac have walked, God that feedeth me from my youth until this present day:
\v 16 The Angel that delivereth me from all evils, bless these children: and be my name called upon them, the names also of my fathers Abraham, and Isaac, and grow they into a multitude upon the earth. \ef - \fr 48.16 \fq The Angel that delivereth me. \ft It is evident by this plain text, that Jacob was delivered from evils by an Angel ¹, and that he invocated the same Angel to bless his nephews, S. Basil \+it (li. 3. cont. Eunom. in initio)\+it* sheweth by this place among others, that an Angel is present with every one, \+it as a pedagogue, and pastor, directing his life\+it*. S. Chrysostom also \+it (ho. 7. in laud. S. Pauli)\+it* citeth this place in testimony that proper Angels are deputed to protect men. Yet Protestants say ², that \+it this Angel must be understood of Christ\+it*: remitting their gloss to the 31. ch. v. 13. and 32. v. 1. of Genesis, where it cannot be proved. ³ But the ancient Fathers teach the patronage & Invocation of Angels grounded in holy Scripture. Namely in this place, and many other places in the old Testament. Also Mat. 18. Act. 12. 1. Cor. 11. and the like. For example, S. Justinus Martyr in explic. qq. necess. q. 30. affirming it for a known truth, declareth that those Angels which receive the charge of guarding men, continue the same office either to both soul and body, or to the soul after it is parted from the body. S. Cyril of Alexandria (lib. 4. cont. Iulian. prope init.) shewing how God useth the ministry of holy Angels for men's salvation, saith: \+it Hi noxias a nobis abigunt feras\+it*, &c. These (Angels) drive away noisome wild beasts from us, and rescue those that are caught, from their cruelty, and teach what soever is laudable, to make our passage free, and not pestered, when with us they glorify one sovereign God. S. Chrysostom (ho. 60. in Math. 18.) S. Hierome upon the same place, S. Ambrose in Psal. 38. S. Augustin li. 83. qq. q. 79. and li. Soliloq. c. 27. S. Gregory li. 4. c. 31. in 3. Iob, S. Bernard ser. 5. Dedicat. Eccles. and ser. 12. in Psal. 90. and others, so universally teach the same, that Calvin (li. Instit. c. 14. sect. 38.) dare not deny it, and yet will needs doubt of it. \fq ¹ \ft Protection & Invocation of Angels. \fq ² \ft \+it Bible 1603.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Proved by ancient Fathers.\ef* \ef - \fr 48.16 \fq Be my name called upon them. \ft This place hath two good literal senses. For first it importeth that Ephraim and Manasses were made participant among the Tribes, of the blessings of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. Secondly, that God would bless them, for Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob's sake ¹: So Moyses praying for the whole people (Exodi. 31.) besought God to remember Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, and God was therewith pacified. \fq ¹ \ft God for his Saints' sake sheweth favour to their friends.\ef*
\v 17 And Joseph seeing that his father had put his right hand upon the head of Ephraim, took it heavily: and taking his father's hand he went about to lift it from Ephraim's head & to remove it upon the head of Manasses.
\v 18 And he said to his father: It is not convenient father so to be: because this is the first-begotten put thy right hand upon his head.
\v 19 Who refusing, said: I know my son, I know: and this same indeed shall be into peoples, and shall be multiplied: but his younger brother shall be greater than he: and his seed shall grow into nations.
\v 20 And he blessed them at that time, saying: In thee shall Israel be blessed, and it shall be said: God do unto thee as unto Ephraim, & as unto Manasses. And he set Ephraim before Manasses.
\v 21 And he said to Joseph his son: Behold I die, & God will be with you, and will bring you back into the land of your fathers.
\v 22 I do give thee one portion above thy brethren, which I took out of the hand of the Amorrhean with my sword and bow.
\c 49
\cd Jacob replenished with the spirit of prophecy, a little before his death, foretelleth his sons many things that shall happen to their posterity. Chargeth some of them with faults past, blesseth every one. 29. Appointeth where to bury him, 32. and dieth.
\p
\v 1 And Jacob called his sons, and said to them: Come together, that I \f a \fr 49.1 \ft These are predictions, not all blessings. \+it S. Amb. li. de Benedict Patriar.\+it*\f* may shew you the things that shall come to you in the last days.
\v 2 Come together, and hear you sons of Jacob, hear ye Israel your father.
\v 3 Ruben my first-begotten, thou art my strength, & the beginning of my sorrow: former in gifts, greater in Empire.
\v 4 Thou art poured out as water, \f b \fr 49.4 \ft A prophecy not an imprecation. \+it S. Aug. li. 12. c. 33. cont. Faust.\+it*\f* increase thou not: because thou didst ascend thy father's bed, and didst defile his couch. \ef - \fr 49.4 \fq Because thou didst ascend thy father's bed. \ft ¹ For this crime of incest Ruben was deprived of his first-birth-right. Who being by order of birth \+it former in gifts\+it*, whereby he should have had double portion; \+it and greater in empire\+it*, whereby he should have been Prince or Lord over his brethren, the former prerogative was given to Joseph, whose two sons were heads of two Tribes, the other was given to the Tribe of Juda, in David and his posterity. He was also deprived of his prerogative in Priesthood, which was after annexed to the Tribe of Levi, whereupon the Chaldee paraphrasis speaketh thus to Ruben: It belonged to thee to have received three better letters than thy brethren, Priesthood, Best-portion, and the Kingdom: But because thou hast sinned, the double portion is given to Joseph, the Kingdom to Judas, and Priesthood to Levi. \fq ¹ \ft Ruben for his sin was put from the prerogatives of the first-born.\ef*
\v 5 Simeon and Levi brethren: vessels of iniquity warring. \ef - \fr 49.5 \fq Vessels of iniquity. \ft ¹ ² Albeit Simeon and Levi were moved with just zeal to punish the foul crime committed by Sichem against their sister and whole family; yet in their manner of revenging were many sins worthily condemned by Jacob, both immediately after the fact, and here at his death. For before the slaughter they committed three great sins; in that they rashly did it unknown to their father, thereby putting him and themselves in extreme danger, if God had not marvelously protected them; in falsely pretending agreement and league with the Sichemites, which they meant not to perform; and in sacrilegiously abusing the Sacrament of Circumcision, making it a cloak to deceive their enemies. In the fact also they committed other four gross crimes: cruelly killing those that offered other aboundant satisfaction; murdering others that were altogether innocent; sacking and destroying the city, and carrying away women and children captive. Mystically S. Ambrose, Ruffinus, Isidorus, and others understand this prophecy of the Scribes and the Priests ³, descending of Simeon and Levi, who were most eager against our Saviour, as himself more plainly foretold, saying: \+it The son of man shall be betrayed to the chief Priests, and to the Scribes, and they shall condemn him to death.\+it* ⁴ Their fury was cursed above all fury, \+it Because\+it* (as Jacob here saith) \+it it was stubborn\+it*, or obstinate, for they did not only condemn Christ to death in their wicked Councel ⁵, but also urged and pressed Pilate, endeavouring to save him, and stirred up the people to cry: \+it Take him away, Crucify him\+it* ⁶. Yea their \+it indignation\+it* was so \+it hard\+it*, that they preferred Barabbas before Christ. \fq ¹ \ft The zeal of Simeon and Levi was commendable: but their manner of revenge, was many ways faulty. \fq ² \ft \+it Judith. 9.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The Priests' & Scribes' fury, obstinacy, and hard hearts against Christ. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Mat. 20.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Ioan. 11.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Ioan. 18.\+it*\ef*
\v 6 Into their counsel come not my soul, and in their congregation be not my glory: because in their fury they slew a man, and in their willfulness they undermined a wall.
\v 7 Cursed be their fury, because it is stubborn; and their indignation, because it is hard: I will divide them in Jacob, and will disperse them in Israel.
\v 8 Judas, thee thy brethren shall praise: thy hand shall be in the neck of thine enemies: thy father's children shall adore thee.
\v 9 Alion's whelp Judas: to the prey my son thou didst ascend: taking thy rest thou didst lie as a lion, and as it were a lioness, who shall raise him up?
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 49.10 \ft That these are most profound Mysteries, is easy to conceive, but most hard to understand them. In some the Patriarch recounteth things past in his life, foretelling the effects thereof to come. Other things he foresheweth pertaining to the division of the Land of Chanaan, others to the times of the Judges, of the Kings, of the Captivity, of Delivery from thence, of Christ, of Antichrist, and of the end of this world. Of all which diverse ancient fathers have written large commentaries, and godly treatises.\f* \sc The Sceptre Shall Not be Taken Away From Judas, And A duke Out of His Thigh, Till he do Come That is to be Sent, And The Same Shall be The Expectation of The Gentiles\sc*. \ef - \fr 49.10 \fq The sceptre shall not be taken away. \ft Here the Patriarch Jacob foretelleth the time, when the promised Messias should come into the world, by this sign that the sceptre should not be taken from Juda, till the same Redeemer of mankind were at hand ¹. Not that the regal sceptre should remain in the Tribe of Juda from Jacob's death till Christ's coming: for that Kingdom began first in David, about six hundred years after Jacob's death, and after the captivity of Babylon the high Priests of the Tribe of Levi did govern also the state, and not only the Church, other six hundred years. But the sense is, that of the Tribe of Juda should rise most glorious Kings, whose crown and Kingdom should remain with the Jewish Nation, until their expected Messias should draw near, and then be taken from them by the Gentiles. Which was done by Herod, whose father was an Idumean, his mother an Arabic. thus the Ancient fathers with one accord understood this Prophecy. S. Justinus Martyr \+it ser. cum Triphone\+it*. Eusebius Cesarien. lib. Hist. Eccles. cap. 6. S. Athanasius. \+it lib. de Incarnat.\+it* S. Ambrose \+it lib. de Benedict. Patriarch. c. 4.\+it* S. Chrysostom. \+it Ho. 67. in Gen.\+it* S. Augustin lib. 18. c. 45. de civit. Theodoretus q. ult. in Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Taking away of the regal sceptre from the Jews a sign of Christ's coming.\ef*
\v 11 Tying to the vineyard his colt, and to the vine, o my son, his she ass. He shall wash his stole in wine, and in the blood of the grape his cloak. \ef - \fr 49.11 \fq He shall wash his stole in wine. \ft ¹ By wine, and blood of the grape, what other thing is shewed (saith S. Cyprian Epist. ad Coecil. 63.) ² but the wine of the Chalice of our Lord's blood? Likewise Tertullian (lib. 4. contra Marcionem) expoundeth the stole to signify Christ's flesh, and the wine his blood. In all which book his drift is to shew that Christ did not destroy the old Testament, but fulfilled the figures and prophecy thereof. And not that Christ gave his body in figure only, as our adversaries allege him. \fq ¹ \ft Our Lord's real presence in the B. Sacrament prophesied. \fq ² \ft \+it li. 2. ep. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 12 His eyes are more beautiful than wine, and his teeth whiter than milk.
\v 13 Zabulon shall dwell in the shore of the sea, and in the road of ships reaching as far as Sidon.
\v 14 Issachar a strong ass lying at rest between the borders.
\v 15 He saw rest that it was good; and the earth that it was very good: and he put under his shoulder to carry, and became serving under tributes.
\v 16 Dan shall judge his own people, as also another tribe in Israel.
\v 17 Be Dan a snake in the way, a serpent in the path, biting the hoofs of the horse, that his rider may fall backward. \ef - \fr 49.17 \fq Be Dan a snake in the way. \ft ¹ This prophecy most ancient Fathers understood of Anti-Christ, namely S. Irenæus, \+it lib. 5. advers. Hæres.\+it* S. Hyppolytus Martyr \+it Ort. de consummat. soeculi.\+it* S. Ambrose. \+it c. 7. de Benedict. Patriarch.\+it* S. Augustin. q. 12. \+it in Iosue.\+it* Prosper. \+it lib. de promiss. & prædict. Dei. P. 4.\+it* Theodoret. q. \+it ult. in Gen.\+it* S. Gregory, \+it lib. 30. Moral. c. 18.\+it* and many others upon the 7. chap. of the Apocalypse. Where they suppose S. John did omit Dan from amongst the Elect of the Israelitical Tribes, in detestation of Antichrist, to be born of that Tribe. And certain it is, that the Jews will receive and follow him for their Messias ², as our Saviour himself saith: Which maketh it very probable, that he shall be a Jew born, else they would not so easily admit him. \fq ¹ \ft Ancient Fathers suppose that Antichrist shall be of the tribe of Dan. \fq ² \ft \+it Ioan. 5.\+it*\ef*
\v 18 I will expect \sc Thy Salvation\sc* o Lord.
\v 19 Gad, the girded shall fight before him: and himself shall be girded backward.
\v 20 Aser, his bread is fat, and he shall give dainties to kings.
\v 21 Nephthali, a hart let forth, and giving speeches of beauty.
\v 22 Joseph a child increasing, increasing and comely to behold: the daughters coursed to and fro upon the wall. \ef - \fr 49.22 \fq Joseph a child increasing. \ft ¹ Joseph was in many respect a figure of Christ, especially in that he was loved of his father before all his brethren, sold by his brethren to the Gentiles of envy and for money, advanced to dignity and authority, the deliverer of Ægypt from famine, and called Saviour of the world; all performed in Christ, the true Child increasing. \fq ¹ \ft Joseph in many things prefigured Christ.\ef*
\v 23 But the dart men did exasperate him, and brawled, and envied him.
\v 24 His bow sat upon the strong, and the bands of his arms and his hands were dissolved, by the hands of the Mighty of Jacob: thence came forth a pastor, the son of Israel.
\v 25 The God of thy father shall be thy helper, and the Almighty shall bless thee with the blessings of heaven from above, with the blessings of the depth that lieth beneath, with the blessings of the paps and of the womb.
\v 26 The blessings of thy father were strengthened with the blessings of his fathers: until the desire of the eternal hills came: be they upon the head of Joseph, and upon the crown of the Nazarite among his brethren.
\v 27 Benjamin \f c \fr 49.27 \ft This prophecy S. Augustin understandeth of S. Paul, of the tribe of Benjamin, who was first a persecutor, and after an Apostle of Christ. \+it Ser. 14. de Sanctis.\+it*\f* a ravening wolf, in the morning shall eat the prey, and in the evening shall divide the spoil.
\v 28 All these in the tribes of Israel twelve: these things spake their father to them, and he blessed every one, with their proper blessings.
\v 29 And he commanded them, saying: I am gathered unto my people: bury you me with my fathers in the double cave, which is in the field of Ephron the Hethite,
\v 30 Against Mambre in the Land of Chanaan, which Abraham bought with the field of Ephron the Hethite for a possession to bury in.
\v 31 There they buried him, and Sara his wife: there was Isaac buried with Rebecca his wife: there also Lia doth lie buried.
\v 32 And when he had finished the precepts wherewith he instructed his sons, he plucked up his feet upon the bed, and died; and he was put unto his people.
\c 50
\cd Joseph causeth his father's body to be embalmed; 3. the days of mourning being expired, 9. with Pharao's leave, Joseph with the ancients of Ægypt, all his brethren, and elder sort of Israelites, go and solemnly bury the body in Chanaan. 14. After their return, his brethren fearing lest Joseph will now revenge former injuries, he freely forgiveth all. 22. At the age of 110. years, adjuring the posterity to carry his bones into Chanaan he dieth, and is put in a coffin.
\p
\v 1 Which Joseph seeing fell upon his father's face weeping and kissing him.
\v 2 And he commanded his servants the physicians, that they should embalm his father with spices.
\v 3 Who fulfilling his commandments, there passed forty days; for this was the manner of corpses embalmed, and Ægypt mourned him seventy days.
\v 4 And the mourning time being expired, Joseph spake to the family of Pharao: If I have found grace in your sight, speak in the ears of Pharao:
\v 5 for so much as my father did adjure me, saying: Behold I die, in my sepulchre \f a \fr 50.5 \ft Jacob digged a sepulchre for himself, though it be not heretofore mentioned when he did it. \+it S. Aug. q. 170. in Gen.\+it*\f* which I digged for myself in the land of Chanaan, thou shalt bury me. I will go up therefore, and bury my father, and return.
\v 6 And Pharao said to him: Go up and bury thy father as thou wast adjured.
\v 7 Who going up, there went with him all the ancients of Pharao's house, and all the elders of the Land of Ægypt:
\v 8 the house of Joseph with his brethren, saving their little ones, and the flocks and herds, which they had left in the Land of Gessen.
\v 9 He had also in his train chariots and horsemen: and it became no small multitude.
\v 10 And they came to the floor of Atad, which is situate beyond Jordan: where celebrating the exequies with great and vehement mourning, they spent full seven days.
\v 11 Which when the Inhabiters of the Land of Chanaan had seen, they said: This is a great mourning unto the Ægyptians. And therefore the name of that place was called, The mourning of Ægypt.
\v 12 Therefore the sons of Jacob did as he commanded them:
\v 13 and carrying him into the Land of Chanaan, they buried him in the double cave, which Abraham had bought with the field for a possession to bury in of Ephron the Hethite against Mambre.
\v 14 And Joseph returned into Ægypt with his brethren, and with all the train, his father being buried.
\v 15 After whose death his brethren fearing, & talking one with another: Lest perhaps he be mindful of the injury which he suffered, and requite us all the evil that we have done;
\v 16 they advertised him saying: Thy father commanded us before he died,
\v 17 that we should say thus much to thee in his words: I beseech that thou forget the wicked fact of thy brethren, and the sin and malice which they have exercised against thee: we also desire thee, that to the servants of the God of thy father thou remit this iniquity. Whom when Joseph had heard he wept.
\v 18 And his brethren came to him; and \f b \fr 50.18 \ft This word \+it adoring\+it* often in holy Scripture signifieth civil honour; as here it can have no other sense.\f* adoring prostrate on the ground they said: We are thy servants.
\v 19 To whom he answered: Fear not; can we resist the will of God?
\v 20 You thought evil against me, but God turned that into good, that he might exalt me, as presently you see, and might save many peoples. \ef - \fr 50.20 \fq You thought evil. \ft ¹ This plain distinction sheweth, that sin is wholly of the sinner; and that God hath no part therein, but turneth it to good. For those things which Joseph's brethren did against him, were occasions of his advancement in Ægypt, through the omnipotent wisdom of God. Whose property is, out of every evil to draw good. S. Chrysost. ho. 67. in Gen. S. Aug. \+it Enchirid. c. 11. & li. 14. c. 27. de civit.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Man's will, not God the cause of sin.\ef*
\v 21 Fear not: I will feed you and your little ones, and he comforted them, and spake gently & mildly.
\v 22 And he dwelt in Ægypt, with all his father's house: and lived an hundred and ten years. And he saw the children of Ephraim unto the third generation. Also the children of Machir the son of Manasses were born in Joseph's knee.
\v 23 Which things being done he spake to his brethren: After my death God will visit you, and will make you go up out of this land, to the land which he sware to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
\v 24 And when he had adjured them and said. God will visit you carry my bones with you out of this place: \ef - \fr 50.24 \fq Carry my bones with you. \ft For the same reason Joseph would be finally buried in Chanaan, for which Jacob desired to be there buried (chap. 47.) but Joseph would not presently be carried thither ¹, lest it might have given offence to the Ægyptians, or at least have diminished their favour towards his brethren: & withal he would confirm his brethren in their hope of returning, seeing he was content, that his body should expect in Ægypt, till the whole Nation should return into Chanaan. \fq ¹ \ft Joseph, for his brethren's sake, deferred his desired burial in Chanaan.\ef*
\v 25 he died, being an hundred and ten years old. And being embalmed with spices, was put in a coffin in Ægypt.
